INSERT INTO `stamps` (`id`, `country`, `scott`, `count`, `condition`, `hinged`, `gum`, `grade`, `description`, `price`, `location`) VALUES
(1, 'United States', '147', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '3¢ Green – George Washington, 1870\r\n(Second Bank Note Issue – Engraved, Perf 12, Hard White Wove Paper, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #147 is the 3¢ green value of the Second Bank Note Issue printed by the National Bank Note Company. The design features a left-facing portrait of George Washington within an engraved oval frame, surrounded by scrollwork and fine shading. “U.S. POSTAGE” appears at the top, while “THREE CENTS” is displayed in a curved ribbon below. This used, no gum example retains strong green color and clear detail, though the front cancel and reverse bleed-through affect its presentation.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #147 begins with Fine 70 centering, with the bottom and left margins narrower than the top and right while remaining clear of the design. Minor Fault soundness lowers the technical grade to Very Good 50 because of heavy cancellation bleed-through on the reverse. The heavy segmented bar cancellation reduces eye appeal by 10 points. Final Grade: Good/Very Good 40. This is a collectible used example with strong color and intact perforations, though the heavy cancellation and bleed-through prevent a higher final grade.', 0.47, 'NA01-0023-04-01-003'),
(3, 'United States', '147', '004', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '3¢ Green – George Washington, 1870\r\n(Second Bank Note Issue – Engraved, Perf 12, Hard White Wove Paper, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #147 is the 3¢ green value of the Second Bank Note Issue printed by the National Bank Note Company. The design features a left-facing portrait of George Washington within an engraved oval frame, surrounded by scrollwork and fine shading. “U.S. POSTAGE” appears at the top, while “THREE CENTS” is displayed in a curved ribbon below. This used, no gum example retains strong green color and clear detail, though the front cancel and reverse bleed-through affect its presentation.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #147 begins with Fine 70 centering, with the bottom and left margins narrower than the top and right while remaining clear of the design. Minor Fault soundness lowers the technical grade to Very Good 50 because of heavy cancellation bleed-through on the reverse. The heavy segmented bar cancellation reduces eye appeal by 10 points. Final Grade: Good/Very Good 40. This is a collectible used example with strong color and intact perforations, though the heavy cancellation and bleed-through prevent a higher final grade.', 0.47, 'SL01-0001-01-01-004'),
(4, 'United States', '147', '006', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '3¢ Green – George Washington, 1870\r\n(U.S. Bank Note Issue – National Bank Note Company, Engraved, Perf 12, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #147, issued in 1870 by the National Bank Note Company, features a left-facing portrait of George Washington within an oval frame and ornamental scrollwork. “U.S. POSTAGE” appears at the top, with “THREE 3 CENTS” below. The green color remains strong, and the engraved design is identifiable despite the barred cancellation. This used example is without original gum, and the front remains presentable for a Bank Note issue.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #147 begins with Fine/Very Fine 77 centering, with the left margin narrowest and the right margin widest while all sides remain clear of the design. Minor Fault soundness lowers the technical grade to Very Good 57 due to the small corner crease, corner perf crease, and short perforation. The heavy barred cancellation reduces eye appeal by 10 points. Final Grade: Good/Very Good 47. This is a collectible used Bank Note example with solid centering and a distinct cancel, though the corner faults and heavy strike prevent a higher final grade.', 0.47, 'SL01-0001-05-02-006'),
(5, 'United States', '156', '007', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '1¢ Ultramarine – Benjamin Franklin, 1873\r\n(Bank Note Issue – Continental Bank Note Company, Engraved Printing, Perf 12, Hard White Paper)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #156 is the 1¢ ultramarine Bank Note issue printed by the Continental Bank Note Company in 1873. The design features a left-facing portrait of Benjamin Franklin enclosed within an ornate oval frame, with “U.S. POSTAGE” at the top and “ONE CENT” below. Printed on hard white paper and perforated 12, the stamp continues the classic engraved style of the Bank Note period. This used example remains visually complete and identifiable despite several condition issues affecting its overall grade.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #156 begins with Fine/Very Fine 79 centering, with the top and right margins somewhat narrower than the left and bottom while remaining clear of the design. Major Fault soundness lowers the technical grade to Good 34 due to multiple short or pulled perforations, a natural straight edge, a light diagonal crease, and a minor perforation crease. The neat circular cancellation does not require an eye appeal deduction. Final Grade: Good 34. This example retains attractive centering and a clear Franklin portrait, but the combination of perforation defects and edge issues prevents a higher numerical grade.', 1.12, 'SL01-0001-02-06-007'),
(6, 'United States', '156', '009', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Poor', '1¢ Ultramarine – Benjamin Franklin, 1873\r\n(Bank Note Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 12, Hard White Paper)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #156 was issued in 1873 by the Continental Bank Note Company and features a left-facing portrait of Benjamin Franklin within an ornate oval frame. “U.S. POSTAGE” appears at the top and “ONE CENT” at the bottom. Printed in ultramarine on hard white paper and perforated 12, the stamp reflects the classic engraved style of the Bank Note period. This used example remains complete and identifiable, although several condition issues and a strong cancellation affect the final grade.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #156 begins with Very Good 58 centering, with the top and left margins extremely tight and the perforations approaching or touching the design. Fault soundness lowers the technical grade to Fair/Good 23 due to three very short perforations and two perforation tip creases. A very heavy target-style cancellation further reduces eye appeal by 15 points because it obscures much of Franklin’s face. Final Grade: Poor 8. The combination of centering limitations, perforation defects, and heavy cancellation results in a low final numerical grade.', 0.22, 'SL01-0001-03-02-009'),
(7, 'United States', '156', '017', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair', '1¢ Ultramarine – Benjamin Franklin, 1873\r\n(Banknote Issue – National Bank Note Printing, Soft Paper, Perf 12)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #156 is a 1¢ ultramarine stamp issued in 1873 as part of the National Bank Note Company’s Banknote series. The design features a left-facing portrait of Benjamin Franklin enclosed within a dark oval frame, with “U.S. POSTAGE” above and “ONE CENT” below. Printed on soft porous paper and perforated 12, the issue is a familiar classic of the Banknote period. This example is postally used and without original gum. The design remains complete and recognizable despite condition issues and a strong cancellation.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #156 begins with Fine/Very Fine 77 centering, with the left and bottom margins narrower than the top and right while remaining clear of the design. Major Fault soundness lowers the technical grade to Good 32 due to multiple ragged and shortened perforations together with a pulled perforation at the lower left. A very heavy circular cancellation further reduces eye appeal by 15 points because it obscures a significant portion of Franklin’s portrait. Final Grade: Fair 17. The combination of perforation defects and heavy cancellation outweighs the attractive centering and results in a low final numerical grade.', 0.45, 'SL01-0001-05-05-017'),
(8, 'United States', '156', '018', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Poor', '1¢ Ultramarine – Benjamin Franklin, 1873\r\n(Banknote Issue – National Bank Note Printing, Soft Paper, Perf 12)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #156 is a 1¢ ultramarine stamp from the 1873 National Bank Note Company series. It features a left-facing portrait of Benjamin Franklin within a shaded oval frame, with “U.S. POSTAGE” above and “ONE CENT” below. Printed on soft porous paper and perforated 12, the issue was widely used for circulars and printed matter. This example is postally used and without original gum. Although the design remains identifiable, significant edge alteration affects both appearance and technical grade.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #156 begins with Very Good 56 centering, with the left and bottom sides extremely close to the design while the top and right margins are more open. Severe Fault soundness lowers the technical grade to Fair 15 due to trimmed left and bottom edges where perforations are absent. A heavy horizontal cancellation further reduces eye appeal by 10 points. Final Grade: Poor 5. The combination of altered edges and a prominent cancellation outweighs the remaining design quality and results in the minimum grade assigned to an intact, non-damaged example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.22, 'SL01-0001-06-01-018'),
(9, 'United States', '156', '013', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Poor', '1¢ Ultramarine – Benjamin Franklin, 1873\r\n(Banknote Issue – National Bank Note Printing, Soft Paper, Perf 12)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #156 is a 1¢ ultramarine stamp issued in 1873 during the Banknote period. The design features a left-facing portrait of Benjamin Franklin within an ornate oval frame, with “U.S. POSTAGE” above and “ONE CENT” below. Printed on soft porous paper and perforated 12, the issue is a familiar classic of nineteenth-century United States philately. This example is postally used and without original gum. Heavy wear and multiple faults affect both appearance and grade, though the stamp remains intact and identifiable.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #156 begins with Good 32 centering, with perforations cutting into the design on the left side. Major Fault soundness lowers the technical grade to Fair 12 due to a moderate crease, a pulled perforation, a short perforation, and a perforation tip crease. A very heavy grid cancellation further reduces eye appeal by 15 points because it obscures much of Franklin’s portrait and portions of the lettering. Final Grade: Poor 5. The combination of severe centering issues, multiple faults, and a very heavy cancellation results in the minimum grade assigned to an intact, non-damaged stamp under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.22, 'SL01-0001-04-02-013'),
(10, 'United States', '157', '006', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '2¢ Red Brown – Andrew Jackson, 1873\r\n(Banknote Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 12, No Grill)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #157 is a 2¢ red brown stamp issued in 1873 featuring a finely engraved portrait of Andrew Jackson, the seventh President of the United States. Part of the Banknote series printed by the Continental Bank Note Company, the design shows Jackson facing left within an ornate oval frame. “U.S. POSTAGE” appears across the top and “TWO 2 CENTS” is displayed below. This example is postally used and without original gum. The design remains complete and readily identifiable, although several condition issues affect the final grade.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #157 begins with Fine/Very Fine 77 centering, with the left and bottom margins narrower than the top and right while remaining clear of the design. Fault soundness lowers the technical grade to Good 47 due to multiple ragged and shortened perforations, a short perforation, perforation creases, and light ink transfer on the reverse. A heavy circular cancellation further reduces eye appeal by 10 points. Final Grade: Good 37. The combination of attractive centering, noticeable perforation faults, and a moderately strong cancellation results in a collector-grade example that remains visually recognizable but falls well below premium condition.', 5.06, 'LA01-0007-01-02-006'),
(11, 'United States', '156', '005', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Poor', '1¢ Ultramarine – Benjamin Franklin, 1873\r\n(Bank Note Issue – Engraved, Hard White Paper, Perf 12)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #156 was issued in 1873 as the first 1¢ stamp produced under the Continental Bank Note Company contract. The design features a left-facing portrait of Benjamin Franklin within an ornate oval frame, with “U.S. POSTAGE” above and “ONE CENT” below. Printed in ultramarine on hard white paper and perforated 12, it continued the engraved Bank Note style familiar to collectors of the period. This example is postally used and without original gum. While the design remains identifiable, significant centering problems, physical faults, and a strong cancellation affect the overall grade.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #156 begins with Good 30 centering, with perforations cutting into the design on the top and left sides. Fault soundness lowers the technical grade to Fair 13 due to a corner crease, gum disturbance, two perforation tip creases, and a pencil annotation on the reverse. A very heavy cancellation further reduces eye appeal by 15 points because it obscures much of Franklin’s portrait and upper lettering. Final Grade: Poor 5. The combination of severe centering deficiencies, multiple faults, and a dominant cancellation results in the minimum grade assigned to an intact, non-damaged stamp under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.22, 'SD22-0001-04-01-005'),
(12, 'United States', '158', '004', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair', '3¢ Green – George Washington, 1873\r\n(Bank Note Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 12, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #158 was issued in 1873 by the Continental Bank Note Company as part of the Bank Note series. The design features a left-facing portrait of George Washington surrounded by ornate scrollwork, with “U.S. POSTAGE” above and “THREE CENTS” below. Printed by line engraving on unwatermarked paper and perforated 12, the issue continued the long tradition of Washington portraits on United States postage. This example is postally used and without original gum. The design remains complete and recognizable despite several condition issues.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #158 begins with Very Good 55 centering, with the design shifted toward the lower right and the perforations touching or nearly touching the frameline along the bottom and right sides. Fault soundness lowers the technical grade to Fair/Good 24 due to three short perforations and two corner perforation creases. A heavy segmented cancellation further reduces eye appeal by 10 points because it obscures portions of Washington’s portrait. Final Grade: Fair 14. The combination of tight margins, multiple perforation faults, and a noticeable cancellation results in a lower-grade but collectible example of this Bank Note issue.', 0.08, 'SD22-0001-04-03-004'),
(13, 'United States', '159', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '6¢ Dull Pink – Abraham Lincoln, 1873\r\n(Bank Note Issue – Engraved, Perf 12, Hard White Paper)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #159 is a 6¢ dull pink stamp issued in 1873 as part of the Continental Bank Note Company’s Bank Note series. The design features a left-facing portrait of Abraham Lincoln within an oval frame surrounded by ornate engraved ornamentation. “U.S. POSTAGE” appears above the portrait, while the denomination is displayed below. Printed on hard white paper and perforated 12, the issue served a variety of domestic and international postal rates. This example is postally used and without original gum. The design remains complete and attractive despite several perforation faults and a noticeable cancellation.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #159 begins with Fine/Very Fine 77 centering, with all perforations clear of the design and the top margin slightly narrower than the others. Fault soundness lowers the technical grade to Good 47 due to one pulled perforation and several shortened perforations. A heavy grid cancellation further reduces eye appeal by 5 points because it obscures portions of Lincoln’s face. Final Grade: Good/Very Good 42. The combination of attractive centering, moderate perforation faults, and a noticeable but not overwhelming cancellation results in a respectable collector-grade example of this classic Bank Note issue.', 5.60, 'LA01-0007-02-01-003'),
(14, 'United States', '178', '009', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '2¢ Vermilion – Andrew Jackson, 1875\r\n(1875 Continental Bank Note Printing – National Bank Note Design, Grilled, Perf 12)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #178 is a 2¢ vermilion stamp featuring a profile portrait of Andrew Jackson, printed by the Continental Bank Note Company in 1875. The design reused the earlier National Bank Note portrait format, with Jackson enclosed in an ornate oval frame and surrounded by decorative engraving. The vivid vermilion color is characteristic of the issue and remains one of its most recognizable features. This example is postally used and without original gum. While the design remains complete and attractive, several minor faults and a strong cancellation affect the final grade.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #178 begins with Fine 71 centering, with narrow but visible margins on the left and bottom sides and more open spacing on the top and right. Very Minor Fault soundness lowers the technical grade to Very Good/Fine 61 due to a corner perforation crease and one shortened perforation. A very heavy cancellation further reduces eye appeal by 15 points because it obscures much of Jackson’s portrait and central oval. Final Grade: Good/Very Good 46. The combination of attractive centering, minor perforation faults, and a strong cancellation results in a sound collector-grade example of this Continental Bank Note issue.', 4.13, 'SL01-0001-06-02-009'),
(15, 'United States', '178', '014', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '2¢ Vermilion – Andrew Jackson, 1875\r\n(Continental Bank Note Co. Printing – National Design, Grilled, Perf 12)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #178 is a 2¢ vermilion stamp issued in 1875 by the Continental Bank Note Company. The design features a profile portrait of Andrew Jackson enclosed within an ornate frame, with “U.S. POSTAGE” above and “TWO 2 CENTS” below. Printed on grilled paper and perforated 12, the issue represents a classic example of the late Bank Note period. This example is postally used and without original gum. The design remains complete and attractive, with only minor condition issues affecting the final grade.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #178 begins with Fine 71 centering, with the bottom margin narrower than the remaining sides while retaining visible white space around the design. Minor Fault soundness lowers the technical grade to Good/Very Good 41 due to light reverse smudging and a corner perforation crease. A neat circular date cancellation has no impact on eye appeal and results in no further adjustment. Final Grade: Good/Very Good 41. The combination of acceptable centering, minor faults, and a clean cancellation produces a respectable collector-grade example of this Continental Bank Note issue.', 4.13, 'SL01-0001-07-01-014'),
(17, 'United States', '178', '015', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '2¢ Vermilion – Andrew Jackson, 1875\r\n(Continental Bank Note Co. Printing – National Design, Grilled, Perf 12)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #178 is a 2¢ vermilion stamp from the 1875 Continental Bank Note Company printing. The design features a left-facing portrait of Andrew Jackson within an ornate oval frame, with “U.S. POSTAGE” above and “TWO 2 CENTS” below. Printed on grilled paper and perforated 12, it is a classic issue of the Bank Note period. This example is postally used and without original gum. The design remains intact, although a very heavy cancellation significantly affects visual appeal.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #178 begins with Fine 71 centering and Very Minor Fault soundness, producing a preliminary grade of Very Good/Fine 61. A single perforation tip crease is the only condition issue. An obliterated cancellation reduces eye appeal by 20 points because it obscures most of Jackson’s portrait and inscriptions. Final Grade: Good/Very Good 41. The strong centering and otherwise sound condition are offset by the exceptionally heavy cancellation.', 4.13, 'SL01-0001-07-02-015'),
(18, 'United States', '178', '017', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '2¢ Vermilion – Andrew Jackson, 1875\r\n(Banknote Re-Issue of the 1870-71 National Bank Note Issue – Engraved, Hard White Paper, Perf 12)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #178 is the 2¢ vermilion Andrew Jackson stamp issued by the Continental Bank Note Company as part of the 1875 reissues of earlier National Bank Note stamps. Printed from the original engraved designs on hard white paper and perforated 12, the stamp features a profile portrait of Jackson within an ornate oval frame. “U.S. POSTAGE” appears above and “TWO 2 CENTS” below. This example is postally used and without original gum. The design remains complete and attractive despite creasing and a noticeable cancellation.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #178 begins with Fine/Very Fine 79 centering, with clear margins surrounding the design and only minor imbalance between sides. Fault soundness lowers the technical grade to Good/Very Good 49 due to a moderate crease, a secondary crease, and light reverse bleed-through. A heavy target cancellation further reduces eye appeal by 10 points because it obscures part of Jackson’s portrait. Final Grade: Good 39. The attractive centering and intact perforations are offset by creasing and a strong cancellation, resulting in a lower-grade but collectible example of this desirable reissue.', 2.95, 'SL01-0001-07-04-017'),
(19, 'United States', '182', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair/Good', '1¢ Blue – Benjamin Franklin, 1879\r\n(Banknote Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 12, Soft Paper)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #182 is a 1¢ blue stamp issued in 1879 as part of the American Bank Note Company printings of the classic Banknote designs. The stamp features a finely engraved portrait of Benjamin Franklin enclosed within an ornate oval frame, with “U.S. POSTAGE” above and “ONE CENT” below. Printed on soft porous paper and perforated 12, the issue continued a design that had become familiar to American postal users during the nineteenth century. This example is postally used and without original gum. The design remains complete and recognizable, although perforation defects and a strong cancellation substantially affect the final grade.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #182 begins with Fine 70 centering, with narrow top and right margins but visible white space surrounding the design. Major Fault soundness lowers the technical grade to Fair/Good 25 due to multiple short, clipped, and pulled perforations. A heavy circular cancellation further reduces eye appeal by 5 points because it obscures portions of Franklin’s portrait. Final Grade: Fair/Good 20. The combination of acceptable centering, significant perforation damage, and a noticeable cancellation results in a lower-grade but still identifiable example of this classic Banknote issue.', 0.76, 'NA01-0029-01-01-002'),
(20, 'United States', '183', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '2¢ Red Brown – Andrew Jackson, 1879\r\n(1879 American Bank Note Company Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 12)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #183 is a 2¢ red brown issue released in 1879 by the American Bank Note Company following its assumption of United States stamp production. The design features a left-facing portrait of Andrew Jackson enclosed within an ornate oval frame, with “U.S. POSTAGE” above and “TWO CENTS” below. Printed by engraving and perforated 12, the issue continued the familiar Bank Note style while introducing subtle production differences associated with the new contract. This example is postally used and without original gum. The design remains complete and well defined, although the cancellation reduces visual clarity.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #183 begins with Fine 70 centering, with visible white space surrounding the design despite somewhat uneven margins. Very Minor Fault soundness lowers the technical grade to Very Good/Fine 65 due to a hinge remnant on the reverse. A moderate circular cancellation further reduces eye appeal by 5 points because it partially obscures Jackson’s portrait. Final Grade: Very Good/Fine 60. The combination of sound perforations, acceptable centering, and only minor condition concerns results in a respectable collector-grade example of this American Bank Note Company issue.', 2.66, 'NA01-0029-01-02-002'),
(21, 'United States', '183', '001', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair', '2¢ Vermilion – Andrew Jackson, 1879\r\n(Banknote Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 12, Soft Paper)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #183 is a 2¢ vermilion stamp issued in 1879 by the American Bank Note Company as part of its continuation of the classic Banknote designs. The stamp features a left-facing portrait of Andrew Jackson within an ornate oval frame, surrounded by decorative scrollwork. “U.S. POSTAGE” appears above the portrait and “TWO CENTS” below. Printed on soft porous paper and perforated 12, the issue remained a common sight on domestic mail during the late nineteenth century. This example is postally used and without original gum. While the design remains complete and visually appealing, a significant structural fault has a major impact on the final grade.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #183 begins with Fine 71 centering, with narrower top and left margins balanced by more open spacing on the right and bottom. Major Fault soundness lowers the technical grade to Fair/Good 26 due to a large tear along the left edge. An average circular cancellation has no effect on eye appeal and results in no additional adjustment. Final Grade: Fair/Good 26. The attractive centering and generally sound appearance are outweighed by the significant tear, resulting in a lower-grade but still identifiable example of this American Bank Note issue.', 0.62, 'LA01-0008-01-02-001'),
(22, 'United States', '221', '005', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '3¢ Purple – Andrew Jackson, 1890\r\n(U.S. Regular Issue – First Bureau Issue, Unwatermarked Soft Paper)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #221 features a left-facing engraved portrait of Andrew Jackson surrounded by classical scrollwork and ornamental engraving. Large denomination numerals appear in the lower corners, with “THREE CENTS” centered beneath the portrait. Issued in 1890 as part of the final series produced by the American Bank Note Company, the stamp was printed on unwatermarked soft paper and perforated 12. This example is postally used and without original gum. The design remains attractive and well centered, although several reverse faults affect the technical grade.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #221 begins with Fine/Very Fine 78 centering, with balanced side margins and clear white space visible on all four sides. Fault soundness lowers the technical grade to Good/Very Good 48 due to a small thin, hinge remnant, and light pencil notation on the reverse. A light circular cancellation improves eye appeal by 10 points because it preserves the portrait and overall design clarity. Final Grade: Very Good 58. The combination of strong centering, intact perforations, and an attractive cancellation helps offset the reverse faults and produces a pleasing collector-grade example of this 1890 issue.', 3.62, 'SD22-0001-06-04-005'),
(23, 'United States', '222', '004', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '4¢ Dark Brown – Abraham Lincoln, 1890\r\n(U.S. Regular Issue – First Bureau Issue, Lincoln Portrait)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #222, issued in 1890, features an engraved portrait of Abraham Lincoln enclosed within an ornate oval frame. The 4¢ dark brown denomination is part of the First Bureau Issue series, which honored prominent American historical figures through detailed engraved designs. Large denomination numerals appear in the lower corners, with “FOUR CENTS” centered beneath the portrait. This example is postally used and without original gum. The design remains complete and recognizable, although perforation faults and a strong cancellation affect the final grade.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #222 begins with Fine/Very Fine 76 centering, with visible white space surrounding the design despite narrower top and right margins. Minor Fault soundness lowers the technical grade to Very Good 56 due to three perforation tip creases and two shortened perforations. A heavy black cancellation further reduces eye appeal by 10 points because it obscures a significant portion of Lincoln’s portrait. Final Grade: Good/Very Good 46. The combination of attractive centering, noticeable perforation faults, and a strong cancellation results in a respectable collector-grade example of this popular First Bureau Issue.', 1.26, 'LA01-0009-02-02-004'),
(24, 'United States', '225', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '8¢ Violet Brown – William T. Sherman, 1890\r\n(U.S. Regular Issue – First Bureau Issue, Sherman Portrait)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #225 features a finely engraved portrait of General William Tecumseh Sherman printed in violet brown. Issued as part of the 1890 First Bureau Issue series, the stamp displays Sherman within an ornate frame surrounded by classical engraving. Prominent denomination numerals appear in the lower corners, while the portrait serves as the central focal point of the design. This example is postally used and without original gum. The design remains complete and recognizable, although centering and minor condition issues affect the final grade.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #225 begins with Very Good 52 centering, with narrow left and bottom margins and an overall uneven appearance. Minor Fault soundness lowers the technical grade to Good 32 due to a natural unpunched perforation and reverse notation. A light straight-line cancellation has no effect on eye appeal and results in no additional adjustment. Final Grade: Good 32. The combination of off-center positioning and minor condition issues outweighs the otherwise intact design, producing a lower-grade but collectible example of this First Bureau Issue Sherman stamp.', 3.51, 'LA01-0009-03-03-003'),
(25, 'United States', '226', '005', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair', '10¢ Green – Daniel Webster, 1890\r\n(U.S. Regular Issue – First Bureau Issue, Type I)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #226 was issued in 1890 as part of the First Bureau Issue series and features an engraved portrait of Daniel Webster surrounded by ornate scrollwork and decorative framing. Printed in deep green on unwatermarked soft paper and perforated 12, the stamp served higher postal rates including international mail and registry services. This example is postally used and without original gum. Although the design remains complete and identifiable, several significant condition issues substantially affect the final grade.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #226 begins with Fine 71 centering, with narrower top and left margins balanced by more open spacing on the right and bottom. Major Fault soundness lowers the technical grade to Fair/Good 26 due to a damaged upper-left corner, discoloration, hinge remnant, and a shortened perforation. A heavy circular cancellation further reduces eye appeal by 10 points because it obscures portions of Webster’s portrait and frame. Final Grade: Fair 16. The acceptable centering and recognizable design are outweighed by significant structural faults and a strong cancellation, resulting in a lower-grade but collectible example of this First Bureau Issue stamp.', 0.40, 'NA01-0033-04-04-005'),
(26, 'United States', '234', '001', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair', '5¢ Chocolate – Columbus Soliciting Aid of Isabella, 1893\r\n(Columbian Exposition Issue – Engraved, Perf 12)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #234 is the 5¢ value of the celebrated 1893 Columbian Exposition series. The design depicts Christopher Columbus appealing to Queen Isabella for support of his proposed voyage, surrounded by elaborate ornamental engraving characteristic of the Columbian commemoratives. “UNITED STATES OF AMERICA” appears across the top, while “POSTAGE FIVE CENTS” and the scene title are incorporated into the lower framework. This example is postally used and without original gum. Although the design remains complete and identifiable, condition issues significantly affect the stamp\'s overall grade.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #234 begins with Very Good 54 centering, with narrow and uneven margins that touch or slightly cut into the frameline on multiple sides. Major Fault soundness lowers the technical grade to Fair 17 due to numerous clipped, pulled, and shortened perforations along with slight overall color fading. No cancellation adjustment is applied because no distinct cancel is visible. Final Grade: Fair 17. The attractive Columbian design remains intact, but significant perforation faults and fading limit the stamp to a lower-grade collector or reference example.', 0.72, 'LA01-0010-02-01-001'),
(27, 'United States', '247', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair/Good', '1¢ Ultramarine – Benjamin Franklin, 1894\r\n(U.S. Regular Issue – First Bureau Issue, Unwatermarked, Soft Paper)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #247 was issued in 1894 as part of the First Bureau Issue series and was among the first stamps produced by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing. The design features a left-facing portrait of Benjamin Franklin within an ornate oval frame surrounded by decorative scrollwork. Printed in ultramarine on unwatermarked soft paper and perforated 12, it became a common workhorse issue of the period. This example is postally used and without original gum. While the design remains complete and recognizable, centering irregularities, perforation faults, and a strong cancellation reduce the overall grade.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #247 begins with Very Good 52 centering, with the right perforations cutting into the design and noticeably uneven margins. Minor Fault soundness lowers the technical grade to Good 32 due to a corner perforation crease and two shortened perforations. A heavy black cancellation further reduces eye appeal by 10 points because it obscures much of Franklin’s portrait and lower frame. Final Grade: Fair/Good 22.', 0.44, 'LA01-0011-01-02-002'),
(28, 'United States', '248', '004', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine/Very Fine', '2¢ Carmine – George Washington, 1894\r\n(U.S. Regular Issue – First Bureau Issue, Unwatermarked, Soft Paper)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #248 is a 2¢ carmine stamp issued as part of the 1894 First Bureau Issue produced by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing. The design features a left-facing portrait of George Washington enclosed within an ornate oval frame surrounded by detailed scrollwork. Printed on unwatermarked soft paper and perforated 12, the stamp continued the classic appearance of earlier United States definitive issues. This example is postally used and without original gum. The design remains sharp and well preserved, with strong centering and only minor condition concerns affecting the final grade.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #248 begins with Very Fine 81 centering, with full margins and clear white space surrounding the design on all sides. Very Minor Fault soundness lowers the technical grade to Fine/Very Fine 76 due solely to a hinge mark on the reverse. An average cancellation has no effect on eye appeal and results in no additional adjustment. Final Grade: Fine/Very Fine 76. The combination of strong centering, intact perforations, and minimal condition concerns makes this an attractive collector-grade example of the First Bureau Issue Washington stamp.', 5.86, 'LA01-0011-01-03-004'),
(29, 'United States', '248', '007', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair/Good', '2¢ Carmine – George Washington, 1894\r\n(First Bureau Issue – Engraved, Perf 12)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #248 is a 2¢ carmine stamp from the 1894 First Bureau Issue, produced by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing. It features a left-facing portrait of George Washington within an oval, framed by detailed geometric ornamentation. The inscriptions “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” and “TWO CENTS” complete the design. The engraving is clean and well-defined, typical of Bureau production. This example is used, hinged, and without original gum, showing moderate handling consistent with postal use.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #248 example shows Fine/Very Fine centering with clear separation between design and perforations, though with uneven margins. A small tear and stain elevate the stamp to the Major Fault category, resulting in a substantial deduction. The heavy cancel further reduces eye appeal, though the design remains legible. The final grade of 29 (Fair-Good) reflects the balance between decent centering and notable condition issues, leaving it as a modest but still collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 1.04, 'NA01-0037-02-01-007'),
(30, 'United States', '252', '001', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '2¢ Carmine – George Washington, 1895\r\n(U.S. Regular Issue – Bureau of Engraving and Printing, Unwatermarked, Soft Paper)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #252 is a 2¢ carmine definitive issued in 1895 by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing. The design features a left-facing portrait of George Washington enclosed within an ornate oval frame, surrounded by decorative scrollwork typical of late nineteenth-century Bureau issues. Printed on unwatermarked soft paper and perforated 12, the stamp served as a standard workhorse denomination of the period. This example is postally used and without original gum. The design remains attractive and well preserved despite a minor structural fault.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #252 begins with Fine/Very Fine 77 centering, with visible white space surrounding the design and no perforations approaching the frameline. Minor Fault soundness lowers the technical grade to Very Good 57 due to a light crease. An average wavy-line cancellation has no effect on eye appeal and results in no additional adjustment. Final Grade: Very Good 57. The combination of attractive centering, intact perforations, and strong color is offset only by the light crease, resulting in a solid collector-grade example of this Bureau Issue Washington stamp.', 5.28, 'LA01-0011-02-032-001'),
(31, 'United States', '252', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '2¢ Carmine – George Washington, 1895\r\n(U.S. Regular Issue – First Bureau Issue, Watermarked USPS, Soft Paper)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #252 was issued in 1895 as part of the First Bureau Issue series and features an engraved portrait of George Washington facing left within an ornate oval frame. Decorative corner ornaments and bold denomination tablets reflect the classic style of late nineteenth-century United States definitive stamps. Printed on soft paper and bearing the USPS watermark, the issue marked an important stage in the Bureau of Engraving and Printing\'s production of definitive postage. This example is postally used and without original gum. The design remains complete and attractive, though several faults reduce the final grade.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #252 begins with Very Good/Fine 62 centering, with the left perforations touching the frameline and stronger spacing along the bottom margin. Fault soundness lowers the technical grade to Fair/Good 27 due to a light vertical crease, reverse ink transfer, and three shortened perforations. A light circular cancellation improves eye appeal by 10 points because it remains unobtrusive and preserves the portrait. Final Grade: Good 37. The attractive cancellation and generally pleasing appearance help offset several condition issues, resulting in a lower-grade but collectible example of this watermarked Bureau Issue stamp.', 2.64, 'NA01-0037-02-05-003'),
(32, 'United States', '279', '004', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair/Good', '1¢ Deep Green – Benjamin Franklin, 1898\r\n(U.S. Regular Issue – Bureau Small Bank Notes, 1895–1898)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #279 is a 1¢ deep green definitive from the Bureau Small Bank Note series issued during the closing years of the nineteenth century. The design features an engraved profile portrait of Benjamin Franklin surrounded by ornate scrollwork and decorative framing typical of the period. Printed in deep green and perforated 12, the stamp was widely used for printed matter and local postal rates. This example is postally used and without original gum. While the design remains complete and recognizable, centering deficiencies and significant creasing substantially affect the final grade.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #279 begins with Very Good 50 centering, with the right and bottom perforations cutting into the design and creating an uneven appearance. Severe Fault soundness lowers the technical grade to Fair 15 due to two full-height vertical creases affecting the paper structure. A light cancellation improves eye appeal by 5 points because it remains unobtrusive and preserves the portrait. Final Grade: Fair/Good 20. Although the stamp retains an attractive front appearance and a favorable cancellation, the severe creasing remains the dominant factor in determining its final grade.', 0.05, 'LA01-0012-04-01-004'),
(33, 'United States', '279B', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '2¢ Carmine – George Washington, 1898\r\n(First Bureau Issue – Soft Paper, Type IV Design by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #279B is a 2¢ carmine Washington definitive from the First Bureau Issue period. The design features a left-facing portrait of George Washington enclosed within an ornate oval frame accented by decorative scrollwork and finely engraved detail. Printed on soft paper, this Type IV design was widely used for ordinary domestic mail and remains a popular issue among Bureau-era collectors. This example is postally used and without original gum. The stamp presents well overall, though a light crease, reverse ink transfer, and a strong cancellation affect the final grade.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #279B begins with Fine/Very Fine 79 centering, with clear margins surrounding the design and only a slightly narrow top margin. Minor Fault soundness lowers the technical grade to Very Good 59 due to a light crease and reverse ink transfer. A heavy horizontal-bar cancellation further reduces eye appeal by 10 points because it obscures portions of Washington’s portrait and engraving. Final Grade: Good/Very Good 49. The attractive centering and intact perforations are offset by the crease and strong cancellation, producing a respectable mid-grade example of this Bureau Issue stamp.', 0.10, 'NA01-0039-04-02-002'),
(34, 'United States', '285', '004', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine', '1¢ Deep Green – “Marquette on the Mississippi”, 1898\r\n(Trans-Mississippi Exposition Issue – Engraved by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #285 is the 1¢ value of the celebrated 1898 Trans-Mississippi Exposition series and depicts Father Jacques Marquette exploring the Mississippi River. The design is among the most admired engraved commemoratives issued by the United States, featuring a detailed landscape scene framed by elaborate inscriptions and ornamentation. Printed in deep green and formatted horizontally, the stamp commemorates western exploration and expansion. This example is postally used and without original gum. The stamp remains structurally sound and visually attractive, though a very heavy cancellation has a substantial impact on eye appeal.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #285 begins with Fine/Very Fine 76 centering, with all perforations clear of the design and visible white space present on all sides. Faultless soundness preserves the technical grade at Fine/Very Fine 76 due to the absence of structural defects. A very heavy circular date stamp reduces eye appeal by 15 points because it obscures much of the central vignette. Final Grade: Very Good/Fine 61. Despite the strong cancellation, the stamp remains a sound and collectible example of one of the most iconic issues in United States philately.', 3.48, 'NA01-0041-01-01-004'),
(35, 'United States', '288', '001', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Damaged', '5¢ Blue – “Fremont on Rocky Mountains”, 1898\r\n(Trans-Mississippi Exposition Issue – Engraved by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #288 is the 5¢ value of the 1898 Trans-Mississippi Exposition Issue and depicts John C. Frémont raising the American flag atop the Rocky Mountains. The dramatic engraved scene symbolizes exploration, expansion, and national ambition during the nineteenth century. Printed in deep blue and produced by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, the issue is among the most popular commemoratives in United States philately. This example is postally used and without original gum. While the central design remains identifiable, extensive physical damage severely affects both its appearance and collectibility.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #288 begins with Good 34 centering, with perforations cutting deeply into the design and substantial frame loss on multiple sides. Damaged soundness lowers the technical grade to 4 due to severe perforation loss, missing paper, torn edges, corner damage, and heavy reverse disturbance. The cancellation has no additional effect on eye appeal because the structural damage overwhelmingly dominates the presentation. Final Grade: Damaged 4. While the stamp remains identifiable as a Trans-Mississippi issue, its extensive condition problems limit it primarily to reference or space-filler status.', 0.18, 'LA01-0013-02-01-001'),
(36, 'United States', '306', '005', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine', '8¢ Black – Martha Washington, 1903\r\n(Series of 1902 – Engraved Printing, Perf 12)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #306 is the 8¢ value of the Series of 1902 and features an engraved portrait of Martha Washington surrounded by an ornate wreath and decorative frame. Her birth and death dates, “1732–1802,” appear beside the portrait, while “UNITED STATES OF AMERICA” spans the top and “POSTAGE EIGHT CENTS” appears below. Printed in black and perforated 12, the issue is one of the more distinctive values of the 1902 series. This example is postally used and without original gum. The stamp retains a strong impression and attractive appearance, with only minor condition concerns affecting the final grade.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #306 begins with Fine/Very Fine 77 centering, with all margins clear of the design and the top margin providing the greatest spacing. Very Minor Fault soundness lowers the technical grade to Fine 72 due solely to a hinge remnant on the reverse. An average cancellation has no effect on eye appeal and results in no additional adjustment. Final Grade: Fine 72. The combination of strong centering, intact perforations, and an unobtrusive cancellation makes this a pleasing collector-grade example of the 8¢ Martha Washington issue.', 1.95, 'LA01-0014-02-04-005'),
(37, 'United States', '319', '005', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '2¢ Carmine – George Washington, 1903\r\n(First Bureau Issue – Engraved, Perf 12, Double-Line Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #319 is a 2¢ carmine issue from the First Bureau series, featuring a finely engraved portrait of George Washington within a decorative frame. The design includes “UNITED STATES OF AMERICA” at the top and “POSTAGE TWO CENTS” at the bottom, reflecting the Bureau of Engraving and Printing’s detailed line work typical of early 20th century definitives. This example is used, hinged, and without original gum. The design remains intact, though condition is affected by perforation faults and centering imbalance.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #319 achieves a final grade of 38 (Good), combining Fine centering with Fault-level soundness due to perforation issues. The narrow left margin and uneven balance limit centering, while the pulled and shortened perforations significantly reduce the technical grade. The cancel remains moderate and does not detract heavily from the design. Overall, it is a collectible example with clear detail but notable condition limitations.', 0.04, 'LA01-0015-01-01-005');
INSERT INTO `stamps` (`id`, `country`, `scott`, `count`, `condition`, `hinged`, `gum`, `grade`, `description`, `price`, `location`) VALUES
(38, 'United States', '329', '005', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '2¢ Carmine – Founding of Jamestown, 1607, 1907\r\n(Jamestown Exposition Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 12)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #329 is the 2¢ carmine value of the 1907 Jamestown Exposition Issue, commemorating the founding of the first permanent English settlement in America. The central vignette depicts the arrival of the Jamestown colonists, with figures disembarking from boats while sailing vessels remain anchored in the background. Decorative corn stalks flank the scene, symbolizing the agricultural promise of the New World. Printed by engraved intaglio and perforated 12, the stamp combines historical significance with attractive commemorative design. This example is unused with original gum and has been previously hinged. The color remains rich and the engraved impression sharp, though centering and minor reverse faults affect the final grade.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #329 begins with Fine 71 centering, with narrow top and right margins balanced by stronger spacing on the left and bottom. Very Minor Fault soundness lowers the technical grade to Very Good/Fine 61 due to a pencil notation and hinge remnant on the reverse. No eye appeal adjustment is applied because the stamp remains attractive from the front, with strong color and a sharp engraved impression. Final Grade: Very Good/Fine 61. The combination of pleasing appearance, intact perforations, and only minor reverse faults makes this a respectable collector-grade example of the Jamestown Exposition Issue.', 13.10, 'LA01-0015-04-02-005'),
(39, 'United States', '331', '006', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '1¢ Green – Benjamin Franklin, 1908\r\n(Washington-Franklin Series – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 12, Double-Line Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #331 features a left-facing engraved portrait of Benjamin Franklin within an oval frame, surrounded by laurel leaves and framed inscriptions reading “U.S. POSTAGE” and “ONE CENT.” Issued in 1908 as part of the Washington-Franklin series, the design reflects classical symmetry and fine line engraving typical of early Bureau issues. The submitted example is used, without original gum, and hinged, with visible cancellation across the face and minor condition issues on both front and reverse.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #331 example demonstrates solid Fine/Very Fine centering with intact perforations and no major structural defects. The presence of discoloration and a moderately heavy cancel reduces both technical grade and visual appeal, resulting in a final grade of 49 (Good/Very Good). While not a premium example, it remains a respectable and collectible representative of the Washington-Franklin series.', 0.10, 'LA01-0016-01-01-006'),
(40, 'United States', '332', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '2¢ Carmine – George Washington, 1908\r\n(Washington-Franklin Series – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 12, Double-Line Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #332 features a left-facing engraved portrait of George Washington enclosed within an oval frame, flanked by laurel branches and bordered by bold inscriptions reading “U.S. POSTAGE” and “TWO CENTS.” Issued in 1908 as part of the Washington-Franklin series, the design reflects classical symmetry and refined engraving typical of early Bureau issues. The submitted example is used, without original gum, and hinged, showing a visible horizontal cancellation and some condition impact on the reverse.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #332 example benefits from strong Very Fine/Extremely Fine centering and intact perforations, placing it above average in structural quality. The heavy ink transfer on the reverse introduces a minor fault, and the moderately heavy cancel reduces visual appeal, resulting in a final grade of 62 (Very Good/Fine). Despite these factors, the stamp remains a solid and collectible example of this classic Washington-Franklin issue.', 0.16, 'LA01-0016-01-02-003'),
(41, 'United States', '333', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Poor', '3¢ Violet – George Washington, 1908\r\n(Washington-Franklin Series – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 12, Double-Line Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #333 depicts a left-facing engraved portrait of George Washington enclosed within an oval frame, bordered by laurel branches and inscriptions reading “U.S. POSTAGE” and “3 CENTS.” Issued in 1908 as part of the Washington-Franklin series, the stamp reflects the refined engraving and classical layout characteristic of early Bureau issues. The submitted example is used, without original gum, and hinged, with a heavy cancellation and visible structural issues affecting both the design and physical integrity.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #333 example exhibits significant centering deficiencies, major structural faults, and a very heavy cancel that obscures much of the design. These combined factors result in a final grade of 5 (Poor). While heavily compromised, it remains identifiable and may serve as a placeholder example for this commonly collected Washington-Franklin issue.', 0.11, 'LA01-0016-01-03-003'),
(42, 'United States', '335', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '5¢ Blue – George Washington, 1908\r\n(U.S. Washington-Franklin Issue – Perf 12, Double-Line Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #335 is a 5¢ blue Washington-Franklin definitive issued in 1908. The design features an engraved portrait of George Washington facing left within an oval frame bordered by laurel leaves and ornamental scrollwork. Printed on double-line watermarked paper and perforated 12, the issue represents one of the earliest Washington-Franklin stamps produced by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing. This example is postally used and without original gum. The color remains strong and the engraved impression is crisp, though a significant corner defect has a major impact on the final grade.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #335 begins with Fine/Very Fine 76 centering, with all perforations remaining clear of the design and margins showing reasonable balance. Major Fault soundness lowers the technical grade to Good 31 due to the clipped upper-left corner and minor reverse ink transfer. An average machine cancellation has no effect on eye appeal and results in no additional adjustment. Final Grade: Good 31. Although the stamp retains attractive color, strong engraving, and respectable centering, the clipped corner remains the dominant factor limiting its overall grade.', 0.46, 'LA01-0016-01-05-002'),
(43, 'United States', '338', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '10¢ Yellow – George Washington, 1909\r\n(U.S. Washington-Franklin Series – Perf 12, Single-Line Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #338 is a 10¢ yellow Washington-Franklin definitive issued in 1909. The design features a left-facing portrait of George Washington enclosed within an oval frame accented by laurel leaves and decorative scrollwork. Printed from flat plates on single-line watermarked paper and perforated 12, the stamp served a variety of higher domestic and international postal rates. This example is postally used and without original gum. The bright yellow color remains identifiable, though a strong cancellation and several condition issues reduce both technical quality and visual appeal.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #338 begins with Fine/Very Fine 76 centering, with all perforations remaining clear of the design and margins showing reasonable balance. Fault soundness lowers the technical grade to Good/Very Good 46 due to a light crease, cancellation bleed-through, and a hinge remnant. A heavy cancellation further reduces eye appeal by 10 points because it obscures much of Washington’s portrait. Final Grade: Good 36. Although the stamp retains acceptable centering and recognizable color, the combination of structural defects and a strong cancellation substantially limits its overall grade.', 0.34, 'NA01-0049-02-05-003'),
(44, 'United States', '372', '001', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair/Good', '2¢ Carmine – Hudson-Fulton Celebration, 1909\r\n(U.S. Commemorative Issue – Perf 12, Double-Line Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #372 was issued in 1909 to commemorate the Hudson-Fulton Celebration, honoring Henry Hudson’s exploration of the Hudson River and Robert Fulton’s pioneering work in steam navigation. The design depicts Hudson’s ship, the Half Moon, alongside Fulton’s steamboat, the Clermont, illustrating the transition from sail to steam. Printed in carmine and perforated 12, the stamp remains one of the more popular early twentieth-century United States commemoratives. This example is postally used and without original gum. While the design remains recognizable and reasonably well centered, significant perforation faults and a thin substantially reduce its grade.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #372 begins with Fine/Very Fine 78 centering, with all perforations remaining clear of the design and visible white space surrounding the frame. Major Fault soundness lowers the technical grade to Good 33 due to multiple clipped, pulled, and shortened perforations. A heavy cancellation further reduces eye appeal by 10 points because it obscures portions of the maritime vignette. Final Grade: Fair/Good 23. Although the stamp retains attractive centering and a recognizable design, substantial perforation damage and a strong cancellation significantly limit its overall grade and desirability.', 0.50, 'LA01-0017-03-03-001'),
(45, 'United States', '372', '005', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '2¢ Carmine – Hudson-Fulton Celebration, 1909\r\n(Hudson-Fulton Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 12)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #372 was issued in 1909 to commemorate the Hudson-Fulton Celebration, honoring Henry Hudson’s 1609 exploration of the Hudson River and Robert Fulton’s successful steamboat navigation. The design depicts Hudson’s Half Moon alongside Fulton’s Clermont, representing the transition from sail to steam. Printed in carmine and perforated 12, the issue is among the most popular early United States commemoratives. This example is postally used and without original gum. The design remains complete and attractive despite several minor faults and a noticeable machine cancellation.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #372 combines Fine/Very Fine 76 centering with Minor Fault soundness resulting from reverse cancel bleed-through, a corner perf crease, and one shortened perforation. The heavy machine cancellation further reduces eye appeal by 5 points because it partially obscures the vignette. Final Grade: Very Good 51. Despite several minor condition issues, the stamp remains a respectable collector-grade example of this popular commemorative issue.', 1.68, 'SD22-0002-06-01-005'),
(46, 'United States', '381', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '10¢ Yellow – Daniel Webster, 1911\r\n(Washington-Franklin Series – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 12, Single-Line Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #381 is a 10¢ yellow definitive from the Washington-Franklin series issued in 1911. The design features an engraved portrait of Daniel Webster facing left within an ornate oval frame surrounded by decorative scrollwork. Produced by flat plate printing on single-line watermarked paper and perforated 12, the stamp served a variety of domestic and international postal rates. Webster\'s portrait distinguishes this denomination from the more common Washington and Franklin designs found throughout the series. This example is postally used, hinged, and without original gum. The color remains attractive and the engraved detail is clearly visible despite uneven margins and a visible cancellation.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #381 exhibits Very Good 51 centering with perforations touching or slightly cutting into the frameline along the top and left sides while the right margin remains substantially wider. Faultless soundness preserves the technical grade, and the perforations remain complete and unaltered. The diagonal cancellation confirms postal use without significantly obscuring the portrait or inscriptions. Final Grade: 51 (Very Good – VG). Despite its centering limitations, the stamp remains a sound and collectible example of this Daniel Webster Washington-Franklin issue with strong color and pleasing overall appearance.', 1.59, 'LA01-0017-05-05-002'),
(47, 'United States', '397', '001', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '1¢ Green – Vasco Núñez de Balboa, 1913\r\n(Panama-Pacific Exposition Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 12, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #397 is the 1¢ green value of the Panama-Pacific Exposition commemorative series, issued in 1913 in anticipation of the 1915 exposition in San Francisco. The stamp features an engraved portrait of Vasco Núñez de Balboa, the Spanish explorer credited with becoming the first European to view the Pacific Ocean from the Americas. Ornamental framing, palm trees, and elaborate engraving surround the portrait, while inscriptions reference both Balboa\'s historic achievement and the Panama-Pacific Exposition. The issue remains one of the most recognizable early United States commemorative series. This example is postally used, without original gum, and was never hinged before use. The design remains clear and attractive, though a stain affects both condition and appearance.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #397 begins with Fine/Very Fine 76 centering, with all perforations clear of the design and visible white space surrounding the frame. Fault soundness resulting from a small stain lowers the technical grade to Good/Very Good 46. The perforations remain intact, the cancellation is unobtrusive, and the engraved detail remains strong. A further 5-point eye appeal deduction reflects the visual impact of the stain. Final Grade: 41 (Good/Very Good – G/VG). Despite the condition issue, the stamp remains a collectible example of the popular Panama-Pacific Exposition series with pleasing centering and solid overall presentation.', 0.52, 'NA01-0061-01-01-001'),
(48, 'United States', '397', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '1¢ Green – Vasco Núñez de Balboa, 1913\r\n(Panama-Pacific Exposition Commemorative – Engraved Printing, Perf 12, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #397 is the 1¢ green value of the Panama-Pacific Exposition commemorative series issued in 1913. The design features an engraved portrait of Vasco Núñez de Balboa, the explorer credited with becoming the first European to view the Pacific Ocean from the Americas. Palm trees and decorative scrollwork frame the portrait, while inscriptions commemorate both Balboa’s achievement and the Panama-Pacific Exposition. The issue remains one of the most recognizable early United States commemoratives. This example is postally used, hinged, and without original gum. The color remains attractive and the engraved impression is well preserved despite minor condition issues.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #397 combines Fine/Very Fine 77 centering with Minor Fault soundness resulting from a small corner crease and light reverse ink transfer. The perforations remain complete and unaltered, while the circular cancellation leaves the portrait and principal design elements fully visible. No eye appeal adjustment is required. Final Grade: 57 (Very Good – VG). Although affected by minor condition issues, the stamp remains a well-presenting and collectible example of the popular Panama-Pacific Exposition commemorative series.', 0.75, 'LA01-0018-03-01-003'),
(49, 'United States', '405', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '1¢ Green – George Washington, 1912\r\n(Washington-Franklin Series – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 12, Single-Line Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #405 is a 1¢ green Washington-Franklin definitive issued in 1912. The design features a left-facing portrait of George Washington enclosed within an oval frame and laurel wreath. Denomination numerals appear in the lower corners, while “U.S. POSTAGE” and “ONE CENT” complete the classic layout used throughout the series. Printed from flat plates on single-line watermarked paper and perforated 12, the issue served as a common workhorse stamp for everyday mail. This example is postally used, hinged, and without original gum. The front presents well, with strong color, a sharp impression, and no visible cancellation affecting the portrait.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #405 combines Fine/Very Fine 77 centering with Minor Fault soundness resulting from reverse ink transfer, a corner perf crease, a perf tip crease, and a small pencil notation. The perforations remain intact and the margins are comfortably clear of the design. A face-free used appearance enhances eye appeal and adds 5 points to the final result. Final Grade: 62 (Very Good/Fine – VG/F). Despite several minor reverse faults, the stamp remains an attractive and collectible Washington-Franklin example with strong color, clear detail, and pleasing overall presentation.', 0.10, 'LA01-0019-01-01-003'),
(50, 'United States', '405', '004', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Good', '1¢ Green – George Washington, 1912\r\n(Washington-Franklin Issue – Engraved, Perf 12, Single-Line Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #405 is a 1¢ green Washington-Franklin definitive issued in 1912 by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing. The design features a left-facing portrait of George Washington within a laurel-wreath oval, framed by elegant scrollwork characteristic of the series. “U.S. POSTAGE” appears prominently above the portrait, while “ONE CENT” is displayed below with numeral ornaments in the lower corners. Printed by the engraved method on single-line watermarked paper and perforated 12, this denomination was widely used for postcards and other low-rate postal purposes. This example is unused, retains original gum, and has been previously hinged.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #405 displays Very Good 55 centering with a narrow top margin, tight right margin, and stronger left and bottom margins. Soundness is classified as Minor Fault due to a hinge remnant and light ink transfer on the reverse, resulting in a 20-point deduction. Perforations are genuine and intact, the original gum remains present, and the stamp benefits from fresh color and a clear engraved impression. With no cancellation and no eye appeal adjustment required, the final grade is 35 (Good – G). Despite its centering imbalance and minor reverse faults, it remains a collectible unused example of this important Washington-Franklin issue.', 1.28, 'NA01-0063-01-01-004'),
(51, 'United States', '405', '005', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '1¢ Green – George Washington, 1912\r\n(Washington-Franklin Series – Engraved Printing, Perf 12, Single-Line Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #405 is a 1¢ green definitive from the Washington-Franklin series issued in 1912. The design features a left-facing portrait of George Washington surrounded by a laurel wreath within an oval frame. “U.S. POSTAGE” appears above the portrait and “ONE CENT” below. Printed by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing on single-line watermarked paper and perforated 12, this issue was widely used for postcards and printed matter. This example is used, hinged, and without original gum. It retains strong color and a clear engraved impression.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #405 combines Fine/Very Fine 77 centering with Minor Fault soundness resulting from a hinge remnant and light reverse ink transfer. Perforations are intact and genuine, margins remain clear of the design, and the light cancellation leaves Washington’s portrait fully visible. Strong color, a sharp engraved impression, and an unobtrusive cancel support a +5 eye appeal adjustment. The final grade is 62 (Very Good/Fine – VG/F). This remains a pleasing and collectible example of the Washington-Franklin series with attractive overall presentation.', 0.10, 'SL01-0001-05-04-005'),
(52, 'United States', '418', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Poor', '15¢ Gray – Benjamin Franklin, 1912\r\n(Washington-Franklin Series – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 12, Single-Line Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #418 is a 15¢ gray Washington-Franklin definitive issued in 1912. The design features a left-facing portrait of Benjamin Franklin enclosed within an oval frame surrounded by laurel leaves. The inscriptions “U.S. POSTAGE” and “15 CENTS 15” complete the layout, reflecting the elegant style of higher-denomination Washington-Franklin issues. Printed by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing from flat plates on single-line watermarked paper and perforated 12, the stamp was commonly used for heavier domestic and foreign mail rates. This example is used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #418 exhibits Very Good 53 centering with narrow top and right margins and a broader left margin. Soundness is classified as Minor Fault due to reverse bleed-through, light ink transfer, and a hinge remnant, reducing the preliminary grade to 23. Perforations remain intact and genuine, but a Very Heavy circular cancellation obscures much of Franklin’s portrait and results in a 15-point eye appeal deduction. The final grade is 8 (Poor – PO). Despite its technical shortcomings, it remains a collectible example of the 15¢ Washington-Franklin issue and an affordable representative of this classic series.', 0.17, 'LA01-0019-04-01-002'),
(53, 'United States', '425', '003', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'PG - Partial Gum', 'Fair/Good', '2¢ Carmine – George Washington, 1914\r\n(Washington-Franklin Series – Offset Printing, Perf 10, Single-Line Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #425 is a 2¢ carmine Washington-Franklin definitive issued in 1914. Produced by offset printing rather than engraving, it features a left-facing portrait of George Washington enclosed within a laurel-wreath frame. “U.S. POSTAGE” appears above the portrait and “2 CENTS” below within decorative scrollwork. Printed on single-line watermarked paper and perforated 10, this issue represents one of the more distinctive production methods used during the Washington-Franklin era. This example is unused, hinged, and retains partial original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #425 displays Very Good 56 centering with tight top and right margins and broader left and bottom margins. Soundness is classified as Fault due to partial original gum, a hinge remnant, reverse pencil notation, and a shorter perforation, resulting in a 35-point deduction. The perforations remain largely intact and genuine, and the unused condition avoids any cancellation-related deductions. With no eye appeal adjustment required, the final grade is 21 (Fair/Good – FR-G). Despite its condition issues, it remains a collectible example of the offset-printed Washington-Franklin series and a representative example of this specialized issue.', 0.26, 'LA01-0020-01-02-003'),
(54, 'United States', '428', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair/Good', '5¢ Blue – George Washington, 1914\r\n(Washington-Franklin Issue – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 10, Single-Line Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #428 features a left-facing engraved portrait of George Washington enclosed within an oval frame, flanked by laurel branches and topped by “U.S. POSTAGE,” with “5 CENTS 5” at the base. The design reflects the clean, classical symmetry of the Washington-Franklin series. This submitted example is used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #428 displays solid Fine/Very Fine centering with the bottom and right margins tighter than the top and left. However, a heavy crease and additional perf-related faults significantly reduce its structural integrity, resulting in a Major Fault classification. The cancel is moderate and does not overly detract, but the crease impacts overall presentation. The stamp earns a final grade of 28 (Fair/Good) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, remaining a recognizable but condition-limited example of the issue.', 0.10, 'LA01-0020-02-01-003'),
(55, 'United States', '462', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '1¢ Green – George Washington, 1916\r\n(Washington-Franklin Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 10, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #462 presents a left-facing portrait of George Washington in an oval frame, surrounded by laurel branches and bordered by the inscriptions “U.S. POSTAGE” and “1 CENT.” Issued in 1916 using rotary press printing, the design exhibits slightly coarser detail compared to earlier flat plate issues but maintains strong visual clarity. The submitted example is used, without original gum, and hinged, with a visible cancel and notable condition effects on the reverse.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #462 example shows solid Fine/Very Fine centering and generally intact perforations, but its overall grade is significantly impacted by multiple condition issues. Heavy cancellation bleed-through, ink transfer on the reverse, and a short perforation reduce both technical and visual quality, resulting in a final grade of 37 (Good). Despite these drawbacks, it remains a serviceable and collectible example of this widely collected Washington-Franklin issue.', 0.06, 'LA01-0022-01-01-002'),
(56, 'United States', '463', '001', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good', '2¢ Carmine – George Washington, 1916\r\n(Washington-Franklin Series – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #463 is a 2¢ carmine Washington-Franklin definitive issued in 1916. The design features a left-facing portrait of George Washington enclosed within an oval frame bordered by laurel branches. “U.S. POSTAGE” appears above the portrait, while “2 CENTS 2” is displayed below. Printed by flat plate methods, perforated 11, and issued without watermark, this stamp represents one of the later major varieties of the Washington-Franklin series. This example is unused, hinged, and retains original gum. The color remains vibrant and the printed impression is clear.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #463 displays Fine/Very Fine 77 centering with clear margins on all sides and particularly strong spacing at the left. Soundness is classified as Minor Fault due to a hinge remnant and two shortened perforation tips, resulting in a 20-point deduction. The perforations remain otherwise intact and genuine, and the unused condition eliminates any cancellation concerns. Strong color, a sharp impression, and pleasing overall balance support the final grade of 57 (Very Good – VG). Despite its minor technical faults, it remains a respectable and collectible example of this unwatermarked Washington-Franklin issue.', 1.72, 'LA01-0022-01-02-001'),
(58, 'United States', '486', '006', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good', '1¢ Green – George Washington, 1917\r\n(Washington-Franklin Series – Offset Printing, Perf 11, Double-Line Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #486 is a 1¢ green Washington-Franklin definitive issued in 1917. Produced by offset printing during a period when the Bureau of Engraving and Printing sought alternatives to traditional engraved production, the stamp features a left-facing portrait of George Washington enclosed within a laurel wreath. “U.S. POSTAGE” appears above the portrait, while “1 CENT 1” is displayed below. Printed on double-line watermarked paper and perforated 11, this issue occupies a distinctive place within the Washington-Franklin series. This example is unused, hinged, and retains original gum. The front presents well, with fresh color and a strong impression characteristic of the issue.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #486 exhibits Fine/Very Fine 77 centering with all margins clear of the design and well-formed perforations throughout. Soundness is classified as Minor Fault due to a small toning spot on the reverse, resulting in a 20-point deduction and an adjusted base grade of 57. The stamp remains visually attractive, with strong color, a crisp offset impression, original gum, and no cancellation concerns. With no additional eye appeal adjustment required, the final grade is 57 (Very Good – VG). It remains a pleasing and collectible example of this offset-printed Washington-Franklin issue.', 0.30, 'LA01-0023-01-01-006'),
(59, 'United States', '492', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '2¢ Carmine – George Washington, 1917\r\n(Washington-Franklin Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #492 is a 2¢ carmine Washington-Franklin definitive issued in 1917 and produced by rotary press printing. The design features a left-facing portrait of George Washington enclosed within a laurel wreath, with “U.S. POSTAGE” at the top and “2 CENTS” below. Rotary press production created subtle differences from earlier flat plate issues and forms an important collecting specialty within the Washington-Franklin series. This example is used, hinged, and without original gum. The carmine color remains attractive, though condition issues and a strong cancellation affect its overall presentation.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #492 displays Fine 70 centering with visible white space surrounding the design and a particularly generous bottom margin. Soundness is classified as Minor Fault due to multiple ragged, fuzzy, and shortened perforations, reducing the grade to Good/Very Good 40 before eye appeal adjustments. A very heavy black machine cancel obscures much of Washington’s portrait and warrants a further 10-point deduction. Despite its attractive color and authentic rotary press characteristics, the perforation defects and strong cancellation limit the stamp to a final grade of 30 (Good – G). It remains a collectible representative of the Washington-Franklin series, though condition issues significantly affect its desirability.', 0.18, 'LA01-0023-02-03-003'),
(60, 'United States', '493', '002', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine/Very Fine', '3¢ Violet – George Washington, 1917-1921\r\n(Washington-Franklin Series – Imperforate Coil, Type I Schermack Perforations, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #493 is a 3¢ violet Washington-Franklin coil stamp produced from imperforate sheets and privately perforated with Type I Schermack perforations for vending machine use. The design features a left-facing portrait of George Washington enclosed within a laurel wreath. “U.S. POSTAGE” appears above the portrait, while “3 CENTS” is displayed below. The issue represents an important specialty within the Washington-Franklin series because of its private perforation characteristics and limited method of distribution. This example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum. The stamp displays strong color, a clear impression, and an attractive overall appearance.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #493 exhibits Fine/Very Fine 77 centering with all margins clear of the design and well-formed Type I Schermack perforations. Soundness is Faultless, with original gum intact and no evidence of creases, thins, tears, stains, or perforation defects. The fresh violet color, sharp impression, and clean paper contribute to above-average visual appeal, supporting a modest two-point eye appeal bonus. The resulting final grade is 79 (Fine/Very Fine – F/VF). This is an attractive and well-preserved example of a desirable privately perforated Washington-Franklin coil issue.', 8.10, 'LA01-0023-02-04-002'),
(61, 'United States', '504', '001', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '5¢ Blue – George Washington, 1917–1919\r\n(Washington-Franklin Series – Engraved Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #504 is a 5¢ blue Washington-Franklin definitive issued between 1917 and 1919 on unwatermarked paper. The design features a left-facing portrait of George Washington enclosed within a laurel wreath, with “U.S. POSTAGE” above and “5 CENTS” below. The engraved printing provides strong detail and depth, particularly in the portrait and surrounding frame. This example is used, hinged, and without original gum. The rich blue color remains attractive despite a prominent cancellation.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #504 displays Fine/Very Fine 76 centering with visible white space surrounding the design and intact perforations throughout. Soundness is classified as Very Minor Fault due to a hinge remnant, resulting in a five-point deduction and an adjusted base grade of 71. A heavy black cancellation obscures much of Washington’s portrait and warrants an additional five-point eye appeal deduction. Despite the cancellation, the stamp retains strong blue color, a clear engraved impression, and sound perforations. The final grade is 66 (Fine – F), making it a respectable and collectible used example of this Washington-Franklin issue.', 0.16, 'LA01-0024-02-02-001'),
(62, 'United States', '504', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair/Good', '5¢ Blue – George Washington, 1917\r\n(Washington-Franklin Series – Offset Printing, Perf 11, Single-Line Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #504 is a 5¢ blue Washington-Franklin issue produced during the period when offset printing supplemented traditional engraved production. The design features a left-facing portrait of George Washington enclosed within a laurel wreath, with “U.S. POSTAGE” above and “5 CENTS 5” below. Offset printing creates a flatter appearance than engraved issues while retaining the familiar Washington-Franklin layout. This example is postally used, never hinged, and without original gum. A natural straight edge is present along the left side, and the stamp bears a noticeable circular cancellation.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #504 displays Fine 70 centering with visible white space surrounding the design and adequate separation from the perforations. Soundness is classified as Fault due to the natural straight edge, with additional minor issues including a perforation tip crease and light ink transfer on the reverse. The heavy circular cancellation further reduces visual appeal and warrants a ten-point deduction. Despite retaining strong blue color and a clear offset impression, the combination of soundness issues and cancellation limits the stamp to a final grade of 20 (Fair/Good – FR/G). It remains a representative example of this offset-printed Washington-Franklin issue.', 0.04, 'NA01-0081-02-02-003'),
(63, 'United States', '504', '004', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair/Good', '5¢ Blue – George Washington, 1917\r\n(Washington-Franklin Series – Offset Printing, Perf 11, Single-Line Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #504 is a 5¢ blue Washington-Franklin issue produced by offset printing in 1917. The design features a left-facing portrait of George Washington surrounded by a laurel wreath, with “U.S. POSTAGE” above and “5 CENTS 5” below. Offset printing was adopted during World War I as a practical alternative to engraved production and is distinguished by its smooth, flat appearance. This example is postally used, hinged, and without original gum. The color remains attractive, though condition issues and a strong cancellation affect the overall presentation.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #504 displays Fine/Very Fine 79 centering with visible white space surrounding the design and full margins on all sides. Soundness is classified as Major Fault due to multiple ragged and shortened perforations, reverse discoloration, cancel bleed-through, and a hinge remnant, resulting in a 45-point deduction. A heavy circular cancellation further reduces visual appeal by obscuring much of Washington’s portrait and warrants an additional ten-point deduction. Despite retaining strong blue color and a clear offset impression, the combination of soundness issues and cancellation limits the stamp to a final grade of 24 (Fair/Good – FR/G). It remains a representative example of this wartime Washington-Franklin issue.', 0.04, 'SL01-0001-01-05-004'),
(64, 'United States', '504', '005', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair/Good', '5¢ Blue – George Washington, 1917\r\n(Washington-Franklin Series – Offset Printing, Perf 11, Single-Line Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #504 is a 5¢ blue Washington-Franklin issue produced by offset printing during 1917. The design depicts a left-facing portrait of George Washington enclosed within a laurel wreath, with “U.S. POSTAGE” above and “5 CENTS 5” below. Offset printing was adopted to meet wartime production demands and produces a flatter, smoother appearance than engraved issues while preserving the familiar Washington-Franklin design. This example is postally used, never hinged, and without original gum. It retains strong color and a complete design but shows several minor condition issues and a very heavy cancellation.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #504 displays Fine 70 centering with visible white space surrounding the design and no perforations cutting into the frameline. Soundness falls within the Minor Fault category due to ragged perforations, light cancel bleed-through, and reverse ink transfer, resulting in a 30-point deduction. A very heavy circular cancellation obscures much of Washington’s portrait and significantly impacts eye appeal, producing an additional 15-point deduction. Although the stamp retains strong blue color and a complete design, the combined effects of the minor faults and dominant cancel reduce the final grade to 25 (Fair/Good – FR/G). It remains a collectible example of the wartime offset-printed Washington-Franklin issue.', 0.04, 'SL01-0001-01-06-005'),
(65, 'United States', '504', '006', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '5¢ Blue – George Washington, 1917\r\n(Washington-Franklin Series – Offset Printing, Perf 11, Single-Line Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #504 is a 5¢ blue Washington-Franklin issue produced by offset printing in 1917. The design features a left-facing portrait of George Washington framed within a laurel wreath, with “U.S. POSTAGE” at the top and “5 CENTS 5” below. Offset printing was introduced during World War I to supplement engraved production and is characterized by a smoother appearance while retaining clear design detail. This example is postally used, hinged, and without original gum. The stamp retains pleasing color and a complete design, with only minor condition issues affecting the final grade.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #504 displays Fine/Very Fine 77 centering with visible white space surrounding the design and no perforations approaching the vignette. Soundness falls within the Minor Fault category due to a single short perforation and light reverse ink transfer, resulting in a 20-point deduction. The stamp benefits from strong blue color, a clean offset impression, and an exceptionally light cancellation that leaves Washington’s portrait fully visible, earning a five-point eye appeal bonus. These factors combine to produce a final grade of 62 (Very Good/Fine – VG/F). Despite the minor reverse issues, it remains an attractive and collectible example of this offset-printed Washington-Franklin issue.', 0.16, 'SL01-0001-02-01-006'),
(66, 'United States', '504', '008', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '5¢ Blue – George Washington, 1917\r\n(Washington-Franklin Series – Offset Printing, Perf 11, Single-Line Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #504 is a 5¢ blue Washington-Franklin issue produced by offset printing in 1917. The design features a left-facing portrait of George Washington enclosed within a laurel wreath and rectangular frame, with “U.S. POSTAGE” above and “5 CENTS” below. Offset printing was introduced during World War I to supplement engraved production and is recognized by its smoother appearance and flatter impression. This example is postally used, never hinged, and retains partial original gum. The stamp remains visually attractive from the front but is affected by several condition issues and a prominent machine cancel.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #504 displays Fine/Very Fine 79 centering with visible white space surrounding the design and intact perforations on all sides. Soundness is classified as Fault due to partial original gum, slight discoloration, reverse ink transfer, and cancellation bleed-through, resulting in a 30-point deduction. A heavy seven-bar machine cancel crosses the portrait and significantly affects eye appeal, producing an additional 10-point reduction. Although the stamp retains strong blue color and a pleasing offset impression, the combined impact of the soundness issues and prominent cancellation limits the final grade to 39 (Good). It remains a collectible representative of the offset-printed Washington-Franklin series.', 0.06, 'SL01-0001-02-03-008'),
(67, 'United States', '504', '009', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '5¢ Blue – George Washington, 1917\r\n(U.S. Washington-Franklin Issue – Offset Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #504 is a 5¢ blue Washington-Franklin issue produced by offset printing during 1917. The design features a left-facing portrait of George Washington enclosed within a laurel wreath, with “U.S. POSTAGE” at the top and “5 CENTS” below. Offset printing was adopted to supplement engraved production during World War I and is characterized by a smoother appearance and flatter impression. This example is postally used, hinged, and without original gum. Despite condition issues, the stamp retains strong color and a complete design.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #504 displays Fine/Very Fine 77 centering with visible white space surrounding the design and no perforations encroaching on the frame. Soundness is classified as Fault due to multiple ragged and shortened perforations together with light reverse ink transfer, resulting in a 30-point deduction. A heavy wavy-line machine cancel further reduces eye appeal and warrants an additional five-point deduction. While the stamp retains strong blue color and a complete design, the combined impact of the perforation faults and cancellation limits the final grade to 42 (Good/Very Good – G/VG). It remains a representative and collectible example of this offset-printed Washington-Franklin issue.', 0.09, 'SL01-0001-10-03-009'),
(68, 'United States', '517', '004', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '50¢ Violet – Benjamin Franklin, 1917\r\n(Washington-Franklin Series – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11, Single-Line Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #517 is a 50¢ violet Benjamin Franklin issue from the Washington-Franklin series, issued in 1917. The design features an engraved left-facing portrait of Franklin within an oval laurel frame. “U.S. POSTAGE” appears at the top and “50 CENTS” at the bottom. Printed by the flat plate method and bearing the single-line watermark, the issue is representative of the classic Washington-Franklin period. This example is postally used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #517 combines Fine/Very Fine centering with strong color and a clear engraved appearance. Several perf creases, a hinge remnant, light reverse ink transfer, and a heavy circular cancel reduce the overall grade and visual appeal. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 42 (Good/Very Good). Despite its faults, it remains a collectible example of the 1917 50¢ Benjamin Franklin issue and retains solid representative value within the Washington-Franklin series.', 0.19, 'NA01-0081-05-02-004'),
(69, 'United States', '542', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair', '1¢ Green – George Washington, 1920\r\n(Washington-Franklin Series – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #542 is a 1¢ green issue released in 1920 as part of the final phase of the Washington-Franklin series. The design features a left-facing portrait of George Washington enclosed within an oval laurel frame. “U.S. POSTAGE” appears at the top, while the denomination is displayed in panels at the lower corners. Printed by the flat plate method on unwatermarked paper and perforated gauge 11, the stamp reflects the familiar appearance of late Washington-Franklin definitives. The submitted example is postally used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #542 displays Fine/Very Fine centering with clear margins and an identifiable design. However, two pulled perfs, additional creases, shortened perforations, and a heavy cancellation result in a Major Fault classification and substantial deductions. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 21 (Fair). Despite its condition issues, it remains a collectible example of the 1920 1¢ Washington issue and retains value as a representative Washington-Franklin stamp.', 0.10, 'LA01-0026-02-01-003'),
(70, 'United States', '551', '003', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good', '½¢ Olive Brown – Nathan Hale, 1925\r\n(U.S. Regular Issue – Fourth Bureau Issue, Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #551 is a ½¢ olive brown stamp issued in 1925 as part of the Fourth Bureau Issue. The design features a portrait of Revolutionary War patriot Nathan Hale within a formal, symmetrical frame. Printed by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing on unwatermarked paper using the flat plate process and perforated gauge 11, the issue is representative of the low-denomination definitives of the period. The submitted example is unused, never hinged, and retains its original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #551 exhibits Fine/Very Fine centering, strong color, and a clean overall presentation. A small gum skip and light reverse ink transfer result in a Minor Fault classification, but neither defect affects the front appearance. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 58 (Very Good). The combination of never hinged status, original gum, and attractive eye appeal makes this a desirable mid-grade example of the 1925 Nathan Hale issue.', 0.09, 'LA01-0027-01-01-003'),
(71, 'United States', '551', '004', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Good', '½¢ Yellow Green – Nathan Hale, 1925\r\n(4th Bureau Issue – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #551 is a ½¢ yellow green stamp issued in 1925 as part of the 4th Bureau Issue. The design features a portrait of Nathan Hale framed by laurel leaves, with “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” at the top, “NATHAN HALE” on a ribbon below the portrait, and the denomination in the lower corners. Printed by the flat plate process on unwatermarked paper and perforated gauge 11, the issue is a familiar low-value definitive of the period. The submitted example is unused, hinged, and retains its original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #551 remains structurally sound and visually complete despite tight top and right margins. A hinge remnant and two shortened perforations result in a Minor Fault classification, while the Very Good centering limits the overall grade. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 30 (Good). The stamp retains collector appeal through its original gum, clear design, and strong color despite its centering and perforation limitations.', 0.04, 'NA01-0091-01-01-004'),
(72, 'United States', '551', '005', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair/Good', '1/2¢ Olive Brown – Nathan Hale, 1922\r\n(Regular Issue Series – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #551 features a portrait of Nathan Hale centered within an oval frame, surrounded by ornate geometric borders and inscriptions reading “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” and “ONE HALF CENT.” Issued in 1922 as part of the Fourth Bureau Issue, the design reflects a shift toward more modern engraving styles with sharper contrast and simplified ornamentation. The submitted example is used, without original gum, and hinged, with a visible cancel and minor structural imperfections affecting the perforations.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #551 example shows Very Good centering with perforations that approach the design and a Minor Fault due to multiple perf creases. The cancel is moderate and does not significantly obscure the design, but overall visual balance is reduced by centering and perforation issues. The final grade of 29 (Fair/Good) reflects a lower-grade but still collectible example of this distinctive 1922 issue.', 0.02, 'SL01-0002-03-03-005'),
(73, 'United States', '552', '004', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'PG - Partial Gum', 'Fair/Good', '1¢ Green – Benjamin Franklin, 1922\r\n(4th Bureau Issue – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #552 is a 1¢ green stamp issued in 1922 as part of the Fourth Bureau Issue. The design features a left-facing portrait of Benjamin Franklin within an ornate oval frame, with “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” at the top and the denomination at the bottom. Printed on unwatermarked paper by the flat plate process and perforated gauge 11, it is one of the most familiar Bureau definitive issues. The submitted example is used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #552 combines Fine centering with a clean and attractive front appearance. However, a small tear, reverse ink transfer, partial gum, pencil writing, and a visible guideline result in a Major Fault classification and substantial deduction. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 25 (Fair/Good). Despite its condition issues, it remains a collectible example of the 1922 Benjamin Franklin Fourth Bureau issue.', 0.14, 'NA01-0091-01-02-004');
INSERT INTO `stamps` (`id`, `country`, `scott`, `count`, `condition`, `hinged`, `gum`, `grade`, `description`, `price`, `location`) VALUES
(74, 'United States', '553', '004', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '1½¢ Brown – Warren G. Harding, 1923\r\n(4th Bureau Issue – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #553 is a 1½¢ brown stamp issued in 1923 as part of the Fourth Bureau definitive series. The design features a right-facing portrait of President Warren G. Harding within an oval frame accented by ornamental panels. “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” appears above the portrait, while the denomination is displayed below. Printed by the flat plate method on unwatermarked paper and perforated gauge 11, the issue reflects the standard definitive style of the period. The submitted example is used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #553 exhibits Very Good centering with a tight top margin but otherwise sound overall presentation. A hinge remnant and pencil writing on the reverse result in a Very Minor Fault classification, while the clean machine cancel preserves the visibility of the design. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 43 (Good/Very Good). Despite modest centering and minor handling flaws, it remains a collectible example of the 1923 Warren G. Harding issue.', 0.05, 'NA01-0091-01-03-004'),
(75, 'United States', '554', '003', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '2¢ Carmine – George Washington, 1923\r\n(U.S. Regular Issue – Fourth Bureau Issue, Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #554 is a 2¢ carmine stamp issued in 1923 as part of the Fourth Bureau Issue. The design features a profile portrait of George Washington within a classic oval frame flanked by denomination numerals. Printed by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing using the flat plate process on unwatermarked paper and perforated gauge 11, the stamp represents one of the most common definitive issues of the period. The submitted example is unused, hinged, and retains its original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #554 combines Fine centering, strong carmine color, and complete perforations with original gum. A hinge remnant on the reverse results in a Very Minor Fault classification, but it does not affect the front appearance. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 62 (Very Good/Fine). Its attractive presentation and limited faulting make it a solid collectible example of the 1923 2¢ Washington issue.', 0.58, 'LA01-0027-01-04-003'),
(76, 'United States', '554', '004', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '2¢ Carmine – George Washington, 1923\r\n(4th Bureau Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11x10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #554 is a 2¢ carmine rotary press issue released in 1923 as part of the Fourth Bureau definitive series. The design features a portrait of George Washington within an ornate oval frame, with “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” above and the denomination below. Printed on unwatermarked paper by the rotary press method and perforated gauge 11x10½, the issue differs from its flat plate counterpart in both production method and dimensions. The submitted example is used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #554 combines Fine/Very Fine centering with an attractive, cancel-free appearance that showcases the full design. Several ragged and shortened perforations result in a Fault classification, while a hinge remnant and pencil writing contribute additional deductions. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 53 (Very Good). Despite its perforation issues, the stamp remains a visually appealing and collectible example of the 1923 rotary press Washington issue.', 0.08, 'NA01-0091-01-04-004'),
(77, 'United States', '555', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '3¢ Violet – Abraham Lincoln, 1923\r\n(Fourth Bureau Issue – Flat Plate Printing, Type I, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #555 was issued in 1923 as part of the Fourth Bureau Issue. The design features an engraved portrait of Abraham Lincoln in violet within a rectangular ornamental frame. Printed by the flat plate method and perforated gauge 11, the stamp reflects the classic Bureau Issue style. This example is used, hinged, and without original gum. A black “LOS ANGELES, CALIF.” precancel is applied across the face but does not obscure the portrait.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #555 combines Fine/Very Fine centering with strong color, a clear impression, and an attractive precancel that does not obscure the design. A corner perf crease and one shortened perforation result in a Minor Fault classification and corresponding deduction. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 56 (Very Good). The stamp remains a collectible example of the 1923 Lincoln issue with solid visual appeal and desirable postal character.', 0.40, 'NA01-0091-02-01-003'),
(78, 'United States', '555', '005', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '3¢ Violet – Abraham Lincoln, 1923\r\n(Fourth Bureau Issue – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #555 features a portrait of Abraham Lincoln centered within an oval frame, surrounded by geometric ornamentation and inscriptions reading “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” and “3 CENTS.” Issued in 1923 as part of the Fourth Bureau Issue, the design reflects a refined and modernized engraving style with strong contrast and balanced symmetry. The submitted example is used, without original gum, and hinged, showing a horizontal machine cancel and moderate wear along the perforations.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #555 example demonstrates solid Fine/Very Fine centering with margins clear of the design, but its overall grade is reduced by perforation irregularities and a moderately heavy cancel. The resulting final grade of 47 (Good/Very Good) reflects a mid-grade collectible example that retains good color and identifiable design despite its condition limitations.', 0.28, 'LA01-0027-01-05-005'),
(79, 'United States', '558', '001', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair/Good', '6¢ Orange – James A. Garfield, 1923\r\n(Fourth Bureau Issue – Flat Plate Printing, Type I)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #558 was issued in 1923 as part of the Fourth Bureau Issue. The design features a profile portrait of President James A. Garfield engraved in orange and surrounded by an ornate frame with corner value numerals. Printed by the flat plate method and perforated gauge 11, the issue is representative of the definitive stamps of the period. This example is used, hinged, and without original gum. A black machine cancel of wavy bars crosses the face of the stamp.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #558 exhibits Fine/Very Fine centering and strong orange color, providing an attractive basic presentation. A heavy crease results in a Major Fault classification, while two perforation tip creases add further condition concerns. The prominent machine cancel moderately affects eye appeal but does not obscure the design. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 27 (Fair/Good). Despite its faults, it remains a recognizable and collectible example of the 1923 James A. Garfield issue.', 0.10, 'LA01-0027-02-03-001'),
(80, 'United States', '559', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair/Good', '7¢ Black – William McKinley, 1923\r\n(Regular Issue – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #559 is a 7¢ black engraved stamp issued in 1923 as part of the Fourth Bureau Issue. The design features a portrait of President William McKinley within an oval frame decorated with laurel leaves and ornamental scrollwork. “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” appears above the portrait, while the denomination is displayed in the lower corners. Printed by the flat plate method and perforated gauge 11, the issue is representative of the definitive stamps of the period. This example is used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #559 exhibits Fine centering with complete perforations and a strong engraved impression. A hinge remnant and reverse ink transfer result in a Minor Fault classification, while the very heavy cancellation substantially reduces eye appeal by obscuring key portrait details. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 25 (Fair/Good). Despite the severe cancel, it remains a collectible example of the 1923 William McKinley issue.', 0.08, 'LA01-0027-02-04-003'),
(81, 'United States', '562', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine', '10¢ Orange Yellow – James Monroe, 1923\r\n(Regular Issue – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #562 is a 10¢ orange yellow stamp issued in 1923 as part of the Fourth Bureau Issue. The design features a portrait of President James Monroe within an oval frame surrounded by ornamental elements. “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” appears above the portrait, while the denomination is displayed below. Printed by the flat plate method and perforated gauge 11, the issue is representative of the higher-denomination Bureau definitives. This example is used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #562 combines Fine/Very Fine centering, strong color, and clean perforations with only minor reverse-condition concerns. Pencil writing and a small paper adhesion result in a Very Minor Fault classification, but the light to moderate cancellation does not significantly affect presentation. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 72 (Fine). It remains an attractive and collectible example of the 1923 James Monroe issue.', 0.19, 'NA01-0091-03-03-003'),
(82, 'United States', '566', '004', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '15¢ Gray – Statue of Liberty, 1923\r\n(Fourth Bureau Issue – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #566 features a detailed depiction of the Statue of Liberty standing prominently within an oval frame, with New York Harbor elements faintly visible below. The design is enclosed by bold geometric borders and inscriptions reading “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” and “15 CENTS.” Issued in 1923 as part of the Fourth Bureau Issue, this stamp reflects a more pictorial and architectural theme compared to earlier portrait issues. The submitted example is used, hinged, and without original gum, with a visible cancellation and minor perforation irregularities.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #566 example demonstrates solid Fine/Very Fine centering and only minimal soundness concerns, with two short perforations. However, a moderately heavy cancel reduces the overall visual appeal, resulting in a final grade of 61 (Very Good/Fine). Despite this, it remains a strong and collectible example of this attractive Statue of Liberty design.', 0.13, 'LA01-0027-03-05-004'),
(83, 'United States', '571', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair/Good', '$1.00 Violet Brown – Lincoln Memorial, 1922\r\n(Fourth Bureau Issue – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #571 is a $1 high-value definitive issued in 1922 as part of the Fourth Bureau Issue. The design features an engraved view of the Lincoln Memorial framed by ornate scrollwork, with “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” at the top and the denomination at the bottom. Printed by the flat plate method on unwatermarked paper and perforated gauge 11, it is one of the most visually distinctive high-value Bureau issues. This example is used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #571 exhibits Fine centering with complete perforations and a recognizable Lincoln Memorial design. Partial gum disturbance, a hinge remnant, and pencil writing result in a Minor Fault classification, while the very heavy cancellation substantially reduces eye appeal by obscuring much of the vignette. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 25 (Fair/Good). Despite its condition issues, it remains a collectible example of the classic $1 Lincoln Memorial high value.', 0.08, 'NA01-0091-06-02-003'),
(84, 'United States', '571', '005', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'PG - Partial Gum', 'Very Good', '$1 Deep Brown – Lincoln Memorial, 1923\r\n(Fourth Bureau Issue – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #571 is the $1 high-value definitive of the Fourth Bureau Issue, printed by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing using the flat plate process and perforated gauge 11. The design depicts the Lincoln Memorial framed by classical scrollwork, with “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” above and “ONE DOLLAR” below. This example is used, never hinged, and retains partial original gum on the reverse.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #571 combines Fine/Very Fine centering with intact perforations, strong color, and a clear engraved appearance. Partial original gum and light reverse ink transfer result in a Minor Fault classification, while the heavy oval cancellation moderately reduces eye appeal. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 52 (Very Good). Despite the cancellation and minor faults, it remains a solid collectible example of the $1 Lincoln Memorial high value from the Fourth Bureau Issue.', 0.28, 'SL01-0001-06-06-005'),
(85, 'United States', '582', '003', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fair', '1½¢ Brown – Warren G. Harding, 1923\r\n(Fourth Bureau Issue – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #582 is a 1½¢ brown Warren G. Harding stamp issued in 1923 as part of the Fourth Bureau Issue. The design features an engraved portrait of President Harding within an oval frame, with “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” at the top and the denomination at the bottom. Printed by the flat plate method and perforated gauge 11, the stamp is representative of the definitive issues of the period. The submitted example is unused, hinged, and retains its original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #582 exhibits Good centering with perforations that cut into the design and significantly affect presentation. A small tear and body crease result in a Major Fault classification and substantial deduction. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 10 (Fair). While structurally compromised, it remains a collectible example suitable as a representative space-filler for the 1923 Warren G. Harding issue.', 0.47, 'NA01-0093-02-02-003'),
(86, 'United States', '582', '004', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair/Good', '1½¢ Yellow Brown – Warren G. Harding, 1923\r\n(Series of 1922–1925 – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 10, Single-Line Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #582 is a 1½¢ yellow brown definitive from the 1922–1925 Regular Issue series. The design features a left-facing portrait of President Warren G. Harding within an oval frame surrounded by decorative scrollwork. “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” appears at the top, with “1½ CENTS” at the bottom. Printed by the flat plate process on single-line watermarked paper and perforated gauge 10, this example is used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #582 exhibits Very Good centering with a clear, fully legible Philadelphia precancel and an attractive overall appearance from the front. A small thin, hinge remnant, corner perforation crease, and pencil writing result in a Fault classification and substantial deduction. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 23 (Fair/Good). Despite its condition issues, it remains a collectible example of the Harding issue and offers additional interest to precancel specialists.', 0.07, 'SL01-0001-01-02-004'),
(87, 'United States', '583', '002', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Good', '2¢ Red – George Washington, 1923\r\n(Fourth Bureau Issue – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #583 is a 2¢ red George Washington stamp issued as part of the 1923 Fourth Bureau Issue. The design features an engraved portrait of Washington within an oval frame, with “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” at the top and the denomination at the bottom. Printed by the flat plate method and perforated gauge 11, it is one of the most recognizable definitive issues of the period. The submitted example is unused, hinged, and retains its original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #583 combines Fine/Very Fine centering, complete perforations, and strong color with the benefit of original gum. A heavy crease results in a Major Fault classification and substantially reduces the overall grade despite the otherwise attractive appearance. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 32 (Good). While affected by a significant structural fault, it remains a collectible example suitable as a representative space-filler for the 1923 Washington issue.', 0.58, 'LA01-0028-01-03-002'),
(88, 'United States', '583', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '2¢ Carmine – George Washington, 1924\r\n(Series of 1922–1925 – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11, Single-Line Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #583 is a 2¢ carmine definitive from the 1922–1925 Regular Issue series. The design features a left-facing portrait of George Washington within an ornate oval frame surrounded by decorative scrollwork. “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” appears at the top, while the denomination is displayed in the lower corners. Printed by the flat plate process on single-line watermarked paper and perforated gauge 11, the issue is a classic Bureau definitive. This example is used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #583 combines Fine/Very Fine centering with strong color and a clear engraved appearance. Several minor perforation issues, a hinge remnant, and pencil writing result in a Minor Fault classification, while the heavy machine cancellation further reduces eye appeal. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 51 (Very Good). Despite its faults and cancellation, it remains a solid collectible example of the 1924 Washington definitive.', 0.10, 'NA01-0093-02-03-003'),
(89, 'United States', '586', '001', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '5¢ Blue – Theodore Roosevelt, 1923\r\n(Fourth Bureau Issue – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #586 is a 5¢ blue definitive issued in 1923 as part of the Fourth Bureau Issue. The design features an engraved portrait of Theodore Roosevelt within a bold oval frame surrounded by decorative panels. “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” appears at the top, while the denomination is displayed in the lower corners. Printed by the flat plate process on unwatermarked paper and perforated gauge 11, the issue is one of the more popular presidential definitives of the series. The submitted example is used, never hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #586 is structurally sound with complete perforations, strong color, and no measurable condition faults. Its Very Good centering is limited by the close top and right margins, while a very heavy cancellation significantly reduces eye appeal by obscuring Roosevelt’s portrait. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 36 (Good). Despite the cancellation, it remains a collectible example of the popular Theodore Roosevelt issue from the Fourth Bureau series.', 0.08, 'LA01-0028-01-06-001'),
(90, 'United States', '599', '004', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'PG - Partial Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '2¢ Carmine – George Washington, 1926\r\n(Fourth Bureau Issue – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #599 is a 2¢ carmine stamp issued in 1926 as part of the Fourth Bureau Issue. The design features a left-facing portrait of George Washington within a classical oval frame accented by ornamental scrollwork and corner numerals. “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” appears at the top, while the denomination is displayed below. Printed by the flat plate method on unwatermarked paper and perforated gauge 11, the issue is a standard definitive of the period. The submitted example is unused, hinged, and retains partial original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #599 combines Fine centering with strong color and an attractive front presentation. Multiple perforation irregularities, partial original gum, and a hinge remnant result in a Fault classification and corresponding deduction. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 40 (Good/Very Good). Despite its condition issues, it remains a collectible example of the 1926 Washington definitive and retains solid visual appeal from the front.', 0.09, 'LA01-0028-03-03-004'),
(91, 'United States', '604', '003', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '1¢ Green – Benjamin Franklin, 1926\r\n(Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #604 is a 1¢ green rotary press issue released in 1926 as part of the Fourth Bureau series. The design features a left-facing portrait of Benjamin Franklin enclosed within an ornate oval frame, with “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” at the top and the denomination below. Printed on unwatermarked paper by the rotary press method and perforated gauge 11, the issue is readily distinguished from earlier flat plate counterparts. The submitted example is an unused vertical pair, hinged, and retains its original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #604 vertical pair combines Fine centering, complete perforations, original gum, and a strong engraved appearance. A hinge remnant on the reverse results in a Very Minor Fault classification, but otherwise the pair remains structurally sound and visually attractive. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 61 (Very Good/Fine). The intact format, bold color, and clean presentation make it a desirable example of a Fourth Bureau rotary press pair.', 0.40, 'NA01-0099-05-02-003'),
(92, 'United States', '605', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine', '1½¢ Brown – Warren G. Harding, 1923\r\n(Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #605 is a 1½¢ brown stamp issued in 1923 as part of the Fourth Bureau Issue. The design features a portrait of President Warren G. Harding within an oval frame, with the denomination displayed in ornate corner tablets and Harding’s name beneath the portrait. Printed by the flat plate method and perforated gauge 11, the issue became a common workhorse stamp of the period. The submitted example is used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #605 combines Fine/Very Fine centering, clean perforations, and an attractive overall appearance. A hinge remnant and pencil writing on the reverse result in a Very Minor Fault classification, but these defects have minimal impact on presentation. The light to moderate cancellation remains unobtrusive and preserves the clarity of the portrait. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 72 (Fine), making this a solid and collectible example of the 1923 Warren G. Harding issue.', 0.11, 'LA01-0028-04-03-002'),
(93, 'United States', '606', '003', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '2¢ Carmine – George Washington, 1923\r\n(Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #606 is a 2¢ carmine rotary press issue released in 1923 as part of the Fourth Bureau series. The design features a left-facing portrait of George Washington within an ornate oval frame, with “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” above and the denomination displayed below. Printed on unwatermarked paper and perforated gauge 11, the issue is a standard rotary press definitive of the period. The submitted example is an unused vertical pair, hinged, and retains its original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #606 vertical pair exhibits strong color, intact perforations, and a clean front appearance. Off-center positioning limits the centering grade to Very Good, while a hinge remnant on the reverse results in a Very Minor Fault classification. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 42 (Good/Very Good). Despite the centering limitations, the pair remains an attractive and collectible example of a Fourth Bureau rotary press issue.', 0.20, 'NA01-0099-05-03-003'),
(94, 'United States', '610', '004', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '2¢ Black – Warren G. Harding Memorial Issue, 1923\r\n(Flat Plate Printing – Perf 11 – Unwatermarked Paper)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #610 is the 2¢ black commemorative issued in 1923 to honor President Warren G. Harding following his death. The design features a side-facing portrait of Harding within an ornate memorial frame, with “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” above and the denomination below. The years “1865” and “1923” appear in the upper corners, marking Harding’s birth and death. Printed by the flat plate method on unwatermarked paper and perforated gauge 11, this example is unused, hinged, and retains its original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #610 exhibits a strong engraved impression, original gum, and complete perforations. Tight left and right margins limit the centering grade to Very Good, while a hinge remnant and faint pencil writing result in a Very Minor Fault classification. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 43 (Good/Very Good). Despite its centering limitations, it remains a collectible example of the historically significant 1923 Harding Memorial Issue.', 0.14, 'LA01-0028-05-01-004'),
(95, 'United States', '610', '005', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Good', '2¢ Black – Warren G. Harding Memorial Issue, 1923\r\n(Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #610 is a 2¢ black memorial stamp issued in 1923 following the death of President Warren G. Harding. The design features a left-facing profile of Harding within an ornate oval frame surrounded by decorative scrollwork. “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” appears above the portrait, while the denomination is displayed below. The years “1865” and “1923” flank the portrait, commemorating Harding’s birth and death. Printed by the flat plate method on unwatermarked paper and perforated gauge 11, the submitted example is unused, hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #610 exhibits a strong engraved impression, original gum, and complete perforations. Very Good centering is limited by the tight bottom and right margins, while a hinge remnant and reverse ink transfer result in a Minor Fault classification. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 34 (Good). Although the centering and reverse faults restrict its grade, it remains a collectible example of the historically important Warren G. Harding Memorial Issue.', 0.10, 'NA01-0089-03-01-005'),
(96, 'United States', '610', '006', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '2¢ Black – Warren G. Harding Memorial Issue, 1923\r\n(Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #610 is a 2¢ black memorial stamp issued in 1923 to honor President Warren G. Harding following his death in office. The design features a left-facing portrait of Harding within an ornate oval frame embellished with scrollwork and decorative elements. The years “1865” and “1923” appear beside the portrait, commemorating his birth and death, while the denomination is displayed below. Printed by the flat plate method on unwatermarked paper and perforated gauge 11, it holds a unique place as the first U.S. memorial postage stamp. The submitted example is unused, never hinged, and retains its original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #610 combines original gum, never hinged status, and a strong engraved appearance with complete perforations and clean paper. Off-center margins limit the centering grade to Very Good, while light reverse ink transfer and a guideline on the perforation tips result in a Very Minor Fault classification. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 44 (Good/Very Good). Despite its centering limitations, it remains a visually appealing and collectible example of the historically significant Harding Memorial Issue.', 0.17, 'SL01-0001-07-06-006'),
(97, 'United States', '610', '009', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '2¢ Black – Warren G. Harding Memorial, 1923\r\n(Harding Memorial Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #610 is a 2¢ black commemorative issued in 1923 as the Harding Memorial Issue. The design features a left-facing portrait of President Warren G. Harding within an ornate oval frame, with “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” above and the denomination below. The years “1865” and “1923” appear on either side of the portrait, commemorating Harding’s birth and death. Produced by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing using the engraved process, the stamp is noted for its sharp detail and deep black color. The submitted example is used, never hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #610 combines Fine centering, complete perforations, and a remarkably attractive front appearance. A minor reverse ink transfer results in a Very Minor Fault classification, but the absence of a visible cancellation allows the engraved portrait and design details to remain fully visible. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 65 (Very Good/Fine). Its strong visual presentation makes it an especially appealing example of the historic Harding Memorial Issue.', 0.10, 'SL01-0001-09-06-009'),
(98, 'United States', '612', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good', '2¢ Black – Warren G. Harding, 1923\r\n(Rotary Press Printing – Memorial Issue, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #612 is the rotary press version of the 2¢ Warren G. Harding Memorial Issue released in 1923. The design features a left-facing portrait of Harding within an ornate oval frame accented by decorative scrollwork. The years “1865” and “1923” appear beside the portrait, commemorating his birth and death, while “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” arches above and the denomination is displayed below. Printed by the rotary press method and perforated gauge 11, this example is unused, never hinged, and retains full original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #612 combines faultless soundness, full original gum, and a sharp engraved appearance with the advantages of never hinged condition. The primary limitation is the noticeably off-center positioning that brings the right perforations very close to the design, resulting in a Very Good centering grade. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 50 (Very Good). Despite the centering limitations, it remains a fresh and collectible example of the rotary press Harding Memorial Issue.', 7.25, 'LA01-0028-05-03-002'),
(99, 'United States', '614', '003', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Good', '1¢ Green – Huguenot-Walloon Tercentenary, 1924\r\n(Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #614 is a 1¢ green commemorative issued in 1924 to celebrate the 300th anniversary of the Huguenot-Walloon settlement in New Netherland. The design depicts the ship Nieuw Nederland under full sail, symbolizing the voyage that brought the settlers to North America. Above the ship appears the inscription “Huguenot-Walloon Tercentenary,” flanked by the dates “1624” and “1924.” Printed by the flat plate method and perforated gauge 11, the issue is a popular early U.S. commemorative. The submitted example is unused, hinged, and retains its original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #614 combines Fine/Very Fine centering, strong color, and an attractive front presentation with the historical appeal of a classic U.S. commemorative issue. A heavy gum crease, together with additional minor reverse and perforation faults, results in a Major Fault classification and substantial deduction. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 31 (Good). Although the reverse condition limits its overall grade, it remains a visually appealing and collectible example of the Huguenot-Walloon Tercentenary issue.', 0.46, 'LA01-0029-01-01-003'),
(100, 'United States', '619', '001', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '5¢ Blue – The Minute Man, 1925\r\n(Lexington-Concord Sesquicentennial Commemorative – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #619 is the 5¢ blue commemorative issued in 1925 as part of the Lexington-Concord Sesquicentennial series. The design features the famous Minute Man statue, symbolizing the citizen soldiers who answered the call at the opening of the American Revolution. Decorative columns, engraved inscriptions, and patriotic motifs frame the central image, creating one of the more distinctive commemoratives of the 1920s. Printed by the flat plate method and perforated gauge 11, the submitted example is unused, never hinged, and retains its original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #619 combines Fine/Very Fine centering, full original gum, and an attractive engraved appearance with strong color and excellent paper quality. The only noted defect is light reverse ink transfer, resulting in a Very Minor Fault classification. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 72 (Fine). Its strong visual presentation and desirable never hinged status make it an appealing example of the Lexington-Concord Sesquicentennial commemorative issue.', 11.96, 'LA01-0029-02-03-001'),
(101, 'United States', '619', '004', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good', '5¢ Blue – The Minute Man, 1925\r\n(Lexington-Concord Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #619 was issued in 1925 to commemorate the 150th anniversary of the Battles of Lexington and Concord. The design features Daniel Chester French’s famous Minute Man statue framed by inscriptions adapted from Ralph Waldo Emerson’s Concord Hymn. Decorative elements, including rifles and drums, reinforce the Revolutionary War theme. Printed in deep blue by the engraved process and perforated gauge 11, the stamp is among the most popular commemoratives of the 1920s. The submitted example is unused, hinged, and retains its original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #619 combines Fine/Very Fine centering, strong color, and a crisp engraved appearance with the historical appeal of the Lexington-Concord commemorative series. A hinge remnant and reverse ink transfer result in a Minor Fault classification, reducing the final grade despite the attractive front presentation. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 59 (Very Good). It remains a visually appealing and collectible example of one of the most popular U.S. commemorative issues of the 1920s.', 5.80, 'NA01-0101-01-03-004'),
(102, 'United States', '602', '005', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair/Good', '5¢ Blue – Theodore Roosevelt, 1923\r\n(1922 Regular Issue – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #602 features Theodore Roosevelt in a formal frontal portrait within an oval frame, with “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” above, “ROOSEVELT” below the portrait, and large “5” numerals in the lower corners. The design belongs to the 1922 Regular Issue, a definitive series honoring major American figures in a clean engraved style. This submitted example is postally used, hinged, and retains partial original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #602 shows Fine/Very Fine centering, with the left margin serving as the limiting side and the top margin giving the stamp its most open spacing. A major tear, very short or nibbed perf, and two shorter perfs reduce soundness significantly. The heavy machine cancel adds a modest eye appeal deduction, resulting in a final grade of 26 (Fair/Good). Despite its faults, it remains a collectible example of the Theodore Roosevelt 5¢ issue under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.02, 'LA01-0097-03-02-005'),
(103, 'United States', '631', '005', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine/Very Fine', '1½¢ Brown – Warren G. Harding, 1926\r\n(Harding Memorial Issue – Imperforate)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #631 is a 1½¢ brown imperforate stamp issued in 1926 as part of the Harding Memorial Issue honoring President Warren G. Harding. The design features a left-facing portrait of Harding enclosed within an oval frame accented by decorative scrollwork. The denomination appears in circular tablets in the lower corners surrounding the word “CENTS.” Unlike the perforated versions, this imperforate issue was produced without perforations and was intended primarily for collectors and special distribution. The submitted example is unused, hinged, and retains its original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #631 combines Fine/Very Fine centering, attractive imperforate margins, and a strong engraved appearance. A light pencil marking on the reverse represents only an Extremely Minor Fault and has no impact on the front presentation. The generous bottom margin, rich brown color, and sharp impression contribute positively to eye appeal. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 75 (Fine/Very Fine), making this a desirable example of the imperforate Harding Memorial Issue for collectors of classic U.S. commemoratives.', 1.23, 'LA01-0031-02-04-005'),
(104, 'United States', '633', '008', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '1½¢ Brown – Warren G. Harding, 1926\r\n(Series of 1922 – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #633 is a 1½¢ brown definitive from the Series of 1922, featuring a left-facing portrait of President Warren G. Harding within a classic oval frame. “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” appears above the portrait, while the denomination is displayed prominently in the lower corners. Originally introduced as a memorial design following Harding’s death, the issue was later incorporated into the regular definitive series. Printed by the flat plate method on unwatermarked paper and perforated gauge 11, the submitted example is unused, hinged, and retains its original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #633 exhibits a strong engraved appearance, original gum, and attractive color despite noticeably tight centering on the left side. A shortened perforation and a natural gum bend result in a Very Minor Fault classification, reducing the final grade while leaving the overall presentation largely intact. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 43 (Good/Very Good). Although not a premium example, it remains a respectable and collectible representative of the Harding definitive issue.', 0.44, 'NA01-0105-02-03-008'),
(105, 'United States', '634', '010', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine', '2¢ Carmine Red – George Washington, 1926\r\n(Fourth Bureau Issue – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #634 is a 2¢ carmine red definitive issued in 1926 as part of the Fourth Bureau Issue. The design features a left-facing portrait of George Washington enclosed within an ornate oval frame, with “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” above and the denomination displayed below. Printed by the flat plate method and perforated gauge 11, the stamp was produced in enormous quantities for everyday postal use and remains one of the most familiar Washington portrait issues of the twentieth century. The submitted example is used and bears a circular date stamp cancellation.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #634 combines Fine/Very Fine centering, complete perforations, and faultless soundness with the strong color and sharp engraving expected of the issue. The principal limitation is the moderately heavy circular date stamp that crosses Washington’s portrait and reduces visual appeal. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 70 (Fine). Despite the cancellation, it remains an attractive and collectible example of one of the most recognizable Washington definitives of the Fourth Bureau era.', 0.11, 'LA01-0031-02-05-010'),
(106, 'United States', '637', '011', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '5¢ Blue – Theodore Roosevelt, 1925\r\n(Roosevelt Memorial Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #637 was issued in 1925 as part of the Roosevelt Memorial Issue honoring President Theodore Roosevelt. The design features an engraved portrait of Roosevelt within an oval frame, surrounded by geometric ornamentation typical of Bureau Issue designs. “United States Postage” appears above the portrait, while the denomination is displayed in the lower corners. The deep blue color and engraved detail give the issue strong visual appeal and make it popular with collectors of presidential material.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used example of Scott #637 displays Fine centering with visible white space on all sides and intact perforations. Multiple perforation creases reduce soundness to the Minor Fault category, lowering the final grade to 45 (Good/Very Good). Despite the faults, the portrait remains clear and the stamp retains collector appeal as a solid representative example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.05, 'NA01-0105-03-03-011'),
(107, 'United States', '639', '008', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Good', '7¢ Black – William McKinley, 1926\r\n(Series of 1922 – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #639 is a 7¢ black definitive from the Series of 1922, featuring a right-facing portrait of President William McKinley within an ornate oval frame. “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” appears prominently at the top, while the denomination is displayed in large numeral tablets in the lower corners. Printed by the flat plate process on unwatermarked paper and perforated gauge 11, the issue served as one of the higher-value denominations of the series. The submitted example is unused, hinged, and retains its original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #639 combines attractive Fine/Very Fine centering with strong color, crisp engraving, and an appealing front presentation. A large thin on the reverse, accompanied by several lesser defects, results in a Major Fault classification and substantial deduction under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide. Despite the technical shortcomings, the stamp maintains good visual appeal from the front and remains a collectible example of the higher-value McKinley issue. The final grade is 33 (Good).', 0.36, 'LA01-0031-04-01-008'),
(108, 'United States', '680', '003', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Good', '2¢ Carmine – General Anthony Wayne Memorial, 1929\r\n(Regular Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #680 commemorates General Anthony Wayne, a Revolutionary War figure, depicted standing with two companions in a classical engraved vignette. The design includes the inscriptions “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” and “GENERAL ANTHONY WAYNE MEMORIAL,” with ornamental scrollwork framing the scene. The engraving is detailed and balanced, typical of late 1920s commemorative issues. This example is unused, hinged, and with original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #680 example presents Fine centering with a clear and well-defined engraved design. However, multiple minor faults, including heavy ink transfer and irregular perforations, reduce its structural quality and overall desirability. With a final grade of 33 (Good), it remains a collectible but condition-limited example of this commemorative issue under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.12, 'LA01-0033-01-04-003'),
(109, 'United States', '684', '004', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '1½¢ Brown – Warren G. Harding, 1925–1931\r\n(Fourth Bureau Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11x10½)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #684 is a 1½¢ brown definitive from the Fourth Bureau Issue, featuring a finely engraved portrait of President Warren G. Harding within an ornate oval frame. “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” appears across the top, while the denomination is displayed prominently in the lower corners and the word “CENTS” anchors the design. Printed by the rotary press method and perforated gauge 11x10½, this stamp was widely used for third-class and bulk-rate mail during the late 1920s and early 1930s. The submitted example is used and bears a circular grid cancellation.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #684 is distinguished by outstanding Extremely Fine centering, with wide and well-balanced margins that are rarely encountered on this issue. A small bottom-edge tear results in a Fault classification and substantial deduction, while the moderately heavy circular grid cancellation further reduces the final score. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 63 (Very Good/Fine). Despite the tear, the stamp remains an attractive and highly collectible example due to its exceptional centering and strong visual presentation.', 0.10, 'NA01-0115-01-01-004'),
(110, 'United States', '685', '004', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair/Good', '4¢ Brown – William H. Taft, 1925\r\n(Series of 1922–1925 – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 10, Single-Line Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #585 is a 4¢ brown definitive from the Series of 1922–1925. The design features a right-facing portrait of President William H. Taft within an oval frame surrounded by ornamental scrollwork. “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” appears at the top, while the denomination is displayed below. Printed by the flat plate method on single-line watermarked paper and perforated gauge 10, the stamp was issued for everyday postal use. The submitted example is used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #585 combines complete perforations and acceptable structural integrity with Very Good centering. A hinge remnant, two corner perforation tip creases, and pencil writing result in a Minor Fault classification, while the heavy multi-line cancellation further reduces eye appeal. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 27 (Fair/Good). Despite its technical shortcomings, it remains a collectible example of the William H. Taft definitive from the Series of 1922–1925.', 0.02, 'LA01-0033-03-04-004'),
(111, 'United States', '701', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '50¢ Violet – Arlington Amphitheatre, 1931\r\n(Regular Issue – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #701 is a 50¢ violet definitive issued in 1931 featuring the Arlington Memorial Amphitheater at Arlington National Cemetery. The design depicts the neoclassical structure built to honor American military service and sacrifice, framed by ornate scrollwork with “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” above and the denomination displayed in the lower corners. Printed by the flat plate method and perforated gauge 11, this high-value definitive is noted for its architectural subject matter and rich violet coloration. The submitted example is used, never hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #701 combines Fine/Very Fine centering, complete perforations, and attractive violet color with only minor structural defects. Two very minor perforation-related faults result in a Minor Fault classification, while the heavy circular cancellation further reduces eye appeal. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 52 (Very Good). Despite the cancellation, the stamp remains a respectable and collectible example of this distinctive Arlington Amphitheater high-value definitive issue.', 0.08, 'LA01-0034-02-05-003'),
(112, 'United States', '684', '007', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '1½¢ Brown – Warren G. Harding, 1929\r\n(Regular Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #684 features a portrait of President Warren G. Harding centered within an oval frame, surrounded by a geometric border and inscriptions reading “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” and “1½ CENTS.” Issued in 1929, this design reflects the clean, balanced engraving style of late Bureau issues. The submitted example is used, hinged, and without original gum, showing a horizontal machine cancel and visible wear to the perforations.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #684 example demonstrates Fine/Very Fine centering with margins clear of the design, but its overall grade is significantly impacted by multiple perforation defects and a moderately heavy cancel. The resulting final grade of 39 (Good) reflects a mid- to lower-grade collectible example that retains decent visual identity despite its condition limitations.', 0.04, 'LA01-0033-03-01-007');
INSERT INTO `stamps` (`id`, `country`, `scott`, `count`, `condition`, `hinged`, `gum`, `grade`, `description`, `price`, `location`) VALUES
(113, 'United States', '704', '006', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '½¢ Olive Brown – George Washington, 1932\r\n(1932 Washington Bicentennial Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #704 is a ½¢ olive brown stamp issued in 1932 as part of the Washington Bicentennial series commemorating the 200th anniversary of the birth of George Washington. The engraved design features a portrait of Washington within an oval frame, with “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” above and “WASHINGTON” below. Denomination numerals appear on either side of the portrait. Printed by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing using the intaglio process and perforated gauge 11, the issue is known for its sharp detail and attractive coloration. The submitted example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #704 combines Fine/Very Fine centering, attractive original gum, and strong engraved detail with only minor technical imperfections. A perforation tip crease and a small natural gum bend result in a Very Minor Fault classification, producing a modest deduction from the centering grade. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 72 (Fine). The stamp remains an attractive and collectible example of the Washington Bicentennial issue, offering solid eye appeal and desirable never hinged status.', 0.14, 'SL01-0001-10-05-006'),
(114, 'United States', '705', '007', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '1¢ Green – George Washington, 1932\r\n(Washington Bicentennial Issue – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #705 is a 1¢ green stamp from the Washington Bicentennial Issue of 1932, commemorating the 200th anniversary of the birth of George Washington. The design features a left-facing portrait of Washington within an oval vignette framed by classical architectural elements. The dates “1732–1932” appear below the portrait, while denomination tablets occupy the lower corners. Printed by the flat plate method and perforated gauge 11, the issue is one of the most widely collected stamps in the Bicentennial series. The submitted example is used, never hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #705 displays complete perforations and recognizable Bicentennial design detail, but its grade is limited by tight left-side centering, two perforation-related faults, and a heavy machine cancellation. The blot across the lower vignette substantially reduces visual appeal and obscures portions of the engraved design. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 34 (Good). While not a premium example, it remains a collectible representative of the popular Washington Bicentennial series.', 0.04, 'SL01-0001-03-01-007'),
(115, 'United States', '705', '008', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair/Good', '1¢ Green – George Washington, 1932\r\n(Washington Bicentennial Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #705 was issued in 1932 to commemorate the bicentennial of George Washington’s birth. The design presents a left-facing profile of Washington within an oval frame, flanked by classical columns, with inscriptions including “United States Postage” and the dual dates “1732–1932.” The engraving is crisp and formal, typical of Bureau issues of the period, emphasizing symmetry and neoclassical balance. This example is postally used, hinged, and without original gum, with a visible machine cancel across the face and minor handling evidence on the reverse.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #705 example presents Very Good centering with noticeable margin imbalance, particularly along the left and bottom edges. Minor soundness issues further reduce the technical standing, and the heavy machine cancel diminishes visual clarity. Despite these factors, the stamp remains a solid, identifiable example of the Washington Bicentennial issue, earning a final grade of 21 (Fair/Good) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.02, 'LA01-0034-03-02-008'),
(116, 'United States', '706', '006', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '1½¢ Brown – George Washington, 1932\r\n(Washington Bicentennial Issue – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #706 is a 1½¢ brown stamp from the Washington Bicentennial Issue of 1932, commemorating the 200th anniversary of the birth of George Washington. The engraved design portrays Washington in military uniform within an oval frame flanked by classical columns. The dates “1732–1932” appear in decorative banners, while denomination tablets occupy the lower corners. Printed by the flat plate method and perforated gauge 11, the issue is one of the most distinctive values of the Bicentennial series. The submitted example is used, never hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #706 displays complete perforations and a clearly identifiable Washington Bicentennial design, but its grade is limited by uneven centering, several ragged perforation tips, and a heavy oval cancellation that obscures much of the portrait. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 38 (Good). While not a premium example, it remains a collectible representative of this popular Bicentennial issue and retains sufficient detail for inclusion in a general collection.', 0.04, 'NA01-0117-01-03-006'),
(117, 'United States', '707', '004', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '2¢ Carmine – George Washington, 1932\r\n(Washington Bicentennial Series – Engraved, Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #707 is a 2¢ carmine stamp from the Washington Bicentennial Series issued in 1932 to commemorate the 200th anniversary of the birth of George Washington. The engraved design features a portrait of Washington framed by ornamental scrollwork and the dates “1732” and “1932.” “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” appears at the top, while “WASHINGTON” and the denomination are displayed below. Printed by the flat plate method on unwatermarked paper and perforated gauge 11, the issue is among the most widely collected stamps of the Bicentennial set. The submitted example is unused, hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #707 combines Fine/Very Fine centering, fresh original gum, and strong engraved detail with only minor reverse imperfections. The hinge remnant and natural gum bend result in a modest Very Minor Fault deduction, but neither affects the attractive front presentation. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 72 (Fine). The stamp remains a pleasing and collectible example of the popular Washington Bicentennial issue, offering solid centering, vibrant color, and strong overall eye appeal.', 0.19, 'NA01-0117-01-04-004'),
(118, 'United States', '707', '008', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Poor', '2¢ Carmine – George Washington, 1932\r\n(Washington Bicentennial Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #707 was issued in 1932 to commemorate the bicentennial of George Washington’s birth. The design features a finely engraved portrait of Washington centered within a rectangular frame, with “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” above and “WASHINGTON” with the dual dates 1732–1932 below. The engraving is crisp, with strong line detail typical of Bureau issues of the period. This example is used, hinged, and without original gum, consistent with normal postal usage.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #707 presents as a heavily used example with Very Good centering offset by a moderate crease and a dominant, very heavy machine cancel. The combination of structural fault and visual obstruction drives the grade down to 8 (Poor). While far from a premium piece, it remains a complete and identifiable example of the Washington Bicentennial issue, suitable as a low-grade filler within the framework of the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.01, 'LA01-0034-03-04-008'),
(119, 'United States', '712', '005', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '7¢ Black – George Washington, 1932\r\n(1932 Washington Bicentennial Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #712 is a 7¢ black stamp from the Washington Bicentennial Issue of 1932, commemorating the 200th anniversary of the birth of George Washington. The engraved design features a frontal portrait of Washington within an oval frame, with “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” across the top and the dates “1732” and “1932” flanking the lower portion of the portrait. “WASHINGTON” appears beneath the vignette, while the denomination is displayed in the lower corners. Printed by the intaglio process and perforated gauge 11, the issue is known for its sharp detail and bold appearance. The submitted example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #712 combines Fine/Very Fine centering, fresh original gum, and strong engraved detail with only a minor production-related imperfection. The natural gum bend results in a small deduction under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide but has no meaningful effect on the stamp’s visual presentation. The final grade is 73 (Fine). With its bold black color, intact perforations, and desirable never hinged status, it remains an attractive and collectible example of the Washington Bicentennial series.', 0.40, 'SD22-0003-05-02-005'),
(120, 'United States', '716', '002', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine/Very Fine', '2¢ Carmine Rose – Olympic Winter Games, 1932\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #716 was issued to commemorate the III Olympic Winter Games held in Lake Placid, New York, during February 1932. The design portrays a ski jumper in flight above a snow-covered landscape, symbolizing the athletic competition and winter setting of the event. “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” and “III OLYMPIC WINTER GAMES” appear prominently across the upper portion of the design, while the dates and location of the games are incorporated into the lower inscription. Printed by the flat plate process in carmine rose and perforated gauge 11, the stamp remains one of the most recognizable American Olympic commemoratives.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis example of Scott #716 combines Very Fine centering, full original perforations, and strong engraved detail with only minor age-related discoloration affecting the paper. The slight yellowing results in a modest soundness deduction, but the stamp\'s attractive layout and rich carmine rose color support a small eye appeal bonus. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final numerical grade is 77 (Fine/Very Fine), making it a desirable mid-grade example of this popular Olympic commemorative issue.', 0.21, 'LA01-0035-01-01-002'),
(121, 'United States', '716', '004', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '2¢ Carmine – Olympic Winter Games, 1932\r\n(Lake Placid Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #716 commemorates the 1932 Winter Olympic Games held in Lake Placid, New York. The design features a skier descending a slope with a mountainous background, accompanied by the inscriptions “III OLYMPIC WINTER GAMES” and “LAKE PLACID NEW YORK FEBRUARY 4–13, 1932.” The engraving conveys motion and depth, characteristic of early 1930s commemorative issues. This example is unused, never hinged, and with original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #716 example presents Fine/Very Fine centering with a sharp and visually appealing design. While minor back smudging and ink transfer result in a Minor Fault classification, these issues have minimal impact on the front appearance. With a final grade of 60 (Very Good/Fine), it remains a clean and collectible example of this classic Olympic commemorative under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.17, 'NA01-0117-05-02-004'),
(122, 'United States', '727', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '3¢ Violet – Washington’s Headquarters, Newburgh, N.Y., 1933\r\n(Washington Bicentennial Issue – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #727 was issued in 1933 as part of the Washington Bicentennial commemorative series honoring the 150th anniversary of George Washington’s headquarters at Newburgh, New York. The engraved design depicts the historic headquarters building surrounded by landscape elements in a horizontal format. “U.S. POSTAGE” appears at the top, while “WASHINGTON’S HEADQUARTERS NEWBURGH N.Y. 1783–1933” is incorporated below the vignette. Printed in violet by the flat plate process and perforated gauge 11, the stamp remains a popular historical commemorative. This example is postally used, never hinged, and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #727 combines Fine/Very Fine centering with intact perforations but is limited by several perforation creases and a very heavy cancellation. The large slogan-style strike obscures much of the headquarters vignette, reducing eye appeal and visual balance. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, these factors result in a final grade of 42 (Good/Very Good). Despite the deductions, it remains a collectible example of this popular Washington Bicentennial commemorative issue.', 0.05, 'LA01-0035-04-02-003'),
(123, 'United States', '728', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '1¢ Green – Fort Dearborn, 1933\r\n(Chicago Century of Progress Issue – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #728 was issued in 1933 as part of the Chicago Century of Progress commemorative series celebrating Chicago’s centennial and the World’s Fair held that year. The engraved design depicts historic Fort Dearborn, one of Chicago’s earliest landmarks, framed by trees and landscape details. “CHICAGO CENTURY OF PROGRESS” arches above the vignette, while the dates “1833” and “1933” appear in the upper corners. The inscription “FORT DEARBORN” is placed below the central image, with the denomination shown in the lower corners. This example is postally used, never hinged, and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #728 displays Fine centering with complete perforations and strong engraved detail, but several perforation creases place it in the Minor Fault category. The heavy machine cancel further reduces eye appeal by obscuring portions of the Fort Dearborn vignette. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, these combined factors result in a final grade of 35 (Good). Despite the deductions, it remains a collectible example of the popular Chicago Century of Progress commemorative issue.', 0.04, 'LA01-0036-02-01-003'),
(124, 'United States', '728', '004', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good', '1¢ Green – Fort Dearborn, 1933\r\n(Chicago Century of Progress Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #728 was issued for the 1933 Chicago World’s Fair, titled “A Century of Progress.” The design depicts historic Fort Dearborn, with inscriptions “CHICAGO CENTURY OF PROGRESS” and the dates 1833–1933 across the top. The vignette is framed by a clean rectangular border, typical of early 1930s commemoratives, with a focus on architectural and historical imagery. This example is unused, hinged, and with original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #728 example exhibits Fine/Very Fine centering and a strong engraved design with good visual presence. The grade is limited by irregular perforations and minor structural faults, which place it in the Fault category. With a final grade of 50 (Very Good), it remains a solid and collectible example of this Chicago Century of Progress issue under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.08, 'NA01-0119-01-02-004'),
(125, 'United States', '732', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '3¢ Violet – National Recovery Administration, 1933\r\n(National Recovery Administration Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #732 was issued in 1933 during the early years of the Great Depression to promote the National Recovery Administration (NRA), a major New Deal program intended to encourage economic recovery and national cooperation. The design depicts four workers advancing together, representing agriculture, industry, labor, and civic participation under the slogan “IN A COMMON DETERMINATION.” “U.S. POSTAGE” appears at the top, “THREE CENTS” at left, and “N.R.A.” prominently within the design. Printed in violet, the issue reflects the social and economic concerns of the period. This example is used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #732 presents attractive Fine/Very Fine centering with a fully readable NRA design and an unobtrusive cancel. The moderate crease lowers the stamp to the Fault category, reducing the numerical grade, though the stamp remains visually appealing and historically collectible. Final grade: 46 (Good/Very Good).', 0.05, 'LA01-0035-04-03-003'),
(126, 'United States', '733', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '3¢ Blue – Byrd Antarctic Expedition II, 1933\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #733 was issued in 1933 to commemorate the second Antarctic expedition led by Admiral Richard E. Byrd. The design features a polar projection of the globe centered on the Americas, with routes marking several of Byrd’s pioneering flights and exploration achievements. The inscription “BYRD ANTARCTIC EXPEDITION II” appears across the top, while “U.S. POSTAGE 3 CENTS” is incorporated into the lower frame. Printed in deep blue by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing using the flat plate process and perforated gauge 11, the issue reflects the public fascination with polar exploration during the early twentieth century. The submitted example is postally used, never hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #733 combines Fine centering, complete perforations, and an attractive overall presentation. The top and right margins are somewhat narrow but remain clear of the design, supporting the assigned centering grade. A light ink transfer on the reverse results in a Very Minor Fault classification and the corresponding deduction, while the light cancellation preserves the clarity of the globe vignette and expedition routes. Strong color, sharp engraving, and clean visual appearance contribute positively to the stamp’s appeal. The final grade is 60 (Very Good/Fine), making this a pleasing and collectible example of the Byrd Antarctic Expedition II commemorative under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.20, 'LA01-0038-03-01-002'),
(127, 'United States', '735a', '002', 'Unused', 'Never Hinged', 'NGAI - No Gum As Issued', 'Very Good', '3¢ Blue – Byrd Antarctic Expedition II, 1933\r\n(Byrd Antarctic Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, No Watermark, No Gum as Issued)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #735a commemorates the second Byrd Antarctic Expedition and features a detailed globe centered on the Western Hemisphere, with expedition routes marked across the polar regions. The inscriptions “BYRD ANTARCTIC EXPEDITION II” and “U.S. POSTAGE” frame the design, while dotted lines trace the exploratory paths undertaken during Byrd’s Antarctic ventures. The finely engraved design emphasizes geographic exploration and scientific achievement, making it one of the more distinctive United States commemoratives of the era. This example is unused, hinged, and from the ungummed version of the issue, which was distributed without gum as issued.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #735a exhibits Fine/Very Fine centering, strong color, and a well-defined engraved impression. The heavy ink transfer on the reverse results in a Minor Fault classification and limits the final numerical grade, but the front presentation remains attractive and undisturbed. Complete perforations, balanced overall appearance, and the collectible nature of the ungummed Byrd Antarctic variety support its desirability. The final grade is 58 (Very Good), making it a solid representative example of this distinctive Antarctic commemorative issue.', 0.35, 'LA01-0038-03-03-002'),
(128, 'United States', '735a', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NGAI - No Gum As Issued', 'Very Fine', 'Stamp Evaluation – Scott #735a (Used, No Gum as Issued – Single Stamp)\r\n3¢ Blue – Byrd Antarctic Expedition II, 1933\r\n(Byrd Antarctic Issue – Engraved, Imperforate, No Watermark, No Gum as Issued)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview:\r\nScott #735a commemorates the second Byrd Antarctic Expedition and features a detailed engraved globe centered on the Western Hemisphere, with dotted expedition routes crossing the polar regions and dates marking Byrd’s earlier explorations. “BYRD ANTARCTIC EXPEDITION II” appears prominently at the top, while “U.S. POSTAGE” and “CENTS” frame the denomination tablets below. The stamp was issued without gum, and this example is postally used with no original gum as issued.\r\n\r\nGrading Breakdown:\r\nCentering Grade: 85 (Very Fine/Extremely Fine)\r\nThe stamp is well centered for the imperforate format, with all margins clear of the design. The top margin is the most narrow, while the bottom margin is the most generous. The left margin is slightly narrower than the right, but the overall balance remains strong. The design is comfortably separated from all outer edges, supporting a Very Fine/Extremely Fine centering grade.\r\n\r\nSoundness Evaluation:\r\nExtremely Minor Fault: Pencil writing on the reverse.\r\nSoundness Classification: Extremely Minor Fault.\r\nSoundness Deduction: –3 points.\r\n\r\nPerforations and Margins:\r\nAs an imperforate issue, perforation condition is not applicable. Margin evaluation is based on cut, balance, and design clearance. The margins are clean and complete, with no evidence of trimming into the design. The top is closest, the bottom widest, and the side margins remain adequate for the assigned centering grade.\r\n\r\nCancellation Evaluation:\r\nThe stamp bears a light gray-black postal cancellation with vertical lettering crossing the upper and right portions of the design. The cancel is visible but does not obscure the globe, expedition routes, denomination, or major inscriptions. Under PSE eye appeal standards, this is best classified as a Light cancel, with no deduction warranted.\r\n\r\nCollector’s Note:\r\nScott #735a is desirable because it represents the no-gum-as-issued variety of the Byrd Antarctic commemorative. Used examples with clear design visibility and balanced imperforate margins remain appealing to both U.S. commemorative and polar exploration collectors.\r\n\r\nEye Appeal Adjustment:\r\nThe stamp displays strong blue color, crisp engraved detail, and a clean front presentation. The light cancel is unobtrusive, and the pencil writing on the reverse does not affect the face. Eye Appeal Adjustment: 0 points.\r\n\r\nFinal Numerical Grade Calculation:\r\nCentering Grade: 85 (Very Fine/Extremely Fine)\r\nSoundness Deduction: –3 (Extremely Minor Fault)\r\nAdjusted Base Grade: 82\r\nEye Appeal Adjustment: 0\r\nFinal Grade: 82 (Very Fine)\r\n\r\nSummary:\r\nScott #735a presents strong Very Fine/Extremely Fine centering for the imperforate no-gum-as-issued format, with clean margins and a sharp engraved globe design. The only noted soundness issue is pencil writing on the reverse, resulting in an Extremely Minor Fault deduction. The light postal cancellation confirms use while preserving the clarity of the design and requiring no eye appeal penalty. The final grade is 82 (Very Fine), making this a clean, attractive, and collectible used example of the Byrd Antarctic Expedition II issue under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.95, 'SL01-0001-08-02-003'),
(129, 'United States', '739', '003', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '3¢ Violet – Wisconsin Tercentenary, 1934\r\n(Wisconsin Tercentenary Issue – Engraved, Perf. 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #739 commemorates the 300th anniversary of Jean Nicolet’s landing at Green Bay in 1634. The engraved design portrays Nicolet meeting Native Americans along the shoreline, framed by decorative side panels and inscriptions marking the tercentenary. The scene is rendered with fine line detail and balanced composition typical of Bureau engraving of the period. The submitted example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #739 example displays attractive Fine/Very Fine centering and strong engraved detail, supported by its never hinged status. However, the presence of a natural straight edge along with additional minor faults significantly reduces the technical grade to 49 Good/Very Good. Despite this, the stamp maintains solid visual appeal and remains a collectible, though less desirable, example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.06, 'LA01-0041-04-02-003'),
(130, 'United States', '740', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '1¢ Green – Yosemite National Park, 1934\r\n(National Parks Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #740 was issued in 1934 as part of the National Parks Series, a popular commemorative set honoring America’s scenic landmarks. The design depicts Yosemite National Park, with Half Dome rising above the valley floor and framed by evergreen trees in the foreground. “YOSEMITE” appears in a banner at the bottom, while the denomination and postal inscriptions are incorporated into the frame. Printed in green by the rotary press method and perforated gauge 11, the stamp reflects the growing conservation movement of the early twentieth century. The submitted example is postally used, never hinged, and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #740 displays Fine/Very Fine centering, intact perforations, and a pleasing Yosemite vignette. Two creases result in a Major Fault classification and account for the majority of the grade reduction, while the light reverse ink transfer has minimal impact on presentation. The machine cancel remains typical of postal use and preserves the key design elements. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the stamp earns a final grade of 31 (Good) and remains a collectible example of this popular National Parks issue.', 0.04, 'LA01-0039-01-01-002'),
(131, 'United States', '743', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair/Good', '4¢ Brown – Mesa Verde National Park, 1934\r\n(National Parks Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #743 was issued in 1934 as part of the National Parks Series, a commemorative set celebrating America\'s most significant natural and historic landmarks. The design depicts the famous cliff dwellings of Mesa Verde National Park in Colorado, showcasing the remarkable architecture of the Ancestral Puebloans. Vertical side panels contain the inscriptions “U.S. POSTAGE” and “FOUR CENTS,” while “MESA VERDE” appears in the lower panel beneath the vignette. Printed in brown by the rotary press method and perforated gauge 11, the stamp combines historical and archaeological significance with the artistic style characteristic of the National Parks Series. The submitted example is postally used, never hinged, and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #743 displays Fine/Very Fine centering with complete perforations and an attractive National Parks design. However, a large tear results in a Major Fault classification, and the additional crease further impacts condition. The heavy circular cancel obscures important portions of the Mesa Verde vignette and reduces eye appeal. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the stamp receives a final grade of 21 (Fair/Good), remaining a collectible but heavily compromised example of this popular National Parks issue.', 0.04, 'LA01-0039-02-03-002'),
(132, 'United States', '744', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '5¢ Blue – Yellowstone National Park, 1934\r\n(National Parks Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #744 was issued in 1934 as part of the National Parks Series, one of the most admired commemorative sets of the twentieth century. The design depicts Old Faithful erupting in Yellowstone National Park, with the famous geyser plume rising above the surrounding landscape. “YELLOWSTONE” appears in a banner above the denomination, while “U.S. POSTAGE” is incorporated into the lower frame. Printed in blue by the rotary press method and perforated gauge 11, the stamp celebrates one of America\'s most iconic natural landmarks. The submitted example is postally used, never hinged, and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #744 displays Fine/Very Fine centering with the design fully clear of the perforations and a visually appealing Yellowstone vignette. A moderate crease and natural straight edge result in a Major Fault classification, while additional minor faults further affect condition. The heavy circular cancel obscures part of the geyser plume and lowers eye appeal. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the stamp receives a final grade of 20 (Fair/Good) and remains a collectible but heavily compromised example of this popular National Parks issue.', 0.04, 'LA01-0039-01-02-002'),
(133, 'United States', '745', '002', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good', '6¢ Blue – Crater Lake, 1934\r\n(Regular Issue – Engraved, Perf 11 x 10½, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #745 is a 6¢ blue engraved issue depicting Crater Lake in Oregon, released in 1934 as part of the Presidential-era landscape definitives. The design features a panoramic view of the lake with Wizard Island in the foreground and surrounding cliffs rendered in fine engraved detail. “U.S. POSTAGE” appears at the top, with “CRATER LAKE” centered below the vignette and denomination panels in the lower corners. This example is unused, with original gum present and hinged.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #745 presents as a solid mid-grade example with Fine/Very Fine centering and intact perforations. While minor back faults reduce the technical grade to Very Good, the stamp retains strong color, a clean engraved appearance, and balanced visual appeal. It remains a respectable and collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, offering good value within its grade tier.', 0.42, 'LA01-0039-03-01-002'),
(134, 'United States', '747', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '8¢ Green – Zion National Park, 1934\r\n(National Parks Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #747 was issued in 1934 as part of the National Parks Series, a commemorative set celebrating some of America\'s most scenic and historically significant landscapes. The design depicts the dramatic sandstone cliffs and canyon scenery of Zion National Park in Utah, with a winding river in the foreground creating depth within the vignette. The denomination appears in the upper right corner, while “ZION” is inscribed beneath the scene and “U.S. POSTAGE” is incorporated into the lower frame. Printed in green by the rotary press method and perforated gauge 11, the issue is among the more visually striking stamps of the National Parks Series. The submitted example is postally used, hinged, and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #747 combines Fine/Very Fine centering, complete perforations, and an attractive Zion National Park vignette. A perf tip crease and light reverse ink transfer result in a Minor Fault classification, accounting for the reduction from the centering grade. The moderate machine cancel preserves the key design elements and does not materially diminish eye appeal. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the stamp earns a final grade of 57 (Very Good) and remains a solid collectible example of this popular National Parks issue.', 0.48, 'LA01-0039-01-03-002'),
(135, 'United States', '747', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '8¢ Green – Zion National Park, 1934\r\n(National Parks Issue – Engraved, Perf 11 x 10½, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #747 is part of the 1934 National Parks issue, featuring a detailed engraved depiction of Zion National Park in Utah. The design centers on a towering rock formation rising above a winding river, rendered in fine line engraving typical of Bureau issues of the period. The denomination appears in the upper right, with “ZION” at the base and “U.S. POSTAGE” below. This example is used, never hinged, and without original gum, consistent with postally handled material lacking gum retention.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #747 offers a pleasing example of the Zion National Park issue with Fine/Very Fine centering and strong visual appeal. While minor perforation faults and light reverse ink transfer reduce the technical grade, the nearly face-free cancel and crisp engraving enhance its presentation. The final grade of 61 (Very Good/Fine) reflects a balanced and collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.62, 'NA01-0127-04-01-003'),
(136, 'United States', '750', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Good', '3¢ Violet – Mount Rainier, 1934\r\n(Farley National Parks Issue – Imperforate Souvenir Pane, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #750 is the 3¢ violet Mount Rainier imperforate souvenir pane of six from the 1934 National Parks issue, prepared for the American Philatelic Society convention at Atlantic City. The pane shows six Mount Rainier designs in a two-by-three layout, with the mountain, lake, and forest scene repeated across the sheet. The surrounding inscriptions identify the Treasury Department, Bureau of Engraving and Printing, Postmaster General James A. Farley, the APS convention, Atlantic City, August 1934, and plate number 21303. The violet printing remains clear, and the designs retain detail.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #750 begins with Very Fine 80 centering, limited by the narrow top margin and right side, while the bottom is most generous. Major Fault soundness lowers the technical grade to Good 35 because of the large stain or several small stains. No cancellation is present, and no eye appeal adjustment is applied. Final Grade: Good 35. This is a complete unused pane with intact marginal inscriptions, but staining limits the final numerical grade.', 3.96, 'LA01-0040-01-01-002'),
(137, 'United States', '772', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '3¢ Purple – Connecticut Tercentenary (Charter Oak), 1935\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #772 was issued in 1935 to commemorate the 300th anniversary of the founding of Connecticut. The design features the historic Charter Oak, a symbol of colonial self-government and independence associated with Connecticut\'s early history. The inscriptions “CONNECTICUT TERCENTENARY” and the dates “1635–1935” frame the vignette, while the denomination appears in the lower corners. Printed in purple by the rotary press method and perforated gauge 11, the stamp commemorates one of the most enduring symbols of colonial America. The submitted example is postally used, hinged, and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #772 combines Fine/Very Fine centering, intact perforations, and strong color with only a minor production-related paper inclusion. The principal limitation is the heavy machine cancel, which obscures a substantial portion of the Charter Oak vignette and reduces eye appeal. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the stamp earns a final grade of 63 (Very Good/Fine) and remains a collectible example of this popular Connecticut Tercentenary commemorative.', 0.10, 'LA01-0045-01-01-002'),
(138, 'United States', '773', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '3¢ Violet – California Pacific International Exposition, 1935\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11×10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #773 was issued in 1935 to commemorate the California Pacific International Exposition held in San Diego. The design presents an elevated view of Balboa Park with the California Tower serving as the central focal point. The exposition title and denomination are incorporated into the frame, while the rich violet color gives the stamp a distinctive appearance among mid-1930s commemoratives. Printed by the rotary press method and perforated gauge 11×10½, the issue celebrates one of the major expositions of the era. The submitted example is postally used, never hinged, and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #773 combines Fine/Very Fine centering with intact perforations and a historically interesting exposition design. However, the combination of reverse condition issues results in a Minor Fault classification, while the very heavy “U.S. SAVINGS” slogan cancel significantly reduces eye appeal and obscures much of the vignette. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the stamp earns a final grade of 35 (Good) and remains a collectible but heavily canceled example of this exposition commemorative.', 0.04, 'LA01-0045-01-02-002'),
(139, 'United States', '774', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair', '3¢ Violet – Boulder Dam, 1935\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11×10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #774 was issued in 1935 to commemorate the completion of Boulder Dam, later renamed Hoover Dam, one of the most significant engineering achievements of the twentieth century. The engraved vignette presents the massive dam spanning Black Canyon, emphasizing both the scale of the project and its importance to the development of the American Southwest. The inscription “BOULDER DAM – 1935” appears below the scene, while the denomination is placed in the lower corners. Printed in violet by the rotary press method and perforated gauge 11×10½, the stamp remains a popular commemorative among collectors of engineering and western Americana topics. The submitted example is postally used, hinged, and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #774 combines Fine/Very Fine centering with intact perforations and a historically important commemorative design. However, the presence of a severe crease or multiple creases results in a Severe Fault classification that substantially lowers the technical grade. The very heavy barred cancel further reduces eye appeal by obscuring significant portions of the vignette. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the stamp earns a final grade of 12 (Fair) and serves primarily as a representative example of the Boulder Dam commemorative issue.', 0.02, 'LA01-0045-03-01-002'),
(140, 'United States', '775', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair', '3¢ Violet – Michigan Centenary, 1935\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11×10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #775 was issued in 1935 to commemorate the centennial of Michigan\'s admission to the Union in 1835. The design features the Michigan state seal flanked by the United States and Michigan state flags, with industrial and agricultural motifs representing the state\'s growth and prosperity. The denomination appears in the lower corners, while commemorative inscriptions frame the central vignette. Printed in violet by the rotary press process and perforated gauge 11×10½, the stamp is a popular member of the classic 1930s commemorative era. The submitted example is postally used, hinged, and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #775 exhibits Very Good centering but is significantly affected by multiple perforation defects and a very heavy slogan cancel. The Fault classification substantially reduces the technical grade, while the cancel further lowers eye appeal by obscuring portions of the Michigan seal. Despite these limitations, the stamp remains an identifiable and collectible example of the Michigan Centenary commemorative issue. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 12 (Fair).', 0.02, 'LA01-0045-02-01-002'),
(141, 'United States', '775', '004', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '3¢ Violet – Michigan Statehood Centennial, 1935\r\n(Michigan Statehood Centennial Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #775 was issued in 1935 for the centennial of Michigan statehood. The violet design shows the Great Seal of Michigan between draped United States and Michigan flags, with “1835” and “1935” at top and “U.S. POSTAGE” below. The seal and mottos give the stamp its statehood identity. This example is used, hinged, and without original gum. Hinging is not treated as a grading fault here.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #775 has Fine/Very Fine centering, with the bottom margin narrowest, the right margin widest, and all perforations clear of the design. Soundness is reduced by discoloration, heavy back smudging and spotting, one shortened perforation, and paper residue. The heavy cancel further limits eye appeal, but the design remains identifiable under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide. Final Grade: 41 (Good/Very Good).', 0.05, 'NA01-0139-02-01-004'),
(142, 'United States', '776', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair/Good', '3¢ Violet – Texas Centennial, 1936\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11×10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #776 was issued in 1936 to commemorate the centennial of Texas independence. The design combines three important elements of Texas history: the Alamo at center, Sam Houston at left, and Stephen F. Austin at right. The inscription “UNITED STATES POSTAGE TEXAS CENTENNIAL 1836–1936” spans the upper portion of the design, while “THE ALAMO” appears below the vignette. Printed in violet by the rotary press process and perforated gauge 11×10½, the stamp is one of the most popular issues of the Texas Centennial celebrations. The submitted example is postally used, hinged, and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #776 benefits from Fine/Very Fine centering and intact perforations, providing a stronger technical foundation than many surviving examples of the issue. However, the large thin results in a Major Fault classification, and the very heavy barred cancel substantially obscures the Alamo and both portraits. These factors combine to reduce the final grade to 21 (Fair/Good). Despite its condition limitations, the stamp remains a collectible representative of the popular Texas Centennial commemorative issue under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.02, 'LA01-0045-04-01-002'),
(143, 'United States', '782', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '3¢ Violet – Arkansas Centennial, 1936\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11×10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #782 was issued in 1936 to commemorate the centennial of Arkansas statehood. The design features the Old State House at center, flanked by views of Arkansas Post on the left and the modern State Capitol on the right, symbolizing the state\'s progression from frontier settlement to established statehood. The inscriptions “ARKANSAS 1836” and “CENTENNIAL 1936” frame the central vignette, while the denomination appears in the lower corners. Printed in violet by the rotary press process and perforated gauge 11×10½, the stamp is one of several attractive statehood centennial commemoratives issued during the 1930s. The submitted example is postally used, hinged, and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #782 benefits from Fine/Very Fine centering, faultless soundness, and fully intact perforations, giving it a strong technical foundation. The only significant detracting factor is the heavy circular cancel, which obscures portions of the Old State House vignette and reduces visual impact. Even with the eye appeal deduction, the stamp earns a final grade of 68 (Very Good/Fine) and remains a desirable collectible example of the Arkansas Centennial commemorative issue under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.10, 'LA01-0045-04-02-002'),
(144, 'United States', '783', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '3¢ Purple – Oregon Territory Centennial, 1936\r\n(Oregon Territory Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #783 was issued in 1936 to commemorate the centennial of the Oregon Territory. Printed in purple, the design features a central map of the Oregon country with route markings, flanked by western imagery including settlers, oxen, and frontier figures. The inscriptions read “1836 OREGON TERRITORY 1936” above and “U.S. POSTAGE THREE CENTS” below. This example is postally used, never hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #783 grades 35 (Good). The stamp begins with Very Good centering, limited mainly by the very narrow right margin and tighter bottom margin. Soundness is reduced only by one short perforation, keeping the structural faults modest. The heavy machine cancel lowers eye appeal, but the Oregon Territory map design remains identifiable and the stamp remains a collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.04, 'LA01-0045-04-03-002'),
(145, 'United States', '784', '003', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '3¢ Violet – Susan B. Anthony, 1936\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Perf 11 x 10½, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #784 was issued in 1936 to honor Susan B. Anthony and the women’s suffrage movement. The engraved design features a left-facing portrait of Anthony within an oval frame, with “SUFFRAGE FOR WOMEN” prominently inscribed below. “U.S. POSTAGE” appears at the top, and the denomination is shown in circular panels at the lower corners. The design is clean and symmetrical, with strong engraving typical of the period. This example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #784 example presents as a solid Fine-centered stamp with strong engraving and excellent eye appeal. A minor natural gum bend slightly reduces the technical grade, but the stamp retains a clean and attractive appearance. The final grade of 68 (Very Good/Fine) reflects a well-preserved and collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.16, 'NA01-0139-02-02-003'),
(146, 'United States', '801', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '3¢ Violet – Puerto Rico (La Fortaleza), 1937\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Perf 11 x 10½, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #801 was issued in 1937 depicting La Fortaleza in San Juan, Puerto Rico, one of the oldest executive mansions in continuous use in the Western Hemisphere. The engraved design shows the historic coastal structure with surrounding walls and waterfront, framed by ornamental corner motifs. “U.S. POSTAGE” appears at the top, with “PUERTO RICO” and “LA FORTALEZA” below. This example is used, never hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #801 example presents as a well-centered Fine/Very Fine stamp with a clean and appealing engraved design. While minor perforation faults reduce the technical grade, the nearly face-free cancel and strong visual clarity enhance its overall appeal. The final grade of 64 (Very Good/Fine) reflects a balanced and collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.10, 'LA01-0048-02-02-002'),
(147, 'United States', '803', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '½¢ Orange – Benjamin Franklin, 1938\r\n(Presidential Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11×10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #803 is the ½¢ orange Benjamin Franklin stamp from the 1938 Presidential Issue, commonly known as the Prexies. The design features a left-facing portrait of Franklin within a clean frame bearing the inscriptions “UNITED STATES POSTAGE,” “BENJAMIN FRANKLIN,” and “½ CENT.” The series was created to honor all deceased U.S. Presidents and Franklin, making it one of the most collected definitive issues in American philately. This example is postally used, never hinged, and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #803 combines Very Fine centering with strong color and a pleasing overall layout. The grade is reduced by a Fault classification resulting from the nibbed perforation and additional minor condition issues, while the heavy machine cancel further impacts eye appeal. Despite these deductions, the stamp remains a collectible example of the popular Presidential Issue and earns a final grade of 48 (Good/Very Good) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.05, 'LA01-0049-01-01-003');
INSERT INTO `stamps` (`id`, `country`, `scott`, `count`, `condition`, `hinged`, `gum`, `grade`, `description`, `price`, `location`) VALUES
(148, 'United States', '803', '006', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '½¢ Orange – Benjamin Franklin, 1938\r\n(Presidential Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11×10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #803 is the ½¢ orange Benjamin Franklin stamp from the 1938 Presidential Issue, commonly known as the Prexies. The design features a left-facing portrait of Franklin within a clean frame bearing the inscriptions “UNITED STATES POSTAGE,” “BENJAMIN FRANKLIN,” and “½ CENT.” The series was created to honor all deceased U.S. Presidents and Franklin, making it one of the most collected definitive issues in American philately. This example is postally used, never hinged, and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #803 combines Very Fine centering with strong color and a pleasing overall layout. The grade is reduced by a Fault classification resulting from the nibbed perforation and additional minor condition issues, while the heavy machine cancel further impacts eye appeal. Despite these deductions, the stamp remains a collectible example of the popular Presidential Issue and earns a final grade of 48 (Good/Very Good) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.08, 'NA01-0147-01-01-006'),
(149, 'United States', '804', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair/Good', '1¢ Green – George Washington, 1938\r\n(Presidential Issue – Rotary Press, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #804 is the 1¢ green value from the 1938 Presidential Issue, featuring a left-facing portrait of George Washington in a clean, utilitarian layout. The design includes “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” at the upper right and “1 CENT” at the bottom, with Washington’s name and dates centered within the vignette. The engraving is fine but functional, typical of the Prexie series. This example is used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #804 example offers solid Fine/Very Fine centering with a light, unobtrusive cancel that preserves the clarity of the design. However, the presence of heavy creasing places it firmly in the Major Fault category, significantly reducing its overall grade. The final grade of 26 (Fair/Good) reflects a stamp with decent visual balance but notable structural issues, leaving it as a lower-grade yet still serviceable example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.02, 'LA01-0049-01-02-003'),
(151, 'United States', '804', '004', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '1¢ Green – George Washington, 1938\r\n(Presidential Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 x 10, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #804 is the 1¢ green value from the 1938 Presidential Series, commonly known as the “Prexies.” The design features a right-facing engraved portrait of George Washington, framed within a rectangular border with “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” at the upper right and “1 CENT 1” at the bottom. The fine engraved lines and consistent color reflect rotary press production of the period, resulting in a crisp and widely recognizable design.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #804 example shows Fine/Very Fine centering with all margins clear of the design, though uneven, with the bottom margin most restricted. A light crease establishes a Minor Fault classification, and shorter perforations slightly affect edge uniformity. The heavy cancel reduces eye appeal but leaves the design readable. The final grade of 52 (Very Good) reflects a balanced assessment under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide and results in a solid mid-grade collectible example.', 0.08, 'NA01-0147-01-02-004'),
(152, 'United States', '648', '003', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '5¢ Blue – Theodore Roosevelt, 1928 (Hawaii Overprint)\r\n(Regular Issue with Overprint – Engraved, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #648 is a 5¢ blue Roosevelt issue overprinted “HAWAII 1778–1928,” commemorating the 150th anniversary of Captain Cook’s arrival in Hawaii. The underlying design features a portrait of Theodore Roosevelt in an oval frame with ornate scrollwork and “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” across the top. The bold black overprint is centrally applied and forms a key part of the stamp’s visual identity. This example is unused, never hinged, and with original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #648 example presents Fine centering with a sharp, well-defined overprint and strong visual appeal. Minor back smudging and a single short perforation result in a Very Minor Fault classification, but these issues have limited impact on the overall presentation. With a final grade of 65 (Very Good/Fine), it remains a clean and attractive collectible example of this desirable Hawaii overprint issue under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 6.69, 'LA01-0031-05-03-003'),
(153, 'United States', '805', '006', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '1 1/2¢ Brown – Martha Washington, 1938\r\n(Presidential Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 x 10, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #805 is the 1 1/2¢ brown Martha Washington value from the 1938 Presidential Series, commonly known as the “Prexies.” The design features a right-facing portrait of Martha Washington, with “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” at the upper right and the denomination “1 1/2 CENTS” across the bottom. The engraved design uses fine horizontal shading and a restrained brown color, giving the stamp a formal, dignified appearance within the series.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #805 presents Fine/Very Fine centering, with all perforations clear of the design despite a narrower left margin and somewhat tight bottom margin. The light crease establishes the Minor Fault classification, while the hinge remnant is noted separately and does not further reduce the numerical grade.', 0.08, 'LA01-0049-01-03-006'),
(154, 'United States', '806', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '2¢ Carmine – John Adams, 1938\r\n(Presidential Issue – Rotary Press, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #806 is the 2¢ carmine value from the 1938 Presidential Issue, featuring a left-facing portrait of John Adams rendered in fine engraved detail. The design includes “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” at the upper right and “2 CENTS” at the bottom, with Adams’ name and dates beneath the portrait. The layout is clean and functional, consistent with the Prexie series. This example is used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #806 example presents as a solid mid-grade stamp with Fine centering and intact overall structure. However, the presence of multiple ragged perforations and a heavy cancel reduces both technical quality and visual appeal. The final grade of 35 (Good) reflects a serviceable but clearly worn example, still collectible within the framework of the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.04, 'LA01-0049-01-04-003'),
(155, 'United States', '807', '004', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair/Good', '3¢ Violet – Thomas Jefferson, 1938\r\n(Presidential Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11×10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #807 is the 3¢ violet Thomas Jefferson stamp from the 1938 Presidential Issue, popularly known as the Prexies. The design features a right-facing portrait of Jefferson accompanied by his presidential dates, 1801–1809. “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” appears at upper right, while the denomination is displayed in the lower corners. The Presidential Series remains one of the most extensively collected definitive issues in United States philately. This example is postally used, never hinged, and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #807 benefits from Fine/Very Fine centering and retains strong color with a clear overall design. However, the presence of two natural straight edges results in a Major Fault classification, substantially reducing the technical grade. The heavy machine cancel further affects eye appeal by obscuring portions of Jefferson’s portrait and surrounding detail. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the stamp earns a final grade of 27 (Fair/Good) and remains a representative collectible example of the popular Presidential Issue.', 0.02, 'LA01-0049-01-05-004'),
(156, 'United States', '807', '005', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '3¢ Violet – Thomas Jefferson, 1938\r\n(Presidential Issue – Rotary Press, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #807 is the 3¢ violet denomination from the 1938 Presidential Issue, featuring a left-facing engraved portrait of Thomas Jefferson. The design includes “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” at the upper right and “3 CENTS” at the bottom, with Jefferson’s name and dates beneath the portrait. The engraving is clean and balanced, typical of the Prexie series. This example is used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #807 example brings solid Very Fine centering with full, well-balanced margins, giving it a strong structural presence. However, a light crease and a heavy cancel pull the final result down to 57 (Very Good). It is still a respectable and collectible example, with better-than-average centering for the issue, though not quite clean enough to climb higher on the scale.', 0.08, 'NA01-0147-01-05-005'),
(157, 'United States', '810', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '5¢ Bright Blue – James Monroe, 1938\r\n(Presidential Series Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #810 is the 5¢ James Monroe issue from the 1938 Presidential Series, commonly known as the “Prexies.” The design features a left-facing engraved portrait of Monroe, the fifth President of the United States (1817–1825), rendered in bright blue and framed by a clean rectangular border with bold denomination numerals. The 5¢ denomination was frequently used for international mail and heavier domestic rates. This example is used, never hinged, and without original gum. Hinging is not treated as a grading fault here.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #810 presents Fine/Very Fine centering with strong color and a recognizable James Monroe portrait. However, multiple faulty perforations and a heavy machine cancel significantly reduce soundness and eye appeal. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the stamp earns a final grade of 31 (Good), making it a respectable space-filling example for a Presidential Series collection.', 0.04, 'LA01-0049-02-03-002'),
(158, 'United States', '810', '004', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair', '5¢ Blue – James Monroe, 1938\r\n(Presidential Issue – Rotary Press, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #810 is the 5¢ blue value of the 1938 Presidential Issue, depicting a left-facing engraved portrait of James Monroe. The design follows the standard Prexie layout with “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” at the upper right and “5 CENTS” at the bottom, with Monroe’s name and dates beneath the portrait. The engraving is crisp and utilitarian, designed for clarity in mass production. This example is used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #810 example presents with respectable Fine/Very Fine centering and clear margins, but it is heavily compromised by multiple major faults, including creasing and thinning. The strong cancel further reduces visual clarity. The resulting grade of 12 (Fair) reflects a stamp with solid positional balance but significant structural and aesthetic drawbacks, placing it firmly in the lower tier of collectible examples.', 0.02, 'NA01-0147-02-03-004'),
(159, 'United States', '812', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '7¢ Brown – Andrew Jackson, 1938\r\n(Presidential Issue – Rotary Press, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #812 is the 7¢ brown value from the 1938 Presidential Issue, featuring a left-facing engraved portrait of Andrew Jackson. The design follows the standard Prexie format, with “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” at the upper right and “7 CENTS” at the bottom, along with Jackson’s name and dates beneath the portrait. The engraving is clean and well-defined, typical of the series. This example is used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #812 example offers solid Fine/Very Fine centering with clearly defined margins and a well-presented design. However, multiple ragged perforations and a heavy cancel reduce both technical quality and visual appeal. The final grade of 49 (Good/Very Good) reflects a stamp that remains structurally sound and collectible, though clearly affected by handling and postal use under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.05, 'LA01-0049-03-01-003'),
(160, 'United States', '813', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '8¢ Olive Green – Martin Van Buren, 1938\r\n(Presidential Issue – Rotary Press, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #813 is the 8¢ olive green value from the 1938 Presidential Issue, depicting a left-facing engraved portrait of Martin Van Buren. The design follows the standard Prexie format, with “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” at the upper right and “8 CENTS” at the bottom, along with Van Buren’s name and dates beneath the portrait. The engraving is sharp and evenly inked, typical of the series’ functional style. This example is used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #813 example presents with solid Fine/Very Fine centering and clearly defined margins, but it is significantly affected by multiple perforation issues and a very heavy cancel. These factors reduce both structural quality and visual appeal, resulting in a final grade of 31 (Good). It remains a complete and identifiable example, though best suited as a lower-grade representative within a Prexie collection under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.04, 'LA01-0049-03-02-003'),
(161, 'United States', '814', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair/Good', '9¢ Rose – William Henry Harrison, 1938\r\n(Presidential Issue – Rotary Press, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #814 is the 9¢ rose value from the 1938 Presidential Issue, depicting a left-facing engraved portrait of William Henry Harrison. The design follows the standard Prexie format, with “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” at the upper right and “9 CENTS” at the bottom, with Harrison’s name and date beneath the portrait. The engraving is clean and consistent with the utilitarian style of the series. This example is used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #814 example shows typical Fine centering with visible but uneven margins. However, multiple ragged perforations and significant back-side soiling reduce its technical quality, while the heavy cancel further impacts eye appeal. The final grade of 25 (Fair/Good) reflects a lower-grade but complete example, suitable as a filler within a Presidential Issue collection under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.02, 'LA01-0049-03-03-003'),
(162, 'United States', '816', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair', '11¢ Blue – James K. Polk, 1938\r\n(Presidential Series Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #816 is the 11¢ James K. Polk issue from the 1938 Presidential Series, commonly known as the “Prexies.” The series depicted each U.S. president in chronological order through Calvin Coolidge. This blue engraved design features a left-facing portrait of Polk, the 11th President of the United States (1845–1849), framed by “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” at top and “11 CENTS 11” below. This example is postally used, hinged, and retains partial original gum. Hinging is evaluated separately and does not affect grading here.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #816 presents Very Good centering, with the top and left margins closest to the design and fuller spacing below and at right. A small thin and partial original gum reduce soundness to the Fault category, while the heavy cancel further lowers eye appeal. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the stamp receives a final grade of 19 (Fair), making it a representative but lower-grade example of the James K. Polk Presidential issue.', 0.02, 'LA01-0049-03-05-002'),
(163, 'United States', '818', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good', '13¢ Green – Millard Fillmore, 1938\r\n(Presidential Issue – Engraved, Perf 11 x 10½, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #818 is part of the 1938 Presidential Series, widely known as the Prexies. The design features a finely engraved left-facing portrait of Millard Fillmore, with inscriptions “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” and “MILLARD FILLMORE 1850–1853,” and bold denomination numerals at the base. The layout is clean and balanced, reflecting the utilitarian elegance of the series. This example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #818 example shows Fine/Very Fine centering and retains a clear engraved design, but is impacted by toning spots and minor reverse smudging. These faults reduce both technical grade and eye appeal, resulting in a final grade of 54 (Very Good). Despite these issues, it remains a serviceable collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 1.30, 'LA01-0049-04-02-002'),
(164, 'United States', '822', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Good', '17¢ Red – Andrew Johnson, 1938\r\n(Presidential Issue – Engraved, Perf 11 x 10½, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #822 is part of the 1938 Presidential Series, commonly referred to as the Prexies. The design features a left-facing engraved portrait of Andrew Johnson, with inscriptions “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” and “ANDREW JOHNSON 1865–1869,” and bold denomination numerals at the bottom. The engraving is sharp and formal, consistent with the series’ uniform style. This example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #822 example offers Very Fine centering and a clear engraved design, but is significantly impacted by major staining and multiple perforation creases. These faults reduce both technical grade and visual appeal, resulting in a final grade of 32 (Good). Despite its strong centering, it is best regarded as a space-filler example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.36, 'LA01-0049-05-02-002'),
(165, 'United States', '825', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '20¢ Green – James A. Garfield, 1938\r\n(Presidential Issue – Rotary Press, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #825 is the 20¢ green value from the 1938 Presidential Issue, depicting a left-facing engraved portrait of James A. Garfield. The design follows the familiar high-value Prexie format, with bold outer framelines, “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” at upper right, and “20 CENTS” in the lower corners. The portrait is crisply modeled against a lined background, giving the stamp a formal, dignified appearance. This example is used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #825 example has better-than-average placement for a used higher-value Prexie, with Fine/Very Fine centering and a cancel that stays out of the way instead of stomping all over Garfield’s face like a drunken mail clerk. The heavy crease, though, is the decisive condition issue and pushes the stamp firmly into the Major Fault category. Even so, the stamp remains a presentable and identifiable example with a final grade of 37 (Good) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.04, 'LA01-0049-05-05-003'),
(166, 'United States', '829', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '25¢ Rose – William McKinley, 1938\r\n(Presidential Issue – Rotary Press, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #829 is the 25¢ rose value from the Presidential Issue, featuring a left-facing engraved portrait of William McKinley. The design follows the consistent Prexie format with strong outer framelines, denomination numerals in the lower corners, and “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” at the upper right. The engraving is finely detailed, with a dense lined background that enhances contrast. This example is used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #829 example shows solid Fine centering but is brought down by perforation irregularities and a heavy, design-obscuring cancel. The combination of faults places it in a lower collectible tier, resulting in a final grade of 30 (Good). Even so, it remains a serviceable example of the 25¢ McKinley within the Presidential Issue series under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.04, 'LA01-0050-01-04-003'),
(167, 'United States', '831', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '50¢ Violet – William Howard Taft, 1938\r\n(Presidential Issue – Rotary Press, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #831 is the 50¢ violet denomination of the 1938 Presidential Issue, featuring a left-facing engraved portrait of William Howard Taft. The design follows the higher-value Prexie format, with strong double framelines, “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” at upper right, and large denomination numerals in the lower corners. The engraving is crisp and dignified, giving the stamp a formal presentation appropriate to the issue. This example is used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #831 is a solid Fine/Very Fine centered example with only a very minor soundness issue from the hinge remnant. Its main drag is the heavy cancel, which cuts into the stamp’s visual appeal more than the physical condition does. The final result is 62 (Very Good/Fine), making it a respectable and collectible example of the 50¢ Taft under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.10, 'LA01-0057-02-01-003'),
(168, 'United States', '836', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine/Very Fine', '3¢ Rose – Landing of the Swedes and Finns, 1938\r\n(Tercentenary Issue – Engraved, Perf 11×10½, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #836 was issued in 1938 to commemorate the 300th anniversary of the arrival of Swedish and Finnish settlers in the Delaware Valley. The engraved design depicts settlers coming ashore in period dress, symbolizing one of the earliest European settlements in the region. Framed by a bold geometric border and printed in rose, the stamp combines strong historical significance with attractive engraving. The submitted example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #836 combines attractive Fine/Very Fine centering with strong color, crisp engraving, and desirable never hinged original gum. The only noted condition issue is a single shortened perforation, resulting in an Extremely Minor Fault classification and a modest deduction. Because the fault has little impact on the stamp’s visual presentation, a small eye appeal bonus is warranted. The final grade of 75 (Fine) reflects a clean, attractive, and highly collectible example of this classic Tercentenary commemorative under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.21, 'LA01-0051-01-01-002'),
(169, 'United States', '840', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair', '1½¢ Brown – Martha Washington, 1938\r\n(Presidential Issue – Rotary Press, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #840 is the 1½¢ brown denomination from the 1938 Presidential Issue, notable for depicting Martha Washington, the only woman honored in the series. The engraved portrait faces right, with “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” at the upper right and “1½ CENTS” at the bottom. The design is compact and balanced, with fine line engraving typical of the Prexie series. This example is used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #840 example shows respectable Fine/Very Fine centering with balanced margins and a clear, recognizable design. However, the presence of a deep thin or multiple thins places it firmly in the Severe Fault category, significantly lowering its overall grade. The heavy machine cancel further reduces visual appeal, resulting in a final grade of 13 (Fair). Even so, it remains a complete and identifiable example, though firmly in the lower-grade collecting tier.', 0.02, 'LA01-0050-03-02-002'),
(170, 'United States', '842', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '3¢ Purple – Thomas Jefferson, 1939\r\n(Presidential Series “Prexies” – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11×10½)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #842 is the 3¢ purple Thomas Jefferson issue from the Presidential Series, popularly known as the Prexies. The design features a left-facing portrait of Jefferson, accompanied by his presidential dates, 1801–1809. “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” appears at upper right, while “3 CENTS 3” is displayed prominently at the bottom. As the standard first-class letter rate of its era, the 3¢ Jefferson became one of the most heavily used denominations in the series. The submitted example is postally used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #842 combines Fine/Very Fine centering with sound perforations and attractive color, giving it a solid technical foundation. The light crease results in a Minor Fault classification, while the heavy wavy-line cancel further reduces eye appeal by obscuring portions of Jefferson’s portrait. These deductions lower the final grade to 46 (Good/Very Good). Despite its faults, it remains a collectible and representative example of the widely collected Presidential Series under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.05, 'LA01-0050-03-04-002'),
(171, 'United States', '845', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine', '5¢ Blue – James Monroe, 1939\r\n(Presidential Series “Prexies” – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11×10½)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #845 is the 5¢ blue James Monroe issue from the 1938 Presidential Series, widely known as the Prexies. The design features a left-facing portrait of Monroe, the fifth President of the United States, accompanied by his presidential dates, 1817–1825. “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” appears at upper right, while “5 CENTS 5” is displayed prominently at the bottom. The 5¢ denomination served a variety of domestic and international postal purposes and was a frequently used value within the series. The submitted example is postally used, never hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #845 benefits from Very Fine centering, faultless soundness, and strong color, making it a technically superior example of the James Monroe issue. The sole significant detractor is the heavy savings bond slogan cancel, which obscures portions of the portrait and reduces overall eye appeal. Even with that deduction, the stamp remains a desirable and historically interesting example of the Presidential Series, earning a final grade of 74 (Fine) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.45, 'LA01-0050-04-03-002'),
(172, 'United States', '848', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair/Good', '1¢ Green – George Washington, 1939\r\n(Presidential Series “Prexies” – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11×10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #848 is the 1¢ green George Washington issue from the Presidential Series, commonly known as the Prexies. The design features a left-facing engraved portrait of Washington with his years of service, 1789–1797, below the vignette. “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” appears at upper right and the denomination is shown in the lower corners. As the lowest denomination in the series, it was heavily used on postcards, circulars, and printed matter. The submitted example is postally used, never hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #848 combines Fine/Very Fine centering with strong color and an otherwise pleasing engraved design. The cut corner results in a Major Fault classification and is responsible for the largest reduction in grade despite generally sound remaining perforations and margins. The heavy wartime savings bond slogan cancel further lowers eye appeal by obscuring portions of the portrait and inscriptions. Even with these limitations, the stamp remains a recognizable and historically interesting example of the 1¢ Washington Prexie. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the combination of Fine/Very Fine centering, Major Fault soundness, and a Heavy cancel yields a final grade of 23 (Fair/Good), making it a collectible representative of this extensively used definitive issue.', 0.04, 'LA01-0050-05-01-002'),
(173, 'United States', '853', '004', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '3¢ Ultramarine – Trylon and Perisphere, 1939\r\n(New York World’s Fair Issue – Engraved, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #853 depicts the Trylon and Perisphere from the 1939 New York World’s Fair in a clean modernist style. The tall Trylon and round Perisphere create a bold geometric composition, supported by fine engraved lines and deep ultramarine color. This used example shows no visible cancellation on the face, so the fair symbols, denomination, and inscriptions remain unobstructed.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #853 begins with Fine/Very Fine 78 centering, limited mainly by the narrow top margin and somewhat tight left side. Minor Fault soundness lowers the technical grade to Very Good 58 because of several ragged, fuzzy, or shortened perforations. The clean face and absence of a visible cancellation add 1 point for eye appeal. Final Grade: Very Good 59. This is an attractive used single with strong visual presence, though perforation faults keep it grounded in the Very Good range.', 0.08, 'LA01-0051-03-02-004'),
(174, 'United States', '854', '004', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine', '3¢ Purple – Sesquicentennial of Washington’s Inauguration, 1939\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11×10½)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #854 commemorates the 150th anniversary of George Washington’s inauguration as the first President of the United States in 1789. The engraved design depicts Washington taking the oath of office on the balcony of Federal Hall before a gathered crowd. Surrounding inscriptions identify the sesquicentennial observance, while the dates 1789–1939 appear below the central vignette. Issued as a 3¢ commemorative, the stamp celebrated a defining event in American political history. The submitted example is postally used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #854 displays Fine/Very Fine centering with intact perforations, attractive color, and a well-executed commemorative design honoring Washington’s inauguration. A light ink transfer on the reverse results in a Very Minor Fault classification and a modest deduction from the centering grade. The heavy machine cancel remains consistent with normal postal use and leaves the principal design elements recognizable, resulting in no further eye appeal adjustment. The final grade is 72 (Fine). This remains a collectible and visually appealing used example of this historic 1939 commemorative issue.', 0.11, 'LA01-0051-03-03-004'),
(175, 'United States', '854', '005', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '3¢ Violet – Presidential Inauguration Sesquicentennial, 1939\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Perf 11×10½, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #854 commemorates the 150th anniversary of George Washington’s inauguration as the first President of the United States. The engraved design depicts Washington taking the oath of office beneath a grand architectural setting, surrounded by detailed scrollwork and commemorative inscriptions. The rich violet color and intricate engraving create a visually engaging composition that celebrates a pivotal moment in American history. The submitted example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #854 exhibits Fine centering, strong original color, crisp engraved detail, and full original gum. The right margin is the limiting factor for centering, while a single shortened perforation results in an Extremely Minor Fault classification and a modest deduction. Because the stamp is unused and free from cancellation, the design remains clear and visually appealing, requiring no additional eye appeal adjustment. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the combination of Fine centering and Extremely Minor Fault soundness yields a final grade of 65 (Very Good/Fine). It remains an attractive and collectible example of this historic Washington Inauguration commemorative issue.', 0.49, 'NA01-0153-04-02-005'),
(176, 'United States', '856', '003', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '3¢ Purple – 25th Anniversary of the Panama Canal, 1939\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 11×10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #856 was issued in 1939 to commemorate the twenty-fifth anniversary of the opening of the Panama Canal. The engraved design features portraits of President Theodore Roosevelt and Chief Engineer George W. Goethals flanking a central view of a ship transiting the canal locks. The layout symbolizes one of the greatest engineering achievements of the twentieth century and America\'s growing role in global commerce. Printed in a rich purple shade, the stamp combines strong portraiture with an intricate central vignette. The submitted example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #856 combines Fine/Very Fine centering, intact perforations, fresh original gum, and strong engraved detail. The bottom and left margins are slightly tighter than the top and right, but all remain clear of the design. A light ink transfer on the reverse results in a Very Minor Fault classification and a modest deduction from the centering grade. Because the stamp retains vibrant color, clean paper, and an attractive front appearance, a small eye appeal bonus is warranted. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the combination of Fine/Very Fine centering, Very Minor Fault soundness, and positive eye appeal yields a final grade of 73 (Fine). It remains a handsome and collectible example of this popular Panama Canal commemorative issue.', 0.27, 'LA01-0051-04-02-003'),
(177, 'United States', '857', '005', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good', '3¢ Violet – Printing Press, 1939\r\n(300th Anniversary of Printing in Colonial America – Rotary Press, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #857 commemorates the 300th anniversary of printing in Colonial America, depicting the Stephen Daye press, the first printing press used in the American colonies. The design features a detailed illustration of the wooden press, with inscriptions “300th Anniversary of Printing in Colonial America” and the dates 1639–1939. The engraving is clean and functional, emphasizing historical significance over ornate styling.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #857 example presents attractive Fine/Very Fine centering with a crisp, well-defined front design. The presence of heavy ink transfer on the reverse results in a Minor Fault classification and a significant deduction, bringing the final grade to 58 (Very Good). Despite the back condition, the stamp remains a solid collectible example with pleasing front-facing appearance under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.12, 'LA01-0051-03-04-005'),
(178, 'United States', '867', '004', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '5¢ Blue – Walt Whitman, 1940\r\n(Famous Americans Series – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #867 was issued in 1940 as part of the Famous Americans Series honoring notable figures in United States history and culture. The engraved design depicts poet Walt Whitman in his characteristic hat and beard, framed within an ornamental border. “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” appears across the top, while the denomination “5 CENTS 5” is displayed at the bottom beneath a winged globe motif. Printed in blue, the stamp commemorates one of America\'s most influential literary figures. The submitted example is postally used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #867 combines Fine/Very Fine centering with strong color and a well-executed engraved portrait of Walt Whitman. Several perf creases result in a Minor Fault classification and reduce the technical grade, while the hinge remnant is a secondary consideration. A heavy circular date stamp crossing the portrait further lowers eye appeal by obscuring significant design detail. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the combination of Fine/Very Fine centering, Minor Fault soundness, and a Heavy cancel produces a final grade of 48 (Good/Very Good). Despite its condition issues, it remains a collectible example of this popular Famous Americans issue.', 0.09, 'LA01-0052-02-04-004'),
(179, 'United States', '868', '004', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '10¢ Brown – James Whitcomb Riley, 1940\r\n(Famous Americans Series – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #868 was issued in 1940 as part of the Famous Americans Series honoring notable figures in literature, science, education, and public service. This 10¢ brown issue depicts James Whitcomb Riley, the celebrated “Hoosier Poet,” in a three-quarter facing portrait framed by an ornate engraved border. The denomination “10 CENTS 10” appears at the bottom along with the series’ distinctive winged globe emblem. The submitted example is postally used, hinged, and retains partial original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #868 combines Fine/Very Fine centering with strong color and a well-executed engraved portrait of James Whitcomb Riley. Partial original gum and light reverse cancellation bleed-through result in a Minor Fault classification and reduce the technical grade. A heavy circular date stamp crossing the portrait further lowers eye appeal by obscuring significant facial detail. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the combination of Fine/Very Fine centering, Minor Fault soundness, and a Heavy cancel produces a final grade of 47 (Good/Very Good). Despite its condition issues, it remains a collectible example of this popular Famous Americans issue.', 0.70, 'LA01-0052-02-05-004'),
(180, 'United States', '881', '001', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine/Very Fine', '3¢ Purple – Victor Herbert, 1940\r\n(Famous Americans Series – Composers Group, Perf 11×10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #881 was issued in 1940 as part of the Famous Americans Series honoring notable figures in American culture. This 3¢ purple stamp commemorates composer and conductor Victor Herbert, whose operettas and orchestral works helped shape American musical theater. The engraved design features a bust portrait of Herbert framed by decorative elements including musical instruments and a laurel branch, symbolizing artistic achievement. The submitted example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #881 combines Fine/Very Fine centering with faultless soundness, strong original color, and fully intact original gum. The left margin is somewhat narrow and limits the centering grade, but all margins remain clear of the design. Because the stamp is free of faults and displays excellent freshness, a modest eye appeal bonus is appropriate. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the combination of Fine/Very Fine centering, faultless soundness, and positive eye appeal results in a final grade of 78 (Fine/Very Fine). It remains an attractive and desirable example of this Famous Americans Series composer issue.', 0.17, 'LA01-0053-01-03-001'),
(181, 'United States', '881', '004', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '3¢ Violet – Victor Herbert, 1940\r\n(Famous Americans Series – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #881 was issued in 1940 as part of the Famous Americans Series honoring composer and conductor Victor Herbert. The engraved design features a portrait of Herbert within an oval frame surrounded by decorative musical motifs that reflect his importance in American operetta and orchestral music. “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” appears at the top, while the denomination is displayed at the lower left. Printed in violet, the stamp combines an attractive portrait with strong engraved detail characteristic of the series. The submitted example is postally used, never hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #881 combines Fine/Very Fine centering with a strong engraved design and attractive violet color. A small stain, several perforation disc indents, and a corner perforation crease result in a Fault classification and substantially reduce the technical grade. A Heavy circular date stamp crossing the portrait further lowers eye appeal by obscuring significant facial detail. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the combination of Fine/Very Fine centering, Fault soundness, and a Heavy cancel produces a final grade of 38 (Good). Despite its condition issues, it remains a collectible example of the Victor Herbert issue from the Famous Americans Series.', 0.04, 'NA01-0157-01-03-004'),
(182, 'United States', '882', '004', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '5¢ Blue – Edward A. MacDowell, 1940\r\n(Famous Americans Series – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #882 was issued in 1940 as part of the Famous Americans Series honoring distinguished figures in American culture. This 5¢ blue stamp commemorates composer Edward A. MacDowell, one of the first American composers to achieve international acclaim through his piano and orchestral works. The engraved design presents MacDowell’s portrait within an oval frame accented by laurel branches, with his name inscribed below and the denomination at lower left. The submitted example is postally used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #882 combines Fine/Very Fine centering with strong color, a sharp engraved portrait, and generally sound perforations. A single shortened perforation results in an Extremely Minor Fault classification and only a modest deduction from the technical grade. The primary detracting factor is a Heavy circular date stamp crossing the portrait, which significantly reduces eye appeal and visual clarity. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the combination of Fine/Very Fine centering, Extremely Minor Fault soundness, and a Heavy cancel produces a final grade of 63 (Very Good/Fine). Despite the prominent cancellation, it remains a collectible example of the Edward A. MacDowell issue from the Famous Americans Series.', 0.20, 'LA01-0053-01-04-004'),
(183, 'United States', '884', '004', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '1¢ Green – Gilbert Charles Stuart, 1940\r\n(Famous Americans Series – Engraved, Perf 11×10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #884 was issued in 1940 as part of the Famous Americans Series honoring Gilbert Charles Stuart, one of the most celebrated portrait painters in American history. Best known for his iconic portraits of George Washington, Stuart played a significant role in shaping the visual image of the early republic. The engraved green design presents his portrait within an oval frame accented by laurel leaves, with “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” above and denomination tablets in the lower corners. The submitted example is postally used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #884 combines Fine/Very Fine centering with strong color and an attractive engraved portrait of Gilbert Charles Stuart. A corner perforation crease and two perforation tip creases result in a Minor Fault classification and reduce the technical grade. A Very Heavy circular cancellation crossing the portrait further lowers eye appeal by obscuring a significant portion of the design. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the combination of Fine/Very Fine centering, Minor Fault soundness, and a Very Heavy cancel produces a final grade of 44 (Good/Very Good). Despite its condition limitations, it remains a collectible example of this Famous Americans Series issue.', 0.05, 'LA01-0053-02-01-004'),
(184, 'United States', '888', '005', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '10¢ Brown – Frederic Remington, 1940\r\n(Famous Americans Series – Engraved, Perf 11×10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #888 was issued in 1940 as part of the Famous Americans Series honoring Frederic Remington, the celebrated artist, illustrator, and sculptor whose work helped define the visual image of the American West. The engraved design presents a portrait of Remington within an oval frame surrounded by laurel leaves and artistic symbols, including a painter’s palette and tools. Printed in brown by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, the stamp reflects Remington’s importance in American art and culture. The submitted example is postally used, never hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #888 combines Fine/Very Fine centering with strong color and a well-executed engraved portrait of Frederic Remington. A corner perforation crease and two shortened perforations result in a Minor Fault classification and reduce the technical grade. A Very Heavy cancellation crossing the portrait further lowers eye appeal by obscuring a substantial portion of the design. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the combination of Fine/Very Fine centering, Minor Fault soundness, and a Very Heavy cancel produces a final grade of 43 (Good/Very Good). Despite its condition limitations, it remains a collectible example of this Famous Americans Series issue.', 0.70, 'LA01-0053-02-05-005'),
(185, 'United States', '889', '004', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '1¢ Green – Eli Whitney, 1940\r\n(Famous Americans Series – Engraved, Perf 11×10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #889 was issued in 1940 as part of the Famous Americans Series honoring Eli Whitney, inventor of the cotton gin and an important pioneer in the development of interchangeable manufacturing parts. The engraved green design features a portrait of Whitney framed within a rectangular layout incorporating industrial motifs that reflect his contributions to American technology and manufacturing. His name and denomination are displayed prominently within the design. The submitted example is postally used, never hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #889 combines Fine/Very Fine centering with strong color and a well-executed engraved portrait of Eli Whitney. A corner perforation crease and three shortened perforations result in a Minor Fault classification and reduce the technical grade. A Heavy wavy-line machine cancel crossing the portrait further lowers eye appeal by obscuring significant design detail. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the combination of Fine/Very Fine centering, Minor Fault soundness, and a Heavy cancel produces a final grade of 47 (Good/Very Good). Despite its condition limitations, it remains a collectible example of this Famous Americans Series issue.', 0.05, 'LA01-0053-03-01-004'),
(186, 'United States', '894', '003', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '3¢ Carmine – Pony Express 80th Anniversary, 1940\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #894 was issued in 1940 to commemorate the 80th anniversary of the Pony Express. The engraved design depicts a mounted rider galloping across a western landscape, symbolizing the speed and rugged determination associated with the historic mail service. Smaller vignette panels flank the central image, while the inscription “PONY EXPRESS 80TH ANNIVERSARY” anchors the lower frame. The carmine color and dynamic engraving give the issue a strong sense of motion and depth. This example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum. Hinging is not treated as a grading fault here.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #894 is a visually attractive example with strong engraving, vibrant color, and original gum. The centering is solidly Fine/Very Fine, with only slightly tighter spacing at the bottom and right margins. A minor natural gum bend introduces only a small deduction, while the clean face and strong detail support above-average presentation. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 74 (Fine).', 0.49, 'LA01-0053-04-01-003'),
(187, 'United States', '895', '003', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '3¢ Violet – Pan-American Union, 1940\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Perf 11 x 10½, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #895 commemorates the fiftieth anniversary of the Pan-American Union. The design presents three allegorical female figures symbolizing the nations of the Western Hemisphere, adapted in a classical style and framed by ornate lettering above and below. The violet color and dense engraved lines give the stamp a decorative, almost mural-like appearance. This example is unused, never hinged, and with original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #895 is a Fine/Very Fine centered never hinged example with intact perforations and an attractive engraved design, but the small stain moves it firmly into the Fault category. The minor natural gum bend is secondary, while the stain has the real effect on both technical condition and eye appeal. The final grade of 45 (Good/Very Good) reflects a presentable but condition-compromised example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.10, 'LA01-0054-01-01-003');
INSERT INTO `stamps` (`id`, `country`, `scott`, `count`, `condition`, `hinged`, `gum`, `grade`, `description`, `price`, `location`) VALUES
(188, 'United States', '906', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good', '5¢ Blue – China Resistance Issue (U.S.–China Commemorative), 1942\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11x10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1942 during World War II, Scott #906 commemorates China’s resistance to Japanese invasion and honors democratic ideals shared by the United States and China. The stamp shows Abraham Lincoln and Dr. Sun Yat-sen flanking a map of China, symbolizing unity and courage. The Republic’s Sun emblem bears the dates “July 7, 1937” and “July 7, 1942,” marking the fifth anniversary of China’s resistance. The phrase below Lincoln, “Of the People, By the People, For the People,” reflects the nations’ shared philosophy of self-governance. This example is unused, has original gum, and has been previously hinged.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis unused, hinged example of the 5¢ 1942 China Resistance commemorative shows Fine/Very Fine centering with slightly narrow right and top margins. Minor production flaws—a faint gum bend, one short perf, and light ink transfer—lower the technical grade but not the visual appeal. Its strong color, clear impression, and intact gum earn a final grade of 58 (Very Good) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, representing a bright and historically important mid-grade wartime commemorative.', 0.52, 'LA01-0054-03-03-002'),
(189, 'United States', '906', '003', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '5¢ Blue – China Resistance, 1942\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #906 was issued in 1942 to commemorate Chinese resistance during World War II and symbolize cooperation between the United States and China. The design features portraits of Abraham Lincoln at left and Sun Yat-sen at right, flanking a central map of China with a radiating emblem bearing dates associated with the conflict. The inscription “OF THE PEOPLE, BY THE PEOPLE, FOR THE PEOPLE” reinforces the democratic ideals shared by both nations. The engraving is detailed and symmetrical, with strong contrast and refined line work typical of wartime commemoratives. This example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum. Hinging is not treated as a grading fault here.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #906 presents strong Fine/Very Fine centering and excellent engraved detail, with the top margin narrowest and the bottom most generous. The small stain places the stamp in the Fault category, significantly reducing the technical grade despite otherwise solid presentation. With no cancellation and strong visual clarity, it remains a collectible wartime commemorative example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, earning a final grade of 47 (Good/Very Good).', 0.37, 'NA01-0159-03-03-003'),
(190, 'United States', '907', '002', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '2¢ Carmine – Nations United for Victory, 1943\r\n(World War II Issue – Rotary Press, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #907 was issued in 1943 during World War II and symbolizes Allied unity and determination. The design features a stylized sheaf of wheat held aloft by multiple hands, representing collective strength, with bayonets forming a defensive backdrop. The bold inscription “NATIONS UNITED FOR VICTORY” reinforces the wartime message. The engraving is strong and graphic, typical of wartime issues designed for visual impact.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #907 example exhibits solid Fine/Very Fine centering with a bold, attractive design and vibrant color. A minor corner perf crease results in a Very Minor Fault classification, slightly reducing the grade, but the stamp retains strong visual appeal. With a final grade of 74 (Fine), it remains a desirable and representative example of this wartime issue under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.11, 'LA01-0054-04-02-002'),
(191, 'United States', '907', '003', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good', '2¢ Carmine – Nations United for Victory, 1943\r\n(World War II Issue – Rotary Press, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #907 was issued in 1943 to promote Allied unity during World War II. The design features a stylized sheaf of wheat held by multiple hands, backed by upright bayonets, symbolizing strength through cooperation. The inscription “NATIONS UNITED FOR VICTORY” reinforces the wartime message. The engraving is bold and graphic, designed for strong visual impact.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #907 example presents acceptable Fine/Very Fine centering and strong color with a clear, bold design. However, multiple irregular and shortened perforations result in a Minor Fault classification and a substantial deduction. With a final grade of 54 (Very Good), the stamp remains a solid but clearly mid-grade example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, retaining collector interest despite its technical shortcomings.', 0.08, 'NA01-0159-04-02-003'),
(192, 'United States', '907', '005', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '2¢ Carmine – Nations United for Victory, 1943\r\n(World War II Issue – Rotary Press, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #907 was issued in 1943 during World War II and symbolizes Allied unity. The design features a sheaf of wheat grasped by multiple hands, backed by bayonets, representing strength through cooperation. The inscription “NATIONS UNITED FOR VICTORY” reinforces the wartime theme. The engraving is bold and utilitarian, designed for strong visual communication.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #907 example presents Fine centering and the added benefit of a nearly invisible cancel, allowing the full design to remain clear and attractive. However, the presence of two light creases places it in the Fault category, significantly limiting the final grade. With a final grade of 38 (Good), it remains a collectible example, with eye appeal slightly rescued by the faint cancel but ultimately governed by its structural condition.', 0.04, 'SD22-0005-05-05-005'),
(193, 'United States', '908', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '1¢ Green – “Four Freedoms” Issue, 1943\r\n(Defense Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #908 was issued in 1943 as part of the World War II “Four Freedoms” series inspired by President Franklin D. Roosevelt’s 1941 State of the Union address. The design features an allegorical head facing right and holding a torch symbolizing liberty, framed by laurel leaves. At the base appears the inscription “FREEDOM OF SPEECH AND RELIGION, FROM WANT AND FEAR,” reinforcing the patriotic wartime message. The denomination “1¢” appears in the upper corners, with “U.S. POSTAGE” across the top. This example is postally used, never hinged, and without original gum. Hinging is not treated as a grading fault here.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #908 presents Fine/Very Fine centering with strong color and a recognizable wartime design. Ragged perforations and a corner perforation crease reduce soundness, while the heavy grid cancel significantly affects eye appeal by obscuring central details. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the stamp earns a final grade of 49 (Good/Very Good), though it remains a collectible example of the patriotic “Four Freedom', 0.05, 'LA01-0054-04-03-002'),
(194, 'United States', '908', '004', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Good', '1¢ Green – Four Freedoms, 1943\r\n(World War II Issue – Rotary Press, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #908 was issued in 1943 as part of the Four Freedoms series inspired by President Franklin D. Roosevelt’s wartime ideals. The design depicts an allegorical female figure holding a torch, framed by laurel branches, with the inscription “Freedom of Speech and Religion, From Want and Fear” in the tablet below. The engraved presentation is compact, symbolic, and distinctly wartime in tone. This example is unused, hinged, and with original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #908 shows solid Fine/Very Fine centering and an attractive wartime design, but the moderate crease, multiple perf creases, and several ragged or shortened perforations drive the soundness down to Major Fault status. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, it earns a final grade of 33 (Good), remaining collectible but clearly governed by its faults.', 0.04, 'NA01-0159-04-03-004'),
(195, 'United States', '909', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine/Very Fine', '5¢ Red, White & Blue – Polish Relief Issue, 1943\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #909 was issued in 1943 to support Polish relief efforts during World War II and symbolize solidarity with occupied Poland. The design prominently features the Polish flag in red and white at center, flanked by patriotic and allegorical imagery, including an eagle at left and a robed figure at right. The engraving is sharp and highly symbolic, with the vivid red flag standing out against the otherwise monochromatic frame. This example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum. Hinging is not treated as a grading fault here.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #909 is a well-centered and visually appealing wartime commemorative with strong color, crisp engraving, and original gum. The minor perforation irregularities are negligible in overall presentation and only slightly affect the technical grade. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade of 79 (Fine/Very Fine) reflects a high-quality example that narrowly misses a full Very Fine designation.', 0.17, 'LA01-0055-01-01-002'),
(196, 'United States', '910', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '5¢ Blue, Red & White – Czechoslovakia Flag, 1943\r\n(Overrun Countries Series – Engraved, Perf 11 x 10½, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #910 was issued in 1943 as part of the World War II Overrun Countries series, honoring nations occupied during the conflict. The central vignette depicts the Czechoslovakian flag in blue, white, and red, surrounded by engraved allegorical elements including an eagle at left and a robed female figure symbolizing liberation at right. The combination of engraved detail and multicolor printing creates strong visual contrast. This example is unused, never hinged, with original gum present and a clean front appearance.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #910 is a visually appealing example with Fine/Very Fine centering, strong engraving, and vibrant color. The reverse toning limits the technical grade but does not detract from the front-facing appearance. The final grade of 60 (Very Good–Fine) reflects a solid and collectible example that balances good centering with a minor condition fault under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.13, 'LA01-0055-01-02-002'),
(197, 'United States', '913', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good', '5¢ Blue, Red & Blue – Netherlands Flag, 1943\r\n(Overrun Countries Series – Engraved, Perf 11 x 10½, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #913 was issued in 1943 as part of the Overrun Countries series, produced during World War II to honor nations occupied by Axis powers. The design features the flag of the Netherlands in red, white, and blue at center, framed by a finely engraved patriotic border. An eagle rising from flames appears at left, while a robed allegorical female figure symbolizing liberation stands at right. UNITED STATES POSTAGE is inscribed across the top, NETHERLANDS appears below the flag, and 5 CENTS is shown at the bottom. The submitted example is unused, never hinged, with original gum present. The front remains bright and attractive, while the reverse shows minor toning spots and a minor natural gum bend.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #913 is a pleasing example with Fine/Very Fine centering, sharp engraved detail, and strong front-facing color. The toning spots and minor natural gum bend reduce the soundness to the Minor Fault level and bring the technical grade down accordingly. Even so, the stamp remains a collectible and visually attractive example, with a final grade of 59 (Very Good) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.10, 'LA01-0055-02-02-002'),
(198, 'United States', '920', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Fine/Extra Fine', '5¢ Blue & Red – Denmark Flag, 1943\r\n(Overrun Countries Series – Engraved, Perf 11 x 10½, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #920 was issued in 1943 as part of the Overrun Countries series, recognizing nations occupied during World War II. The design features the Danish flag in red with a white cross at center, framed by a finely engraved border. At left is an eagle rising from flames, and at right a robed allegorical figure symbolizing liberation. UNITED STATES POSTAGE appears at the top, DENMARK beneath the flag, and 5 CENTS at the bottom. The submitted example is unused, never hinged, with original gum present. The front shows strong color and a clear impression, while the reverse exhibits a minor natural gum bend.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #920 is a well-centered and visually appealing example with Extremely Fine centering, strong color, and crisp engraved detail. The minor natural gum bend has minimal impact on the overall condition, allowing the stamp to retain excellent eye appeal. The final grade of 89 (Very Fine–Extremely Fine) reflects a high-quality collectible with only negligible condition limitations under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.29, 'LA01-0055-05-02-002'),
(199, 'United States', '929', '002', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '3¢ Olive Green – Iwo Jima, 1945\r\n(1945 Iwo Jima Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #929 commemorates the raising of the U.S. flag on Mount Suribachi, Iwo Jima, on February 23, 1945. The design is based on Joe Rosenthal’s Pulitzer Prize–winning photograph, engraved by Charles A. Brooks. It depicts six Marines lifting the American flag, with “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” inscribed above, the denomination “3¢” at the upper right, and “IWO JIMA” below the vignette. Issued on July 11, 1945, this stamp celebrated courage and victory at the close of World War II. The submitted example is unused, bears original gum, and has been previously hinged.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis unused hinged example of Scott #929, the 3¢ Iwo Jima commemorative, presents Fine/Very Fine centering with balanced color and sound perforations. Minor natural gum irregularity and a few short perfs account for a modest deduction, yielding a final numerical grade of 73 (Fine) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide. The stamp retains sharp visual appeal and remains a strong collectible representative of this celebrated patriotic issue.', 0.11, 'LA01-0057-01-02-002'),
(200, 'United States', '929', '003', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '3¢ Green – Iwo Jima Flag Raising, 1945\r\n(WWII Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Perf. 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nThe 3¢ Iwo Jima stamp commemorates one of the most iconic moments of World War II, depicting U.S. Marines raising the American flag atop Mount Suribachi. Issued in 1945, the design is based on Joe Rosenthal’s famous photograph and symbolizes victory, unity, and sacrifice. The engraved lines emphasize motion and depth, with the flag drawing the viewer’s eye upward. The submitted example is unused with original gum and shows evidence of hinging.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #929 example presents as a solid, collectible stamp with attractive centering in the Fine/Very Fine range and strong visual design clarity. The hinge remnant and minor gum bend reduce the technical grade into the Fine category, but the stamp retains good color and overall appeal. Despite minor faults, it remains a desirable and historically significant piece that fits well within a representative U.S. collection under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.19, 'NA01-0165-02-01-003'),
(201, 'United States', '930', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '1¢ Green – Franklin D. Roosevelt, Hyde Park, 1945\r\n(Prominent Americans / Memorial Issue – Engraved, Perf. 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nThe 1¢ Roosevelt Memorial issue honors President Franklin D. Roosevelt shortly after his death in 1945. The design features a left-facing portrait of Roosevelt within an oval frame, paired with a detailed depiction of his Hyde Park home. Inscriptions include his name, birth and death years, and U.S. Postage. The engraved style provides fine detail and depth, particularly in the architectural background. The submitted example is unused with original gum and shows evidence of hinging.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #930 example presents as a solid collectible with attractive engraved detail and historically significant subject matter. The centering falls within the Fine/Very Fine range, though slightly unbalanced, and a minor gum bend reduces the final grade into the Fine category. Despite this small fault, the stamp retains good color, intact perforations, and overall pleasing appearance, making it a respectable and collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.15, 'LA01-0056-03-02-002'),
(202, 'United States', '930', '004', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '1¢ Green – Roosevelt at Hyde Park, 1945\r\n(Franklin D. Roosevelt Memorial Issue – Rotary Press, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #930 was issued in 1945 as a memorial to Franklin D. Roosevelt shortly after his death. The design combines a portrait of Roosevelt at left with a view of his Hyde Park home at right, creating a dignified commemorative layout that emphasizes both the man and his personal setting. The inscription includes his birth and death years, with “U.S. Postage” above and the denomination at lower right. This example is unused, never hinged, and with original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #930 shows solid Fine/Very Fine centering, a fresh green color, and a strong memorial design honoring Roosevelt. The minor natural gum bend lowers the soundness only slightly, and the stamp remains an attractive collectible example. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, it earns a final grade of 74 (Fine).', 0.15, 'NA01-0163-04-01-004'),
(203, 'United States', '930', '005', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '1¢ Green – Roosevelt at Hyde Park, 1945\r\n(Franklin D. Roosevelt Memorial Issue – Rotary Press, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #930 was issued in 1945 as a memorial tribute to Franklin D. Roosevelt. The design pairs an oval portrait of Roosevelt at left with a detailed view of his Hyde Park home at right, creating a dignified commemorative composition that links the man with a place closely associated with his life and legacy. The lettering is simple and elegant, and the green color suits the restrained tone of the issue. This submitted example is unused, never hinged, and with original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #930 is a solid Fine example with pleasing centering, fresh color, and a crisp memorial design. The three shortened perforations and minor natural gum bend reduce the soundness to Very Minor Fault status, but the stamp still presents well overall. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, it earns a final grade of 73 (Fine), remaining a very respectable collectible example.', 0.15, 'SD22-0005-07-01-005'),
(204, 'United States', '930', '006', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good', '1¢ Green – Roosevelt at Hyde Park, 1945\r\n(Franklin D. Roosevelt Memorial Issue – Rotary Press, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #930 was issued in 1945 to honor Franklin D. Roosevelt following his death. The design pairs a left-facing portrait of Roosevelt with a detailed view of his Hyde Park residence, creating a balanced commemorative composition that reflects both personal and national legacy. The engraving is clean and finely detailed, with a restrained green tone appropriate to the issue. The submitted example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #930 example offers strong Fine/Very Fine centering with fresh color and a well-executed commemorative design. However, multiple perforation creases reduce the soundness to a Minor Fault classification, significantly impacting the final grade. With a final grade of 58 (Very Good), it remains a solid collectible example, with visual appeal tempered by its technical imperfections.', 0.10, 'SL01-0001-09-01-006'),
(205, 'United States', '931', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '2¢ Carmine Rose – Franklin D. Roosevelt, Little White House, 1945\r\n(Roosevelt Memorial Issue – Engraved, Perf. 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #931 honors Franklin D. Roosevelt and depicts his portrait in an oval at left, paired with the Little White House at Warm Springs, Georgia, a site strongly associated with his presidency and final years. The inscriptions include U.S. Postage, the denomination, Roosevelt’s name, and the dates 1882 and 1945. The engraved design is richly detailed, with the architectural setting and surrounding trees giving the issue both memorial dignity and visual warmth. The submitted example is an unused single with original gum and evidence of hinging.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #931 is a well-presented unused example with original gum, attractive color, and a strong engraved appearance. Its centering reaches the Fine/Very Fine level, with the right margin the tightest and the left the most open, while a minor natural gum bend reduces the technical grade to 72 Fine. Grim little arithmetic, but still a handsome and collectible example that remains appealing under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.15, 'LA01-0056-04-01-002'),
(206, 'United States', '932', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine/Very Fine', '3¢ Violet – Franklin D. Roosevelt, White House, 1945\r\n(Roosevelt Memorial Issue – Engraved, Perf. 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #932 completes the Roosevelt memorial set, presenting a left-facing portrait of Franklin D. Roosevelt paired with the White House in Washington, D.C. The design includes his name and life dates, with UNITED STATES POSTAGE and the denomination prominently displayed. The engraved execution is crisp and detailed, particularly in the architectural rendering and shaded portrait. The submitted example is unused with original gum and shows evidence of hinging.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #932 presents with premium Extremely Fine centering and excellent visual clarity, making a strong first impression. However, several ragged and shortened perforations reduce the technical grade significantly to 79 Fine/Very Fine. Even so, the stamp retains strong eye appeal and historical importance, remaining a desirable collectible example within the Roosevelt memorial series under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.17, 'LA01-0056-04-02-002'),
(207, 'United States', '933', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good', '5¢ Bright Blue – Franklin D. Roosevelt, Four Freedoms, 1945\r\n(Roosevelt Memorial Issue – Engraved, Perf. 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #933 concludes the 1945 Roosevelt memorial series with a symbolic design representing the Four Freedoms articulated by President Franklin D. Roosevelt. His portrait appears at left, while a globe at right bears the inscription “Freedom of Speech and Religion, From Want and Fear.” The composition blends portraiture with allegorical messaging, rendered in a finely engraved style that emphasizes clarity and contrast. The submitted example is unused with original gum and shows evidence of hinging.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #933 presents with pleasing Fine/Very Fine centering and strong visual impact from its bold design and color. However, several ragged and shortened perforations significantly reduce the technical grade to 57 Very Good. Even so, the stamp retains good overall eye appeal and remains a collectible example of this historically meaningful Roosevelt issue under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.10, 'LA01-0056-04-03-002'),
(208, 'United States', '940', '001', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good', '3¢ Purple – Honoring Those Who Have Served, 1948\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #940 was issued in 1948 to honor American military veterans and those who served in the nation\'s armed forces. The engraved design features a powerful eagle with outstretched wings enclosed within a circular frame and radiating lines, symbolizing national strength, sacrifice, and service. The inscriptions “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” and “HONORING THOSE WHO HAVE SERVED” frame the central design, while stars provide additional patriotic symbolism. Produced by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, the issue is noted for its sharp engraving and rich purple color. The submitted example is an unused plate block of four, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #940 plate block combines Fine/Very Fine centering, intact perforations, strong engraved detail, and preserved original gum. A prominent guideline and slight gum discoloration result in a Minor Fault classification and reduce the technical grade despite the absence of structural defects. Because the front appearance remains attractive and the faults have minimal visual impact, no additional eye appeal adjustment is warranted. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the combination of Fine/Very Fine centering and Minor Fault soundness yields a final grade of 58 (Very Good). It remains a collectible and visually pleasing plate block from this popular postwar commemorative issue.', 0.90, 'SL01-0002-03-01-001'),
(209, 'United States', '942', '001', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good', '3¢ Blue – Iowa Statehood Centennial, 1946\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #942 was issued in 1946 to commemorate the one hundredth anniversary of Iowa’s admission to the Union. The engraved design features an outline map of Iowa overlaid by the state flag, with dates marking the centennial and stylized corn stalks framing each side to symbolize the state\'s agricultural heritage. The denomination appears at lower right, while “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” and the centennial inscription complete the design. Produced by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, the issue is noted for its sharp engraving and rich blue color. The submitted example is an unused plate block of four, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #942 plate block combines Fine/Very Fine centering, intact perforations, strong engraved detail, and preserved original gum. A minor natural gum bend, visible guideline, and slight discoloration result in a Minor Fault classification and reduce the technical grade despite the absence of structural defects. Because these faults have minimal impact on the front appearance, no additional eye appeal adjustment is warranted. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the combination of Fine/Very Fine centering and Minor Fault soundness yields a final grade of 58 (Very Good). It remains a collectible and visually appealing plate block from this Iowa Statehood Centennial commemorative issue.', 0.90, 'SL01-0002-07-01-001'),
(210, 'United States', '942', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '3¢ Deep Ultramarine – Iowa Statehood Centennial, 1946\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #942 commemorates the 100th anniversary of Iowa’s admission to the Union in 1846. Issued August 3, 1946, the design shows the state flag superimposed over a map of Iowa, with cornstalks at each side symbolizing its agricultural heritage. “1846–IOWA STATEHOOD CENTENNIAL–1946” appears at top, with “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” and “3¢” below. Engraved by Charles A. Brooks and printed in deep ultramarine, this patriotic commemorative marked postwar renewal. The submitted example is postally used, previously hinged, and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used, hinged example of Scott #942 shows Fine/Very Fine centering and full perforations with no structural damage. Minor perf creasing and light soiling are moderate faults, and the heavy cancel reduces visual clarity of the central design. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, these factors yield a final numerical grade of 48 (Good/Very Good). Despite deductions, the stamp remains sound, legible, and a representative collectible o', 0.05, 'LA01-0057-04-02-003'),
(211, 'United States', '945', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '3¢ Bright Violet – Thomas A. Edison, 1947\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #945 honors inventor Thomas Alva Edison on the centennial of his birth. Issued February 11, 1947, in Menlo Park, New Jersey, the design shows Edison’s portrait at right beside an electric-light emblem within a framed tablet marked “THOMAS A. EDISON 3¢.” “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” appears across the top. Printed in bright violet by rotary press, this commemorative reflects postwar innovation. The submitted example is used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used, hinged example of Scott #945—the 3¢ Thomas A. Edison commemorative—exhibits Fine/Very Fine centering with sound paper and complete perforations. Perf creases and a heavy cancel lower its grade to 48 (Good/Very Good). Despite minor wear, the stamp retains vivid color and historical appeal under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.05, 'LA01-0058-01-02-002'),
(212, 'United States', '946', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '3¢ Deep Violet – Joseph Pulitzer, 1947\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #946 honors journalist Joseph Pulitzer (1847–1911) on the centennial of his birth. Issued April 10, 1947, in Columbia, Missouri, the design features Pulitzer’s portrait at right and the Statue of Liberty’s torch at left, with his quote, “OUR REPUBLIC AND ITS PRESS WILL RISE OR FALL TOGETHER,” spanning the center. “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” and “3¢” appear along the bottom. Printed in deep violet by rotary press, the issue symbolizes liberty, truth, and the importance of a free press. The submitted example is used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used, hinged example of Scott #946—the 3¢ Joseph Pulitzer commemorative—shows Fine/Very Fine centering, complete perforations, and strong color. Perf creasing, hinge remnants, and light soiling constitute moderate faults, while a heavy cancel further reduces clarity. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, these deductions yield a final grade of 41 (Good/Very Good). The stamp remains sound, historically significant, and collectible within its grade rang', 0.05, 'LA01-0058-01-03-002'),
(213, 'United States', '948', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '3¢ Multicolor Souvenir Sheet – Centenary International Philatelic Exhibition, 1947\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Offset and Engraved Printing, Imperforate, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #948 was issued May 17, 1947, to commemorate the 100th anniversary of the first U.S. postage stamps and the Centenary International Philatelic Exhibition in New York City. The sheet reproduces the 1847 designs of Benjamin Franklin (5¢ blue) and George Washington (10¢ brown orange) surrounded by inscriptions crediting the Postmaster General, the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, and the exhibition. Printed in two colors by offset and line engraving, the souvenir sheet is imperforate with wide borders. The submitted example is unused, never hinged, and retains its original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis never hinged souvenir sheet of Scott #948—the 1947 CIPEX issue—exhibits Fine/Very Fine centering with full margins, bright paper, and crisp impressions. A minor gum skip constitutes its only flaw, reducing the grade slightly to 73 (Fine). The sheet retains fresh color and excellent balance, remaining a desirable, collectible example of this centennial commemorative under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.75, 'LA01-0058-02-02-002'),
(214, 'United States', '953', '002', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good', '3¢ Purple – Dr. George Washington Carver, 1948\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #953 honors Dr. George Washington Carver (1864–1943), the pioneering agricultural scientist and educator whose work in crop rotation and soil improvement transformed Southern farming. Issued January 5, 1948, in Tuskegee, Alabama, the design features a portrait of Carver framed by fine double-line borders, with “DR. GEORGE WASHINGTON CARVER” inscribed below. The denomination “3¢” appears in a tablet at the lower right, balanced by a lamp of learning motif at left. Printed in purple ink by rotary press, this issue was the first U.S. commemorative to recognize an African American scientist. The submitted example is unused, previously hinged, and retains its original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis unused, hinged example of Scott #953—the 3¢ George Washington Carver commemorative—shows Fine/Very Fine centering with strong color, crisp impression, and full gum. Several short and fuzzy perfs constitute minor faults, reducing the technical grade to 59 (Very Good/Fine). Still, its sharp design and fresh presentation make it a desirable representative of this historic 1948 issue und', 0.08, 'LA01-0059-02-02-002'),
(215, 'United States', '954', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '3¢ Deep Violet – California Gold Centennial, 1948\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #954 commemorates the 100th anniversary of the discovery of gold at Sutter’s Mill in Coloma, California, which triggered the Gold Rush of 1849. Issued January 24, 1948, the design shows the historic mill along the American River, framed by tall pines and distant mountains. A crossed pick and shovel emblem appears at the upper right, while banners below mark “1848” and “1948.” Printed in deep violet by rotary press, this issue honors the pioneer spirit that transformed the nation. The submitted example is used, never hinged, and shows no visible cancellation or gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used, never hinged example of Scott #954—the 3¢ California Gold Centennial commemorative—shows Fine/Very Fine centering, strong color, and no visible cancellation, granting a +5 eye appeal bonus. Minor perf creases and irregular tips result in a final grade of 54 (Very Good). The stamp remains fresh, balanced, and visually superior for its issue under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.08, 'LA01-0059-01-03-002'),
(216, 'United States', '956', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine', '3¢ Dark Violet – The Immortal Chaplains, 1948\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #956 was issued in 1948 to honor the four chaplains who sacrificed their lives during the sinking of the U.S. Army Transport Dorchester in World War II. The engraved design depicts the chaplains above the sinking vessel with inscriptions emphasizing faith, unity, and service. The dark violet color and detailed engraving create a distinctive and memorable commemorative. This example retains sharp detail throughout the portraits, lettering, and central vignette.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #956 displays Fine/Very Fine centering, with the top margin establishing the centering limit and stronger spacing visible along the right and bottom sides. Two shorter perforations place the stamp in the Very Minor Fault category and result in a preliminary Fine 70 grade. Strong color, sharp engraving, and an exceptionally light cancellation support a positive eye appeal adjustment. The final assigned grade is Fine/Very Fine 75, representing an attractive used example of this historically important commemorative issue.', 0.11, 'LA01-0059-02-04-002'),
(217, 'United States', '958', '002', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good', '5¢ Blue – Swedish Pioneer Centennial, 1948\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #958 was issued May 23, 1948, to commemorate the centennial of Swedish immigration to the United States. Designed by Charles R. Chickering and engraved by Charles A. Brooks, the stamp portrays a Swedish pioneer leading an ox-drawn wagon westward beneath “SWEDISH PIONEER CENTENNIAL 1848–1948.” Thirteen stars symbolize the early states of the Union. The submitted example is unused with original gum and has been previously hinged.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis hinged, unused 5¢ Swedish Pioneer Centennial (Scott #958) displays Fine/Very Fine centering and bright presentation. Minor soundness flaws—ragged perforations and a natural gum bend—moderately reduce the grade but not the appeal. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the stamp’s final grade of 59 (Very Good) reflects a collectible mid-grade example with sound paper, original gum, and attractive color.', 0.08, 'LA01-0059-03-02-002'),
(218, 'United States', '962', '002', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Good', '3¢ Deep Red – Francis Scott Key, 1948\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #962 was issued September 14, 1948, to honor Francis Scott Key, author of The Star-Spangled Banner. Designed by Charles R. Chickering and engraved by C. A. Brooks, the vignette shows Key framed by two U.S. flags with “Old Key Home” at lower left and “Fort McHenry” at lower right—the scene that inspired his lyrics. The design reflects patriotic optimism in postwar America. This example is unused, retains original gum, and has been previously hinged.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis hinged, unused 3¢ Francis Scott Key commemorative displays Fine/Very Fine centering with a vivid carmine tone. A large thin and several rough perforations reduce its numerical grade, yet the front remains intact and well-presented. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade of 32 (Good) reflects a lower-grade but visually acceptable example suitable for collectors seeking a representative issue from the 1948 commemorative series.', 0.04, 'LA01-0059-04-02-002'),
(219, 'United States', '963', '002', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '3¢ Blue – Youth Month, 1948\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #963 was issued September 11, 1948, to promote “Youth Month,” a national campaign recognizing the promise and responsibility of American youth. Designed by Charles R. Chickering and engraved by Charles A. Brooks, the stamp features a young man and woman walking forward confidently beside a large banner reading “YOUTH MONTH SEPT. 1–30, 1948.” The banner rests above five stars symbolizing achievement and progress. The message “SALUTING YOUNG AMERICA” appears across the top. This example is unused with original gum and shows evidence of previous hinging.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis unused 3¢ “Youth Month” commemorative (Scott #963) displays Fine/Very Fine centering and solid visual balance. The minor ink offset and faint gum bend reduce the grade slightly but do not detract from its clean, well-preserved presentation. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade of 72 (Fine) represents a sound, collectible example with bright color, intact perforations, and good overall appearance.', 0.11, 'LA01-0059-04-03-002'),
(220, 'United States', '966', '002', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '3¢ Deep Blue – Palomar Mountain Observatory, 1948\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #966 was issued August 30, 1948, celebrating the completion of the Palomar Mountain Observatory and its 200-inch Hale Telescope, then the world’s largest. Designed by Charles R. Chickering and engraved by Charles A. Brooks, the image shows the massive observatory dome with its shutters open to the sky, symbolizing mid-century scientific progress. The example is unused, retains original gum, and has been previously hinged.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis unused 3¢ Palomar Mountain Observatory commemorative (Scott #966) shows Fine/Very Fine centering and vivid deep-blue color. Minor faults—ragged perfs, light ink offset, and a small gum bend—reduce the numerical grade but not its aesthetic quality. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide and numeric grading scale, the final grade of 61 (Very Good/Fine) reflects a collectible hinged example with sound paper, bright presentation, and pleasing overall balance.', 0.10, 'LA01-0060-01-03-002'),
(222, 'United States', '978', '002', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '3¢ Deep Blue – Abraham Lincoln, 1948\r\n(Lincoln Memorial Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11×10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued on November 19, 1948, the 3¢ Lincoln “Gettysburg Address” commemorative honors Abraham Lincoln and his immortal words, “that government of the people, by the people, for the people, shall not perish from the earth.” Designed by Charles R. Chickering and engraved by Charles A. Brooks, the stamp features a left-facing bust of Lincoln beside an inset of the quotation and a torch representing freedom and democracy. This example is a used single, previously hinged with no original gum. The color remains strong, typical of rotary press impressions of the late 1940s.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used, hinged example of Scott #978 shows Fine/Very Fine centering, fresh color, and a notably clean appearance. The small perf tip crease and two short perfs are minor and not visually obtrusive. With its light cancel and balanced design, it earns a final grade of 64 (Very Good–Fine) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide. Despite light imperfections, it remains a collectible Lincoln commemorative with strong presentation and pleasing visual appeal.', 0.10, 'LA01-0061-02-02-002'),
(223, 'United States', '979', '002', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good', '3¢ Carmine Rose – American Turners, 1948\r\n(American Turners Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11×10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued on November 19, 1948, the 3¢ American Turners commemorative marks the centennial of the American Turners Society, celebrating “A Century of Health.” Designed by Charles R. Chickering and engraved by George A. L. Woytuk, it features a discus thrower encircled by the motto “Sound Mind, Sound Body,” flanked by “1848” and “1948.” Above is a torch symbolizing enlightenment, framed by oak leaves and bells. Printed by rotary press on unwatermarked paper, this example is unused, previously hinged, and retains its original gum with vivid carmine color and sharp impression typical of the issue.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis unused, hinged example of Scott #979 displays Fine/Very Fine centering, strong color, and intact perforations. Minor gum and perf irregularities account for its Minor Fault classification, but the design remains sharp and balanced. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, this results in a final grade of 58 (Very Good)—a sound, collectible mid-grade example with pleasing eye appeal and strong presentation.', 0.08, 'LA01-0061-01-03-002'),
(224, 'United States', '979', '002', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good', '3¢ Carmine Rose – American Turners, 1948\r\n(American Turners Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11×10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued on November 19, 1948, the 3¢ American Turners commemorative marks the centennial of the American Turners Society, celebrating “A Century of Health.” Designed by Charles R. Chickering and engraved by George A. L. Woytuk, it features a discus thrower encircled by the motto “Sound Mind, Sound Body,” flanked by “1848” and “1948.” Above is a torch symbolizing enlightenment, framed by oak leaves and bells. Printed by rotary press on unwatermarked paper, this example is unused, previously hinged, and retains its original gum with vivid carmine color and sharp impression typical of the issue.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis unused, hinged example of Scott #979 displays Fine/Very Fine centering, strong color, and intact perforations. Minor gum and perf irregularities account for its Minor Fault classification, but the design remains sharp and balanced. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, this results in a final grade of 58 (Very Good)—a sound, collectible mid-grade example with pleasing eye appeal and strong presentation.', 0.08, 'LA01-0061-01-03-002'),
(225, 'United States', '982', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine', '3¢ Deep Blue – Washington and Lee University, 1949\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #982 was issued in 1949 to commemorate the bicentennial of Washington and Lee University. The design features the university’s administration building flanked by portraits of George Washington and Robert E. Lee. Deep blue engraving provides strong contrast and highlights the architectural and portrait details. In this example, the principal design elements remain clear, with the building, portraits, and inscriptions retaining good visibility despite normal postal use.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #982 displays Fine/Very Fine centering, with the bottom margin establishing the centering limit and the top margin providing the greatest spacing. Light dirt smudging and spotting on the reverse place the stamp in the Very Minor Fault category and result in a preliminary Fine 70 grade. An average postal cancellation has no direct grading impact, while slightly reduced eye appeal warrants a one-point deduction. The final assigned grade is Very Good/Fine 69, representing a solid collectible example of this 1949 commemorative issue.', 0.11, 'LA01-0061-03-02-002'),
(226, 'United States', '984', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '3¢ Light Blue – Annapolis Tercentenary, 1949\r\n(Annapolis Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11×10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued March 23, 1949, the 3¢ Annapolis Tercentenary commemorative honors the 300th anniversary of the founding of Annapolis, Maryland. Designed by Charles R. Chickering, the stamp shows a map of the Severn River and Chesapeake Bay region, with a colonial ship at right, the state seal of Maryland at left, and a compass rose above. The legend “Annapolis Tercentenary – 1649–1949” appears below. Printed by rotary press on unwatermarked paper and perforated 11×10½, this example is used, previously hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used, hinged example of Scott #984 shows Fine/Very Fine centering with intact perforations and bright color. Minor perf creasing and a heavy cancel lower the grade but not the stamp’s integrity or legibility. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, these factors yield a final grade of 49 (Good–Very Good)—a sound, collectible example with clear design and balanced presentation.', 0.05, 'LA01-0061-04-01-002'),
(227, 'United States', '992', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '3¢ Violet – Washington National Capital Sesquicentennial, 1950\r\n(Washington, D.C. Sesquicentennial Issue – Engraved, Perf. 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #992 commemorates the 150th anniversary of Washington, D.C. as the U.S. capital, issued in 1950. The engraved design features the Capitol building with strong architectural detail and inscriptions marking the sesquicentennial. The composition emphasizes balance and national symbolism. The submitted example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nWith the corrected deduction, this Scott #992 lands at a more respectable 71 Fine. The stamp maintains solid Fine/Very Fine centering, crisp engraving, and strong color, with only minor reverse imperfections holding it back. In other words, it is one of those pieces that looks better than its number suggests—which, frankly, is half the charm of collecting.', 0.15, 'LA01-0062-03-01-002');
INSERT INTO `stamps` (`id`, `country`, `scott`, `count`, `condition`, `hinged`, `gum`, `grade`, `description`, `price`, `location`) VALUES
(228, 'United States', '1008', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine/Very Fine', '3¢ Violet – NATO 1952, Peace, Strength, Freedom\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1008 was issued in 1952 to commemorate the North Atlantic Treaty Organization. The design depicts two clasped hands raising a flaming torch above a globe, symbolizing international cooperation and collective security. Bold lettering and radiating lines emphasize the themes of peace, strength, and freedom. Printed in violet, the stamp presents a striking Cold War-era design with strong visual contrast. This example retains clear detail throughout the central imagery and inscriptions.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #1008 displays Fine/Very Fine centering, with the bottom margin establishing the centering limit and the right margin slightly narrower than the left. Faultless soundness results in no deduction, while intact perforations and a neat machine cancel preserve the stamp\'s overall appearance. Strong color, sharp detail, and neutral eye appeal support the final assigned grade of Fine/Very Fine 75, making this an attractive collectible example of the NATO commemorative issue.', 0.13, 'LA01-0063-04-02-002'),
(229, 'United States', '1012', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '3¢ Deep Ultramarine – Centennial of Engineering, 1952\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf. 11x10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1012 was issued in 1952 to commemorate the centennial of the American Society of Civil Engineers. The design contrasts a covered wooden bridge representing early engineering accomplishments with the George Washington Bridge symbolizing modern engineering achievement. The ASCE emblem appears at left, while inscriptions recognize the society’s founding in 1852 and its centennial anniversary. Printed in deep ultramarine, the stamp presents a distinctive combination of historical and modern imagery. This example retains clear design detail despite the effects of postal use.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #1012 displays Very Fine centering, with the bottom margin establishing the centering limit and the left margin providing the greatest spacing. Several ragged and shortened perforations place the stamp in the Minor Fault category and reduce the preliminary grade to Very Good/Fine 60. A heavy machine cancellation further reduces eye appeal and warrants a ten-point deduction. The final assigned grade is Very Good 50, representing a sound but visibly used example of this engineering-themed commemorative issue.', 0.08, 'LA01-0064-01-03-002'),
(230, 'United States', '1013', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '3¢ Bright Blue – Women in Our Armed Services, 1952\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf. 11x10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1013 was issued on September 11, 1952, to honor women who served in the U.S. Armed Forces. The design depicts four uniformed women representing the Army, Navy, Air Force, and Marine Corps, with the U.S. Capitol building in the background. The inscriptions read “WOMEN IN OUR ARMED SERVICES” at the top and “UNITED STATES POSTAGE 3¢ OF AMERICA” along the bottom scroll. The issue was printed by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing on the rotary press, perforated 11x10½, and issued without watermark. The submitted example is postally used, previously hinged, and retains partial original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used, previously hinged example of Scott #1013 is graded 46 (Good/Very Good) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide. It displays Fine/Very Fine centering with clear margins but is impacted by rough perforations, a heavy machine cancel, and evidence of hinging on the reverse. Despite these issues, the stamp retains full design clarity and good color, making it a collectible and historically meaningful representation of the Women in Our Armed Services commemorative.', 0.05, 'LA01-0064-02-01-002'),
(231, 'United States', '1013', '003', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '3¢ Blue – Women in Our Armed Services, 1952\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Perf. 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1013 honors the contributions of women in the United States Armed Services. The engraved design features four uniformed women representing different branches of service, with the U.S. Capitol faintly visible in the background. The inscription “Women in Our Armed Services” underscores the commemorative purpose. The composition is balanced and dignified, with fine engraved detail in both portraiture and uniform textures. The submitted example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1013 example presents with solid Fine/Very Fine centering and strong engraved detail, enhanced by its never hinged status. A minor natural gum bend results in only a small deduction, yielding a final grade of 73 Fine. With its appealing design and clean front presentation, it remains a desirable and collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.15, 'NA01-0183-03-01-003'),
(232, 'United States', '1014', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '3¢ Violet – Johann Gutenberg / 500th Anniversary of Printing the First Book, 1952\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf. 11x10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1014 was issued in 1952 to commemorate the 500th anniversary of Johann Gutenberg’s invention of movable-type printing and the printing of the Gutenberg Bible. The design combines a Renaissance print shop scene with a scroll inscription honoring Gutenberg’s achievement. Printed in violet by rotary press, the issue remains a popular commemorative among collectors of printing and communications history. This example retains clear identification of the principal design elements despite postal use.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #1014 displays Fine/Very Fine centering, with the bottom margin establishing the centering limit and the top margin providing the greatest spacing. Heavy cancellation bleed-through and multiple rough or shortened perforations place the stamp in the Fault category and reduce the preliminary grade to Good/Very Good 40. A heavy machine cancellation further reduces eye appeal and warrants a ten-point deduction. The final assigned grade is Good 30, representing a heavily used but collectible example of this Gutenberg commemorative issue.', 0.04, 'LA01-0064-02-02-002'),
(233, 'United States', '1014', '003', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '3¢ Violet – 500th Anniversary of Gutenberg Bible, 1952\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Perf. 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1014 commemorates the 500th anniversary of the printing of the Gutenberg Bible. The design features a vignette of a 15th-century printing scene, paired with text referencing Johann Gutenberg and the significance of movable type. The engraved composition blends historical imagery with typographic elements, giving the stamp both visual and intellectual weight. The submitted example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1014 example presents with attractive Fine/Very Fine centering and a detailed engraved design celebrating printing history. A minor natural gum bend results in a modest deduction, bringing the final grade to 73 Fine. With its crisp appearance and never hinged status, it remains a pleasing and collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.15, 'NA01-0183-03-03-003'),
(234, 'United States', '1015', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '3¢ Purple – Newspaperboys Commemorative, 1952\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf. 11x10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1015 was issued in 1952 to honor America\'s newspaperboys and their contributions to local communities. The design depicts a young paperboy delivering newspapers through a residential neighborhood while a torch labeled “Free Enterprise” symbolizes initiative and opportunity. Printed in purple by rotary press, the issue reflects the postwar emphasis on civic responsibility and youth enterprise. This example retains clear detail throughout the principal design elements despite postal use.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #1015 displays Very Fine centering, with the top margin establishing the centering limit and the left margin providing the greatest spacing. Several rough or shortened perforations place the stamp in the Minor Fault category and reduce the preliminary grade to Very Good/Fine 60. A heavy machine cancellation further reduces eye appeal and warrants a ten-point deduction. The final assigned grade is Very Good 50, representing a well-centered but visibly used example of this 1952 commemorative issue.', 0.08, 'LA01-0064-03-01-002'),
(235, 'United States', '1016', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '3¢ Blue and Red – Honoring the International Red Cross, 1952\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf. 11x10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued May 8, 1952, Scott #1016 honors the founding of the International Red Cross in 1864. The design features a globe showing the Western Hemisphere and a bright red cross radiating light, symbolizing worldwide humanitarian aid. Olive branches at the corners represent peace, and inscriptions read “HONORING THE INTERNATIONAL RED CROSS – FOUNDED 1864.” Printed by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing via rotary press, this issue is perforated 11x10½ and unwatermarked. The submitted example is postally used, previously hinged, and retains partial original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used, previously hinged example of Scott #1016 grades 47 (Good/Very Good) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide. It shows Fine/Very Fine centering and bright color but is affected by a light crease and heavy machine cancel. Despite these flaws, the stamp remains visually balanced, historically meaningful, and a collectible representation of the 1952 International Red Cross commemorative.', 0.05, 'LA01-0064-03-02-002'),
(236, 'United States', '1016', '003', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine/Very Fine', '3¢ Blue & Red – International Red Cross, 1952\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Perf. 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1016 commemorates the International Red Cross, founded in 1864. The design features a globe at left and a radiant red cross at right, symbolizing global humanitarian aid and relief. Ornamental borders frame the composition, with inscriptions honoring the organization. The combination of blue engraving and red emblem creates strong visual contrast and thematic clarity. The submitted example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1016 example presents with strong Very Fine centering, vibrant color contrast, and crisp engraved detail. A minor natural gum bend results in a small deduction, bringing the final grade to 79 Fine/Very Fine. Despite this slight technical flaw, the stamp retains excellent eye appeal and remains a highly collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.17, 'NA01-0183-04-02-003'),
(237, 'United States', '1017', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good', '3¢ Light Ultramarine – National Guard of the United States, 1951\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11x10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued December 13, 1951, Scott #1017 honors the National Guard, the nation’s oldest organized military force. Designed by Charles R. Chickering and engraved by Charles A. Brooks and Richard M. Bower, the stamp portrays a Guardsman with rifle at the ready, representing service “In War” and “In Peace.” Combat scenes appear to the left and domestic relief work to the right. The inscriptions “THE NATIONAL GUARD OF THE U.S.” and “THE OLDEST MILITARY ORGANIZATION IN THE U.S.” underscore its dual mission. The submitted example is unused, with full original gum and never hinged.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis never hinged mint example of the 1951 National Guard commemorative shows Fine/Very Fine centering with full design clearance and typical rotary-press perforations. Though several perforations are short or fuzzy, the stamp remains sound and visually fresh. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, these attributes yield a final grade of 59 (Very Good)—a collectible, well-preserved mid-century issue with bright color and original gum.', 0.10, 'LA01-0064-04-01-002'),
(238, 'United States', '1018', '001', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good', '3¢ Brown – Ohio Sesquicentennial, 1953\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11x10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued March 2, 1953, to mark the 150th anniversary of Ohio’s admission to the Union in 1803, Scott #1018 features a brown engraved map of Ohio with the Great Seal of the State centered within. The inscriptions “OHIO SESQUICENTENNIAL,” “1803,” and “1953” appear above and to the sides, with vertical rows of stars framing the design and a Buckeye leaf emblem at lower left. The denomination “3¢” and the caption “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” complete the base. The design by Charles R. Chickering was engraved by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing. The submitted example is unused, with full original gum, never hinged, and retains strong brown color and crisp rotary press impression.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis unused, never hinged 1953 Ohio Sesquicentennial 3¢ commemorative exhibits solid color, sharp engraving, and intact perforations. Centering is Fine/Very Fine, slightly shifted right and low, while soundness is reduced by a natural gum crease and faint bend typical of mid-century rotary issues. The stamp’s freshness and balanced margins yield a final grade of 59 (Very Good/Fine) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, remaining a clean and collectible example of this commemorative issue.', 0.10, 'NA01-0185-01-02-001'),
(239, 'United States', '1018', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '3¢ Brown – Ohio Sesquicentennial, 1953\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1018 was issued in 1953 to commemorate the 150th anniversary of Ohio’s admission to the Union. The design features the Great Seal of Ohio superimposed on the outline of the state, framed by stars representing the original states and accented by a buckeye sprig below. Printed in brown using line engraving, the issue reflects the formal commemorative style of the early 1950s. This example remains visually attractive, with the principal design elements clear and well defined.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #1018 displays Fine centering, with the right margin establishing the centering limit and the bottom margin providing the greatest spacing. Ragged perforations, a corner perforation crease, and pencil writing on the reverse place the stamp in the Minor Fault category and reduce the preliminary grade to Good/Very Good 40. A light, well-positioned cancellation supports a positive eye appeal adjustment. The final assigned grade is Good/Very Good 45, representing a visually appealing but technically flawed example of this Ohio statehood commemorative.', 0.05, 'LA01-0064-04-02-002'),
(240, 'United States', '1020', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '3¢ Purple – Louisiana Purchase Sesquicentennial, 1953\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1020 was issued April 30, 1953, for the 150th anniversary of the Louisiana Purchase. The vignette depicts Monroe, Livingston, and Marbois signing the Louisiana Transfer in Paris in 1803, with inscriptions “LOUISIANA PURCHASE SESQUICENTENNIAL 1803–1953,” “SIGNING THE LOUISIANA TRANSFER PARIS 1803,” and “MONROE, LIVINGSTON AND MARBOIS.” Engraved by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, it exemplifies the refined commemorative style of the 1950s. The evaluated example is a used, never-hinged single with no original gum and a light circular cancel across the top.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used, never-hinged example of the 1953 Louisiana Purchase Sesquicentennial stamp displays Fine/Very Fine centering and complete perforations. A heavy crease limits its grade, yet the stamp’s color, clarity, and balanced cancel preserve strong visual appeal. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade of 39 (Good) accurately represents a collectible example combining Fine/Very Fine centering with Major Fault soundness and pleasing eye appeal.', 0.04, 'LA01-0065-01-01-002'),
(241, 'United States', '1021', '001', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good', '5¢ Blue Green – Centennial of the Opening of Japan, 1953\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11x10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued July 14, 1953, Scott #1021 honors the 100th anniversary of Commodore Matthew C. Perry’s expedition that opened Japan to foreign trade. Designed by Charles R. Chickering and engraved by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, the vignette shows Perry’s squadron entering Edo Bay with his portrait at upper right. “U.S. POSTAGE 5¢” appears vertically at left, and “1853 • CENTENNIAL OF OPENING OF JAPAN • 1953” runs across the bottom. The stamp symbolizes the beginning of diplomatic relations between the United States and Japan. The submitted example is unused, never hinged, with full original gum, fresh paper, and a strong engraved impression.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis unused, never hinged 1953 Opening of Japan 5¢ commemorative shows Fine/Very Fine centering with the design slightly right and low. The stamp remains structurally sound aside from two tiny perf-tip creases, a faint back smudge, and a natural gum bend, qualifying as a Minor Fault under the 2009 PSE Guide. With sharp engraving, bright paper, and fresh gum, it earns a final grade of 59 (Very Good/Fine)—a collectible and historically appealing example from the early 1950s commemorative series.', 0.10, 'NA01-0185-02-02-001'),
(242, 'United States', '1021', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '5¢ Blue – Centennial of the Opening of Japan, 1953\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1021 commemorates the centennial of Commodore Matthew C. Perry’s 1853 expedition that opened Japan to Western trade. The design combines a portrait of Perry with his ships anchored in Edo Bay and a distant view of Mount Fuji. Engraved in blue, the stamp presents both maritime and diplomatic themes within a balanced horizontal format. This example retains strong detail throughout the portrait, ships, and inscriptions despite normal postal use.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #1021 displays Fine/Very Fine centering, with the top margin establishing the centering limit and the bottom margin providing the greatest spacing. A light crease, corner perforation crease, and pencil notation on the reverse place the stamp in the Minor Fault category and reduce the preliminary grade to Very Good/Fine 60. An average machine cancellation has no further grading impact. The final assigned grade is Very Good/Fine 60, representing a respectable used example of this historically significant commemorative issue.', 0.08, 'LA01-0065-01-02-002'),
(243, 'United States', '1022', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair/Good', '3¢ Purple – American Bar Association, 1953\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1022 was issued August 28, 1953, marking the 75th anniversary of the American Bar Association. The design, engraved by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, portrays allegorical figures of Law, Liberty, and Justice before the American flag, symbolizing the motto “Liberty Under Law.” Inscriptions read “AMERICAN BAR ASSOCIATION,” “1878 • LIBERTY UNDER LAW • 1953,” and “U.S. POSTAGE 3¢.” The evaluated example is a used, never-hinged single without original gum, showing a heavy black slogan cancel over the design.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used, never-hinged example of the 1953 American Bar Association commemorative displays Fine/Very Fine centering and full perforations but is limited by two creases and a bold slogan cancel that obscures much of the design. Despite strong color and paper freshness, condition and eye appeal reduce its grade. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade of 24 (Fair/Good) accurately represents a collectible but heavily faulted used example combining Fine/Very Fine centering with Major Fault soundness and poor eye appeal.', 0.02, 'LA01-0065-01-03-002'),
(244, 'United States', '1023', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '3¢ Green – Sagamore Hill, Oyster Bay, N.Y. (Home of Theodore Roosevelt), 1953\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1023 was issued October 27, 1953, to honor Theodore Roosevelt and his home, Sagamore Hill, in Oyster Bay, New York. The stamp depicts the residence framed by trees, symbolizing Roosevelt’s love of family and nature. The inscriptions read “U.S. POSTAGE,” “SAGAMORE HILL, OYSTER BAY, N.Y.,” and “HOME OF THEODORE ROOSEVELT.” Produced by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, this issue reflects the balanced, precise engraving typical of early 1950s commemoratives. The submitted example is a used, never-hinged single without original gum, canceled with horizontal black machine lines.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used, never-hinged example of the 1953 Sagamore Hill commemorative shows Fine/Very Fine centering, strong color, and full perforations. Minor reverse marks and a light corner crease slightly lower the grade but do not detract from overall presentation. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade of 57 (Very Good) accurately represents a collectible used example combining Fine/Very Fine centering with Minor Fault soundness and balanced appearance.', 0.08, 'LA01-0065-02-01-002'),
(245, 'United States', '1024', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '3¢ Blue – Future Farmers of America, 1953\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1024 was issued October 13, 1953, marking the 25th anniversary of the Future Farmers of America. The design shows a young farmer with a plow overlooking a field and distant barns under a rising sun, symbolizing hope and progress in agriculture. The inscriptions read “U.S. POSTAGE 3¢,” “1928 FUTURE FARMERS OF AMERICA,” and “1953.” Produced by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, this blue-toned commemorative reflects the idealism of postwar rural America. The submitted example is a used, hinged single with no original gum and a black machine cancel of five wavy lines.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used, hinged example of the 1953 Future Farmers of America stamp shows Fine/Very Fine centering with full perforations and vivid color. Minor gum residue and short perforations reduce the technical grade, yet the stamp remains visually balanced and attractive. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade of 56 (Very Good) accurately represents a collectible example combining Fine/Very Fine centering with Minor Fault soundness and consistent overall eye appeal.', 0.08, 'LA01-0065-02-02-002'),
(246, 'United States', '1024', '003', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '3¢ Deep Ultramarine – Future Farmers of America, 1953\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1024 commemorates the 25th anniversary of the Future Farmers of America (1928–1953). Issued in 1953, the design by Charles R. Chickering and engraved by M.D. Fenton features a young farmer overlooking plowed fields and a barn under a bright sky, symbolizing agricultural leadership and progress. “U.S. Postage 3¢” appears at the upper left, with “1928–1953” and “Future Farmers of America” below. The submitted example is unused, never hinged, and retains full original gum with a mild natural sheen.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis never hinged example of the 1953 Future Farmers of America 3¢ issue shows Fine/Very Fine centering, full perforations, and strong color. The only detracting factor is light soiling on the reverse, producing a modest deduction and resulting in a final grade of Fine (69). It remains a fresh, well-preserved postwar commemorative and a solid representative under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.13, 'NA01-0185-03-02-003'),
(247, 'United States', '1025', '001', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good', '3¢ Violet – 50th Anniversary of the Trucking Industry, 1953\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11×10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued on September 23, 1953, for the 50th Anniversary of the American Trucking Industry, Scott #1025 honors the importance of transportation in uniting rural and urban America. Designed and engraved by C.A. Brooks, the stamp depicts a truck driver in the foreground with farm buildings, open fields, and a distant city skyline symbolizing economic connection and modernization. The inscription “50TH ANNIVERSARY OF THE TRUCKING INDUSTRY” appears in a banner below the image. The submitted example is unused, never hinged, and retains full original gum with vivid violet color and a sharp rotary press impression.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis unused, never hinged Scott #1025 shows crisp engraving, bright violet ink, and balanced margins within the Fine/Very Fine range. Minor perforation and gum irregularities slightly reduce its technical grade, but its freshness and color strength elevate its presentation. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the stamp earns a final grade of 58 (Very Good/Fine)—a visually appealing and collectible example of this 1953 commemorative issue.', 0.10, 'LA01-0065-03-01-001'),
(248, 'United States', '1026', '001', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '3¢ Deep Violet – General George S. Patton, Jr., 1953\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11×10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued on November 11, 1953, this commemorative honors General George S. Patton, Jr., and the armored forces of the United States Army. Designed and engraved by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, the vignette depicts Patton in uniform with tanks advancing behind him, symbolizing leadership and mobility in modern warfare. The inscription reads “HONORING GEN. GEORGE S. PATTON, JR. AND THE ARMORED FORCES OF THE U.S. ARMY.” The stamp was part of a post-war series celebrating distinguished military leaders. The evaluated example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum with bold color and a clean rotary-press impression.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis unused, never hinged example of Scott #1026 exhibits Fine/Very Fine centering with strong color and crisp engraving. Minor production irregularities—a single perf-tip crease and a faint natural gum bend—place it in the Very Minor Fault category, reducing the grade slightly. Its freshness and even tone maintain strong collector appeal, resulting in a final grade of 72 (Fine) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.15, 'LA01-0065-03-02-001'),
(249, 'United States', '1026', '003', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '3¢ Blue – General George S. Patton, Jr., 1953\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Perf. 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1026 honors General George S. Patton, Jr. and the armored forces of the U.S. Army. The engraved design features a portrait of Patton in uniform at right, with tanks advancing across a battlefield in the background. The composition conveys motion and military strength, with strong line work and contrast typical of mid-century Bureau engraving. The submitted example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1026 example presents with solid Fine/Very Fine centering and a strong engraved depiction of General Patton and armored forces. A minor natural gum bend results in a small deduction, yielding a final grade of 73 Fine. With its crisp detail and desirable never hinged status, it remains a pleasing and collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.15, 'NA01-0185-04-01-003'),
(250, 'United States', '1027', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '3¢ Blue – General George S. Patton, Jr., 1953\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Perf. 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1026 honors General George S. Patton, Jr. and the armored forces of the U.S. Army. The engraved design features a portrait of Patton in uniform at right, with tanks advancing across a battlefield in the background. The composition conveys motion and military strength, with strong line work and contrast typical of mid-century Bureau engraving. The submitted example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1026 example presents with solid Fine/Very Fine centering and a strong engraved depiction of General Patton and armored forces. A minor natural gum bend results in a small deduction, yielding a final grade of 73 Fine. With its crisp detail and desirable never hinged status, it remains a pleasing and collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.13, 'LA01-0065-04-01-002'),
(251, 'United States', '1029', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '3¢ Deep Blue – Columbia University Bicentennial, 1954\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11×10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued May 31, 1954, Scott #1029 commemorates the 200th anniversary of Columbia University’s founding in 1754. The design by Charles R. Chickering features the university’s Low Memorial Library, symbolizing higher education and free inquiry. The detailed engraving captures the stately dome and columns of the building, with the inscription “MAN’S RIGHT TO KNOWLEDGE AND THE FREE USE THEREOF” below the steps. This example is used, shows no original gum, and bears a light machine cancel that leaves the design clearly legible.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #1029 retains strong blue color, fine engraving detail, and balanced margins. Minor perforation irregularities reduce technical soundness, but the well-applied cancel and overall freshness maintain visual appeal. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, it receives a final grade of 60 (Very Good/F', 0.10, 'LA01-0065-04-03-002'),
(252, 'United States', '1029', '003', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '3¢ Deep Ultramarine – Columbia University Bicentennial, 1954\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1954, Scott #1029 honors the 200th anniversary of Columbia University. Designed by Charles R. Chickering and engraved by M.D. Fenton, the stamp depicts Low Memorial Library, the university’s neoclassical centerpiece, representing the pursuit of knowledge. The inscriptions read “1754 – Columbia University – 1954” at the top and “Man’s Right to Knowledge and the Free Use Thereof” below. This example is unused, never hinged, and retains full original gum with natural sheen.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis never hinged 1954 Columbia University Bicentennial 3¢ issue shows Fine/Very Fine centering, full perforations, and deep ultramarine color. Minor corner and gum imperfections modestly lower soundness, but strong color and clarity raise overall appeal. Final grade: 61 (Very Good/Fine) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.13, 'NA01-0185-05-01-003'),
(253, 'United States', '1030', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '½¢ Orange – Benjamin Franklin, 1954\r\n(Liberty Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 x 10½)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1954 as the first value in the Liberty Series, Scott #1030 features a right-facing portrait of Benjamin Franklin, designed by Charles R. Chickering and engraved by Richard M. Bower. The rectangular frame contains “U.S. Postage ½¢” at lower left and “Benjamin Franklin” at upper right. The vivid orange shade identified this as the lowest denomination of the definitive series, used primarily for bulk-rate and third-class mail. The submitted example is unused, never hinged, and retains its full original gum with natural sheen.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis unused 1954 Benjamin Franklin ½¢ Liberty issue shows Fine/Very Fine centering, full original gum, and bright orange color. A few short perforations and a corner irregularity account for minor soundness deductions. The stamp remains clean, fresh, and visually appealing, earning a final grade of 59 (Very Good) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide and Numeric Grading Standards.', 0.08, 'NA01-0187-01-01-003'),
(254, 'United States', '1030', '004', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '½¢ Orange – Benjamin Franklin, 1954\r\n(Liberty Series – Engraved, Perf. 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1030 is part of the Liberty Series and features a finely engraved portrait of Benjamin Franklin. The design emphasizes Franklin’s likeness through bold line engraving framed within a simple, formal layout characteristic of the series. The bright orange color provides strong visual contrast and makes the stamp immediately recognizable. This example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #1030 displays Fine/Very Fine centering, with the left margin establishing the centering limit and the right margin providing the greatest spacing. A minor natural gum bend places the stamp in the Extremely Minor Fault category and reduces the preliminary grade to Fine 70. Strong color, sharp engraving, intact perforations, and original gum support solid overall presentation. The final assigned grade is Fine 70, representing an attractive never hinged example of this Liberty Series issue.', 0.15, 'LA01-0066-01-01-004'),
(255, 'United States', '1030', '005', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '1/2¢ Carmine – Benjamin Franklin, 1954\r\n(Liberty Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1030 is part of the Liberty Series issued beginning in 1954, featuring a finely engraved portrait of Benjamin Franklin. The design is framed within a rectangular border with “U.S. POSTAGE” and denomination clearly displayed. The engraving is crisp, with fine line work typical of mid-20th century Bureau issues.\r\n\r\nSummary:\r\nThis Scott #1030 example presents solid Fine/Very Fine centering with generally intact structure and only minor perforation irregularities. The grouping of short perfs results in a modest soundness deduction, while the heavy cancel further impacts visual appeal. Despite these factors, the stamp remains a respectable and collectible mid-grade example, achieving a final grade of 67 (Very Good/Fine) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.10, 'SL01-0001-06-05-005'),
(256, 'United States', '1030', '006', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '½¢ Orange – Benjamin Franklin, 1954\r\n(Liberty Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1030 is part of the 1954 Liberty Series, featuring a finely detailed portrait of Benjamin Franklin engraved in orange. The design presents Franklin in a three-quarter view within a rectangular frame, with “U.S. POSTAGE” and the denomination prominently displayed. The engraving is crisp, with strong line work typical of mid-20th century Bureau issues. This example is used, with no original gum remaining, consistent with postal usage, and is otherwise intact aside from noted faults.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1030 example presents as a solid Fine/Very Fine centered stamp with pleasing margins and a light, well-placed cancellation that enhances its visual appeal. The primary drawback is the presence of multiple perforation creases, which reduce the soundness classification to Minor Fault and significantly impact the numerical grade. Even so, the stamp retains attractive color, clarity, and overall presentation, making it a respectable and collectible example within the Very Good/Fine range under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.10, 'SD22-0006-01-01-006'),
(257, 'United States', '1030', '007', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '½¢ Orange – Benjamin Franklin, 1954\r\n(Liberty Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1030 is a ½¢ orange stamp from the 1954 Liberty Series, depicting Benjamin Franklin in a finely engraved portrait. The design is framed by a simple rectangular border with “U.S. POSTAGE” and denomination inscriptions, reflecting the clean, utilitarian aesthetic of mid-century definitive issues. This example is used, with no original gum remaining, consistent with postal usage, and shows a light cancel with otherwise intact structure aside from noted faults.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1030 example offers solid Fine/Very Fine centering with clearly defined margins and a light, well-placed cancel that preserves the clarity of the design. The multiple perforation creases reduce the soundness to a Minor Fault classification, significantly impacting the numerical grade. However, the stamp retains strong color, a pleasing overall appearance, and good balance, making it a respectable collectible example grading 63 (Very Good/Fine) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.10, 'SL01-0001-07-03-007'),
(258, 'United States', '1030', '008', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '½¢ Orange – Benjamin Franklin, 1954\r\n(Liberty Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1030 is a ½¢ orange definitive from the 1954 Liberty Series, featuring a finely engraved portrait of Benjamin Franklin. The design is framed in a simple rectangular border with bold denomination and “U.S. POSTAGE” inscriptions, characteristic of mid-century Bureau issues emphasizing clarity and utility. This example is used, with no original gum remaining, consistent with postal handling, and retains a clear overall impression aside from noted structural faults.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1030 example demonstrates solid Fine/Very Fine centering with clearly defined margins and a pleasing, lightly applied cancel that preserves the clarity of the design. The presence of multiple perforation creases places the stamp in the Minor Fault category and significantly reduces the technical grade. Nevertheless, the stamp maintains strong color, a clean impression, and good overall balance, resulting in a final grade of 63 (Very Good/Fine) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.10, 'SL01-0001-07-05-008'),
(259, 'United States', '1031', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '1¢ Deep Green – George Washington, 1954\r\n(Liberty Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 x 10½)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1954, Scott #1031 portrays George Washington in a right-facing engraved bust designed by Charles R. Chickering and engraved by Richard M. Bower. The design features a rectangular frame with “1¢ U.S. Postage” below and “Washington” at upper right. Printed in deep green ink, this issue represents the second denomination of the Liberty Series. The submitted example is used, never hinged prior to cancellation, and bears no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used 1954 George Washington 1¢ Liberty issue displays Fine/Very Fine centering, faultless soundness, and intact perforations. The color remains bright and impression sharp. However, a heavy machine cancel crosses Washington’s face and chest, significantly diminishing eye appeal. The final grade of 67 (Very Good–Fine) accurately reflects the balance of centering, soundness, and cancellation under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide and Numeric Grading Standards.', 0.10, 'NA01-0187-01-02-003'),
(260, 'United States', '1031', '004', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine/Very Fine', '1¢ Green – George Washington, 1954\r\n(Liberty Series – Engraved, Perf. 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1031 is part of the Liberty Series and features a classic engraved portrait of George Washington. The design is simple and dignified, with fine line engraving that emphasizes facial detail and shading, framed within a rectangular border. The green color is characteristic of low-denomination Washington issues. The submitted example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary:\r\nThis Scott #1031 example presents with strong Very Fine centering, clean engraving, and attractive color. Minor reverse faults result in a modest deduction, bringing the final grade to 75 Fine/Very Fine. Despite these small technical issues, the stamp maintains excellent eye appeal and remains a desirable example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.17, 'LA01-0066-01-02-004'),
(261, 'United States', '1031A', '003', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '1¼¢ Blue – Palace of the Governors, Santa Fe, 1954\r\n(Liberty Series – Engraved, Perf. 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1031A is part of the Liberty Series and depicts the Palace of the Governors in Santa Fe, New Mexico, one of the oldest public buildings in the United States. The engraved design emphasizes architectural perspective, with a long colonnade receding into the distance, creating depth and visual rhythm. The inscription identifies the location, reinforcing its historical significance. The submitted example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1031A example presents with solid Fine/Very Fine centering and a detailed architectural engraving. A minor natural gum bend results in a small deduction, bringing the final grade to 73 Fine. With its clean appearance and desirable never hinged status, it remains a strong and collectible Liberty Series example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.15, 'LA01-0066-02-01-003'),
(262, 'United States', '1032', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '1½¢ Maroon – Mount Vernon, 1954\r\n(Liberty Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 × 10½)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1954, this 1½¢ Liberty Series definitive stamp features Mount Vernon, the Virginia estate of George Washington. Designed by Charles R. Chickering and engraved by M. D. Fenton, it depicts the mansion’s portico and cupola, symbolizing Washington’s legacy and early American architecture. The deep maroon engraving accentuates fine structural detail and texture. The example under review is a used single, previously hinged, without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary:\r\nThis used 1954 Mount Vernon 1½¢ Liberty issue exhibits Fine/Very Fine centering and a clean cancel, though perf creases and hinge residue classify it as a Minor Fault example. The color remains vivid, and the design is clear, resulting in a final grade of 59 (Very Good) per the 2009 PSE Grading Guide and Numeric Grading Standards.', 0.08, 'LA01-0066-02-02-002'),
(263, 'United States', '1033', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair', '2¢ Carmine – Thomas Jefferson, 1954\r\n(Liberty Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1033 was issued in 1954 as part of the Liberty Series and portrays Thomas Jefferson, third President of the United States and author of the Declaration of Independence. The engraved design features a right-facing portrait with “JEFFERSON” at left, “2¢” at lower right, and “U.S. POSTAGE” below. Printed in rich carmine by rotary press, the issue reflects the sharp line engraving typical of mid-century Bureau production. This example is postally used, hinged, and without original gum. Hinging is evaluated separately and does not affect grading here.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1033 shows Fine centering with visible white space despite a leftward shift. A light crease, shortened perforations, and a corner perf crease reduce soundness to the Fault category, while the dense machine cancel significantly impacts eye appeal. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 16 (Fair), representing a sound but heavily used Liberty Series example.', 0.02, 'LA01-0066-01-03-002'),
(264, 'United States', '1034', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '2½¢ Blue – Bunker Hill Monument, 1954\r\n(Liberty Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1034 was issued in 1954 as part of the Liberty Series and depicts the Bunker Hill Monument in Charlestown, Massachusetts, commemorating one of the earliest major battles of the American Revolution. The design features the monument with the colonial Pine Tree flag and United States flag behind it, with “BUNKER HILL” at upper right and “2½¢ U.S. POSTAGE” below. Printed in blue by rotary press, the stamp shows the crisp engraving typical of mid-century Bureau production. This example is postally used, never hinged, and without original gum. Hinging is evaluated separately and does not affect grading here.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1034 presents Fine/Very Fine centering with strong color and clear engraving. A single shortened perforation introduces only a minor deduction, while the heavy machine cancel significantly reduces eye appeal. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 63 (Very Good/Fine), representing a solid used Liberty Series example.', 0.10, 'LA01-0066-03-01-002'),
(265, 'United States', '1036', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '4¢ Rose Carmine – Abraham Lincoln, 1954\r\n(Liberty Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1036 was issued in 1954 as part of the Liberty Series and honors Abraham Lincoln, the 16th President of the United States. The engraved design features a right-facing portrait of Lincoln against fine horizontal lines, with “LINCOLN” at right and “4¢ U.S. POSTAGE” below. Printed in rose carmine by rotary press, the stamp reflects the crisp engraving and clean style typical of mid-century Bureau production. This example is postally used, never hinged, and without original gum. Hinging is evaluated separately and does not affect grading here.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1036 displays Very Fine centering with sharp engraving, fresh color, and strong perforations. A minor perf-tip crease introduces only a small deduction, while the heavy machine cancel modestly reduces eye appeal. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 69 (Very Good/Fine), making it a sound and collectible Liberty Series example.', 0.10, 'LA01-0066-03-03-002'),
(266, 'United States', '1041', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '8¢ Carmine & Deep Blue – Statue of Liberty, 1954\r\n(Liberty Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1041, issued in 1954, features the Statue of Liberty as a national symbol of freedom and faith. Designed by Charles R. Chickering and engraved by Bureau of Engraving and Printing artists, the two-color composition presents Liberty in carmine red against a deep blue radiating background, with inscriptions reading “U.S. POSTAGE 8¢,” “IN GOD WE TRUST,” and “LIBERTY.” Printed by rotary press on unwatermarked paper, this example is postally used and shows full design clarity despite heavy cancel coverage.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nUnder the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, this used example of Scott #1041 combines Fine/Very Fine centering with a Minor Fault and a Very Heavy cancel. While the strike obscures key features, the stamp remains structurally sound and well-balanced in color. The final grade of 43 (Good/Very Good) accurately reflects a solid, collectible used Liberty Series issue in honest postal condition.', 0.05, 'LA01-0066-05-01-002'),
(267, 'United States', '1042', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '8¢ Deep Blue & Carmine – Statue of Liberty, 1954\r\n(Liberty Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1042 is the 8¢ Statue of Liberty issue from the Liberty Series. The design depicts Liberty raising her torch, with “IN GOD WE TRUST” encircling the flame, “U.S. POSTAGE” at the top, and “LIBERTY” at the bottom. The two-color blue and carmine printing gives the stamp a vivid, patriotic appearance with strong mid-century engraving detail.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #1042 shows Fine/Very Fine centering with all margins clear of the design, though slightly uneven with the right side most restricted. The principal limiting factors are partial gum and multiple perforation creases, resulting in a Fault classification and significant deduction. The very light cancel improves visual appeal, allowing the Statue of Liberty design to remain clear and attractive. The final grade of 51 (Very Good) reflects a balanced 2009 PSE evaluation, leaving it a visually pleasing but technically limited collectible example.', 0.08, 'LA01-0066-05-03-003');
INSERT INTO `stamps` (`id`, `country`, `scott`, `count`, `condition`, `hinged`, `gum`, `grade`, `description`, `price`, `location`) VALUES
(268, 'United States', '1043', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '9¢ Rose Carmine – The Alamo, 1956\r\n(Liberty Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1043 was issued in 1956 as part of the Liberty Series and depicts the Alamo in San Antonio, Texas, an enduring symbol of courage and independence. The rose-carmine engraved design shows the mission façade, with “THE ALAMO” above and “U.S. POSTAGE 9¢” below. Produced by rotary press printing, the issue reflects the sharp line engraving typical of mid-century Bureau production. This example is postally used, never hinged, and retains partial original gum. Hinging is evaluated separately and does not affect grading here.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1043 presents Fine/Very Fine centering with strong color and sharp engraving. A light crease, partial gum retention, and one shortened perforation reduce soundness to the Fault category, while the heavy machine cancel modestly lowers eye appeal. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 40 (Good/Very Good), representing a sound and collectible Liberty Series example.', 0.05, 'LA01-0066-02-04-002'),
(269, 'United States', '1044', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '10¢ Deep Red Brown – Independence Hall, 1956\r\n(Liberty Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 × 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1044 depicts Independence Hall in Philadelphia, the historic site associated with the signing of both the Declaration of Independence and the United States Constitution. The deep red-brown engraved design emphasizes the building\'s symmetrical architecture and distinctive clock tower. Printed by rotary press for the Liberty Series, the issue combines strong line engraving with a simple, dignified layout. This used example retains clear detail throughout the structure and inscriptions.\r\n\r\nSummary:\r\nScott #1044 displays Fine/Very Fine centering, with the bottom margin establishing the centering limit and the top and right margins providing the greatest spacing. Two corner perforation creases place the stamp in the Minor Fault category and reduce the preliminary grade to Very Good/Fine 60. A light, unobtrusive cancellation and strong color support a positive eye appeal adjustment. The final assigned grade is Very Good/Fine 64, representing an attractive used example of this Liberty Series issue.', 0.10, 'LA01-0066-05-02-002'),
(270, 'United States', '1049', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine/Very Fine', '30¢ Black – Robert E. Lee, 1955\r\n(Liberty Series – Engraved Printing, Perf 11 × 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1049, issued in 1955 as part of the Liberty Series, honors Confederate General Robert E. Lee. The engraved design, based on a photograph by Mathew Brady, presents a somber bust portrait of Lee against a horizontally lined background. The inscriptions “ROBERT E. LEE,” “30¢,” and “U.S. POSTAGE” appear within the design. The stamp was printed by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing on unwatermarked paper and perforated 11 × 10½. The examined example is postally used, retains no gum, and has never been hinged.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used 30¢ Robert E. Lee issue displays Fine/Very Fine centering with clean margins, sharp engraving, and a lightly struck cancel that enhances overall appeal. With one minor perforation crease reducing soundness but offset by a favorable eye appeal adjustment, the final numerical grade is 77 (Fine/Very Fine) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide—an attractive, collectible Liberty Series high-value definitive.', 0.21, 'LA01-0067-02-03-002'),
(271, 'United States', '1049', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '30¢ Black – Robert E. Lee, 1955\r\n(Liberty Series – Engraved Printing, Perf 11 × 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1049, issued in 1955 as part of the Liberty Series, features an engraved portrait of Confederate General Robert E. Lee, adapted from a Mathew Brady photograph. The design presents Lee in formal attire against a lined background with “ROBERT E. LEE” at left and “30¢ U.S. POSTAGE” at lower right. Produced by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, it was printed on unwatermarked paper and perforated 11 × 10½. The example is postally used, has no original gum, and was never hinged.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used 30¢ Robert E. Lee stamp shows Fine/Very Fine centering and intact perforations with one minor fault and a heavy cancel. While the cancel reduces visual balance, the stamp remains sound and well-centered for the issue. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 61 (Very Good–Fine)—a solid, collectible used example of this Liberty Series definitive.', 0.16, 'NA01-0187-04-01-003'),
(272, 'United States', '1050', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '40¢ Red Brown – John Marshall, 1955\r\n(Liberty Series – Engraved Printing, Perf 11 × 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1050, issued in 1955 as part of the Liberty Series, honors Chief Justice John Marshall, whose rulings established the authority of the Supreme Court. The engraved design, from Henry Inman’s portrait, shows “JOHN MARSHALL” at right and “40¢ U.S. POSTAGE” below. Printed by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing on unwatermarked paper, it is perforated 11 × 10½. The example is postally used, has no original gum, and was never hinged.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used 40¢ John Marshall stamp shows Fine/Very Fine centering, full design clarity, and an appealing light magenta cancel. Minor perforation and ink migration faults reduce the base grade, but visual appeal remains solid. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, this stamp earns a final grade of 54 (Very Good)—a well-balanced, collectible Liberty Series high-value issue.', 0.12, 'LA01-0067-02-04-002'),
(273, 'United States', '1052', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '$1 Purple – Patrick Henry, 1955\r\n(Liberty Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1955, the $1 Patrick Henry commemorates the fiery orator of the American Revolution, famed for his “Give me liberty, or give me death!” speech. The deep purple engraved design by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing presents Henry in three-quarter profile with “PATRICK HENRY” at right and “$1 U.S. POSTAGE” at lower left. This high-value definitive served heavier and international mail. The submitted example is used, has no original gum, and was never hinged.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used $1 Patrick Henry from the 1955 Liberty Series combines Fine/Very Fine centering with flawless soundness but suffers an eye appeal reduction due to a heavy machine cancel crossing the portrait. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the proper final grade is 68 (Very Good–Fine – VG-F). The stamp remains a solid, fault-free, and historically significant high-value issue retaining its engraving sharpness and authentic postal character.', 0.10, 'LA01-0067-03-02-002'),
(274, 'United States', '1054', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair', '1¢ Green – George Washington, 1954\r\n(Liberty Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 × 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1054, issued in 1954, portrays George Washington after the Gilbert Stuart portrait. Part of the Liberty Series, it served as the lowest-denomination definitive through the late 1950s. The engraved design features “WASHINGTON” at right and “U.S. POSTAGE 1¢” below the bust. Designed by Charles R. Chickering and printed by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, this rotary-press issue is in green ink on white wove paper. The submitted example is postally used, retains no original gum, and has never been hinged.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #1054 exhibits Very Good centering and intact structure but several minor perforation faults and a heavy machine cancel. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, these deductions yield a final grade of 15 (Fair – FR)—a serviceable, authentic Liberty Series example showing normal postal wear but limited visual appeal.', 0.02, 'LA01-0067-04-01-002'),
(275, 'United States', '1054', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair', '1¢ Green – George Washington, 1954\r\n(Liberty Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 × 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1054, issued in 1954, portrays George Washington based on Gilbert Stuart’s classic painting. The deep green engraved design shows Washington facing right, framed with “WASHINGTON” at right and “1¢ U.S. POSTAGE” below. Designed by Charles R. Chickering and printed by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, it formed part of the long-running Liberty Series. The submitted example is postally used, has been hinged, and retains no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #1054 has Fine/Very Fine centering but multiple severe faults, including a large thin, creases, and discoloration. The design remains intact and legible, yet heavy damage and soiling place it in the Fair (14) range under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide—an example suitable for reference or filler use rather than display.', 0.02, 'NA01-0191-01-01-003'),
(276, 'United States', '1054A', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine/Very Fine', '1¼¢ Blue – Palace of the Governors, Santa Fe, 1957\r\n(Liberty Series – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1054A is part of the Liberty Series and depicts the Palace of the Governors in Santa Fe, New Mexico, rendered in a horizontal engraved format. The long adobe structure with its distinctive portico is shown in perspective, with the inscription “PALACE OF THE GOVERNORS SANTA FE, NEW MEXICO” across the top and “U.S. POSTAGE” with the 1¼¢ denomination at the bottom. The design highlights regional American architecture with fine line engraving. This example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1054A example shows Fine/Very Fine centering with clean margins, intact perforations, and a sharp engraved design. With only an Extremely Minor Fault, the stamp maintains strong eye appeal and benefits from a slight positive adjustment, resulting in a final grade of 75 (Fine/Very Fine). It stands as a desirable and well-preserved Liberty Series example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.13, 'LA01-0067-05-02-002'),
(278, 'United States', '1056', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '2½¢ Blue – Bunker Hill Monument, 1957\r\n(Liberty Series – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1056 is a Liberty Series issue featuring the Bunker Hill Monument with a flowing flag in the background, engraved in a compact vertical format. The design includes “BUNKER HILL” in the upper right, the 2½¢ denomination at lower left, and “U.S. POSTAGE” along the bottom. The composition combines strong patriotic symbolism with the crisp line work typical of the series. This example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1056 example shows Fine/Very Fine centering with intact perforations, strong engraved detail, and original gum in never hinged condition. Slight discoloration places it in the Very Minor Fault category, reducing the final grade to 72 (Fine). Even with that modest deduction, it remains a solid and collectible Liberty Series example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.19, 'LA01-0067-04-03-002'),
(279, 'United States', '1057', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '3¢ Deep Violet – Statue of Liberty, 1954\r\n(Liberty Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1057, issued in 1954 as the lead stamp of the Liberty Series, depicts the Statue of Liberty with the motto “IN GOD WE TRUST.” Designed by Charles R. Chickering and engraved by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, the design features Liberty holding her torch high with radiant light lines in the background, symbolizing freedom and faith. The denomination “3¢” appears at the upper right, and “LIBERTY” is inscribed below. The submitted example is postally used, has never been hinged, and shows normal wear without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nAccording to the 2009 PSE Grading Guide and numeric standards, this used Scott #1057 exhibits Fine/Very Fine centering but is limited by perforation roughness and a heavy cancel, resulting in a final grade of 46 (Good–Very Good). Despite these deductions, the color, paper quality, and strong engraving detail make it a sound, collectible used example of the Liberty definitive.', 0.05, 'LA01-0067-04-04-002'),
(280, 'United States', '1057', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '3¢ Deep Violet – Statue of Liberty, 1954\r\n(Liberty Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1057, issued in 1954 as the first denomination of the Liberty Series, depicts the Statue of Liberty with her torch raised high against radiant light. “IN GOD WE TRUST” arches above the crown, and “LIBERTY” appears boldly below. Designed by Charles R. Chickering and engraved by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, the issue symbolizes American ideals of freedom and faith. The submitted example is postally used, never hinged, and has no original gum, consistent with standard used condition.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nUnder the 2009 PSE Grading Guide and numeric grading scale, this used Scott #1057 combines Fine/Very Fine centering with a small tear, rough perforations, and a heavy cancel. The resulting final grade of 37 (Good) reflects moderate wear and postal use. Despite these faults, the stamp retains strong color, clean paper, and full design integrity, making it a sound and collectible Liberty Series example.', 0.04, 'NA01-0191-01-04-003'),
(281, 'United States', '1058', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair', '4¢ Lilac – Abraham Lincoln, 1954\r\n(Liberty Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1058, issued in 1954 for the Liberty Series, presents Abraham Lincoln in right profile, engraved from a model by Gutzon Borglum. The design is framed by fine vertical lines, with “4¢ U.S. POSTAGE” at the lower left and “LINCOLN” at right within a double-line border. Printed by rotary press on unwatermarked paper, this issue honors Lincoln’s legacy of unity and strength. The submitted stamp is postally used, has no original gum, and has never been hinged.\r\n\r\nSummary:\r\nThis used Scott #1058 displays Fine/Very Fine centering but suffers from multiple major faults, including a heavy crease and a cut corner, resulting in a Severe Fault classification. Despite reduced visual appeal, its legible cancel and intact engraving preserve collectible merit as a study or reference piece.', 0.02, 'LA01-0067-05-01-002'),
(282, 'United States', '1058', '003', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Good', '4¢ Magenta – Abraham Lincoln, 1957\r\n(Liberty Series – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1058 is a Liberty Series issue featuring a finely engraved portrait of Abraham Lincoln in a square frame. The design includes “U.S. POSTAGE,” the 4¢ denomination, and “LINCOLN” at right, rendered in a rich magenta tone with detailed line engraving. The composition emphasizes clarity and dignity, typical of the series’ portrait issues. This example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1058 example shows Fine/Very Fine centering with strong engraved detail and intact perforations. However, significant staining places it in the Major Fault category, resulting in a substantial deduction and a final grade of 30 (Good). Despite its otherwise solid centering and design clarity, the condition issues limit its desirability, though it remains a collectible example within the Liberty Series under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.14, 'NA01-0191-02-01-003'),
(283, 'United States', '1059', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '4½¢ Green – The Hermitage, 1957\r\n(Liberty Series – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1059 is part of the Liberty Series and features The Hermitage, the home of Andrew Jackson, depicted in a horizontal engraved composition. The design presents the mansion framed by trees with “THE HERMITAGE” at the top and “U.S. POSTAGE” with the 4½¢ denomination at the bottom. The engraving highlights architectural symmetry and landscape detail, consistent with the refined style of the series. This example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1059 example exhibits Fine/Very Fine centering with clean margins, intact perforations, and a sharp engraved design. With only an Extremely Minor Fault, the stamp maintains strong eye appeal and benefits from a slight positive adjustment, resulting in a final grade of 74 (Fine). It remains a desirable and well-preserved Liberty Series example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 1.76, 'LA01-0067-05-03-002'),
(284, 'United States', '1059A', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Good', '25¢ Green – Paul Revere, 1957\r\n(Liberty Series – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1059A is a Liberty Series issue featuring a finely engraved portrait of Paul Revere in a square frame. The design includes “U.S. POSTAGE,” the 25¢ denomination, and “PAUL REVERE” at right. The engraving emphasizes clarity and strong portrait detail, typical of higher-denomination Liberty issues. This example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1059A example exhibits Very Good centering with the right margin approaching or touching the design, along with several irregular perforations that place it in the Minor Fault category. These factors reduce the final grade to 32 (Good). While the engraving remains clear and the stamp retains original gum, the centering and perforation issues limit its overall desirability, though it remains a collectible Liberty Series example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.14, 'LA01-0067-05-04-002'),
(285, 'United States', '1061', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine', '3¢ Red Orange – Kansas Territorial Centennial, 1954\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 × 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued May 30, 1954, in Topeka, Kansas, Scott #1061 honors the 100th anniversary of the Kansas Territory (1854–1954). The design features ox-drawn covered wagons crossing the prairie under rolling clouds, symbolizing frontier migration and settlement. “KANSAS TERRITORIAL CENTENNIAL” is printed across the bottom, with “1854–1954” at the top corners. Produced by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, this stamp complements the Nebraska Centennial issue. The submitted example is postally used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used example of the 1954 Kansas Territorial Centennial 3¢ stamp shows Fine centering, full perforations, and vivid color. The “Hire the Handicapped” slogan cancel adds interest while modestly affecting clarity. Reverse smudging accounts for a small deduction, yielding a final grade of 74 (Fine – F) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide and current numeric grading scale.', 0.11, 'LA01-0068-01-02-002'),
(286, 'United States', '1066', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '8¢ Deep Ultramarine – 50th Anniversary of Rotary International, 1955\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 11 × 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1955, Scott #1066 honors the 50th anniversary of Rotary International, founded in 1905. The design shows a strong hand lifting a torch before a globe, symbolizing worldwide fellowship and enlightenment. The Rotary gear emblem appears at lower right beside the motto “SERVICE ABOVE SELF.” Engraved in deep ultramarine by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, it reflects the optimistic, modern style of mid-century commemoratives. The submitted example is unused, retains full original gum, and has never been hinged.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis never hinged example of Scott #1066 shows Fine/Very Fine centering with strong color and full gum. A faint gum bend and light ink transfer constitute a Very Minor Fault, producing a –7 deduction and final grade 72 (Fine) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide. Despite light production flaws, it remains a sound, attractive representative of the Rotary International commemorative issue.', 0.15, 'LA01-0068-03-02-002'),
(287, 'United States', '1066', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Fine', '8¢ Deep Ultramarine – 50th Anniversary of Rotary International, 1955\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 11 × 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1955, Scott #1066 commemorates the 50th anniversary of Rotary International, founded in 1905. The design shows a raised hand holding a torch before a globe, symbolizing enlightenment and worldwide service. The Rotary emblem appears in the lower right with the inscription “SERVICE ABOVE SELF.” Engraved by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, the deep ultramarine tone and strong lines capture the modern style of mid-century commemoratives. The submitted example is used, has never been hinged, and retains no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used example of Scott #1066 exhibits Fine/Very Fine centering with bright ultramarine color, clean perforations, and a sharp impression. The cancel is light and well positioned, enhancing rather than detracting from the design. Faultless condition and strong visual balance yield a final grade of 83 (Very Fine) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.15, 'NA01-0195-03-02-003'),
(288, 'United States', '1067', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '3¢ Purple – Armed Forces Reserve, 1955\r\n(Liberty Series Commemorative – Engraved Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued May 30, 1955, Scott #1067 honors the U.S. Armed Forces Reserve. The deep purple engraving portrays five uniformed figures representing the Marine Corps, Navy, Army, Air Force, and Coast Guard before a large eagle, symbolizing unity and strength. The inscriptions read “ARMED FORCES RESERVE” and “UNITED STATES POSTAGE 3¢.” Produced by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing as part of the Liberty Series, this example is postally used, has no original gum, and has never been hinged.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nUnder the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, this Armed Forces Reserve stamp grades 64 (Very Good/Fine). Centering is balanced with narrow bottom and right margins, and soundness deductions stem from minor perforation creases. A neat cancel, strong impression, and clean presentation enhance eye appeal, making this a pleasing, collectible used example from the Liberty Series.', 0.10, 'LA01-0068-03-03-002'),
(289, 'United States', '1067', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine/Very Fine', '3¢ Purple – Armed Forces Reserve, 1955\r\n(Liberty Series Commemorative – Engraved Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued May 30, 1955, the 3¢ Armed Forces Reserve stamp honors the dedication of America’s reserve military branches. The deep purple design features five servicemen representing the Marine Corps, Navy, Army, Air Force, and Coast Guard standing before an eagle symbolizing unity and defense. “ARMED FORCES RESERVE” and “UNITED STATES POSTAGE 3¢” frame the composition. Produced by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing as part of the Liberty Series, this example is postally used, has no original gum, and has never been hinged.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nUnder the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, this Armed Forces Reserve stamp grades 78 (Fine/Very Fine). It shows balanced centering with slightly narrow lower and left margins, complete perforations, strong color, and a clear cancel. A fault-free example with vivid engraving and solid eye appeal, it stands as a desirable, high-end used copy within its grade range.', 0.13, 'NA01-0195-03-03-003'),
(290, 'United States', '1067', '004', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '3¢ Rose Violet – Armed Forces Reserve, 1953\r\n(Offset Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1067 was issued in 1953 to honor the United States Armed Forces Reserve. The design depicts five uniformed members representing the Army, Navy, Marine Corps, and Air Force before a stylized winged emblem, emphasizing military readiness and unity during the early Cold War period. “ARMED FORCES RESERVE” appears across the top, while “UNITED STATES POSTAGE 3¢” anchors the bottom. Printed in rose violet using offset lithography, the stamp has the softer impression typical of offset printing compared with engraved issues. This example is mint, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1067 displays strong Very Fine/Extremely Fine centering with full margins and intact perforations. A visible front stain places the stamp in the Fault category, while a corner perf crease and minor gum bend add secondary concerns. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 56 (Very Good), representing a visually appealing example whose technical grade is held back primarily by the stain.', 0.08, 'SL01-0001-08-03-004'),
(291, 'United States', '1068', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '3¢ Dark Green – The Old Man of the Mountains, New Hampshire, 1955\r\n(Liberty Series Commemorative – Engraved Printing, Perf 11 × 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued June 21, 1955, Scott #1068 honors New Hampshire’s natural landmark “The Old Man of the Mountains,” a granite cliff formation once overlooking Franconia Notch. The design shows the profile face with sharp engraving and inscriptions “THE OLD MAN OF THE MOUNTAINS” and “NEW HAMPSHIRE – LIVE FREE OR DIE.” Printed in dark green by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, this Liberty Series issue reflects 1950s patriotic regionalism. The submitted example is postally used, has no original gum, and has never been hinged.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nUnder the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, this used Scott #1068 earns a final grade of 62 (Very Good/Fine). Centering is balanced, and the stamp remains fault-free except for a faint perf crease. Its dark machine cancel obscures much of the engraving, lowering eye appeal. Despite this, the color and impression are excellent, making it a sound, collectible Liberty Series example that retains historical and visual interest.', 0.10, 'LA01-0068-02-03-002'),
(292, 'United States', '1069', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '3¢ Deep Blue – A Century of Great Lakes Transportation, 1955\r\n(Liberty Series Commemorative – Engraved Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued June 25, 1955, Scott #1069 commemorates 100 years of Great Lakes transportation and the opening of the Soo Locks at Sault Sainte Marie, Michigan. The design depicts a freighter and passenger vessel beneath a map of the Great Lakes—Superior, Michigan, Huron, Erie, and Ontario—with inscriptions “SOO LOCKS 1855 • SAULT SAINTE MARIE • 1955” and “A CENTURY OF GREAT LAKES TRANSPORTATION.” Printed by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing in rich deep blue, this Liberty Series issue celebrates engineering progress and American maritime industry. The example is unused, retains full original gum, and has never been hinged.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nUnder the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, this unused example of Scott #1069 receives a final grade of 62 (Very Good/Fine). The stamp’s centering is Fine with close but clear margins, and soundness deductions are minimal. The deep blue color, strong engraving, and intact gum maintain its collector appeal, making it a solid mid-grade mint example of the Great Lakes Transportation commemorative.', 0.13, 'LA01-0068-04-01-002'),
(293, 'United States', '1069', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '3¢ Deep Blue – A Century of Great Lakes Transportation, 1955\r\n(Liberty Series Commemorative – Engraved Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued June 25, 1955, Scott #1069 commemorates 100 years of Great Lakes transportation and the opening of the Soo Locks at Sault Sainte Marie, Michigan. The design features a freighter crossing beneath a map of the five Great Lakes—Superior, Michigan, Huron, Erie, and Ontario—symbolizing inland commerce. Inscriptions include “SOO LOCKS 1855 – SAULT SAINTE MARIE – 1955” and “A CENTURY OF GREAT LAKES TRANSPORTATION.” Printed by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing in deep blue, this issue celebrates American maritime trade and engineering progress. The example is postally used, has no original gum, and has never been hinged.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nUnder the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, this used Scott #1069 grades 62 (Very Good/Fine). Its centering and soundness meet Fine/Very Fine standards, but a heavy machine cancel reduces visual clarity. The color remains strong and paper fresh, making it a solid collectible Liberty Series example with attractive centering and minimal physical faults.', 0.10, 'NA01-0195-04-01-003'),
(294, 'United States', '1070', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '3¢ Deep Blue – Atoms for Peace, 1955\r\n(Prominent Americans Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued July 28 1955, Scott #1070 commemorates President Dwight D. Eisenhower’s “Atoms for Peace” address promoting peaceful nuclear progress. Designed by George Oldden and printed by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, the stamp shows two globes surrounded by atomic orbits symbolizing global unity through science. “ATOMS FOR PEACE” spans the center, framed by excerpts from the speech and “UNITED STATES POSTAGE 3¢” below. The submitted example is postally used, never hinged, and has no original gum, typical for circulated copies.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Atoms for Peace 3¢ stamp displays Fine/Very Fine centering with clean perforations and one short tip. The heavy cancel reduces visibility of the design, producing a final grade of 63 (Very Good/Fine) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide. Despite the cancel, the stamp retains strong color, crisp impression, and enduring collector appeal as a solid used example of this historic commemorative.', 0.10, 'LA01-0068-04-02-002'),
(295, 'United States', '1070', '003', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '3¢ Blue – Atoms for Peace, 1955\r\n(Offset Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1070 was issued in 1955 to promote the peaceful use of nuclear energy during the Cold War era. The design features two hemispheres of the globe encircled by atomic orbital paths, with the bold inscription “ATOMS FOR PEACE” across the center. The surrounding text, “THE INVENTIVENESS OF MAN SHALL BE… CONSECRATED TO HIS LIFE,” emphasizes scientific progress directed toward peaceful purposes. Printed in blue using offset lithography, the stamp presents a crisp and modern appearance. This example is mint, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1070 presents Fine/Very Fine centering with strong color, sharp printing, and a clean front appearance. Minor reverse ink transfer and a natural gum bend introduce only limited deductions, while overall freshness supports a modest eye appeal increase. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 74 (Fine), making it a solid collectible example of this Cold War-era commemorative.', 0.15, 'NA01-0195-04-02-003'),
(296, 'United States', '1071', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fair/Good', '3¢ Brown – Fort Ticonderoga Bicentennial, 1955\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 11 × 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued November 10, 1955, Scott #1071 honors the 200th anniversary of Fort Ticonderoga, a vital stronghold during the French and Indian War and the American Revolution. Designed by Charles R. Chickering and engraved by Arthur W. Dintaman, the design depicts a colonial artilleryman beside a cannon, gesturing toward the fort’s star-shaped plan. “FORT TICONDEROGA” appears across the top, with “Bicentennial” at left, “1755–1955” at right, and “UNITED STATES POSTAGE 3¢” below. The submitted example is unused, never hinged, and retains full original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Fort Ticonderoga Bicentennial 3¢ issue displays Fine/Very Fine centering and rich color but is affected by a cut upper-left corner. While gum and paper remain clean, this Major Fault lowers its technical grade. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, its combination of sound color, good centering, and physical defect yields a final grade of 29 (Fair/Good)—a presentable yet clearly flawed example of this historic 1955 commemorative.', 0.03, 'LA01-0068-04-03-002'),
(297, 'United States', '1071', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '3¢ Brown – Fort Ticonderoga Bicentennial, 1955\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 11 × 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued November 10, 1955, Scott #1071 marks the 200th anniversary of Fort Ticonderoga, a key stronghold during the French and Indian War and the American Revolution. Designed by Charles R. Chickering and engraved by Arthur W. Dintaman, the stamp depicts a colonial artilleryman beside a cannon, pointing toward the fort’s plan in the background. “FORT TICONDEROGA” spans the top, with “Bicentennial” at left, “1755–1955” at right, and “UNITED STATES POSTAGE 3¢” below. The submitted example is postally used, never hinged, and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Fort Ticonderoga Bicentennial 3¢ commemorative shows Fine/Very Fine centering with strong color and intact perforations. A light perf tip crease and heavy cancel modestly reduce the grade. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, these combined factors yield a final grade of 62 (Very Good/Fine)—a sound, well-centered, and collectible used example of this historic 1955 issue.', 0.10, 'NA01-0195-04-03-003'),
(298, 'United States', '1073', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine/Very Fine', '3¢ Carmine Rose – Benjamin Franklin, 1956\r\n(250th Anniversary Issue – Engraved, Perf 11 × 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1073 was issued in 1956 to commemorate the 250th anniversary of Benjamin Franklin’s birth. The engraved design depicts Franklin’s famous kite experiment, symbolizing his contributions to science and discovery. Cherubic figures and decorative elements frame the scene, while the inscriptions identify the commemorative purpose of the issue. Printed in carmine rose, the stamp combines strong engraving with an attractive color palette. This example remains visually sharp and well preserved despite postal use.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #1073 displays Very Fine centering, with the right margin establishing the centering limit and the left margin providing the greatest spacing. Light cancellation bleed-through on the reverse places the stamp in the Very Minor Fault category and reduces the preliminary grade to Fine/Very Fine 75. A clean violet cancellation and strong color support a modest positive eye appeal adjustment. The final assigned grade is Fine/Very Fine 76, representing an attractive used example of this Benjamin Franklin commemorative issue.', 0.13, 'LA01-0069-01-02-002'),
(299, 'United States', '1073', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine', '3¢ Carmine Rose – Benjamin Franklin, 1956\r\n(250th Anniversary Issue – Engraved, Perf 11 × 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1073 was issued in 1956 to commemorate the 250th anniversary of Benjamin Franklin’s birth. The engraved design depicts Franklin conducting his famous kite experiment, symbolizing his contributions to science and discovery. Decorative figures and inscriptions frame the scene, creating one of the more distinctive commemoratives of the period. Printed in carmine rose, the stamp combines strong engraving detail with an attractive color scheme. This example remains clear and well defined despite postal use.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #1073 displays Fine/Very Fine centering, with the right margin establishing the centering limit and the left margin providing the greatest spacing. A single shortened perforation places the stamp in the Very Minor Fault category and results in a preliminary Fine 70 grade. A moderately heavy machine cancellation slightly reduces eye appeal and warrants a two-point deduction. The final assigned grade is Very Good/Fine 68, representing a well-preserved used example of this Benjamin Franklin commemorative issue.', 0.11, 'NA01-0197-01-02-003'),
(300, 'United States', '1076', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Fine/Extra Fine', '3¢ Deep Violet – Fifth International Philatelic Exhibition, 1956\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Perf 11 × 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1076 was issued in 1956 to commemorate the Fifth International Philatelic Exhibition held in New York City. The engraved design depicts the New York Coliseum alongside the Statue of Liberty, symbolizing both international philately and American ideals. Printed in deep violet, the stamp combines clean architectural detail with strong engraving characteristic of Bureau production. This example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #1076 displays Very Fine centering, with the right margin establishing the centering limit and the left margin providing the greatest spacing. Faultless soundness results in no deduction, while intact perforations, fresh original gum, and strong color support excellent overall presentation. A modest eye appeal enhancement is warranted for freshness and color intensity. The final assigned grade is Very Fine 81, representing a premium-quality never hinged example of the 1956 FIPEX commemorative.', 0.17, 'LA01-0069-01-03-002'),
(301, 'United States', '1076', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '3¢ Deep Violet – Fifth International Philatelic Exhibition, 1956\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Perf 11 × 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1076 was issued in 1956 to commemorate the Fifth International Philatelic Exhibition held at the New York Coliseum. The engraved design depicts the exhibition hall with the Statue of Liberty at left, symbolizing the international nature of the event and America\'s role in organized philately. Printed in deep violet by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, the stamp combines architectural detail with a clean commemorative layout. This example remains visually attractive despite postal use.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #1076 displays Fine/Very Fine centering, with the right margin establishing the centering limit and the top margin providing the greatest spacing. A corner perforation crease and minor perforation irregularity place the stamp in the Very Minor Fault category and result in a preliminary Fine 70 grade. A heavy machine cancellation slightly reduces eye appeal and warrants a three-point deduction. The final assigned grade is Very Good/Fine 67, representing a sound and collectible used example of the 1956 FIPEX commemorative issue.', 0.10, 'NA01-0197-01-03-003'),
(302, 'United States', '1076', '004', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine/Very Fine', '3¢ Violet – Fifth International Philatelic Exhibition, 1956\r\n(Offset Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1076 commemorates the Fifth International Philatelic Exhibition held in New York City in 1956. The design depicts the New York Coliseum accompanied by a classical column symbolizing the connection between philatelic tradition and modern exhibition culture. Printed in violet by offset process, the stamp presents a clean architectural composition with strong visual balance. This example is mint, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #1076 displays Fine/Very Fine centering, with the right margin establishing the centering limit and the bottom margin providing the greatest spacing. A minor natural gum bend places the stamp in the Extremely Minor Fault category and reduces the preliminary grade to Fine 70. Strong color, original gum, and crisp printing support a positive eye appeal adjustment. The final assigned grade is Fine 72, representing an attractive mint never hinged example of this philatelic commemorative issue.', 0.17, 'SL01-0002-04-01-004'),
(303, 'United States', '1077', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '3¢ Red Brown – Wildlife Conservation, Wild Turkey, 1956\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Perf 11 × 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1077 was issued in 1956 as the first stamp in the Wildlife Conservation Series. The engraved design depicts a wild turkey in flight through a woodland setting, celebrating American wildlife restoration and conservation efforts. Printed in red brown, the stamp combines detailed engraving with a strong natural theme. This example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #1077 displays Fine/Very Fine centering, with the left margin establishing the centering limit and the right margin providing the greatest spacing. Faint reverse ink transfer and a minor natural gum bend place the stamp in the Very Minor Fault category and result in a preliminary Fine 70 grade. Strong color, crisp engraving, and fresh overall appearance support a modest positive eye appeal adjustment. The final assigned grade is Fine 71, representing an attractive never hinged example of the 1956 Wild Turkey commemorative.', 0.15, 'LA01-0070-01-01-002'),
(304, 'United States', '1079', '002', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine/Very Fine', '3¢ Blue Green – Wildlife Conservation, King Salmon, 1956\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Perf 11 × 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1079 was issued in 1956 as the final stamp in the Wildlife Conservation Series. The engraved design depicts a king salmon leaping from a rushing river against a forested backdrop, symbolizing conservation and the preservation of natural resources. Printed in blue green by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, the stamp combines strong engraving with an attractive wildlife theme. This example is unused, retains original gum, and has been previously hinged.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #1079 displays Very Fine centering, with the right margin establishing the centering limit and the left margin providing the greatest spacing. A light reverse ink transfer places the stamp in the Very Minor Fault category and results in a preliminary Fine/Very Fine 75 grade. Strong color, sharp engraving, and fresh overall appearance support a modest positive eye appeal adjustment. The final assigned grade is Fine/Very Fine 76, representing an attractive mint example of the King Salmon issue and the concluding stamp of the Wildlife Conservation Series.', 0.13, 'LA01-0070-01-03-002'),
(305, 'United States', '1082', '001', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '3¢ Deep Blue – Labor Day, 1956\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked Paper)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued September 3, 1956, Scott #1082 commemorates Labor Day and the dignity of American labor. Designed by Victor S. McCloskey Jr., the engraving portrays a standing laborer with tools and rope, behind a seated woman and child symbolizing knowledge and family. The quote “LABOR IS LIFE – CARLYLE” appears at lower left. Printed by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing in deep blue ink, this example is unused, never hinged, and retains full original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis unused, never hinged 3¢ Labor Day commemorative shows Fine/Very Fine centering with slightly narrow top and left margins yet clear separation from the design. Its gum is complete and bright, with intact perforations and vivid engraving. The reverse ink transfer and minor perf flaws reduce the soundness grade but not the overall appeal. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, this example achieves a final grade of 48 (Good/Very Good)—a collectible Bureau engraving retaining full gum and authentic production character.', 0.07, 'LA01-0070-03-02-001'),
(306, 'United States', '1084', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine/Very Fine', '3¢ Purple – 50th Anniversary of Devils Tower National Monument, 1956\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked Paper)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued September 24, 1956, Scott #1084 commemorates the 50th anniversary of Devils Tower National Monument in Wyoming—the first national monument established in the United States. Designed by Charles R. Chickering, the engraving shows the monolithic butte rising above pine forests beneath a billowing sky. The inscriptions “U.S. POSTAGE” and “50th ANNIVERSARY DEVILS TOWER NATIONAL MONUMENT” frame the design, with the denomination “3¢” prominently placed in the lower right. Printed by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing in a deep purple shade, this example is unused, never hinged, and retains full original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis unused, never hinged 3¢ Devils Tower commemorative displays Very Fine centering with narrow right and top margins yet full clearance from the design. Its gum is original and bright, and perforations are complete aside from one light tip crease. With vivid color, clean engraving, and balanced layout, it earns a final grade of 77 (Fine/Very Fine) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide—an attractive, sound example of this national monument issue.', 0.17, 'LA01-0070-03-03-002'),
(307, 'United States', '1085', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '3¢ Blue – Friendship, The Key to World Peace, 1956\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked Paper)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued December 15, 1956, Scott #1085, often called the “Children’s Stamp,” promotes peace and understanding through friendship. Designed by Victor S. McCloskey Jr., the engraving depicts children of diverse backgrounds gazing toward a radiant key symbolizing unity and hope. The inscriptions “FRIENDSHIP – THE KEY TO WORLD PEACE” and “CHILDREN’S STAMP 1956” appear above, with “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” and “3¢” below. Printed by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing in deep blue ink, this example is used, previously hinged, and bears a vertical machine cancel.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used, hinged 3¢ “Friendship – The Key to World Peace” commemorative displays Fine/Very Fine centering with slightly narrow right and bottom margins but full design separation. The stamp retains strong color and sound structure despite light staining, perf creases, and a heavy cancel. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, it earns a final grade of 44 (Good/Very Good)—a complete, collectible used example of this peace-themed mid-century issue.', 0.05, 'LA01-0070-02-03-002'),
(308, 'United States', '1086', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '3¢ Carmine Rose – Alexander Hamilton, 1957\r\n(Alexander Hamilton Bicentennial Issue – Engraved, Perf 11×10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1086 was issued in 1957 to commemorate the bicentennial of Alexander Hamilton’s birth. The engraved design features a portrait of Hamilton paired with Federal Hall in New York City, reflecting his role as the nation’s first Secretary of the Treasury. Printed in carmine rose by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, the stamp combines strong portraiture with architectural detail. This example remains visually appealing despite postal use.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #1086 displays Very Fine/Extremely Fine centering, with the right margin establishing the centering limit and the top margin providing the greatest spacing. Several perforation creases place the stamp in the Minor Fault category and reduce the preliminary grade to Fine 70. A heavy machine cancellation further reduces eye appeal and warrants a ten-point deduction. The final assigned grade is Very Good/Fine 60, representing a well-centered but visibly used example of this Alexander Hamilton Bicentennial commemorative.', 0.10, 'LA01-0070-04-01-002');
INSERT INTO `stamps` (`id`, `country`, `scott`, `count`, `condition`, `hinged`, `gum`, `grade`, `description`, `price`, `location`) VALUES
(309, 'United States', '1087', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Fine', '3¢ Purple – Honoring Those Who Helped Fight Polio, 1957\r\n(Polio Fighters Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Perf 11×10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1087 was issued in 1957 to honor the physicians, researchers, volunteers, and organizations that contributed to the fight against poliomyelitis. The engraved design depicts a symbolic figure of Health protecting two children beside a shield bearing a caduceus. Radiating lines in the background reinforce the theme of hope and recovery. Printed in purple by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, the stamp remains one of the more meaningful health-related commemoratives of the era. This example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #1087 displays Very Fine/Extremely Fine centering, with the top margin establishing the centering limit and the left margin providing the greatest spacing. A minor natural gum bend places the stamp in the Extremely Minor Fault category and reduces the preliminary grade to Very Fine 83. Strong color, intact perforations, original gum, and crisp engraving support excellent overall presentation. The final assigned grade is Very Fine 83, representing an attractive never hinged example of this 1957 Polio Fighters commemorative issue.', 0.20, 'LA01-0070-03-04-002'),
(310, 'United States', '1087', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair/Good', '3¢ Purple – Honoring Those Who Helped Fight Polio, 1957\r\n(Polio Fighters Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Perf 11×10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1087 was issued in 1957 to honor the physicians, researchers, volunteers, and public health workers who helped combat poliomyelitis. The engraved design depicts the allegorical figure of Health shielding two children beside a medical shield bearing the caduceus. Printed in purple by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, the stamp remains a significant tribute to one of the major public health victories of the twentieth century. This example is postally used and retains clear design detail despite wear.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #1087 displays Fine/Very Fine centering, with the bottom margin establishing the centering limit and the top margin providing the greatest spacing. Multiple faulty, clipped, shortened, and pulled perforations place the stamp in the Major Fault category and reduce the preliminary grade to Good 30. A heavy machine cancellation further reduces eye appeal and warrants a ten-point deduction. The final assigned grade is Fair/Good 20, representing a lower-grade but still collectible example of this historically significant Polio Fighters commemorative issue.', 0.02, 'NA01-0199-01-02-003'),
(312, 'United States', '1088', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Fine', '3¢ Deep Blue – U.S. Coast and Geodetic Survey, 1957\r\n(Coast and Geodetic Survey Sesquicentennial Issue – Engraved, Perf 11×10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1088 was issued in 1957 to commemorate the 150th anniversary of the U.S. Coast and Geodetic Survey. The engraved design features the agency’s flag above a group of ships, symbolizing the organization’s contributions to navigation, mapping, and scientific measurement. Radiating lines surrounding the central emblem emphasize precision and service. Printed in deep blue by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, the stamp presents a striking maritime-themed design. This example remains visually strong despite postal use.\r\n\r\nSummary:\r\nScott #1088 displays Extremely Fine/Superb centering, with the right margin establishing the centering limit and the bottom margin providing the greatest spacing. Partial gum residue and light reverse soiling place the stamp in the Minor Fault category and reduce the preliminary grade to Very Fine 80. A neat machine cancellation has no further grading impact. The final assigned grade is Very Fine 80, representing a well-centered and visually attractive example of this 1957 Coast and Geodetic Survey commemorative.', 0.15, 'LA01-0070-04-02-002'),
(313, 'United States', '1089', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '3¢ Lilac – American Institute of Architects Centennial, 1957\r\n(American Institute of Architects Centennial Issue – Engraved, Perf 11×10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1089 was issued in 1957 to commemorate the centennial of the American Institute of Architects. The engraved design contrasts a classical Corinthian column with a modern steel T-beam, symbolizing the connection between traditional and contemporary architectural design. Printed in lilac by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, the stamp reflects the clean lines and modern aesthetic common to mid-century commemoratives. This example remains readily identifiable despite postal use and condition issues.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #1089 displays Fine/Very Fine centering, with the right margin establishing the centering limit and the left margin providing the greatest spacing. Several perforation creases and heavy cancellation bleed-through place the stamp in the Minor Fault category and reduce the preliminary grade to Very Good 45. A heavy magenta machine cancellation further reduces eye appeal and warrants a five-point deduction. The final assigned grade is Good/Very Good 40, representing a collectible but visibly worn example of this 1957 American Institute of Architects Centennial commemorative.', 0.05, 'LA01-0070-04-03-002'),
(315, 'United States', '1089', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine/Very Fine', '3¢ Lilac – American Institute of Architects Centennial, 1957\r\n(American Institute of Architects Centennial Issue – Engraved, Perf 11×10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1089 was issued in 1957 to commemorate the centennial of the American Institute of Architects. The engraved design combines a classical Corinthian column with a modern steel T-beam, symbolizing the evolution of architecture from traditional forms to contemporary engineering. Printed in lilac by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, the issue reflects the clean lines and modern aesthetic of the period. This example remains attractive and well defined despite postal use.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #1089 displays Extremely Fine centering, with the right margin establishing the centering limit and the top margin providing the greatest spacing. Several perforation creases and light reverse spotting place the stamp in the Minor Fault category and reduce the preliminary grade to Fine/Very Fine 75. A clean machine cancellation has no further grading impact. The final assigned grade is Fine/Very Fine 75, representing a well-centered and visually attractive example of this 1957 American Institute of Architects Centennial commemorative issue.', 0.13, 'NA01-0199-02-01-003'),
(316, 'United States', '1090', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine/Very Fine', '3¢ Deep Blue – America and Steel Growing Together, 1957\r\n(Steel Industry Centennial Issue – Engraved, Perf 11×10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1090 was issued in 1957 to commemorate the centennial of the American steel industry. The engraved design depicts a soaring American eagle above a crucible pouring molten steel, symbolizing the relationship between industrial development and national strength. Printed in deep blue by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, the issue combines patriotic imagery with themes of manufacturing and economic growth. This example remains visually appealing and well preserved despite postal use.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #1090 displays Fine/Very Fine centering, with the left margin establishing the centering limit and the right margin providing the greatest spacing. Faultless soundness results in no deduction, while intact perforations, clean paper, and a neat machine cancellation preserve strong overall presentation. The cancellation has no further grading impact. The final assigned grade is Fine/Very Fine 77, representing an attractive and faultless used example of this 1957 Steel Industry Centennial commemorative issue.', 0.13, 'LA01-0071-02-01-002'),
(317, 'United States', '1091', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '3¢ Blue Green – International Naval Review, 1957\r\n(Jamestown Festival Commemorative – Engraved, Perf 11×10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1091 was issued in 1957 to commemorate the International Naval Review held during the Jamestown Festival celebration. The engraved design depicts a modern United States aircraft carrier with aircraft overhead, while an inset illustrates the Susan Constant, Godspeed, and Discovery, linking early colonial exploration with contemporary naval power. Printed in blue green by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, the stamp combines historical and maritime themes in a visually striking composition. This example remains attractive despite postal use.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #1091 displays Fine/Very Fine centering, with the right margin establishing the centering limit and the bottom margin providing the greatest spacing. Several perforation disc indents place the stamp in the Minor Fault category and reduce the preliminary grade to Very Good 55. A neat machine cancellation has no further grading impact. The final assigned grade is Very Good 55, representing a sound and collectible used example of this 1957 International Naval Review commemorative issue.', 0.08, 'LA01-0071-01-01-002'),
(318, 'United States', '1091', '003', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '3¢ Green – International Naval Review, 1957\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Offset Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1091 commemorates the 1957 International Naval Review held in conjunction with the Jamestown Festival. The design features a modern aircraft carrier with accompanying aircraft overhead, symbolizing mid-century naval strength and technological advancement. At left, a circular inset references the Jamestown Festival of 1607–1957, linking early American history with contemporary naval power. The inscription “INTERNATIONAL NAVAL REVIEW” dominates the top, with “U.S. POSTAGE 3¢” at the bottom. This submitted example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum with visible condition issues on the reverse.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis example of Scott #1091 shows respectable Fine/Very Fine centering with full margins, but multiple minor faults collectively reduce its grade to 43 (Good/Very Good). The toning, light crease, and irregular perforations weigh heavily on both soundness and eye appeal. Despite these issues, it remains a complete and identifiable example of a popular mid-century commemorative, and still holds value as a collectible under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.07, 'NA01-0199-02-02-003'),
(319, 'United States', '1092', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine/Very Fine', '3¢ Bright Blue – 50th Anniversary of Oklahoma Statehood, 1957\r\n(“Arrows to Atoms” Commemorative – Engraved, Perf 11×10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1092 was issued in 1957 to commemorate the fiftieth anniversary of Oklahoma statehood. The design illustrates the state motto, “Arrows to Atoms,” by combining a Native American arrow with an atomic orbital superimposed across the outline of Oklahoma. Printed in bright blue by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, the stamp reflects the optimism and technological aspirations of the atomic age. This example remains sharp and attractive despite postal use.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #1092 displays Fine/Very Fine centering, with the right margin establishing the centering limit and the left margin providing the greatest spacing. Faultless soundness results in no deduction, while intact perforations, clean paper, and a light machine cancellation preserve strong overall presentation. The cancellation has no further grading impact. The final assigned grade is Fine/Very Fine 79, representing a technically sound and visually appealing used example of this 1957 Oklahoma Statehood commemorative.', 0.13, 'LA01-0071-01-02-002'),
(320, 'United States', '1092', '003', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good', '3¢ Blue – Oklahoma Statehood 50th Anniversary, 1957\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Offset Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1092 commemorates the 50th anniversary of Oklahoma statehood, marking the transition from frontier history to modern scientific progress. The design features an arrow passing through an atomic symbol, with the inscription “1907 ARROWS TO ATOMS 1957” symbolizing technological advancement. The outline of Oklahoma appears at right with “50TH ANNIVERSARY OF OKLAHOMA STATEHOOD,” reinforcing the theme of growth and transformation. The stamp is rendered in blue using offset printing, typical of mid-century commemoratives. This example is unused, never hinged, with original gum present and showing minor handling characteristics.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis example of Scott #1092 offers solid Fine/Very Fine centering with full margins, but perforation irregularities reduce its overall grade to 55 (Very Good). The design remains clear and attractive, and the faults, while noticeable, do not obscure the stamp’s character. It remains a collectible example of a classic 1957 commemorative under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.10, 'NA01-0199-02-03-003'),
(321, 'United States', '1093', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '3¢ Purple – Honoring the Teachers of America, 1957\r\n(National Education Association Centennial Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued July 1, 1957, Scott #1093 honors the centennial of the National Education Association and celebrates the teaching profession. The design depicts a teacher with two students beside a classroom globe, symbolizing guidance and learning. Printed in a reddish-purple hue by rotary press, it reflects the optimism of 1950s education themes. The submitted example is postally used, previously hinged, and no longer retains its original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #1093 shows Fine/Very Fine centering with strong color and intact perforations. Minor perf creases, a hinge remnant, and a heavy cancel reduce the numerical grade to 51 (Very Good). Despite these moderate deductions, the stamp remains a solid, collectible example of the 1957 NEA Centennial issue under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.08, 'LA01-0071-01-03-003'),
(322, 'United States', '1094', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '4¢ Red and Blue – “Long May It Wave,” 1957\r\n(Liberty Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 × 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued July 4, 1957, Scott #1094 marks the beginning of the 4-cent domestic rate and celebrates American freedom. The design, by Charles R. Chickering, depicts the U.S. flag flying in full motion above the motto “LONG MAY IT WAVE.” Printed in carmine and ultramarine inks by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, the issue became one of the most recognizable symbols of the Liberty Series. The submitted example is postally used, never hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #1094 displays Fine/Very Fine centering with complete but uneven perforations. Short perforation tips and a heavy cancel reduce the final grade to 50 (Very Good). Even with these moderate flaws, the stamp remains sound, colorful, and historically significant as a collectible example of the 1957 “Long May It Wave” issue under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.08, 'LA01-0071-04-01-003'),
(323, 'United States', '1095', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '3¢ Violet – The Virginia of Sagadahock, Shipbuilding, 1957\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11×10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1957 for the 350th anniversary of America’s first shipbuilding at Popham Colony, Maine, Scott #1095 depicts The Virginia of Sagadahock beneath the Maine state seal. “1607 SHIPBUILDING 1957” appears above, with “UNITED STATES POSTAGE 3¢” at the base. The violet engraving, created by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, highlights early colonial craftsmanship and maritime heritage. The examined example is a used, hinged single without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used, hinged 3¢ Shipbuilding Industry stamp shows Fine/Very Fine centering and intact perforations but is affected by rough separation and a heavier machine cancel. The design remains legible and color fresh, resulting in a final grade of 51 (Very Good) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide—a collectible example with clear historical appeal despite moderate postal wear.', 0.08, 'LA01-0071-03-01-002'),
(324, 'United States', '1095', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair/Good', '3¢ Violet – The Virginia of Sagadahock, Shipbuilding, 1957\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11×10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued August 15, 1957, Scott #1095 honors the 350th anniversary of shipbuilding at Popham Colony, Maine. The design depicts The Virginia of Sagadahock beneath the Maine state seal and motto “DIRIGO,” engraved by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing. Printed in violet ink, the stamp commemorates early American industry and maritime craftsmanship. The examined example is used, hinged, and without original gum, showing moderate postal wear but complete design clarity.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used, hinged 3¢ Shipbuilding Industry commemorative shows Fine/Very Fine centering but suffers from staining and several perf creases, producing a Major Fault classification. The moderate cancel and visible discoloration further limit appeal, yielding a final grade of 27 (Fair/Good) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide—a worn but complete example that retains strong historical and design context.', 0.02, 'NA01-0199-03-02-003'),
(325, 'United States', '1096', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '8¢ Multicolor – Ramon Magsaysay, Champion of Liberty, 1957\r\n(Champion of Liberty Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11×10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1096 was issued on August 31, 1957, as the first stamp in the Champion of Liberty series. It honors Ramon Magsaysay, President of the Philippines from 1953 to 1957, whose leadership symbolized integrity and democratic reform. The design features a golden medallion portrait suspended from a red, white, and blue ribbon with a liberty torch and laurel at the top, representing unity and freedom. The inscriptions “CHAMPION OF LIBERTY” and “UNITED STATES POSTAGE 8¢” appear in red and blue, giving the design its patriotic tri-color appearance. The submitted example is unused, never hinged, and retains full original gum with excellent color and printing registration.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis unused, never hinged 8¢ Ramon Magsaysay, Champion of Liberty stamp shows Fine/Very Fine centering with intact perforations and fresh original gum. The faint reverse ink transfer slightly lowers soundness but does not affect visual quality. With vivid color, clear design, and no structural faults, it achieves a final grade of 71 (Fine) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide—a clean and well-balanced example from this historic series.', 0.15, 'LA01-0071-03-02-002'),
(326, 'United States', '1096', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '8¢ Multicolor – Ramon Magsaysay, Champion of Liberty, 1957\r\n(Champion of Liberty Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11×10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued August 31, 1957, Scott #1096 honors Ramon Magsaysay, President of the Philippines (1953–1957), a postwar symbol of democratic reform and integrity. The design, engraved by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, shows a gold medallion portrait of Magsaysay suspended beneath a red, white, and blue ribbon with a liberty torch and laurel branches above. “CHAMPION OF LIBERTY” appears at top, and “UNITED STATES POSTAGE 8¢” anchors the bottom. The examined example is used, never hinged, and without original gum, bearing a dark machine cancel across the portrait.\r\n\r\nSummary:\r\nThis used 8¢ Ramon Magsaysay, Champion of Liberty stamp shows Fine/Very Fine centering but bears several small faults and a heavy machine cancel. Despite wear, the colors remain strong, and the design complete. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, it earns a final grade of 37 (Good)—a representative postally used example from this important commemorative series.', 0.04, 'NA01-0205-01-03-003'),
(327, 'United States', '1096', '004', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '8¢ Multicolored – Ramon Magsaysay, Champion of Liberty, 1957\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Offset Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1096 honors Ramon Magsaysay, President of the Philippines, recognizing him as a “Champion of Liberty.” The design features a central medallion portrait of Magsaysay, surrounded by the inscription “1953 RAMON MAGSAYSAY 1957 PRESIDENT OF THE PHILIPPINES.” Above, a torch and laurel branches symbolize freedom and honor, with red and blue ribbons reinforcing the theme of international alliance and democratic ideals. “UNITED STATES POSTAGE 8¢” appears at the bottom. Issued in 1957, the stamp reflects Cold War–era diplomacy and tribute. This example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum with minor reverse irregularities.\r\n\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis example of Scott #1096 offers solid Fine/Very Fine centering and an attractive, clean front appearance, resulting in a final grade of 74 (Fine). Minor reverse issues limit its technical grade, but they do not significantly detract from display quality. It remains a desirable and collectible example of this Cold War–era commemorative under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.15, 'SL01-0002-01-02-004'),
(328, 'United States', '1097', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine/Very Fine', '3¢ Rose Violet – Marquis de Lafayette, 1957\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11×10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued September 6, 1957, Scott #1097 honors the bicentennial of the Marquis de Lafayette, the French general who aided the American Revolution. The Bureau of Engraving and Printing produced this rose-violet design depicting Lafayette in uniform, holding a document, with “1757 La Fayette 1957” above and “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” below. The ornate frame and “R.F.” emblem symbolize Franco-American unity. This example is used, never hinged, with no original gum and full original detail.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used 3¢ Marquis de Lafayette commemorative shows Very Fine to Extremely Fine centering, intact perforations, and bright rose-violet color. Minor back smudging and a moderate cancel slightly reduce appeal. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, it earns a final grade of 76 (Fine/Very Fine)—a well-centered, historically significant issue showing excellent engraving and preservation.', 0.13, 'LA01-0071-02-02-002'),
(329, 'United States', '1097', '003', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine/Very Fine', '3¢ Carmine Rose – Marquis de Lafayette, 1957\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Offset Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1097 commemorates the bicentennial of the birth of the Marquis de Lafayette, a key figure in the American Revolutionary War and a symbol of Franco-American friendship. The design features a portrait of Lafayette in military attire, framed by decorative elements and inscribed “1757 La Fayette 1957.” The initials “R.F.” appear above, referencing the French Republic, reinforcing the international significance of the subject. The engraving-style offset printing gives the stamp a classic, dignified appearance. This example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum with minor reverse imperfections.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis example of Scott #1097 offers solid Fine/Very Fine centering and a pleasing front appearance, resulting in a final grade of 75 (Fine/Very Fine). Minor reverse imperfections limit its technical grade slightly, but they do not detract from its display quality. It remains a collectible and attractive example of this classic commemorative under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.17, 'NA01-0199-04-01-003'),
(330, 'United States', '1098', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '3¢ Blue, Green & Brown – Whooping Cranes, 1957\r\n(Wildlife Conservation Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued October 22, 1957, Scott #1097 opened the “Wildlife Conservation” series. Designed by Arthur Szyk and engraved by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, it depicts a pair of adult whooping cranes with two chicks along a marsh, symbolizing the protection of endangered species. The color scheme of blue, brown, and green conveys both natural vibrancy and ecological significance. Inscriptions include “WILDLIFE CONSERVATION,” “WHOOPING CRANES,” and “U.S. POSTAGE 3¢.” The submitted example is postally used, has no original gum, and was never hinged.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used example of Scott #1098, the 1957 3¢ “Whooping Cranes” issue, shows Fine/Very Fine centering and faultless structure apart from one tiny perf tip crease. The color remains strong and the engraving sharp. Though the cancel is heavy, the stamp retains freshness and balance. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, it earns a final grade of 62 (Very Good/Fine)—a solid, collectible used Wildlife Conservation issue with pleasing visual integrity.', 0.10, 'LA01-0071-02-03-002'),
(331, 'United States', '1098', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '3¢ Blue, Green & Brown – Whooping Cranes, 1957\r\n(Wildlife Conservation Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued October 22, 1957, Scott #1098 opened the “Wildlife Conservation” series. Designed by Arthur Szyk and engraved by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, it depicts two adult whooping cranes with their chicks along a marsh, symbolizing species protection. The blue, brown, and green tricolor engraving highlights natural tones of sky and grass. The inscriptions read “WILDLIFE CONSERVATION,” “WHOOPING CRANES,” and “U.S. POSTAGE 3¢.” The submitted example is postally used, with no original gum, and was never hinged.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used 1957 3¢ “Whooping Cranes” issue shows Fine/Very Fine centering with bright color and strong impression. The single corner crease and heavy cancel slightly reduce the numeric grade but do not compromise overall integrity. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the stamp earns 62 (Very Good/Fine)—a sound, attractive Wildlife Conservation example suitable for any used collection.', 0.10, 'NA01-0199-04-02-003'),
(332, 'United States', '1099', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair', '3¢ Black – Religious Freedom in America, 1957\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued December 27, 1957, Scott #1099 marks the 300th anniversary of the Flushing Remonstrance of 1657, an early defense of religious liberty in colonial America. Designed by Charles R. Chickering and engraved by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, it depicts a Puritan hat, a quill pen, and a closed book—symbols of faith, learning, and written protest. The scroll inscribed “THE FLUSHING REMONSTRANCE” links the dates “1657–1957.” The presented example is postally used, never hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used 1957 3¢ “Religious Freedom in America” issue shows Very Fine centering but major condition faults. A strong horizontal crease, multiple perf irregularities, and a heavy cancel limit its standing. Still, the design remains legible and centered, offering appeal as a representative used example. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, it receives a final grade of 15 (Fair)—a structurally poor but visually recognizable stamp for study or low-grade display.', 0.02, 'LA01-0071-02-04-003'),
(333, 'United States', '1100', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '3¢ Green – Gardening and Horticulture, 1958\r\n(American Gardens Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1958, this 3¢ commemorative celebrates American gardening and horticulture. Designed by Ervine Metzl and engraved by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, it shows a classical female figure symbolizing abundance, holding a cornucopia overflowing with flowers and produce. “GARDENING” appears above, “HORTICULTURE” down the right, and “U.S. POSTAGE 3¢” below. Printed in deep green by rotary press, this example is unused, never hinged, and retains full original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis unused, never hinged Gardening and Horticulture commemorative shows Fine/Very Fine centering, vivid color, and intact perforations. A light perf-tip crease and minor gum bend produce a Very Minor Fault deduction, resulting in a final numerical grade of 72 (Fine). It remains an attractive, collectible 1950s commemorative, fully conforming to PSE 2009 grading standards.', 0.15, 'LA01-0072-01-02-002'),
(334, 'United States', '1100', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair/Good', '3¢ Green – Gardening and Horticulture, 1958\r\n(American Gardens Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1958, this 3¢ commemorative honors America’s gardening and horticultural heritage. Designed by Ervine Metzl and engraved by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, it portrays a classical female figure carrying a cornucopia overflowing with flowers and fruit. “GARDENING” appears at the top, “HORTICULTURE” vertically along the right, and “U.S. POSTAGE 3¢” at the base. Printed in deep green ink by rotary press, this example is used, previously hinged, and lacks original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Gardening and Horticulture commemorative shows Fine/Very Fine centering but carries severe structural faults, including heavy creasing and a prominent stain. Despite these issues, its cancel is light and the design remains identifiable. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, these combined conditions result in a final numerical grade of 21 (Fair/Good)—a visually serviceable but heavily faulted example appropriate for reference or filler use.', 0.02, 'NA01-0201-02-02-003'),
(335, 'United States', '1100', '004', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '3¢ Green – Gardening and Horticulture, 1958\r\n(American Gardens Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1958, the 3¢ Gardening and Horticulture stamp was created to honor America’s tradition of cultivation and landscape beauty. Designed by Ervine Metzl and engraved by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, it features a classical female figure personifying abundance and growth, holding a cornucopia brimming with flowers and fruit. The inscriptions “GARDENING” and “HORTICULTURE” frame the top and right sides, while “U.S. POSTAGE 3¢” appears at the bottom. This example is postally used, lightly hinged, and shows no remaining original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Gardening and Horticulture commemorative exhibits Fine/Very Fine centering but suffers from several short and fuzzy perforations and a heavier cancel, resulting in a Minor Fault deduction. Though downgraded to a final grade of 54 (Very Good), it retains clear design detail and strong color, making it a presentable used example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.08, 'SL01-0001-01-03-004'),
(336, 'United States', '1104', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '3¢ Red Violet – Brussels Universal and International Exhibition, 1958\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1958 to mark the Brussels Universal and International Exhibition, this 3¢ commemorative features the United States Pavilion, a symbol of modern design and American innovation. Designed and engraved by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, the aerial composition shows the circular pavilion surrounded by landscaped paths. “BRUSSELS” appears vertically at left, “UNIVERSAL AND INTERNATIONAL EXHIBITION 1958” across the top, and “U.S. POSTAGE 3¢” below. This example is postally used, previously hinged, and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used 3¢ Brussels Exhibition commemorative displays Fine/Very Fine centering, full perforations, and vivid color. Heavy cancel bleed-through and a perf-tip crease result in a Minor Fault classification and final grade of 53 (Very Good). Though downgraded by its cancel, the design and condition remain collectible under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.08, 'LA01-0071-04-02-002'),
(337, 'United States', '1104', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '3¢ Red Violet – Brussels Universal and International Exhibition, 1958\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1958, the Brussels Universal and International Exhibition stamp commemorates the United States’ participation in the postwar world’s fair in Belgium. Designed and engraved by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, the 3¢ red violet issue showcases an aerial view of the circular United States Pavilion surrounded by landscaped grounds, symbolizing America’s modern design and global outlook. “BRUSSELS” runs vertically along the left, while “UNIVERSAL AND INTERNATIONAL EXHIBITION 1958” spans the top, and “U.S. POSTAGE 3¢” anchors the bottom. The submitted example is postally used, never hinged, and has no remaining original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used 3¢ Brussels Exhibition commemorative shows Fine/Very Fine centering, crisp printing, and an even cancel. A light crease results in a Minor Fault deduction, reducing the technical grade to 58 (Very Good). Despite this, it remains a visually appealing and structurally sound representative of the 1958 commemorative issue under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.08, 'NA01-0201-01-01-003'),
(338, 'United States', '1105', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '3¢ Violet – James Monroe, 1958\r\n(Liberty Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued April 28, 1958, the 3¢ James Monroe commemorative honors the 200th anniversary of Monroe’s birth. Designed by Charles R. Chickering and engraved by Richard M. Bower and George A. Payne, it presents Monroe’s right-facing portrait framed by “U.S.” at left and the dual years “1758–1958.” The denomination sits within a braided oval at the upper left, symbolizing continuity and national unity. This example is a used single with no original gum, previously hinged.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used 3¢ James Monroe stamp exhibits Fine/Very Fine centering and strong color but shows multiple physical defects including a small thin, creases, and rough perforations. The cancellation is moderate and does not impair the portrait. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the resulting final grade is 35 (Good/Very Good)—a visually balanced, service-used example representing the Liberty Series with acceptable appearance and collectible character.', 0.04, 'LA01-0071-03-03-002'),
(339, 'United States', '1106', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '3¢ Green – Minnesota Statehood, 1958\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued May 11, 1958, this 3¢ commemorative marks the 100th anniversary of Minnesota’s admission to the Union. Designed by Leonard Everett Fisher, it features a northern landscape of Lake Itasca, the source of the Mississippi River, framed by pine branches and forests. “MINNESOTA STATEHOOD 1858–1958” spans the top, while the denomination appears in an oval medallion at the lower right. The presented example is a used single, never hinged, and lacking original gum as expected from postally used material.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used 3¢ Minnesota Statehood commemorative shows Very Fine/Extremely Fine centering with intact perforations and light machine cancel. Minor soundness flaws—partial gum retention and perf creases—reduce the grade, but its color and clarity remain strong. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, this example receives a final grade of 57 (Very Good/Fine), representing a well-centered and visually appealing used example of this 1958 issue.', 0.08, 'LA01-0071-04-03-002'),
(340, 'United States', '1107', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '3¢ Black and Orange Red – International Geophysical Year, 1958\r\n(Commemorative – Rotary Press Intaglio, Perf 11×10½)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued May 31 1958, Scott #1107 commemorates the International Geophysical Year (1957–58), a worldwide scientific effort to study Earth and space. Designed by Ervine Metzl and engraved by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, the stamp shows two outstretched hands inspired by Michelangelo’s Creation of Adam, symbolizing human cooperation and discovery. Beneath them, orange volcanic energy suggests the forces of nature explored through science. “In ternational Geophysical Year 1957–58” appears at top with “U.S. Postage 3¢” below. The submitted example is postally used, previously hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #1107 International Geophysical Year stamp exhibits Fine/Very Fine centering with intact perforations and crisp engraving. A single extremely minor perf fault and a heavy cancel lower its score, yet it remains fresh, well-balanced, and collectible. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the stamp earns a final grade of 68 (Very Good–Fine VG-F) – a sound, attractive example of this 1958 commemorative.', 0.10, 'LA01-0072-01-01-002'),
(341, 'United States', '1108', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '3¢ Green – Gunston Hall, 1958\r\n(Commemorative – Rotary Press Intaglio, Perf 11×10½)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued May 7 1958, Scott #1108 honors the bicentennial of Gunston Hall, the Virginia home of George Mason, author of the Virginia Declaration of Rights. Designed by Charles R. Chickering and engraved by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, the image shows Mason’s Georgian mansion framed by trees with crisp architectural precision. The top inscription reads “1758–1958 GUNSTON HALL,” with “HOME OF GEORGE MASON” at left and “UNITED STATES POSTAGE 3¢” below. The submitted example is postally used, never hinged, and has no remaining gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #1108 Gunston Hall shows Fine/Very Fine centering, rich color, and full perforations. Perf creases and a heavy machine cancel reduce its score, yet the stamp remains sound and collectible. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, it earns a final grade of 52 (Very Good VG)—a representative used example of this 1958 commemorative issue.', 0.08, 'LA01-0072-01-04-002'),
(342, 'United States', '1109', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '3¢ Blue – The Mackinac Bridge, 1958\r\n(Commemorative – Rotary Press Intaglio, Perf 11×10½)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued June 25 1958, Scott #1109 marks the completion of the Mackinac Bridge, linking Michigan’s Upper and Lower Peninsulas. Designed by Ervine Metzl and engraved by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, the design shows a low-angle view of the suspension bridge stretching into the distance, symbolizing engineering progress. “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” appears at top, “3¢” at right, and “THE MACKINAC BRIDGE – CONNECTING THE PENINSULAS OF MICHIGAN” across the base. The submitted example is postally used, never hinged, and lacks original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #1109 Mackinac Bridge stamp shows Fine/Very Fine centering, intact perforations, and strong color. The heavy purple cancel and light ink bleed-through reduce its numeric grade but not its collectability. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, it receives a final grade of 54 (Very Good VG) – a sound, visually stable, and representative used example of this 1958 commemorative.', 0.08, 'LA01-0072-01-03-002'),
(343, 'United States', '1109', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '3¢ Blue – The Mackinac Bridge, 1958\r\n(Commemorative – Rotary Press Intaglio, Perf 11×10½)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued June 25, 1958, Scott #1109 commemorates the completion of the Mackinac Bridge, the suspension link connecting Michigan’s Upper and Lower Peninsulas. Designed by Ervine Metzl and engraved by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, the design presents a sweeping upward view of the bridge with a freighter below, symbolizing mid-century progress. “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” appears at top, “3¢” at right, and “THE MACKINAC BRIDGE – CONNECTING THE PENINSULAS OF MICHIGAN” along the base. The submitted example is postally used, never hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #1109 Mackinac Bridge shows Fine/Very Fine centering, complete perforations, and fresh color. Several perf creases and a heavy cancel lower the score but not its structural integrity. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, it earns a final grade of 53 (Very Good VG)—a sound, collectible example of this 1958 commemorative.', 0.08, 'NA01-0201-02-03-003'),
(344, 'United States', '1110', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '4¢ Brown – Simón Bolívar, 1958\r\n(Champion of Liberty Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 × 10½, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued July 24, 1958, this 4¢ brown stamp honors Simón Bolívar, the South American “Liberator” who led the fight for independence in multiple nations. Designed by Charles R. Chickering and engraved by M. D. Fenton, it depicts Bolívar within a medallion framed by the inscription “1783 SIMON BOLIVAR – THE LIBERATOR 1830.” A torch and laurel symbolize freedom under the legend “CHAMPION OF LIBERTY.” Printed by rotary press on white wove paper, this stamp is part of the definitive Champion of Liberty series. The submitted example is postally used with visible wear.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used 4¢ Simón Bolívar stamp shows Fine/Very Fine centering with the top margin narrowest and left widest. A heavy crease and multiple perf creases create a Major Fault deduction, lowering the grade to Good. Still, the stamp retains clear design details, genuine perforations, and consistent color, offering a representative example of the Champion of Liberty issue under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide with a final numerical grade of 34 (Good).', 0.04, 'LA01-0072-02-01-003'),
(345, 'United States', '1110', '004', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '4¢ Brown – Simon Bolivar, Champion of Liberty, 1958\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Offset Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1110 honors Simón Bolívar, a central figure in the independence movements of South America, recognized here as a “Champion of Liberty.” The design features a central medallion portrait of Bolívar, with the inscription “SIMON BOLIVAR THE LIBERATOR 1830” encircling the image. Above, a torch and laurel branches symbolize freedom and victory, consistent with the Champion of Liberty series theme. The denomination and “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” appear below. Issued in 1958, the stamp reflects Cold War–era recognition of democratic ideals abroad. This example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum with minor handling characteristics.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis example of Scott #1110 offers solid Fine/Very Fine centering with a generally clean appearance, resulting in a final grade of 73 (Fine). The minor perf tip crease limits its technical standing, but the stamp retains good visual appeal and remains a collectible example of this historically significant issue under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.15, 'NA01-0205-01-01-004'),
(346, 'United States', '1111', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine', '8¢ Multicolor (Red, Blue, and Brown) – Simón Bolívar, 1958\r\n(Champion of Liberty Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 × 10½, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued July 24, 1958, the 8¢ Simón Bolívar stamp honors the South American “Liberator,” designed by Charles R. Chickering and engraved by M. D. Fenton. Bolívar’s portrait appears within a brown medallion inscribed “1783 SIMON BOLIVAR – THE LIBERATOR 1830,” surmounted by a torch and laurel branches symbolizing liberty. “CHAMPION OF LIBERTY” and “UNITED STATES POSTAGE 8¢” frame the design in red and blue. Printed by rotary press on white wove paper, this example is postally used with no original gum and shows a violet handstamp cancel.\r\n\r\nSummary:\r\nThis used 8¢ Simón Bolívar stamp displays Fine/Very Fine centering with balanced margins and full perforations. A light cancel bleed-through constitutes a Very Minor Fault, reducing the technical score slightly. Strong color and an unobtrusive cancel maintain its eye appeal. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, its final numerical grade is 72 (Fine), a solid, attractive Champion of Liberty example.', 0.11, 'LA01-0072-02-02-002'),
(347, 'United States', '1111', '003', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '8¢ Multicolored – Simon Bolivar, Champion of Liberty, 1958\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Offset Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1111 is part of the Champion of Liberty series and honors Simón Bolívar, a leading figure in South American independence. The design features a central medallion portrait of Bolívar with the inscription “SIMON BOLIVAR – THE LIBERATOR 1830.” Above, a torch and laurel branches symbolize liberty and victory, accented by red and blue ribbons. The denomination and “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” appear below. Issued in 1958, the stamp reflects mid-century themes of international freedom and democratic ideals. This example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum with only minor production-related characteristics.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis example of Scott #1111 presents solid Fine/Very Fine centering and a clean, attractive front, resulting in a final grade of 76 (Fine/Very Fine). The only detractor is a minor gum bend, which has minimal impact on display quality. Overall, it remains a desirable and collectible example of this Champion of Liberty issue under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.17, 'NA01-0205-01-02-003'),
(348, 'United States', '1112', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '4¢ Violet – Atlantic Cable Centenary, 1958\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 × 10½, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued August 15, 1958, this 4¢ violet commemorative honors the 100th anniversary of the first successful transatlantic telegraph cable. Designed by George Olden and engraved by Charles A. Brooks, the stamp shows a stylized globe crossed by an equatorial line symbolizing the cable that united continents. Neptune, god of the sea, appears on the left holding a trident, while Mercury, messenger of the gods, appears on the right to represent communication. “ATLANTIC CABLE CENTENARY 1858–1958” spans the top, with “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” and the 4¢ denomination below. The submitted example is postally used and retains strong violet color.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used 4¢ Atlantic Cable Centenary stamp displays Fine/Very Fine centering, with the bottom margin narrowest and top widest. A heavy magenta cancel causes minor reverse bleed-through, resulting in a final grade of 58 (Very Good–Fine). Despite the fault, the stamp maintains strong color, clear impression, and full perforations, making it a presentable and collectible 1958 commemorative under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.08, 'LA01-0073-01-01-002'),
(349, 'United States', '1112', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '4¢ Violet – Atlantic Cable Centenary, 1958\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 × 10½, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued August 15, 1958, this 4¢ violet commemorative honors the centennial of the first successful transatlantic telegraph cable connecting Europe and North America. Designed by George Olden and engraved by Charles A. Brooks, it features a central globe crossed by a fine line symbolizing the cable, flanked by Neptune, god of the sea, with his trident to the left, and Mercury, messenger of the gods, to the right. The upper inscription reads “ATLANTIC CABLE CENTENARY 1858–1958,” with “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” and the denomination below. The submitted example is postally used and printed in a deep violet hue typical of the issue.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used 4¢ Atlantic Cable Centenary stamp displays Fine/Very Fine centering with full design visibility and intact perforations. Two small tears reduce its soundness to a Major Fault, resulting in a final grade of 33 (Good). While the defects lower technical standing, color strength and legibility preserve it as a representative example of this 1958 commemorative under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.04, 'NA01-0201-03-01-003');
INSERT INTO `stamps` (`id`, `country`, `scott`, `count`, `condition`, `hinged`, `gum`, `grade`, `description`, `price`, `location`) VALUES
(350, 'United States', '1113', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '1¢ Green – Abraham Lincoln, 1959\r\n(Regular Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1113 features a detailed portrait of Abraham Lincoln, rendered in green with a finely engraved appearance typical of late 1950s definitive issues. The inscription “Lincoln” appears in script at the top, while “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” is printed along the bottom with the 1¢ denomination at right. The design emphasizes clarity and dignity, reflecting Lincoln’s enduring legacy. Issued in 1959, this stamp was part of a widely used definitive series. This example is used, without original gum as expected, and shows minor physical irregularities.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis example of Scott #1113 exhibits strong Fine/Very Fine centering and an attractive, clean front with a light cancel, resulting in a final grade of 62 (Very Good-Fine). While minor perforation irregularities reduce the technical grade, the stamp maintains solid visual appeal and remains a collectible and desirable example of this classic Lincoln definitive under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.10, 'LA01-0072-03-01-003'),
(351, 'United States', '1116', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '4¢ Blue – Abraham Lincoln (Seated Statue), 1960\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1960 as a commemorative, Scott #1116 depicts the seated statue of Abraham Lincoln from the Lincoln Memorial. The design uses heavy blue engraving and deep shadows to emphasize Lincoln’s expression and posture. “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” appears vertically on the left, accompanied by a bold “4¢,” while Lincoln’s engraved signature floats against a textured background on the right.\r\nThe submitted example is postally used, has no original gum, and was never hinged. Its impression and color remain strong, with the design fully intact.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used example of Scott #1116 displays Fine/Very Fine centering, intact perforations, and a desirable light cancel. The staining—classified as a Major Fault—determines the soundness category and limits the numerical grade. With a final grade of 33 (Good), the stamp remains collectible as a used commemorative while reflecting the condition standards of the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.04, 'LA01-0072-04-02-003'),
(352, 'United States', '1117', '003', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine/Very Fine', '4¢ Green – Lajos Kossuth, Champion of Liberty, 1958\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Offset Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1117 honors Lajos Kossuth, Hungarian patriot and leader of the 1848 revolution, recognized in the Champion of Liberty series. The design features a central medallion portrait of Kossuth with the inscription “LAJOS KOSSUTH – GOVERNOR OF HUNGARY 1848–1849.” Above, a torch and laurel branches symbolize liberty and victory, consistent with the thematic elements of the series. The denomination and “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” appear below. Issued in 1958, the stamp reflects Cold War–era recognition of global democratic movements. This example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum with minor natural characteristics.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis example of Scott #1117 presents solid Fine/Very Fine centering with a clean and attractive front, resulting in a final grade of 76 (Fine/Very Fine). The only detractor is a minor gum bend, which has minimal impact on overall appearance. It remains a desirable and collectible example of this Champion of Liberty issue under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.17, 'LA01-0072-02-03-003'),
(353, 'United States', '1118', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '8¢ Multicolor – Lajos Kossuth, Champion of Liberty Series, 1958\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved & Typography, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1118 was issued in 1958 as part of the Champion of Liberty Series, honoring Lajos Kossuth, the Hungarian statesman instrumental in the 1848–1849 revolution. The design features a gold-toned engraved medallion portrait flanked by the dates “1848” and “1849,” with a stylized liberty torch and red-and-blue ribbon elements crowning the top. “CHAMPION OF LIBERTY” sits prominently above the portrait, the denomination “8¢” appears at lower right, and “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” anchors the lower border.\r\nThe submitted example is unused, never hinged, with full original gum. The printing remains crisp and the color bright.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis unused NH example of Scott #1118 shows Fine/Very Fine centering, clean perforations, and vivid color. With only a single Very Minor Fault, the stamp grades 72 (Fine) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide while maintaining strong collector appeal for the Champion of Liberty series.', 0.15, 'LA01-0072-02-04-002'),
(354, 'United States', '1118', '004', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good', '8¢ Multicolored – Lajos Kossuth, Champion of Liberty, 1958\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Offset Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1118 is part of the Champion of Liberty series and honors Lajos Kossuth, leader of Hungary’s 1848 independence movement. The design features a central medallion portrait of Kossuth with the inscription “LAJOS KOSSUTH – GOVERNOR OF HUNGARY 1848–1849.” Above, a torch and laurel branches symbolize liberty and victory, accented by red and blue ribbons reflecting international democratic ideals. “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” and the 8¢ denomination appear below. Issued in 1958, the stamp reflects mid-century U.S. recognition of global freedom movements. This example is unused, never hinged, with original gum showing visible condition issues on the reverse.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis example of Scott #1118 shows solid Fine/Very Fine centering with a clear and attractive front design, but the presence of toning and additional minor faults reduces the final grade to 55 (Very Good). While still structurally complete and visually identifiable, the condition issues limit its desirability for higher-end collectors. It remains a collectible mid-grade example of this Champion of Liberty issue under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.10, 'NA01-0205-01-05-004'),
(355, 'United States', '1126', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '8¢ José de San Martín, Champion of Liberty Series, 1958\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 11×10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1958, Scott #1126 forms part of the multiyear Champion of Liberty series honoring global leaders associated with independence and democratic ideals. This stamp commemorates José de San Martín (1778–1850), general and statesman instrumental in liberating Argentina, Chile, and Peru. The design features an engraved golden medallion portrait encircled by the inscription “HERO OF THE ANDES,” surmounted by a stylized liberty torch with radiating beams and laurel branches in red and blue. The submitted example is unused, never hinged, and retains its original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis unused Scott #1126 receives a final grade of 47 (Good/Very Good), reflecting strong centering for the issue but a notable deduction for the back stain. Despite its technical limitations, the stamp retains clean, sharp engraving and full original gum, making it a presentable and collectible example of the Champion of Liberty series.', 0.07, 'LA01-0073-03-03-002'),
(356, 'United States', '1126', '003', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '8¢ Multicolored – José de San Martín, 1960\r\n(Champion of Liberty Issue – Offset Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1126 honors José de San Martín, one of the principal leaders of South American independence, and continues the Champion of Liberty series. The design features a circular medallion portrait of San Martín with the inscription “JOSE DE SAN MARTIN 1778–1850 HERO OF THE ANDES,” framed above by the series torch, laurel branches, and ribbon motif. The 8¢ denomination appears at lower right, with “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” at the bottom. Issued in 1960, the stamp reflects the Cold War era practice of recognizing international figures associated with liberty and republican ideals. This submitted example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum with minor reverse irregularities.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis example of Scott #1126 combines Fine-Very Fine centering with a pleasing front appearance and strong overall freshness, resulting in a final grade of 74 (Fine). The reverse ink transfer and minor natural gum bend keep it just below the Fine-Very Fine range, but neither significantly detracts from its display quality. It remains a collectible and attractive example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.15, 'NA01-0205-02-02-003'),
(357, 'United States', '1127', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '4¢ Blue – NATO, 1959\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1127 commemorates the tenth anniversary of the North Atlantic Treaty Organization, issued in 1959 during the height of Cold War alliances. The stamp features the NATO four-point compass emblem within a deep blue circular field, symbolizing shared defense and collective direction among member nations. The inscriptions “UNITED FOR FREEDOM,” “1949-1959,” and the denomination “4c” reinforce the central theme of unity and strength. Engraved production provides crisp lines and strong visual presence. The submitted example is postally used, has never been hinged, and retains no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary:\r\nThis used example of Scott #1127 offers above-average centering, intact perforations, and strong engraved detail. The heavy magenta cancel and its bleed-through reduce the numerical grade, but the stamp remains an attractive and collectible VF/XF-centered used example, final grade 64 (VG-F) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.10, 'LA01-0074-02-01-002'),
(358, 'United States', '1128', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine', '4¢ Blue – Arctic Explorations, 1959\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1128 was issued in 1959 to celebrate two milestones in Arctic exploration: Robert Peary’s 1909 North Pole expedition and the 1959 Arctic under-ice transit of the USS Skate (SSN-578). The design places Peary with his dog team above a polar projection map and the submarine Skate cutting through the ice below, uniting classic exploration with modern technological achievement. Engraved printing produces crisp tonal contrast across both scenes.\r\nThe submitted stamp is postally used, never hinged before mailing, and retains no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary:\r\nThis used example of Scott #1128 offers strong VF/XF centering, crisp engraved detail, and attractive face presentation. Although perf creases and slight cancel bleed-through reduce the technical grade, the stamp remains solidly collectible. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 72 (Fine).', 0.11, 'LA01-0073-03-01-002'),
(359, 'United States', '1128', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '4¢ Blue – Arctic Explorations, 1959\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1959, Scott #1128 commemorates two major Arctic milestones: the 1909 North Pole expedition led by Robert Peary and the 1959 under-ice Arctic patrol of the nuclear submarine USS Skate (SSN-578). The design balances historical exploration with modern technological achievement, depicting Peary and his dog team above a polar projection and the Skate navigating icy waters below. Engraved production provides sharp lines and controlled shading throughout the stamp.\r\nThe submitted example is postally used, never hinged prior to use, and carries no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary:\r\nThis used example of Scott #1128 combines Fine/Very Fine centering with a clean, well-impressed design. Though the back smudging and small perf crease reduce the technical grade, the stamp retains solid collector appeal. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, it earns a final grade of 59 (Very Good).', 0.08, 'NA01-0203-02-01-003'),
(360, 'United States', '1131', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair/Good', '4¢ Blue & Red – St. Lawrence Seaway, 1959\r\n(Joint U.S.–Canada Issue – Engraved & Typographed, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1959 to celebrate the opening of the St. Lawrence Seaway, this joint U.S.–Canada commemorative unites the maple leaf and the American eagle in two interlocked rings placed over a simplified map of the Great Lakes. The design reflects the deep cooperation behind the Seaway project, which reshaped inland trade for both nations. The red inscriptions above and below frame the blue central artwork, a hallmark of mid-century U.S. commemoratives.\r\nThe submitted example is postally used, previously hinged, and retains no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used example of Scott #1131 exhibits Fine/Very Fine centering but is substantially affected by perforation damage and multiple perf creases. A heavy cancel further reduces aesthetic strength. Even so, the design remains legible and the stamp’s centering provides some visual stability. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade of 25 (Fair/Good) accurately reflects its faults while still recognizing its collectible historical context.', 0.02, 'LA01-0073-04-02-002'),
(361, 'United States', '1131', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '4¢ Blue & Red – St. Lawrence Seaway, 1959\r\n(Joint U.S.–Canada Issue – Engraved & Typographed, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nReleased in 1959 as a joint commemorative with Canada, Scott #1131 honors the completion of the St. Lawrence Seaway, a milestone that opened the Great Lakes to ocean-going vessels. The bold red inscriptions frame a deep blue map of the Seaway, upon which two interlinked rings hold the maple leaf and the American eagle—an emblematic pairing of the two nations’ partnership. Engraved and typographed, the design exhibits crisp delineation and a pleasing visual rhythm typical of mid-century commemoratives.\r\nThe submitted example is postally used, never hinged, and retains no original gum, consistent with postal handling and removal.\r\n\r\nSummary:\r\nThis used Scott #1131 offers Fine/Very Fine centering and bright, original color, offset by perf creasing and minor smudging on the reverse. The unobtrusive cancel preserves design clarity, supporting a final grade of 59 (Very Good–Fine) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.08, 'NA01-0203-03-01-003'),
(362, 'United States', '1133', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '4¢ Multicolor – Soil Conservation, 1959\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1959 to promote national awareness of modern soil conservation practices, Scott #1133 presents a detailed agricultural landscape engraved in blue, green, and orange. The scene depicts terraced slopes, a farmstead, contour-plowed fields, grazing livestock, and distant industrial structures—symbolizing both rural stewardship and growing environmental responsibility. The bold SOIL CONSERVATION title stretches across the top, reinforcing the educational intent of the stamp.\r\nThe provided example is postally used, has no original gum, and has never been hinged since removal.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #1133 shows Fine/Very Fine centering with clean perforations aside from two short perf tips. A heavy wavy cancel and very minor soundness faults result in a final grade of 64 (VG–F), consistent with the 2009 PSE Grading Guide. Despite its deductions, the stamp retains good color and solid visual balance for a used example.', 0.10, 'NA01-0203-04-01-003'),
(363, 'United States', '1133', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '4¢ Multicolor – Soil Conservation, 1959\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1959, Scott #1133 highlights the national push for responsible land management and erosion prevention. The design depicts a stylized rural landscape: terraced contour fields, forest windbreaks, a farmstead, grazing cattle, and distant industrial structures. The bold blue SOIL CONSERVATION banner emphasizes the educational mission of the issue. Engraved lines convey texture in the hillsides, fields, and sky with impressive clarity for a mid-century three-color commemorative.\r\nThe submitted example is postally used, has never been hinged since removal, and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #1133 combines Fine/Very Fine centering with strong color and clean perforations apart from a single short perf. The heavy cancel and very minor perforation fault bring the final grade to 64 (VG–F) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide. Despite the deductions, the stamp remains a structurally sound and visually appealing used example of this conservation-themed commemorative.', 0.10, 'LA01-0074-01-01-002'),
(364, 'United States', '1134', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '4¢ Brown – Petroleum Industry Centennial, 1959\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1134 was issued in 1959 to commemorate the centennial of the petroleum industry, marking the 1859 Drake well as the beginning of modern oil production. The design features a stylized oil derrick rising centrally, with the dates “1859–1959” flanking the structure and “PETROLEUM INDUSTRY” curving below. The inscription “U.S. POSTAGE” appears at the top. The monochromatic brown tone emphasizes industrial strength and historical significance. This example is postally used and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1134 example presents with Fine/Very Fine centering and a strong, well-defined design. A minor perforation crease and a moderately heavy cancel reduce the final grade to 67 (Very Good/Fine). Even so, it remains a solid and collectible example of a historically significant commemorative issue under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.10, 'LA01-0074-01-02-003'),
(365, 'United States', '1136', '003', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '4¢ Gray Black – Ernst Reuter, 1960\r\n(Champion of Liberty Issue – Offset Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1136 honors Ernst Reuter, Mayor of Berlin and a prominent postwar symbol of democratic resistance during the Cold War. The design features a circular portrait of Reuter with the inscription “ERNST REUTER MAYOR OF BERLIN 1889–1953,” framed by the Champion of Liberty torch, laurel branches, and ribbon motif above. The 4¢ denomination appears at lower right, with “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” at the bottom. Issued in 1960, the stamp continues the Champion of Liberty series, which recognized international figures associated with freedom and resistance to oppression. This submitted example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum with a minor natural gum bend on the reverse.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis example of Scott #1136 combines Fine-Very Fine centering with a clean, attractive front and solid overall freshness, resulting in a final grade of 76 (Fine-Very Fine). The only fault is a minor natural gum bend, which has little effect on display quality. It remains a collectible and appealing example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.17, 'LA01-0074-02-02-003'),
(366, 'United States', '1137', '003', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine/Very Fine', '8¢ Multicolored – Ernst Reuter, 1960\r\n(Champion of Liberty Issue – Offset Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1137 honors Ernst Reuter, Mayor of Berlin and a major symbol of democratic resistance in postwar Europe. The design features a circular portrait of Reuter with the inscription “ERNST REUTER MAYOR OF BERLIN 1889–1953,” framed by the Champion of Liberty series torch, laurel branches, and ribbon motif above. The 8¢ denomination appears at lower right, with “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” at the bottom. Issued in 1960, the stamp reflects the Cold War era emphasis on international champions of freedom. This submitted example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum with a minor natural gum bend on the reverse.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis example of Scott #1137 combines Fine-Very Fine centering with a clean, attractive front and solid overall freshness, resulting in a final grade of 75 (Fine-Very Fine). The only fault is a minor natural gum bend, which has little effect on display quality. It remains a collectible and appealing example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.17, 'LA01-0074-02-03-003'),
(367, 'United States', '1138', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '4¢ Rose Carmine – Dr. Ephraim McDowell, 1959\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1959, this commemorative honors Dr. Ephraim McDowell, the pioneering Kentucky surgeon known for performing the first successful abdominal surgery in 1809. The design presents a detailed engraved portrait set within a rectangular frame, with UNITED STATES POSTAGE at top and the denomination and name integrated cleanly within the portrait field. The rich rose-carmine color emphasizes the shading lines and depth typical of mid-century engraved issues. The submitted example is used and naturally lacks original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used example of Scott #1138 shows balanced Fine/Very Fine centering and strong color but suffers from multiple shortened perforations and a heavy face-obscuring cancel. These factors lower the final grade to 47, placing it in the Good–Very Good range under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide and your numeric grading scale. While not a premium example, it remains a serviceable representative of this mid-century commemorative.', 0.05, 'LA01-0074-02-04-002'),
(368, 'United States', '1139', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Fine', '4¢ Multicolored – Credo Issue (George Washington), 1960\r\n(Credo Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1139 is the 4¢ Credo issue featuring a quotation attributed to George Washington: “Observe good faith and justice toward all nations,” with his signature reproduced beneath. The design is typographic and symbolic rather than portrait-based, incorporating patriotic motifs such as scales of justice and ornamental framing elements. The layout uses red and blue inks to emphasize national themes. This example is used, hinged as submitted, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1139 example exhibits strong Very Fine/Extremely Fine centering with full, well-balanced margins and a clean overall presentation. The single shortened perforation results in only a minor deduction, and while the vertical cancel is noticeable, it does not significantly obscure the design. The stamp remains an attractive and desirable example, achieving a final grade of 83 (Very Fine) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.15, 'LA01-0074-03-01-003'),
(369, 'United States', '1141', '003', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '4¢ Multicolored – Thomas Jefferson, 1960\r\n(Liberty Issue – Offset Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1141 is part of the Liberty Issue and features a typographic and symbolic tribute to Thomas Jefferson rather than a portrait format. The design includes Jefferson’s words, “I have sworn Hostility against every form of TYRANNY over the mind of man,” with his signature beneath, framed by decorative panels and a quill motif. The layout is distinctly modern, blending patriotic ornament with a quotation associated with Jefferson’s defense of liberty and freedom of thought. Issued in 1960, the stamp reflects the clean, graphic style of mid-century U.S. definitives. This submitted example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum, though it shows visible condition problems affecting both soundness and appearance.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis example of Scott #1141 shows Fine-Very Fine centering, but the combination of multiple perf creases and discoloration lowers the stamp to a final grade of 45 (Good-Very Good). The design remains attractive and fully legible, yet the condition issues meaningfully affect both soundness and visual freshness. Even so, it remains a collectible example of this distinctive Jefferson Liberty Issue under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.07, 'LA01-0074-03-03-003'),
(370, 'United States', '1141', '004', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '4¢ Multicolored – Credo Issue (Thomas Jefferson), 1960\r\n(Credo Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1141 is part of the 1960 Credo series and features a quotation attributed to Thomas Jefferson: “I have sworn hostility against every form of tyranny over the mind of man,” with his signature below. The design is typographic and symbolic, incorporating a quill and ornamental elements in red and blue, framed by structured panels. The layout emphasizes Jefferson’s words as the central visual feature. This example is used, hinged as submitted, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1141 example displays Fine/Very Fine centering with perforations clearly clear of the design, though tighter along the right and bottom margins. The presence of multiple ragged and shortened perforations places it in the Minor Fault category, resulting in a significant deduction. Combined with a noticeable cancel, these factors reduce overall eye appeal. Nevertheless, the stamp retains a clear design and remains a collectible example with a final grade of 54 (Very Good) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.08, 'NA01-0207-01-03-004'),
(371, 'United States', '1142', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'PG - Partial Gum', 'Good', '4¢ Red, Blue & Scarlet – Francis Scott Key “In God is Our Trust” Credo Series, 1960\r\n(Credo Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 11×10½, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1960 as part of the patriotic Credo series, Scott #1142 presents a stirring line associated with Francis Scott Key: “And this be our motto, in God is our trust.” The layout features a bold red engraved frame, a deep blue heraldic starburst emblem, and Key’s signature rendered in matching blue. The ornamental framework echoes the colonial visual vocabulary of the entire series. This example is postally used, hinge-disturbed, and shows no original gum, consistent with extended handling and postal circulation.\r\n\r\nSummary:\r\nAlthough centered at the top of the Fine/Very Fine range, the stamp’s physical condition and severe cancellation lower its overall grade significantly. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, this example receives a final grade of 34 (Good), reflecting the combined impact of structural faults and extremely heavy postal obliteration.', 0.04, 'LA01-0074-04-01-002'),
(372, 'United States', '1142', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine', '4¢ Multicolored – Credo Issue (John Adams), 1960\r\n(Credo Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1142 is part of the 1960 Credo series and features a quotation attributed to John Adams, presented in a structured typographic layout with ornamental elements. The design uses contrasting colors, typically red and blue, with decorative panels and script to emphasize the quotation and signature. The layout is balanced and symbolic, focusing on ideals rather than portraiture. This example is used, hinged as submitted, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary:\r\nThis Scott #1142 example displays Fine/Very Fine centering with all perforations clear of the design, though slightly tighter on the right and bottom margins. The light smudging on the reverse places it in the Very Minor Fault category, resulting in only a small deduction. With a clear design and moderate cancel impact, the stamp remains an attractive and collectible example, achieving a final grade of 72 (Fine) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.11, 'NA01-0207-02-01-003'),
(373, 'United States', '1143', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '4¢ Rose & Green – Credo Issue (Abraham Lincoln), 1960\r\n(Credo Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1143 is part of the 1960 Credo series and features a quotation attributed to Abraham Lincoln, “Those who deny freedom to others deserve it not for themselves,” presented in a balanced typographic layout. The design incorporates a classical allegorical figure at left, Lincoln’s facsimile signature, and ornamental panels framing the inscription. The color scheme combines rose and green tones for contrast and visual hierarchy. This example is used, hinged, and with partial original gum remaining.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1143 example shows acceptable Fine/Very Fine centering with all margins clear of the design, though uneven, with the tightest at the top. However, multiple perforation defects, including nibbed and shortened teeth, significantly impact soundness and place the stamp in the Fault category. The moderate cancel further affects presentation. Despite these issues, the stamp remains a collectible representative example of the Credo series, earning a final grade of 45 (Good/Very Good) under the 2009 PSE Gra', 0.05, 'LA01-0074-04-02-002'),
(374, 'United States', '1144', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair', '4¢ Green & Brown – Patrick Henry “Give Me Liberty” Credo Series, 1961\r\n(Credo Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 11×10½, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1961 as part of the U.S. Credo series, Scott #1144 presents Patrick Henry’s iconic moral proclamation: “Give me liberty or give me death.” The engraved green frame and torch motif embody classical symbolism, while Henry’s brown signature supplies a vivid historical accent. The stamp’s bold, clean typesetting emphasizes the clarity and force of Henry’s words. The submitted example is postally used, shows no original gum, and has not been hinge-mounted since use, though it bears several physical faults from handling.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nDespite respectable Fine/Very Fine centering and strong color, multiple significant faults and a very heavy cancel drastically reduce this stamp’s grade. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, it receives a final grade of 17 (Fair), reflecting its condition after substantial postal and physical stress.', 0.02, 'LA01-0074-04-03-002'),
(375, 'United States', '1145', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '4¢ Multicolored – Boy Scouts of America, 1960\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1145 commemorates the 50th anniversary of the Boy Scouts of America and features a scout in uniform giving the Scout salute, symbolizing duty and service. The inscription “Boy Scouts of America 1910–1960” appears prominently, with a strong horizontal composition. The design is executed in contrasting tones, with the figure rendered in brown and the denomination in blue. This example is used, hinged, and shows partial original gum on the reverse.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1145 example exhibits Fine/Very Fine centering with clear margins, though slightly tighter on the right and left sides. However, multiple perforation issues and heavy staining on the reverse significantly reduce soundness, placing the stamp in the Fault category. The heavy cancel further diminishes eye appeal. Despite these drawbacks, the stamp remains a collectible representative of the Boy Scouts commemorative issue, achieving a final grade of 44 (Good/Very Good) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.05, 'LA01-0075-01-01-003'),
(376, 'United States', '1146', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'PG - Partial Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '4¢ VIII Olympic Winter Games, 1960\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued for the 1960 VIII Olympic Winter Games held in Squaw Valley, California, Scott #1146 presents a clean modernist design: the five Olympic rings above a stylized snowflake, both stark white against a textured blue field. The engraving is crisp, and the layout emphasizes balance, symmetry, and the visual language of mid-century Olympic iconography. The submitted example is used, carries no original gum, and shows expected postal wear, but the design remains fully intact.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used example of Scott #1146 displays Fine/Very Fine centering but carries multiple minor faults and a heavy cancellation that significantly impact the final grade. Despite these deductions, the stamp retains clear design elements and remains a collectible Olympic commemorative within the Good/Very Good range under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.05, 'LA01-0075-02-01-002'),
(377, 'United States', '1147', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '4¢ Blue – Tomáš G. Masaryk, 1960\r\n(Champion of Liberty Series – Engraved, Rotary Press, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1960, this 4¢ engraved commemorative honors Tomáš Garrigue Masaryk, the first president of Czechoslovakia. The design features a medallion portrait with the torch-and-wreath emblem of the Champion of Liberty series above and the denomination and country inscription below. Rotary-press printing gives a crisp, deeply impressed blue linework typical of the series. This example is postally used, never hinged, and shows no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Masaryk commemorative shows Fine/Very Fine centering, intact perforations, and strong color, with grade reductions driven by a heavy cancel and associated bleed-through. Under the 2009 PSE system, it finishes at 52 (Very Good)—a solid, collectible representative of the Champion of Liberty series.', 0.08, 'NA01-0205-03-01-003'),
(378, 'United States', '1147', '004', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '4¢ T. G. Masaryk – Champion of Liberty, 1960\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1960 as part of the Champion of Liberty series, Scott #1147 honors Tomáš G. Masaryk, the first president of Czechoslovakia and a leading figure in the birth of the Czech state. The engraved portrait—set within a medallion and crowned with a torch-and-laurel emblem—reflects the classical style and patriotic symbolism typical of the series. The submitted example is used, previously hinged, and no longer retains original gum. Despite postal wear, the design details remain fully legible.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used and previously hinged example of Scott #1147 shows Fine/Very Fine centering but is significantly reduced by a moderate crease and a heavy machine cancel. Although the technical grade falls into the Good/Very Good range, the stamp retains clear design details and remains a collectible representative of the Champion of Liberty series.', 0.05, 'SL01-0001-03-03-004'),
(379, 'United States', '1147', '005', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine/Very Fine', '4¢ Blue – Tomáš G. Masaryk, 1960\r\n(Champion of Liberty Issue – Offset Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1147 honors Tomáš Garrigue Masaryk, the first President of Czechoslovakia and a major figure in the struggle for democratic self-government in Central Europe. The design features a circular medallion portrait of Masaryk in profile, with inscriptions identifying him as President of Czechoslovakia and marking the years 1918–1935. Above the portrait appears the familiar torch, laurel, and ribbon motif of the Champion of Liberty series, while “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” and the 4¢ denomination anchor the lower portion of the design. Issued in 1960, the stamp continues the Cold War-era series recognizing international defenders of liberty. This submitted example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum with a minor natural gum bend.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis example of Scott #1147 combines Fine-Very Fine centering with a clean, attractive front and solid overall freshness, resulting in a final grade of 76 (Fine-Very Fine). The only fault is a minor natural gum bend, which has little effect on display quality. It remains a collectible and appealing example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.17, 'LA01-0075-03-01-005'),
(380, 'United States', '1147', '006', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '4¢ Blue – T. G. Masaryk, 1960\r\n(Champion of Liberty Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1147 is the 4¢ blue Champion of Liberty commemorative honoring Tomáš Garrigue Masaryk, first president of Czechoslovakia. The design shows Masaryk in profile within a medallion inscribed with his name and country, with the dates 1918 and 1935 at right. Above the portrait appears the inscription “CHAMPION OF LIBERTY,” with a torch and decorative rays, reinforcing the democratic theme of the issue. This example is used, with no original gum remaining, consistent with postal use.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1147 is a correctly centered Fine/Very Fine example with all perforations clear of the design and a pleasing overall frame, especially on the left and top. Its principal drawback is the heavy pink cancellation with pronounced bleed-through, which places it in the Minor Fault category and reduces eye appeal. Even so, the stamp retains strong color, clear engraving, and solid collectible value as a 54 (Very Good) example under the 2009', 0.08, 'SL01-0002-01-03-006'),
(381, 'United States', '1147', '007', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '4¢ Blue – T. G. Masaryk, 1960\r\n(Champion of Liberty Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1147 is the 4¢ Champion of Liberty commemorative honoring Tomáš Garrigue Masaryk, first president of Czechoslovakia. The design shows a medallion portrait of Masaryk surrounded by inscriptions naming him and his country, with the dates 1918 and 1935 at right. Above the portrait is the inscription CHAMPION OF LIBERTY with a symbolic torch and radiating bands, while UNITED STATES POSTAGE and the 4¢ denomination anchor the lower portion. This example is used, never hinged as submitted, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1147 is a solid Fine/Very Fine centered example with all perforations clear of the design and the left margin providing the best visual space. Its principal drawback is the heavy pink cancel with pronounced bleed-through, which places it in the Minor Fault category and further reduces eye appeal. Even so, the stamp retains strong color, clear engraving, and sound collectible value as a 54 (Very Good) example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.08, 'SL01-0002-01-04-007'),
(382, 'United States', '1147', '008', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '4¢ Blue – T. G. Masaryk, 1960\r\n(Champion of Liberty Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1147 is the 4¢ Champion of Liberty commemorative honoring Tomáš Garrigue Masaryk, first president of Czechoslovakia. The design features a blue engraved medallion portrait of Masaryk, surrounded by inscriptions naming him and his country, with the dates 1918 and 1935 at right. Above the portrait appears the inscription CHAMPION OF LIBERTY with a symbolic torch and radiating bands, reinforcing the freedom theme of the issue. This example is used, never hinged as submitted, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1147 is a solid Fine/Very Fine centered example with all perforations clear of the design and the left margin giving the stamp its best visual space. Its main drawback is the heavy pink cancellation with strong bleed-through, which lowers the soundness to Minor Fault and further reduces eye appeal. Even so, the stamp retains strong color, sharp engraving, and remains a collectible 54 (Very Good) example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.08, 'SL01-0002-02-03-008'),
(383, 'United States', '1148', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good', '8¢ Multicolored – Tomáš G. Masaryk, 1960\r\n(Champion of Liberty Issue – Offset Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1148 honors Tomáš Garrigue Masaryk, first President of Czechoslovakia and a major statesman of democratic independence in Central Europe. The design features a medallion portrait of Masaryk in profile, with inscriptions identifying him as President of Czechoslovakia and marking the years 1918 and 1935. Above the portrait are the torch, laurel, and ribbon elements used throughout the Champion of Liberty series, reinforcing the theme of resistance to tyranny and support for self-government. Issued in 1960, the stamp reflects the Cold War era practice of honoring international defenders of liberty. This submitted example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum with minor production-related irregularity.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis example of Scott #1148 combines Fine-Very Fine centering with a clean and readable front, but multiple perf creases reduce the final grade to 57 (Very Good). The stamp remains visually appealing overall, yet the perforation faults meaningfully affect both soundness and presentation. Even so, it remains a collectible example of this Masaryk Champion of Liberty issue under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.10, 'LA01-0075-03-02-002'),
(384, 'United States', '1149', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '4¢ World Refugee Year, 1960\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved & Lithographed Combination, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1960 for World Refugee Year, Scott #1149 delivers a somber and symbolic image: a silhouetted refugee family moving toward a bright geometric opening, representing hope, safety, and transition. The blend of engraving for the figures and lithographed shading for the background gives the design a textured depth well suited to its humanitarian theme. The submitted example is used, previously hinged, and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used example of Scott #1149 shows strong Fine/Very Fine centering but carries a tiny thin, a corner perf crease, and a heavy cancellation that lower the final grade. Despite these deductions, the design remains clear, and the stamp retains solid collector value as a Very Good example of this meaningful humanitarian commemorative.', 0.08, 'LA01-0075-01-03-002'),
(385, 'United States', '1150', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'PG - Partial Gum', 'Very Good', '4¢ Water Conservation, 1960\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Multicolor Engraved & Lithographed Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1960 to promote nationwide awareness of resource stewardship, Scott #1150 combines multicolor engraving and lithography to present a vivid, symbolic message. A golden leaf dripping into rippling water anchors the left panel, while a sweeping landscape of farms, water systems, and hills occupies the right, representing both the fragility of natural resources and the benefits of responsible conservation. The submitted example is used, never hinged, and retains partial original gum—typical of stamps that received light postal handling after distribution.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #1150 shows Fine/Very Fine centering with a single Minor Fault and a heavy cancel that reduces overall eye appeal. Despite the deductions, the design remains legible and attractive, making it a solid Very Good example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.08, 'LA01-0075-04-01-002'),
(386, 'United States', '1150', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '4¢ Multicolored – Water Conservation, 1960\r\n(Conservation Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1150 is the 4¢ Water Conservation commemorative, issued in 1960. The design combines symbolic and landscape elements, including a leaf with a falling water droplet at left and a cultivated waterway scene at right, emphasizing responsible stewardship of natural resources. The blue, green, and brown palette gives the stamp a fresh, environmental theme typical of mid-century conservation issues. This example is used, hinged as submitted, and with partial original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1150 is a Fine/Very Fine centered example with all perforations clear of the design and the top margin providing the best visual space. Its principal fault is the presence of two distinctly shortened perforations, which cumulatively reduce the soundness to the Minor Fault category and bring the grade down to 59 (Very Good). Even so, the stamp retains a clear, attractive conservation design and remains a collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.08, 'NA01-0207-04-01-003'),
(387, 'United States', '1151', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '4¢ SEATO – Unity, Peace, Progress, 1960\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1960 to commemorate the Southeast Asia Treaty Organization (SEATO), this stamp showcases a clean blue engraved design emphasizing the alliance’s mission. The central shield bears a globe and laurel branch symbolizing global cooperation and peacekeeping. The inscriptions “UNITY,” “PEACE,” and “PROGRESS” reinforce SEATO’s founding principles during the tense geopolitical climate of the Cold War. The submitted example is used, never hinged, and has no remaining gum, which is normal for a stamp that saw postal handling but was never mounted.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used SEATO commemorative shows Fine/Very Fine centering and typical engraved sharpness but carries multiple perf-related faults and a heavy cancel. Despite these, it remains a solid, collectible Very Good example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.08, 'LA01-0075-01-02-002'),
(388, 'United States', '1151', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '4¢ Blue – SEATO (Southeast Asia Treaty Organization), 1959\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1151 was issued in 1959 to commemorate the Southeast Asia Treaty Organization and its goals of international cooperation, stability, and development. The engraved design features a globe combined with agricultural symbolism within a shield, accompanied by the inscriptions “Unity,” “Peace,” and “Progress.” Printed in blue by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, the stamp reflects the clean geometric style common to late 1950s commemoratives. This example remains identifiable and visually complete despite condition issues.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #1151 displays Fine/Very Fine centering, with the bottom margin establishing the centering limit and the top margin providing the greatest spacing. A large reverse stain and one shortened perforation place the stamp in the Major Fault category and reduce the preliminary grade to Good 30. A moderate to heavy machine cancellation and the effects of the stain further reduce eye appeal by two points. The final assigned grade is Fair/Good 28, representing a lower-grade but still collectible example of this 1959 SEATO commemorative issue.', 0.04, 'NA01-0207-04-02-003'),
(389, 'United States', '1152', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '4¢ Violet – The American Woman, 1960\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1152 was issued in 1960 to recognize the contributions of American women in education, family, civic affairs, arts, and industry. The central vignette depicts a woman and child reading, symbolizing education and family life, while surrounding panels reference broader societal roles. Printed in violet using engraved printing, the issue reflects the sharp detail typical of Bureau production. This example is postally used, hinged, and retains partial original gum. Hinging is evaluated separately and does not affect grading here.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1152 displays Fine/Very Fine centering with clear engraved detail and balanced overall design. However, substantial reverse staining places it in the Major Fault category, significantly lowering the grade. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 31 (Good), making it a representative but lower-grade collectible example.', 0.04, 'LA01-0075-04-02-003');
INSERT INTO `stamps` (`id`, `country`, `scott`, `count`, `condition`, `hinged`, `gum`, `grade`, `description`, `price`, `location`) VALUES
(390, 'United States', '1153', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine/Very Fine', '4¢ Flag Over White House – July 4, 1960\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Multicolor Engraved & Lithographed Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1153 was issued on July 4, 1960, as a patriotic commemorative celebrating American independence. The design features the United States flag prominently displayed above the White House, rendered in bright multicolor printing that emphasized national pride and optimism. The simple vertical format draws immediate attention to the flag while maintaining strong visual balance. This example is postally used and remains attractive despite normal postal handling.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #1153 displays Fine/Very Fine centering, with the left margin establishing the centering limit and the right margin providing the greatest spacing. Faultless soundness results in no deduction, while intact perforations, clean paper, and a moderate machine cancellation preserve strong overall presentation. The cancellation has no further grading impact. The final assigned grade is Fine/Very Fine 78, representing a clean, faultless, and visually attractive used example of this 1960 Flag Over White House commemorative issue.', 0.13, 'LA01-0075-02-02-002'),
(391, 'United States', '1153', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair/Good', '4¢ Multicolored – July 4, 1960 (Flag Issue)\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1153 was issued in 1960 to commemorate Independence Day and features a bold depiction of the American flag rendered in vivid red and blue. The simple, modern composition emphasizes the flag as the sole focal point, creating a strong patriotic image characteristic of mid-century commemorative design. The upward sweep of the flag conveys motion and energy while maintaining a clean, uncluttered presentation. This example is postally used and remains readily identifiable despite condition issues.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #1153 displays Fine/Very Fine centering, with the left margin establishing the centering limit and the right margin providing the greatest spacing. Significant reverse staining and multiple perforation creases place the stamp in the Major Fault category and reduce the preliminary grade to Good 30. A moderate to heavy machine cancellation and reduced visual presentation warrant a further two-point eye appeal deduction. The final assigned grade is Fair/Good 28, representing a lower-grade but still collectible example of this 1960 Independence Day commemorative issue.', 0.02, 'NA01-0209-01-01-003'),
(392, 'United States', '1154', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '4¢ Pony Express Centennial, 1960\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1960 to commemorate the 100th anniversary of the famed Pony Express, this engraved stamp depicts a rider in full gallop, mochila secured, racing across the frontier. The dotted route connects Sacramento to St. Joseph, symbolizing the historic transcontinental relay system. The monochromatic brown palette and bold centennial inscription convey both motion and historical significance. The submitted example is used, never hinged, and carries no remaining original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Pony Express Centennial stamp shows Fine/Very Fine centering and retains strong visual appeal despite several very minor faults typical of postal handling. With a final grade of 59 (Very Good), it remains a collectible and representative example of this popular historical issue under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.08, 'LA01-0075-04-03-002'),
(393, 'United States', '1154', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '4¢ Pony Express Centennial, 1960\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1154 was issued in 1960 to commemorate the centennial of the Pony Express. The engraved design portrays a Pony Express rider galloping across the frontier with mail securely carried in a mochila, while a dotted route line emphasizes the historic transcontinental mail service between St. Joseph and Sacramento. Printed in brown tones, the stamp captures the speed, determination, and romance associated with one of the most famous chapters in American postal history. This example is postally used and retains strong visual character despite normal signs of handling.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #1154 displays Fine/Very Fine centering, with the right margin establishing the centering limit and the bottom margin providing the greatest spacing. Multiple perforation creases place the stamp in the Minor Fault category and reduce the preliminary grade to Very Good 55. An average machine cancellation has no further grading impact. The final assigned grade is Very Good 55, representing a collectible and visually appealing used example of this 1960 Pony Express Centennial commemorative issue.', 0.08, 'NA01-0207-05-01-003'),
(394, 'United States', '1157', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '4¢ Mexican Independence Joint Issue, 1960\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved/Intaglio, Perforated 10¼ × 10½)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued jointly by the United States and Mexico in 1960, this commemorative honors the 150th anniversary of the start of the Mexican War of Independence. The design presents the Liberty Bell of Dolores—Hidalgo’s iconic call to arms—surrounded by radiant lines and flanked by sculptural guardian figures drawn from Mexican artistic traditions. The green inscriptions visually balance the deep red engraving. The submitted example is used, shows signs of hinge removal, and retains no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #1157 retains a clean, vivid front with Fine/Very Fine centering, though a combination of faults—including back smudging, ragged perforations, and a corner crease—lowers the technical grade to 48 (G-VG). Even so, the stamp presents attractively from the front and remains a solid collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.05, 'LA01-0076-02-01-002'),
(395, 'United States', '1157', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine/Very Fine', '4¢ Carmine & Green – Mexican Independence, 1960\r\n(Joint Issue Mexico–United States – Engraved, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1148 commemorates the 150th anniversary of Mexican independence (1810–1960) and was issued as a joint release between the United States and Mexico. The design features the Liberty Bell flanked by stylized figures beneath a radiating arch, symbolizing shared historical ideals of independence and liberty. The dual-color engraving highlights both architectural symmetry and symbolic unity. The submitted example is used, never hinged, and without original gum, with the reverse showing light soiling consistent with handling.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1148 example presents strong VF/XF centering with balanced margins and an appealing engraved design that remains clear and vibrant. While minor faults on the reverse and a small perf crease reduce the technical soundness, the overall presentation remains attractive. The light cancel and solid visual balance support a final grade of 77 (Fine/Very Fine), making this a desirable and collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.13, 'NA01-0209-03-01-003'),
(396, 'United States', '1158', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine', '4¢ United States–Japan Friendship, 1960\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved/Intaglio, Perforated 10¼ × 10½)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1960 to honor the centennial of formal diplomatic relations between the United States and Japan, Scott #1158 presents a serene vertical design integrating symbols from both nations. The Washington Monument rises through a red-to-blue sky representing renewal and partnership, while cherry blossoms frame the composition to signal Japan’s cultural presence. Stylized clouds add balance to the lower section, and crisp intaglio engraving defines each element with clarity. The submitted stamp is classified as used due to the absence of gum but bears no cancellation, preserving full visibility of the design.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis uncanceled used example of Scott #1158 remains visually attractive with bright ink, crisp engraving, and balanced Fine/Very Fine centering. A single short perf introduces only a modest deduction, resulting in a final grade of 72 (Fine) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide. It remains a pleasing and collectible example of this U.S.–Japan friendship commemorative.', 0.11, 'LA01-0076-02-02-002'),
(397, 'United States', '1161', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine', '4¢ Deep Violet – Robert A. Taft, 1960\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1161 was issued in 1960 to honor Senator Robert A. Taft, one of the most influential political figures of the mid-twentieth century. The engraved design presents a detailed portrait of Taft framed within a traditional rectangular layout, accompanied by bold inscriptions and the 4¢ denomination. Printed in deep violet by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, the stamp displays the crisp line engraving and formal appearance typical of commemoratives from this period. This example remains attractive and clearly identifiable despite postal use.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #1161 displays Fine/Very Fine centering, with the top margin establishing the centering limit and the left margin providing the greatest spacing. Two shortened perforations place the stamp in the Very Minor Fault category and reduce the preliminary grade to Fine 72. A neat machine cancellation has no further grading impact. The final assigned grade is Fine 72, representing an attractive and well-preserved used example of this 1960 Robert A. Taft commemorative issue.', 0.11, 'LA01-0076-01-02-002'),
(398, 'United States', '1163', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '4¢ Carmine & Blue – Boys’ Clubs of America Movement, 1960\r\n(Rotary Press Printing – Engraved, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1960 to celebrate the centennial of the Boys’ Clubs of America Movement, Scott #1163 presents a clean, modernist profile of a boy in carmine, facing the organization’s name set vertically in deep blue. The contrasting typography and the simple portrait emphasize the mission of shaping youth through guidance and community. The submitted example is postally used, has no original gum, and shows the typical surface and texture of rotary-press engraved commemoratives.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #1163 balances Fine/Very Fine centering with one short perf and a heavy, but not obliterating, cancel. Though these factors bring the final grade into the Very Good–Fine range, its bright color, clean surface, and solid overall presentation ensure that it remains a worthwhile and collectible representative of this 1960 commemorative issue.', 0.10, 'LA01-0076-02-03-002'),
(399, 'United States', '1164', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair/Good', '4¢ Blue & Carmine – First Automated Post Office, Providence, 1960\r\n(Rotary Press Printing – Engraved, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1960, this commemorative honors the nation’s first automated post office, located in Providence, Rhode Island. The engraved design features the modern processing facility in panoramic view, surrounded by landscaped grounds and set beneath a clear sky. The bottom inscription in carmine highlights the milestone in U.S. postal mechanization.\r\nThe submitted example is postally used, previously hinged, and no longer retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThough attractively centered, this used Scott #1164 is heavily compromised by multiple major faults and significant back damage. The cancellation is ordinary and does not affect grading, but the structural condition firmly places the stamp in the Fair/Good range. Despite its issues, it remains identifiable and complete, but far outside the condition tier sought by quality-focused collectors.', 0.02, 'LA01-0076-01-03-002'),
(400, 'United States', '1165', '003', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '4¢ Blue – Gustaf Mannerheim, Champion of Liberty, 1960\r\n(Champion of Liberty Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1165 commemorates Gustaf Mannerheim, a Finnish military leader and statesman recognized as a liberator of Finland. The engraved design presents a formal portrait within a circular frame, accompanied by the inscription “Champion of Liberty” above and identifying text around the portrait. The clean blue engraving emphasizes clarity and dignity, consistent with the series. This example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum, with minor natural gum irregularity visible on the reverse.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1165 example presents Fine/Very Fine centering with clear margins and a strong engraved design. The presence of a short perforation elevates the soundness classification to Very Minor Fault, resulting in a reduction to the Fine range. Despite this, the stamp retains solid eye appeal and remains a collectible example with a final grade of 73 (Fine) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.15, 'LA01-0076-03-03-003'),
(402, 'United States', '1167', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair/Good', '4¢ Multicolor – Camp Fire Girls 1910–1960\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved & Typographed, Rotary Press, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1960 to mark the 50th anniversary of the Camp Fire Girls, Scott #1167 features the organization’s triangular emblem, containing stylized crossed logs beneath a bright red flame. Blue radiating arcs evoke movement and growth, reflecting the group’s youth-development mission. The years 1910 and 1960 appear at top, anchoring the commemorative theme. The denomination, in bold red, and “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” complete the lower inscription. The submitted example is postally used, previously hinged, and retains no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #1167 shows attractive color and above-average centering, yet its major thin, heavy back smudging, perf creases, and forceful cancel significantly affect its grade. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the stamp concludes at 24 (Fair/Good), still serviceable for a used commemorative collector seeking a low-cost filler with strong front color despite its structural flaws.', 0.02, 'LA01-0076-02-04-002'),
(403, 'United States', '1168', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '4¢ Green – Giuseppe Garibaldi, 1960\r\n(Champion of Liberty Series – Engraved, Rotary Press, Perf 11, Original Gum, Never Hinged)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1960, this engraved commemorative honors Giuseppe Garibaldi, a central figure in Italian unification. His portrait appears in a medallion framed by his name and life dates, with “Italian Unification” reinforcing the theme. Above, the Champion of Liberty torch-and-laurel emblem radiates outward in finely engraved lines. The bottom inscription “United States Postage” and the 4¢ denomination complete the composition. Rotary-press engraving provides clean hatchwork and strong green coloration. The example is unused, never hinged, with full original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis unused NH example of Scott #1168 retains attractive color, sharp engraving, and VF/XF centering. Minor structural issues—perf creases, light reverse ink transfer, and a natural gum bend—place the soundness in the Minor Fault category, resulting in a final grade of 70 (Fine) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.15, 'LA01-0076-04-02-002'),
(404, 'United States', '1168', '003', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '4¢ Green – Giuseppe Garibaldi, Champion of Liberty, 1960\r\n(Champion of Liberty Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1168 features Giuseppe Garibaldi, a key figure in the unification of Italy, depicted in a finely engraved circular portrait. Above the portrait is the symbolic torch of liberty with radiating lines, reinforcing the “Champion of Liberty” theme shared across the series. The green monochrome engraving provides strong contrast and detail, highlighting facial features and inscriptions. This example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum, with minor natural gum irregularity present on the reverse.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1168 example presents Fine/Very Fine centering with full margins and a well-executed engraved design. The combination of a shorter perforation and minor gum bend elevates the soundness classification to Very Minor Fault, reducing the grade into the Fine range. Nonetheless, the stamp maintains good visual appeal and remains a solid collectible example with a final grade of 73 (Fine) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.15, 'NA01-0205-04-03-003'),
(405, 'United States', '1169', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good', '8¢ Multicolor – Giuseppe Garibaldi, Champion of Liberty, 1960\r\n(Champion of Liberty Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1169 is the 8¢ multicolor Garibaldi issue, featuring a gold-toned engraved portrait within a circular medallion, accented by red and blue elements and topped with the torch of liberty. The inscription references Italian unification, reinforcing the historical significance of Garibaldi’s role. The multicolor execution enhances contrast and visual interest. This example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum, though minor gum irregularity is present on the reverse.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1169 example displays Fine/Very Fine centering with full margins and an attractive multicolor engraved design. However, multiple perforation creases significantly impact soundness, reducing the grade into the Very Good range. While still collectible, especially for a general collection, the faults limit its desirability compared to higher-grade examples.', 0.10, 'LA01-0076-04-03-002'),
(406, 'United States', '1171', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '4¢ Rose Carmine – Andrew Carnegie, 1960\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Rotary Press, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1960 to honor philanthropist Andrew Carnegie, Scott #1171 depicts him seated with an open book, symbolizing the vast public library system he endowed across the United States. The engraved portrait is surrounded by bookshelves, rendered in deep rose carmine that reflects the stately tone of mid-century commemoratives. “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” appears above, with the 4¢ denomination at upper right. The submitted example is postally used, never hinged, and retains no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #1171 combines attractive Very Fine centering with several structural faults and a heavy machine cancel that suppresses the design’s clarity. Even so, the stamp retains strong color and remains a collectible example for a mid-grade used commemorative album. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, it earns a final grade of 44 Good/Very Good.', 0.05, 'LA01-0076-04-04-002'),
(407, 'United States', '1173', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '4¢ Purple – Echo I “Communications for Peace,” 1960\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Rotary Press, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1960 to celebrate the success of Project Echo, the world’s first passive communications satellite, Scott #1173 dramatizes the dawning of space-based global communication. The design features the polished Mylar Echo I sphere suspended above Earth, with radiating wave arcs linking planet and satellite, framed beneath the inscription “COMMUNICATIONS FOR PEACE.” The deep violet engraved field emphasizes the Cold War-era optimism for scientific diplomacy. The submitted example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1173 combines above-average centering with multiple back and perforation faults but retains strong color and front presentation. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, it earns a final grade of 49 Good/Very Good, serving as a visually solid but technically mid-grade NH example of the Echo I commemorative.', 0.07, 'LA01-0077-01-02-002'),
(408, 'United States', '1173', '003', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '4¢ Violet – Communications for Peace (Echo Satellite), 1960\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1173 commemorates early satellite communications through the Echo I project, symbolizing global connectivity and peaceful technological progress during the Cold War era. The engraved design depicts Earth transmitting signals toward a reflective satellite, with radiating arcs illustrating communication pathways. The inscription “Communications for Peace” reinforces the theme of cooperation. The violet color provides strong contrast and clarity. This example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum, though gum irregularities are visible on the reverse.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1173 example shows Fine/Very Fine centering with full margins and a visually appealing engraved design tied to early satellite communications. However, a gum crease and additional minor perforation issues result in a Minor Fault classification, reducing the grade to 60 (Very Good–Fine). Despite these faults, the stamp retains solid eye appeal and remains a collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.13, 'NA01-0209-04-02-003'),
(409, 'United States', '1174', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '4¢ Orange Red – Mahatma Gandhi, 1961\r\n(Champion of Liberty Series – Engraved, Rotary Press, Perf 11, Original Gum, Never Hinged)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1174 was issued in 1961 as part of the Champion of Liberty series honoring Mahatma Gandhi. The engraved design features Gandhi’s portrait within a circular medallion inscribed with his dates and the title “Apostle of Non-Violence.” Above the portrait appears the Champion of Liberty torch-and-laurel emblem, reinforcing the theme of moral leadership and peaceful social change. Printed in orange red by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, the stamp remains one of the most popular issues in the series. This example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #1174 displays Fine/Very Fine centering, with the top margin establishing the centering limit and the bottom margin providing the greatest spacing. A perforation-tip crease and minor natural gum bend place the stamp in the Very Minor Fault category and reduce the preliminary grade to Fine 72. As an unused example, cancellation is not applicable, and eye appeal remains unchanged due to the stamp’s strong color, fresh gum, and sharp engraving. The final assigned grade is Fine 72, representing an attractive never hinged example of this 1961 Mahatma Gandhi commemorative issue.', 0.15, 'LA01-0077-02-01-002'),
(410, 'United States', '1174', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair', '4¢ Orange Red – Mahatma Gandhi, 1961\r\n(Champion of Liberty Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1174 was issued in 1961 as part of the U.S. Champion of Liberty series, a mid-20th-century tribute line honoring global figures of human rights. The design features a medallion portrait of Mahatma Gandhi framed by the inscription Apostle of Non-Violence, with his birth and death dates flanking the rim. Above the portrait rises a stylized torch of liberty with radiating beams, symbolizing moral leadership and the light of peaceful resistance. The bottom panel carries UNITED STATES POSTAGE and the 4¢ denomination.\r\nThe submitted example is used, never hinged, and has no original gum remaining.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used example of Scott #1174 shows Fine/Very Fine centering but is significantly affected by a severe crease and rough perforations, placing it firmly in the Fair range under PSE standards. While structurally compromised, the stamp retains strong color and clear design integrity, offering a presentable and collectible example for its grade.', 0.02, 'NA01-0205-05-01-003'),
(412, 'United States', '1175', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '8¢ Multicolor – Mahatma Gandhi, Champion of Liberty, 1961\r\n(Champion of Liberty Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1175 is the 8¢ multicolor issue honoring Mahatma Gandhi, presented as the “Apostle of Nonviolence.” The engraved design features a gold-toned portrait within a circular medallion, accented by red and blue elements and topped with the torch of liberty. The multicolor treatment enhances contrast and visual appeal compared to the single-color counterpart. This example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum, with minor natural gum irregularity visible on the reverse.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1175 example shows Fine-Very Fine centering with full margins and a vibrant multicolor engraved design. The combination of a shortened perforation and minor gum bend results in a Very Minor Fault classification, lowering the grade into the Fine range. Despite these minor issues, the stamp retains strong visual appeal and remains a desirable collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.15, 'LA01-0077-02-02-002'),
(413, 'United States', '1176', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '4¢ Multicolor – Range Conservation “The Trail Boss,” 1961\r\n(Rotary Press Printing – Perf 11 – Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1961 to promote awareness of American rangeland management, Scott #1176 features a striking two-color tableau of Western life. The left vignette shows a trail boss on horseback, surveying the landscape, symbolizing stewardship and the traditional skills required for responsible range use. The right scene, printed in warm brown, depicts grazing cattle before mesas and a windmill, emphasizing the harmony of sustainable grazing and environmental protection.\r\nUNITED STATES POSTAGE and the 4¢ denomination appear boldly at the base, balancing the composition. The submitted example is used, previously hinged, and retains no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #1176 shows premium Extremely Fine centering but is significantly lowered by a moderate crease, additional perf creases, and a heavy cancel. The final grade of 59 reflects solid but fault-compromised condition. Even so, the stamp remains visually appealing and collectible, especially as an XF-centered example of this Western-themed commemorative.', 0.08, 'LA01-0077-03-01-002'),
(414, 'United States', '1181', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '4¢ Green – George W. Norris, 1961\r\n(Rotary Press Printing – Perf 11 – Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1961, this commemorative honors Senator George W. Norris, champion of the Tennessee Valley Authority and a leading advocate for progressive reforms. The design pairs a finely engraved portrait of Norris with the backdrop of a hydroelectric dam, symbolizing his legislative legacy and commitment to public power. The denomination and UNITED STATES POSTAGE appear at upper left, while the quote “GENTLE KNIGHT OF PROGRESSIVE IDEALS” spans the base.\r\nThis example is used, never hinged, and has no original gum remaining.\r\n\r\nSummary:\r\nThis used Scott #1184 shows solid Fine/Very Fine centering but is significantly impacted by heavy cancellation and multiple perforation flaws. With a final grade of 48 (Good/Very Good), it remains a structurally intact example suited for collectors seeking a space-filling or study-grade copy under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.05, 'LA01-0081-03-03-003'),
(415, 'United States', '1187', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'PG - Partial Gum', 'Good', '4¢ Multicolor – Frederic Remington, Artist of the West, 1961\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Multicolor Engraved/Offset Printing, Perforation 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1961 to commemorate Frederic Remington’s 100th birthday, Scott #1187 illustrates two Native American figures gathered around a fire, reflecting Remington’s iconic frontier imagery. The upper frame bears “FREDERIC REMINGTON – ARTIST OF THE WEST – 1861 1961,” combining offset underlayers with engraved detail for the figures and foreground. The submitted example is a used single with partial original gum remaining on the reverse.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #1187 retains Fine/Very Fine centering and complete perforations, but multiple minor faults and a heavy cancel reduce its final grade to 39 (Good). Despite its issues, the stamp remains a collectible example that clearly shows postal history and retains Remington’s signature visual narrative.', 0.04, 'LA01-0078-01-01-002'),
(416, 'United States', '1192', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '4¢ Multicolor – Arizona Statehood, 1962\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved/Offset Combination, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1962 to commemorate Arizona’s 50th year of statehood, Scott #1192 presents a striking desert night scene. Its left side is dominated by blooming cactus blossoms rendered in layered green and rose inks, while the right half opens onto a moonlit landscape with a lone saguaro beneath a sky full of engraved stars. Bold red inscriptions at top and bottom complete the celebratory layout.\r\nThe submitted example is postally used, previously hinged, and lacks original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used example of Scott #1192 displays Fine/Very Fine centering with intact perforations and strong color. Multiple perf creases and a heavy machine cancel reduce the final score to 46 (Good–Very Good), but the stamp still presents a complete, collectible representation of the Arizona Statehood issue under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.05, 'LA01-0078-03-01-002'),
(417, 'United States', '1193', '001', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '4¢ Blue & Multicolor – Project Mercury: U.S. Man in Space, 1962\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved/Offset Combination, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1962 to commemorate NASA’s Project Mercury—the first American manned spaceflight program—Scott #1193 presents a crisp, modernistic rendering of the Mercury spacecraft orbiting above Earth. The design blends engraved and offset printing, producing deep blues for the star field and horizon, bright white text, and a multicolor capsule shading from green-gold to blue. “4¢ U.S. MAN IN SPACE” appears at left, and “PROJECT MERCURY” anchors the bottom in bold white.\r\nThe submitted example is unused, never hinged, and retains full original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis NH example of Scott #1193 exhibits Fine/Very Fine centering and strong visual presence, with intact perforations and fresh color. A minor ink-transfer flaw reduces the grade slightly, producing a final grade of 72 (Fine). The stamp remains an attractive, collectible representative of the Project Mercury issue under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.15, 'LA01-0078-02-02-001'),
(418, 'United States', '1196', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '4¢ Blue, Red & White – Seattle World’s Fair (Space Needle), 1962\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved/Offset Combination, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued for the 1962 Seattle World’s Fair—an event celebrating science, technology, and the dawning “Century 21”—Scott #1196 features the Space Needle rising through a blue gradient sky, with the monorail sweeping across the lower portion of the design. Red vertical inscriptions flank the central vignette: “SEATTLE WORLD’S FAIR 1962” at left and “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” at right.\r\nThe submitted example is used, previously hinged, and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used example of Scott #1196 retains solid Fine/Very Fine centering and clear design strength, though the Heavy cancel and pair of shorter perf tips reduce its numerical standing. The final grade of 62 (Very Good–Fine) reflects both the soundness deductions and eye appeal impact per the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.10, 'LA01-0078-03-03-002'),
(419, 'United States', '1197', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair/Good', '4¢ Multicolor – Louisiana Statehood, 150th Anniversary, 1962\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved/Offset Combination, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1962 to mark the sesquicentennial of Louisiana statehood, Scott #1197 depicts a classic river steamboat with detailed engraving, framed by Spanish moss hanging from a cypress branch. The statehood dates “1812–1962” appear vertically at left in blue, while “LOUISIANA” stretches boldly across the bottom in vibrant red. The 4¢ denomination and “U.S. POSTAGE” sit at upper right.\r\nThe submitted example is used, previously hinged, and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used example of Scott #1197 shows solid Fine/Very Fine centering but is significantly affected by a Major Fault thin and multiple perf creases. The heavy cancel further reduces visual appeal, resulting in a final grade of 22 (Fair/Good) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide. Despite its faults, it remains a collectible space-filler for the Louisiana Statehood commemorative series.', 0.02, 'LA01-0079-01-02-002'),
(420, 'United States', '1198', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good', '4¢ Gray – Homestead Act Centennial, 1962\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1198 commemorates the 100th anniversary of the Homestead Act of 1862, depicting a pioneer scene with settlers outside a sod house, symbolizing westward expansion and agricultural development. The engraved design emphasizes texture and historical realism, with fine line work illustrating the landscape and figures. The gray tone provides a subdued, documentary quality appropriate to the subject. This example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum, though with visible discoloration and minor gum irregularities on the reverse.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1198 example presents Fine-Very Fine centering with full margins and a well-executed engraved depiction of early American settlement. However, discoloration and multiple shortened perforations result in a Minor Fault classification, reducing the overall grade to 58 (Very Good). While still collectible, the stamp’s faults limit its appeal compared to higher-grade examples under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.10, 'LA01-0079-01-03-002'),
(421, 'United States', '1200', '003', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine/Very Fine', '4¢ Violet – Brien McMahon, 1962\r\n(Atomic Energy Act Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1200 was issued in 1962 to commemorate the Atomic Energy Act and honors Brien McMahon, a key figure in U.S. nuclear policy. The design features a right-facing portrait of McMahon alongside an atomic symbol, with the inscription “Atomic Energy Act – Peaceful Uses.” The engraving is crisp, with fine line detail typical of Bureau issues of the period, and the violet color provides strong contrast for the portrait and symbolic motif.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1200 example presents as a clean, well-preserved stamp with solid Very Fine centering and intact perforations. The presence of a visible natural paper inclusion limits the soundness classification to Very Minor Fault, bringing the final grade to 76 (Fine/Very Fine). Despite this, the stamp maintains strong visual appeal and remains a desirable collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.17, 'NA01-0215-01-03-003'),
(422, 'United States', '1201', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '4¢ Yellow-Green – National Apprenticeship Program, 1964\r\n(Engraved Printing – Rotary Press, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1964, this commemorative celebrates the National Apprenticeship Program, an organized system that builds skilled labor through structured training and mentorship. The design depicts a pair of hands—one offering a precisely machined rod, the other checking it with a micrometer—capturing the transfer of technical knowledge and the disciplined craft at the heart of apprenticeship. Engraved lines emphasize texture and workmanship. The submitted example is used, bears a machine cancellation, shows hinging residue, and retains no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #1201 shows F-VF centering and a strong front impression but carries multiple back faults and a heavy machine cancel. These factors produce a final grade of 44 (Good/Very Good) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide. Despite its wear, the stamp remains a serviceable and identifiable example of the National Apprenticeship Program issue.', 0.05, 'LA01-0079-02-03-002'),
(423, 'United States', '1201', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '4¢ Yellow-Green – National Apprenticeship Program, 1964\r\n(Engraved – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1964, this commemorative highlights the National Apprenticeship Program, a system designed to develop technical skills and hands-on expertise in the American workforce. The engraved artwork depicts two hands: one stabilizing a machined part, the other measuring it with a micrometer—symbolizing precision, guidance, and the transmission of knowledge from mentor to trainee. The submitted example is postally used, bears no original gum, and shows the expected signs of normal circulation for the period.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used example of Scott #1201 combines Fine/Very Fine centering with a single very minor perf fault and a moderately heavy cancel, producing a final grade of 67 (Very Good–Fine). Despite these minor detractors, the stamp retains a clean appearance, strong engraving detail, and solid collector appeal within its grade class under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.10, 'NA01-0215-02-02-003'),
(424, 'United States', '1202', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '4¢ Multicolor – Sam Rayburn, 1962\r\n(Engraved & Offset Combination Printing – Rotary Press, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1962, this commemorative honors Sam Rayburn, one of the most influential Speakers of the House in U.S. history. The design uses two-color combination printing—brown for Rayburn’s portrait and blue for the U.S. Capitol—to reflect his legislative legacy and connection to the institution he served. The bold portrait foreground contrasts with the finely detailed Capitol dome, conveying both political authority and national symbolism. The submitted example is used and retains no original gum, as typical for postally circulated copies.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Sam Rayburn 4¢ stamp displays Fine/Very Fine centering, clean perforations, and respectable visual clarity despite a heavy cancel. With only one very minor fault and slight eye appeal deduction, the final grade of 67 (Very Good–Fine) reflects a sound, collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.10, 'LA01-0079-03-02-002'),
(425, 'United States', '1203', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'PG - Partial Gum', 'Very Good', '4¢ Olive Brown – Dag Hammarskjöld, 1962\r\n(U.S. Commemorative Issue – Photogravure, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1203 honors Dag Hammarskjöld (1905–1961), the second Secretary-General of the United Nations. The design pairs his right-facing portrait with the U.N. Headquarters at left, reinforcing the stamp’s diplomatic and memorial tone. The inscriptions identify Hammarskjöld by name and office, with “1905–1961” marking the commemorative intent. The submitted example is postally used and unusually retains partial original gum; it is described as never hinged, with the gum status recorded as a condition modifier rather than a soundness fault.\r\n\r\nSummary:\r\nThis Scott #1203 presents solid Fine–Very Fine centering with all design elements safely clear of the perforations, and an unobtrusive-to-moderate cancel that preserves the stamp’s subject well. The determining factor is soundness: multiple perforation creases place it in the Minor Fault category and pull the final result into the Very Good range. Even so, it remains a presentable, collectible example of a popular modern commemorative, with the unusual partial gum noted as a condition characteristic rather than a grading fault under the 2009 PSE framework.', 0.08, 'LA01-0079-03-01-002'),
(426, 'United States', '1203', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '4¢ Olive Brown – Dag Hammarskjöld, 1962\r\n(U.S. Commemorative Issue – Photogravure, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1203 commemorates Dag Hammarskjöld (1905–1961), the second Secretary-General of the United Nations. Issued in 1962, the design presents a contemplative right-facing portrait paired with the United Nations Headquarters, underscoring themes of diplomacy, international service, and postwar global cooperation. The subdued olive-brown photogravure printing suits the memorial character of the issue. The submitted example is postally used, never hinged, and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used example of Scott #1203 displays solid Fine–Very Fine centering with all design elements safely clear of the perforations. Soundness is reduced by multiple ragged and shortened perforations, placing it in the Minor Fault category, and eye appeal is further limited by a heavy but clearly postal cancellation. Even so, the stamp remains fully identifiable and collectible as a representative used copy, with a final grade of 47 (Good–Very Good) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.05, 'NA01-0215-03-01-003'),
(427, 'United States', '1203', '004', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '4¢ Multicolored – Dag Hammarskjöld, 1962\r\n(Dag Hammarskjöld Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1203 was issued in 1962 to honor Dag Hammarskjöld, Secretary-General of the United Nations from 1953 to 1961. The design features a contemplative portrait of Hammarskjöld at right, with the United Nations headquarters prominently depicted at left. The inscription identifies his role and years of life, emphasizing his legacy in international diplomacy. The engraved printing provides fine detail, particularly in the portrait and architectural elements, while the multicolored palette gives the issue a distinctive and dignified appearance.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1203 example presents as a clean and attractive stamp with Fine/Very Fine centering and solid overall eye appeal. The single shorter perforation limits the soundness classification to Extremely Minor Fault, resulting in a final grade of 73 (Fine). Despite this small imperfection, the stamp remains a pleasing and collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.15, 'SL01-0002-04-02-004'),
(428, 'United States', '1204', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '4¢ Olive Brown – Dag Hammarskjöld, 1962\r\n(U.S. Commemorative Issue – Photogravure, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1204 commemorates Dag Hammarskjöld (1905–1961), Secretary-General of the United Nations. The design pairs a right-facing portrait with the United Nations Headquarters at left, reinforcing themes of diplomacy, international service, and memorial tribute. The olive-brown photogravure printing gives the stamp a smooth, tonal presentation typical of early 1960s commemoratives. The submitted example is postally used, never hinged, and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #1204 shows Fine–Very Fine centering with the design safely separated from the perforations and fully identifiable despite a heavy postal machine cancel. Soundness is the primary limiter due to a thin, and the heavy cancel reduces eye appeal further. Using your centering score minus the Fault deduction to establish the base grade, then applying eye appeal afterward per the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the stamp concludes at 38 (Good) as a representative collectible example.', 0.04, 'LA01-0079-03-03-002'),
(429, 'United States', '1205', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '4¢ Multicolor – Christmas Wreath and Candles, 1962\r\n(U.S. Christmas Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1205 is the 1962 U.S. Christmas issue, an early modern holiday commemorative featuring a traditional evergreen wreath tied with a red bow, with two lit candles at left symbolizing light and warmth. The “Christmas 1962” inscription sits in a red panel that anchors the design and provides strong color contrast against the green wreath and candle vignette. The engraving style shows fine linework in the foliage and background shading typical of the period. The submitted example is postally used, never hinged, and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1205 shows Fine–Very Fine centering with the design cleanly clear of the perforations and intact perforation tips. Soundness is only lightly affected by cancellation bleed-through, but the face presentation is significantly impacted by a very heavy cancel that crosses the key focal areas. Using your stated centering and soundness deduction method, then applying eye appeal afterward per the 2009 PSE framework, the stamp concludes as a collectible, fully identifiable example graded 60 (Very Good–Fine).', 0.10, 'LA01-0079-04-02-002'),
(430, 'United States', '1205', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine', '4¢ Multicolor – Christmas Wreath and Candles, 1962\r\n(U.S. Christmas Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1205 is the 1962 U.S. Christmas issue, featuring a traditional evergreen wreath tied with a red bow, paired with two lit candles at left. The wreath and candles provide classic holiday symbolism (greenery and light), while the red “Christmas 1962” panel anchors the design with strong contrast. The engraving shows fine linework in the wreath foliage and background shading, with bold, clean color blocks in the red inscription band. The submitted example is postally used, never hinged, and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #1205 shows Fine–Very Fine centering with the design cleanly clear of the perforations, faultless physical integrity as submitted, and a light-to-average machine cancel that preserves the holiday motif and lettering. Using your centering score with the stated soundness deduction, then applying eye appeal afterward per the 2009 PSE approach, the stamp finishes as an attractive, clearly identifiable example with a final grade of 73 (Fine).', 0.11, 'NA01-0215-04-02-003'),
(431, 'United States', '1206', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'PG - Partial Gum', 'Fair', '4¢ Green – Higher Education (Lamp of Learning), 1962\r\n(U.S. Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1206 was issued in 1962 to commemorate Higher Education in the United States. The design features a classical lamp of learning set against a stylized map of the United States, symbolizing enlightenment and national progress through education. “EDUCATION” spans the top of the design, with “HIGHER” printed vertically at left. The engraved printing shows typical mid-century linework and solid ink application. The submitted example is postally used, hinged, and retains partial original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1206 retains Fine–Very Fine centering with the design clearly clear of the perforations, but multiple major faults combined with perf creases place soundness firmly in the Severe Fault category. A heavy machine cancel further limits visual appeal. Applying your stated deductions and methodology under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the stamp concludes with a final grade of 19 (Fair).', 0.02, 'LA01-0079-04-01-002'),
(432, 'United States', '1208', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '5¢ Red, White & Blue – U.S. Flag and White House, 1960\r\n(Flag over White House Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1208, issued in 1960, features a patriotic composition combining a waving United States flag above an engraved vignette of the White House. The design contrasts motion and architectural stability and is characteristic of mid-century Bureau of Engraving and Printing workmanship. The submitted example is postally used, has no original gum, and hinging is not a grading consideration.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used example of Scott #1208 combines Fine/Very Fine centering with a Minor Fault in soundness due to multiple perforation creases. A heavy machine cancellation materially affects eye appeal but does not obscure the design. Applying a conservative and defensible eye-appeal deduction under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide results in a final grade of 53 (Very Good).', 0.08, 'LA01-0080-01-03-002');
INSERT INTO `stamps` (`id`, `country`, `scott`, `count`, `condition`, `hinged`, `gum`, `grade`, `description`, `price`, `location`) VALUES
(433, 'United States', '1208', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '5¢ Red, White & Blue – U.S. Flag and White House, 1960\r\n(Flag over White House Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1208 (1960) presents a patriotic vignette: a waving U.S. flag dominates the upper field, with the White House engraved in blue below. The strong line work and clean color separation are typical of mid-century BEP production. The submitted example is postally used, has no original gum, and hinging is not considered in the grading narrative.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #1208 combines Fine/Very Fine centering with only a very minor reverse-side ink transfer fault. The primary visual drawback is a heavy, centrally placed machine cancellation that reduces design clarity and warrants an eye-appeal deduction. Using the 2009 PSE Grading Guide sequence and strict arithmetic, the stamp grades 67 (Very Good-Fine).', 0.10, 'NA01-0219-01-01-003'),
(434, 'United States', '1208', '004', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '5¢ Red, White & Blue – U.S. Flag and White House, 1960\r\n(Flag over White House Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1208, issued in 1960, features a patriotic composition combining a flowing United States flag above a finely engraved view of the White House. The design juxtaposes motion and permanence and reflects the crisp, controlled engraving typical of Bureau of Engraving and Printing production in the period. The submitted example is postally used, has no original gum, and hinging is not a grading consideration.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #1208 combines Fine centering with a Minor Fault soundness classification resulting from two very minor defects. The machine cancellation is typical for the issue and does not require an additional eye appeal deduction. In accordance with the 2009 PSE Grading Guide and strict arithmetic, the correct final grade for this stamp is 41 (Good/Very Good).', 0.05, 'SL01-0001-02-02-004'),
(435, 'United States', '1208', '005', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '5¢ Multicolored – Flag Over White House, 1963\r\n(Flag Issue – Engraved and Offset Combination, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1208 features the United States flag in full color waving above a finely engraved depiction of the White House. The design combines bold patriotic symbolism with architectural detail, using a multicolored flag contrasted against a blue-toned building. The composition is framed simply, allowing the flag to dominate the visual field while the White House anchors the lower portion. This example is used, never hinged as submitted, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1208 example displays Fine centering with the top and left margins closest to the design, while retaining visible spacing on all sides. The light cancellation bleed-through places it in the Very Minor Fault category, resulting in a modest deduction. With strong color, a clear patriotic design, and only moderate cancellation impact, the stamp remains an appealing and collectible example, achieving a final grade of 61 (Very Good/Fine) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.10, 'SL01-0001-09-02-005'),
(436, 'United States', '1208', '006', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine', '5¢ Multicolored – Flag Over White House, 1963\r\n(Flag Issue – Engraved and Offset Combination, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1208 depicts the United States flag in vivid red and blue waving above a detailed engraving of the White House. The composition emphasizes national symbolism, with the flag dominating the upper field and the White House anchoring the lower portion. The design combines engraved line work with offset color, producing a sharp yet vibrant appearance. This example is used, never hinged as submitted, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1208 example presents Fine/Very Fine centering with all perforations clear of the design and the right margin providing the most visual space. The top and left margins are tighter but remain within acceptable limits. A light cancellation bleed-through places the stamp in the Very Minor Fault category, resulting in only a small deduction. With strong color, a clear patriotic design, and moderate cancellation impact, the stamp remains an attractive and collectible example with a final grade of 71 (Fine) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.11, 'SL01-0001-09-03-006'),
(437, 'United States', '1208', '007', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine', '5¢ Multicolored – Flag Over White House, 1963\r\n(Flag Issue – Engraved and Offset Combination, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1208 depicts a brightly colored American flag waving above a detailed engraved view of the White House. The design blends engraved line work in the building with offset color in the flag, creating a strong contrast and a visually dynamic patriotic composition. This example is used, never hinged as submitted, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1208 example presents Fine/Very Fine centering with all perforations clear of the design, though the top and left margins are noticeably tighter than the bottom and right. The light cancellation bleed-through results in only a minimal deduction, and despite a moderately heavy cancel, the stamp maintains strong color and clarity. It remains a solid and collectible example with a final grade of 71 (Fine) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.11, 'SL01-0001-09-04-007'),
(438, 'United States', '1209', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '1¢ Green – Andrew Jackson, 1962\r\n(Liberty Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1209, issued in 1962, features a finely engraved right-facing portrait of Andrew Jackson, seventh President of the United States. Designed by Charles R. Chickering and printed by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, it completed the Liberty Series’ low-value range. The deep green design presents “ANDREW JACKSON” vertically at right and “1¢ U.S. POSTAGE” below. The submitted example is postally used, previously hinged, and shows no remaining gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #1209 shows Fine/Very Fine centering but suffers from multiple perforation faults typical of postal wear. Despite these defects, it retains sharp color, an unobtrusive cancel, and full design clarity. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the result is a final grade of 32 (Good – G)—a structurally flawed yet visually legible Liberty Series example.', 0.04, 'NA01-0189-05-01-003'),
(439, 'United States', '1209', '004', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine', '1¢ Green – Andrew Jackson, 1960\r\n(Andrew Jackson Definitive Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1209 (1960) depicts a right-facing engraved portrait of Andrew Jackson in a rectangular frame, with “U.S. POSTAGE” at the bottom and “ANDREW JACKSON” reading vertically along the right side. The engraved line work is crisp and densely hatched, typical of mid-century BEP definitives. The submitted example is postally used, has no original gum, and hinging is not noted per instructions. \r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #1209 offers Fine/Very Fine centering and a light, unobtrusive machine cancel, with soundness limited by three shortened perforation teeth that combine to a Very Minor Fault. Following the 2009 PSE Grading Guide sequence and strict arithmetic, the correct final grade is 72 (Fine).', 0.11, 'LA01-0080-01-01-004'),
(440, 'United States', '1209', '005', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine', '1¢ Green – Andrew Jackson, 1963\r\n(Prominent Americans Series – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1209 is the 1¢ green Andrew Jackson definitive from the Prominent Americans series. The design features a sharply engraved right-facing portrait of Jackson within a rectangular frame, with “U.S. POSTAGE” and the denomination clearly presented. The engraving emphasizes strong line work and a formal presentation typical of the series. This example is used, never hinged as submitted, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1209 example presents solid Fine/Very Fine centering with all perforations clear of the design, though slightly tighter at the top and right. The presence of a minor perf tip crease places it in the Very Minor Fault category, resulting in a modest deduction. With strong color, clear engraving, and a relatively unobtrusive cancel, the stamp remains an attractive and collectible example, achieving a final grade of 74 (Fine) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.11, 'SD22-0007-02-06-005'),
(441, 'United States', '1213', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair/Good', '5¢ Dark Blue – George Washington, 1960\r\n(Washington Definitive Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1213 is a 1960 definitive portraying George Washington in a finely engraved three-quarter portrait within a simple rectangular frame. The design emphasizes clarity and dignity through dense line engraving and restrained layout, typical of mid-century Bureau of Engraving and Printing production. The submitted example is postally used, has no original gum, and has been hinged, with hinging evaluated separately and not noted here.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #1213 retains Fine/Very Fine centering, but its condition is decisively limited by a Major Fault classification due to natural straight edges on two sides, compounded by additional crease and perforation issues. A heavy machine cancellation further diminishes eye appeal. Applying the grading sequence and arithmetic strictly, the correct final assessment is 23 (Fair/Good).', 0.02, 'LA01-0080-01-02-003'),
(442, 'United States', '1229', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '5¢ Blue – George Washington, 1962\r\n(Liberty Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1229, issued in 1962 as part of the Liberty Series, depicts George Washington in a right-facing engraved bust based on Jean-Antoine Houdon’s sculpture. Designed by Charles R. Chickering and engraved by Bureau artists, the portrait is rendered with a fine crosshatched background, conveying strength and dignity. “5¢ U.S. POSTAGE” appears below the image in a serif font typical of mid-century engravings. The submitted example is postally used, previously hinged, and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nUnder the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, this used Scott #1229 shows Fine/Very Fine centering with a very minor fault and a moderately heavy cancel. The final grade of 66 (Very Good/Fine) reflects minimal wear consistent with typical postal use. Despite its reverse spotting and cancel weight, the stamp retains strong color, full perforations, and crisp engraving, making it a solid collectible Liberty Series example.', 0.10, 'LA01-0068-01-01-003'),
(443, 'United States', '1229', '004', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '5¢ Dark Blue – George Washington, 1962\r\n(Washington Definitive Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1229 depicts George Washington in a three-quarter engraved portrait within a plain rectangular frame, with the denomination at lower left and “U.S. POSTAGE” across the bottom. The design relies on dense line engraving to model the face and hair, a hallmark of early-1960s Bureau of Engraving and Printing definitives. The submitted example is postally used, has no original gum, and hinging is not noted per instructions.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #1229 offers Fine/Very Fine centering and largely sound physical condition, limited only by a single shortened perforation tooth. However, a heavy machine cancellation significantly impacts visual appeal. Applying the grading sequence and arithmetic under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the correct final assessment is 63 (Very Good/Fine).', 0.10, 'NA01-0191-04-02-004'),
(444, 'United States', '1230', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '5¢ Carmine – Carolina Charter, 1963\r\n(Carolina Charter Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1230 commemorates the 300th anniversary of the 1663 Carolina Charter. The design centers on an ornate, illuminated charter with decorative borders and a quill pen laid across it, visually tying “law on paper” to “history in ink.” The engraved line work is crisp by nature, with strong contrast between the dark lettering and the red field. The submitted example is postally used, has been hinged, and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1230 shows Fine/Very Fine centering with clear design separation, but its grade is pulled down by minor-fault soundness from multiple perf creases and by a heavy barred cancel that strongly impacts the face. Even so, the stamp retains solid engraved detail and remains a collectible used example of the Carolina Charter commemorative, grading 47 (Good/Very Good) under the 2009 PSE framework.', 0.05, 'LA01-0080-02-01-002'),
(445, 'United States', '1233', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '5¢ Multicolor – Emancipation Proclamation Centennial, 1963\r\n(Emancipation Proclamation Issue – Printed, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1233 was issued in 1963 for the centennial of the Emancipation Proclamation (1863–1963). The design is a modern symbolic composition of broken chain links in a bold blue-and-black field, emphasizing emancipation through graphic abstraction rather than portraiture. The inscriptions frame the theme clearly, with the dates at top and “EMANCIPATION PROCLAMATION” across the bottom. The submitted example is postally used, has never been hinged, and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1233 shows Fine/Very Fine centering with the design clearly separated from the perforations, but its grade is reduced by minor-fault soundness from multiple perf creases and by a heavy wavy-line cancellation that interferes with the central chain motif. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide framework you specified, the stamp receives a final grade of 47 (Good/Very Good), remaining collectible as a representative used example rather than a premium-quality display piece.', 0.05, 'LA01-0080-04-01-002'),
(446, 'United States', '1234', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '5¢ Multicolor – Alliance for Progress, 1963\r\n(Alliance for Progress Issue – Printed, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1234 was issued in 1963 in support of the Alliance for Progress, a U.S.-backed initiative associated with economic development and cooperation in Latin America. The design is clean, modern, and symbolic: a hand grips a torch set within a forward-pointing white arrow, suggesting guidance and momentum, while the bold lettering anchors the message. The submitted example is postally used, has been hinged, and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1234 shows Fine/Very Fine centering with the design clearly free of the perforations, but condition is limited by multiple shortened teeth and a heavy wavy-line cancel that cuts through the central torch-and-arrow motif. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide framework you specified, the stamp earns a final grade of 48 (Good/Very Good), remaining collectible as a representative used example rather than a premium visual specimen.', 0.05, 'LA01-0080-04-02-002'),
(447, 'United States', '1240', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine/Very Fine', '5¢ Blue – Christmas Tree, 1963\r\n(Christmas Issue – Engraved, Perforation 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1240 was issued in 1963 as the United States Christmas commemorative, depicting the National Christmas Tree displayed near the White House. The engraved design shows a tall, illuminated evergreen crowned with a star against a winter evening sky, with the White House visible in the background at left. The inscription “CHRISTMAS 1963” appears prominently at upper left, while “UNITED STATES” and the denomination are positioned along the lower margin. The engraving emphasizes texture and depth through fine line work in the tree and sky. The submitted example is unused, has original gum, and has been hinged.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1240 grades Fine/Very Fine 79 under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide. It combines faultless soundness with clear, well-separated design elements and acceptable Fine/Very Fine centering. While the right margin limits the centering grade, the stamp remains an attractive and collectible example of the 1963 U.S. Christmas issue.', 0.13, 'LA01-0081-01-02-002'),
(448, 'United States', '1240', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine/Very Fine', '5¢ Blue – Christmas Tree, 1963\r\n(Christmas Issue – Engraved, Perforation 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1240 was issued in 1963 as the U.S. Christmas commemorative depicting the National Christmas Tree near the White House. The engraved design shows a tall, illuminated evergreen topped with a star against a winter sky, with the White House rendered at left for setting and scale. “CHRISTMAS 1963” anchors the upper inscription, while “UNITED STATES” and the 5¢ value complete the lower frame. The submitted example is used and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1240 grades Fine 72 under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide. The stamp is faultless in soundness with intact perforations and Fine/Very Fine centering limited by a narrow right margin. A heavy horizontal cancel reduces visual balance and scene clarity, leading to a modest eye appeal deduction, but the stamp remains a solid, collectible used example of this popular early U.S. Christmas issue.', 0.11, 'NA01-0217-04-02-003'),
(449, 'United States', '1240', '004', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine', '5¢ Blue – Christmas Tree, 1963\r\n(Christmas Issue – Engraved, Perforation 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1240 was issued in 1963 as the U.S. Christmas commemorative depicting the National Christmas Tree near the White House. The engraved design presents a tall, illuminated evergreen topped with a star against a winter sky, with the White House at left and smaller trees at right, creating a calm seasonal scene. “CHRISTMAS 1963” is inscribed at upper left, while “UNITED STATES” and the 5¢ denomination complete the lower inscriptions. The submitted example is used, never hinged, and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1240 grades Fine 72 under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide. It shows Fine/Very Fine centering with the right margin controlling, intact original perforations aside from a corner perf crease, and a clean, attractive holiday design with solid color and impression. The corner perf crease accounts for the soundness deduction, but the stamp remains a collectible used example with pleasing overall presentation.', 0.11, 'SL01-0001-02-04-004'),
(450, 'United States', '1240', '005', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '5¢ Blue – Christmas Tree, 1963\r\n(Christmas Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1240 is the 1963 Christmas issue, featuring a decorated Christmas tree in front of the White House, with “CHRISTMAS 1963” prominently inscribed in the upper left. The design combines symbolic holiday imagery with a recognizable national landmark, rendered in a predominantly blue engraved style with contrasting red denomination. This example is used, never hinged as submitted, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1240 example presents solid Fine/Very Fine centering with all perforations clear of the design, though tighter on the right and bottom. The presence of multiple perf creases and a shortened perforation places it in the Minor Fault category, resulting in a significant deduction. While the design remains clear and the color strong, these structural issues and a moderate cancel reduce overall appeal. Nevertheless, it remains a collectible example with a final grade of 55 (Very Good) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.08, 'SL01-0002-01-05-005'),
(451, 'United States', '1241', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine', '5¢ Multicolor – Audubon, American Artist, 1963\r\n(Audubon Issue – Engraved, Perforation 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1241 was issued in 1963 to honor John James Audubon, the American naturalist and artist celebrated for The Birds of America. The engraved design depicts two stylized blue birds perched among branches and foliage, executed with fine line work and layered color to echo Audubon’s illustrative style. Script lettering at left reads “Audubon, American Artist,” with the denomination and “U.S. Postage” positioned at lower right. The composition emphasizes vertical movement through elongated tail feathers and balanced natural elements. The submitted example is used and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1241 grades Fine 73 under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide. It combines Fine/Very Fine centering with faultless soundness and intact perforations, while a heavy vertical cancellation reduces visual harmony and necessitates an eye appeal deduction. Despite the cancel, the engraved birds remain well defined, making this a solid and collectible used example of the Audubon commemorative.', 0.11, 'LA01-0080-03-04-002'),
(452, 'United States', '1241', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '5¢ Multicolor – Audubon, American Artist, 1963\r\n(Audubon Issue – Engraved, Perforation 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1241 was issued in 1963 honoring John James Audubon, the American naturalist and artist best known for his bird studies. The engraved design shows two blue birds perched among branches and foliage, printed in layered color with fine line engraving that emphasizes feather texture and movement. Script inscription at left reads “Audubon, American Artist,” with “5¢” and “U.S. Postage” at lower right. The submitted example is used and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1241 grades VG-Fine 68 under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide. It offers Fine/Very Fine centering with sound paper integrity and only an extremely minor corner perforation irregularity, but a heavy horizontal cancellation significantly affects visual balance and design clarity, requiring an eye appeal deduction. Despite the bold cancel, the birds remain recognizable, keeping it a collectible used example of this sought-after Audubon commemorative.', 0.10, 'NA01-0217-03-03-003'),
(453, 'United States', '1242', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '5¢ Black – Sam Houston, 1954\r\n(Regular Issue – Engraved, Perf 11×10½)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1242 features a full-length engraved portrait of Sam Houston dressed in frontier attire and holding a long gun. The design is framed within a simple rectangular border and displays the strong line engraving characteristic of the Liberty Series period. Fine crosshatching provides depth to the figure, while Houston’s signature appears prominently near the lower portion of the design. Printed in black by the Bureau of Engraving and Printing, the stamp presents a bold and historically significant image. This example is postally used and remains clearly identifiable despite a substantial cancellation.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #1242 displays Very Fine centering, with the bottom margin establishing the centering limit and the left margin providing the greatest spacing. Two shortened perforations place the stamp in the Very Minor Fault category and reduce the preliminary grade to Fine/Very Fine 75. A heavy machine cancellation further reduces eye appeal by eight points. The final assigned grade is Very Good/Fine 67, representing a well-centered and structurally sound used example whose overall grade is primarily limited by the prominent cancellation.', 0.10, 'LA01-0081-02-01-002'),
(454, 'United States', '1243', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '5¢ Multicolor – C. M. Russell, American Artist, 1955\r\n(Regular Issue, Engraved, Perf 11 × 10½)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1243 reproduces a classic Charles M. Russell western scene: mounted cowboys actively driving cattle across open range. The multicolor engraved production relies on layered ink colors over fine linework to create depth in the horses, cattle, and distant landscape. The inscription “C. M. RUSSELL / AMERICAN ARTIST” anchors the lower left, while “U.S. POSTAGE” appears at upper right and the 5¢ value at upper left. On this example, the design remains sharp and well-defined with strong detail in the figures and terrain.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #1243 presents with appealing artwork and a clear, well-printed face, supported by Fine/Very Fine centering. The grade is primarily limited by multiple perforation creases (Minor Fault), which drop the base grade into the Very Good range. Because the cancellation is relatively light and does not materially obscure the design, a modest positive eye-appeal adjustment is justified. The resulting final numerical grade is 61', 0.10, 'LA01-0081-03-01-002'),
(455, 'United States', '1243', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine/Very Fine', '5¢ Multicolor – C. M. Russell, American Artist, 1955\r\n(Regular Issue – Engraved, Perf 11 × 10½)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1243 commemorates western artist Charles M. Russell and adapts one of his best-known frontier scenes, depicting mounted cowboys driving cattle across open range. The engraved design uses layered multicolor inks and fine linework to convey motion, depth, and the rugged character of the American West. Inscriptions include “C. M. RUSSELL / AMERICAN ARTIST” in the lower-left tablet, with “U.S. POSTAGE” at upper right and the 5¢ denomination at upper left. The submitted example is postally used, never hinged, and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #1243 displays Fine/Very Fine centering, a clean and attractive engraved design, and a clearly identifiable light corner cancel that enhances overall appearance. Soundness is modestly limited by three short perforation tips, but the positive eye appeal offsets part of that impact. The stamp remains a strong and appealing collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide with a final grade of 76 (Fine/Very Fine).', 0.13, 'NA01-0223-01-01-003'),
(456, 'United States', '1244', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '5¢ Blue-Green – New York World’s Fair, 1964–1965\r\n(New York World’s Fair Issue – Engraved, Perf 11 × 10½)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1244 commemorates the 1964–1965 New York World’s Fair, featuring the Unisphere as a symbol of international unity and modern progress, paired with fairground elements and figures in the foreground. The inscriptions “NEW YORK WORLD’S FAIR 1964 1965” and “U.S. POSTAGE 5¢” are cleanly integrated into the engraved scene. The submitted example is postally used, hinged, and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #1244 shows Fine/Very Fine centering with a clear, well-engraved design that remains recognizable despite cancellation. Condition is limited chiefly by multiple perforation creases, with minor reverse smudging also present, placing the base grade in the Very Good range. The heavy wavy-line cancel further reduces eye appeal, resulting in a final grade of 51 (Very Good). Even with these drawbacks, it remains a solid, collectible example of a classic World’s Fair commemorative under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.08, 'LA01-0081-03-02-002'),
(457, 'United States', '1246', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '5¢ Blue – John Fitzgerald Kennedy, 1964\r\n(JFK Memorial Issue – Engraved, Perf 11 × 10½)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1246 is the 5¢ John Fitzgerald Kennedy memorial issue, released in 1964 following his assassination. The design pairs a formal engraved portrait of Kennedy with an eternal flame motif, symbolizing remembrance and continuity. The quotation “AND THE GLOW FROM THAT FIRE CAN TRULY LIGHT THE WORLD” arcs across the top, with the dates “1917” and “1963” flanking the design and “JOHN FITZGERALD KENNEDY” along the bottom. Engraved linework provides strong tonal contrast in the portrait and a soft glow effect around the flame panel. The submitted example is postally used, hinged, and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #1246 exhibits Very Fine centering with full margins and a clearly identifiable engraved JFK memorial design. Condition is substantially limited by multiple perforation defects and perforation creases, which place soundness in the Fault category and reduce the base grade into the Very Good range. A heavy cancellation further diminishes eye appeal, resulting in a final grade of 44 (Good–Very Good). Despite these limitations, it remains a collectible example of this historically signifi', 0.05, 'LA01-0082-01-01-002'),
(459, 'United States', '1247', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '5¢ Blue – New Jersey Tercentenary, 1964\r\n(New Jersey Tercentenary Issue – Engraved, Perf 11 × 10½)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1247 commemorates New Jersey’s 300th anniversary (1664–1964). The design uses a large silhouette map of New Jersey as the backdrop, with three colonial-era figures in the foreground to evoke early settlement and civic beginnings. “NEW JERSEY TERCENTENARY” and the dates “1664 1964” dominate the upper design, while “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” and the 5¢ denomination sit at lower left. The engraving is clean and graphic, with fine line shading separating the figures from the map field. The submitted example is postally used, hinged, and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #1247 shows Fine/Very Fine centering with full margins and a clear, well-engraved commemorative design. Soundness is limited by a perf-tip crease with an additional short perforation, and the heavy wavy machine cancel materially reduces visual appeal. Even with these drawbacks, it remains a solid, collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide at 66 (Very Good/Fine).', 0.10, 'LA01-0081-02-03-002'),
(460, 'United States', '1248', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine/Very Fine', '5¢ Multicolor – Nevada Statehood, 1964\r\n(Nevada Statehood Issue – Engraved, Perf 11 × 10½)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1248 commemorates the centennial of Nevada’s admission to the Union (1864–1964). The engraved multicolor design depicts a western town in the foreground with mountain ranges beyond, symbolizing settlement and development. A bold vertical tablet at right reads “NEVADA 1864 1964 STATEHOOD,” providing strong graphic contrast against the townscape. “U.S. POSTAGE” and the 5¢ denomination appear at upper left. The engraving shows crisp architectural detail and layered color fields typical of mid-1960s commemoratives. The submitted example is postally used, never hinged, and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #1248 presents Fine/Very Fine centering, full margins, and completely sound condition. A neat, corner-placed machine cancel preserves design clarity without impacting visual balance. With no soundness defects and no eye-appeal penalty warranted, the stamp stands as an attractive and collectible used example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide with a final grade of 78 (Fine/Very Fine).', 0.13, 'LA01-0082-01-02-002'),
(461, 'United States', '1249', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '5¢ Multicolor – Register to Vote, 1964\r\n(Voter Registration Issue – Engraved, Perf 11 × 10½)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1249 is a 1964 commemorative promoting voter registration. The bold graphic design features a stylized American flag rendered in red and blue engraving, framed by prominent lettering reading “REGISTER” and “VOTE,” with “5¢ POSTAGE” at the bottom. The composition relies on crisp linework, strong contrast, and generous white space to keep the civic message clear and visually striking. The submitted example is postally used, never hinged, and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #1249 shows Fine/Very Fine centering with full margins and an otherwise clean, sharply engraved “Register to Vote” design. Soundness is limited by four short perforation tips, and eye appeal is further reduced by a heavy diagonal machine cancel that crosses the central flag vignette. Even with these drawbacks, it remains a collectible example of this classic civic commemorative under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, with a final grade of 65 (Very Good/Fine).', 0.10, 'LA01-0081-02-04-002'),
(462, 'United States', '1249', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '5¢ Multicolor – Register to Vote, 1964\r\n(Voter Registration Issue – Engraved, Perf 11 × 10½)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1249 is a 1964 civic commemorative encouraging voter participation. The design features a stylized American flag rendered in bold red and blue engraving, framed by prominent lettering reading “REGISTER” and “VOTE,” with the 5¢ denomination and “POSTAGE” at the bottom. Strong linear engraving and high contrast give the stamp a poster-like clarity typical of mid-1960s public awareness issues. The submitted example is postally used, never hinged, and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #1249 shows Fine/Very Fine centering with full margins and a crisp engraved design. Soundness is modestly limited by light ink transfer on the reverse, and eye appeal is further reduced by a heavy machine cancel crossing the flag vignette. Despite these drawbacks, the stamp remains a collectible example of this mid-century civic issue, earning a final grade of 65 (Very Good/Fine) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.10, 'NA01-0223-03-02-003'),
(463, 'United States', '1250', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair/Good', '5¢ Brown – William Shakespeare, 1964\r\n(Literary Arts Issue – Engraved, Perforated, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nIssued in 1964 to commemorate the 400th anniversary of William Shakespeare’s birth, Scott #1250 features a finely engraved portrait of the playwright framed by architectural elements suggestive of an Elizabethan stage. Symbolic details include a skull and quill, reinforcing themes of mortality and authorship central to Shakespeare’s work. Inscriptions include “UNITED STATES” at the top and “SHAKESPEARE 1564–1964” beneath the portrait. The submitted example is a used single stamp with no original gum; hinging is evaluated separately and does not affect the grade.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis example of Scott #1250 displays Fine/Very Fine centering with full margins and a clear engraved depiction of William Shakespeare. However, major soundness issues, including a pulled perforation and a thin, significantly impact structural integrity. A heavy machine cancel crossing the portrait further reduces eye appeal. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the stamp is appropriately graded 29 (Fair–Good), remaining collectible primarily as a representative example rather than a condition-driven piece.', 0.02, 'LA01-0081-04-01-002'),
(464, 'United States', '1250', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '5¢ Brown – William Shakespeare, 1964\r\n(Literary Arts Issue – Engraved, Perforated, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1250 was issued in 1964 to commemorate the 400th anniversary of William Shakespeare’s birth. The engraved design features a portrait of Shakespeare framed by architectural elements associated with the theater, accompanied by symbolic items including a quill and skull representing authorship and dramatic literature. The brown engraving provides strong contrast and detail, creating a dignified tribute to one of history’s most influential writers. This example is postally used and remains fully identifiable despite condition issues.\r\n\r\nSummary:\r\nScott #1250 displays Fine/Very Fine centering, with the left margin establishing the centering limit and the right margin providing the greatest spacing. Multiple ragged and shortened perforations together with several perforation creases place the stamp in the Fault category and reduce the preliminary grade to Very Good 45. A heavy diagonal machine cancellation further reduces eye appeal by five points. The final assigned grade is Good/Very Good 40, representing a collectible but condition-challenged example of this 1964 William Shakespeare commemorative issue.', 0.05, 'NA01-0223-03-03-003'),
(465, 'United States', '1251', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '5c Green – Doctors Mayo, 1964\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Printed by BEP, Perforated, No watermark noted)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nThis 5¢ commemorative honors the Mayo brothers, landmark American physicians whose work helped define modern clinical practice. The design is a clean two-portrait layout with a medical caduceus at left and bold “DOCTORS MAYO” inscription anchoring the lower panel, emphasizing the subject’s professional legacy over ornament. The submitted example is used, previously hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nUnder the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, this Scott #1251 grades 62 (Very Good-Fine). It presents nicely from the front with a light machine cancel and strong overall clarity, supported by VF centering. The small perf-tip crease and reverse ink soak-through are the key limiting factors, but it remains an appealing, clearly collectible used example.', 0.10, 'LA01-0081-04-02-002'),
(466, 'United States', '1252', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '5¢ – American Music, 1964\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Perforated, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1252 celebrates American music through a symbolic arrangement of musical instruments, sheet music, and laurel branches. The central engraved vignette is printed in black and is complemented by red and blue inscriptions, creating a striking tricolor appearance uncommon among United States commemoratives of the period. The design combines artistic and cultural themes while maintaining the crisp engraving quality typical of Bureau of Engraving and Printing production. This example is postally used and remains visually attractive despite condition issues.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #1252 displays premium Extremely Fine centering, with the right margin serving as the limiting factor and the top margin providing the greatest spacing. Multiple perforation creases place the stamp in the Minor Fault category and reduce the preliminary grade to Fine/Very Fine 75. A prominent heavy machine cancellation further reduces eye appeal by eight points. The final assigned grade is Very Good/Fine 67, representing a well-centered but condition-challenged example of this attractive American Music commemorative.', 0.10, 'LA01-0082-01-03-002'),
(467, 'United States', '1257b', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '5¢ Multicolor – Christmas Block of Four, 1964\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1257b was issued in 1964 as a se-tenant Christmas block of four depicting traditional holiday symbols: holly, mistletoe, poinsettia, and evergreen with cone. Each vignette is finely engraved with clean line work and arranged around central intersecting perforations, unified visually by repeated “5¢” denominations. The red and green color palette provides strong seasonal contrast against the white paper and gives the block a festive appearance. This example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1257b Christmas block presents strong color and attractive engraved holiday imagery, though centering limits it to the Very Good range due to the tight right margin. Light reverse ink transfer introduces a Very Minor Fault and further reduces the technical grade. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 42 (Good/Very Good), representing a clean and collectible holiday block with intact structure.', 0.28, 'LA01-0082-02-02-002'),
(468, 'United States', '1260', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair', '5¢ Violet – Amateur Radio, 1964\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1260 was issued in 1964 to honor amateur radio operators and features a bold, modern composition centered around a stylized radio wave crossing the design. A tuning dial appears at right, while “AMATEUR RADIO” is inscribed at upper left. The vivid magenta-violet color combined with white graphic elements gives the stamp a distinctly technical and mid-century appearance. Despite condition problems, the design remains immediately recognizable. This example is postally used and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1260 retains respectable Fine/Very Fine centering and a bold, recognizable design, but severe thinning and heavily compromised perforations substantially reduce soundness. A dark manuscript cancel further impacts presentation. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 15 (Fair), making it a heavily worn but still identifiable example of this distinctive commemorative issue.', 0.02, 'LA01-0081-04-04-002'),
(469, 'United States', '1261', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '5¢ Multicolor – Battle of New Orleans, 1965\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1261 was issued in 1965 to commemorate the 150th anniversary of the Battle of New Orleans. The design depicts Andrew Jackson mounted on horseback, sword raised as he rallies American troops, framed by medallions and contrasting red and blue inscriptions. Historical dates “1815” and “1965” appear prominently, reinforcing the commemorative theme. The dynamic composition and vivid multicolor engraving make the issue visually striking even when postally used. This example is used and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1261 presents respectable Fine/Very Fine centering with strong color and an energetic historical design. A small corner crease and shortened perforation reduce soundness modestly, but the very heavy machine cancel does most of the grading damage by obscuring key elements of the vignette. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 43 (Good/Very Good), making it a structurally sound but visually challenged example.', 0.05, 'LA01-0082-03-01-002'),
(470, 'United States', '1261', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine', '5¢ Multicolor – Battle of New Orleans, 1965\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1261 was issued in 1965 to commemorate the 150th anniversary of the Battle of New Orleans. The design depicts Andrew Jackson mounted on horseback, sword raised as he rallies American troops, framed by medallions and contrasting red and blue inscriptions. Historical dates “1815” and “1965” appear prominently, reinforcing the commemorative theme. The dynamic composition and vivid multicolor engraving make the issue visually striking even when postally used. This example is used and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1261 presents respectable Fine/Very Fine centering with strong color and an energetic historical design. A small corner crease and shortened perforation reduce soundness modestly, but the very heavy machine cancel does most of the grading damage by obscuring key elements of the vignette. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 43 (Good/Very Good), making it a structurally sound but visually challenged example.', 0.11, 'NA01-0225-02-02-003'),
(471, 'United States', '1264', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '5¢ Black – Winston Churchill, 1965\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1264 was issued in 1965 to honor Sir Winston Churchill and features a finely engraved portrait emphasizing his distinctive expression through strong line work and tonal depth. The monochromatic black design gives the issue a formal, dignified appearance, with “CHURCHILL” prominently displayed above and “U.S. 5 CENTS” below. The restrained presentation reflects the solemn respect associated with the statesman’s legacy. This example is postally used and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1264 combines respectable Fine/Very Fine centering with a strong engraved portrait and only minor structural faults. However, the heavy cancel crossing Churchill’s face significantly reduces visual appeal. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 62 (Very Good/Fine), making it a solid but visibly postally used example of this popular commemorative.', 0.10, 'LA01-0084-01-04-002'),
(472, 'United States', '1265', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '5¢ Multicolor – Magna Carta, 1965\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1265 commemorates the 750th anniversary of the Magna Carta, a cornerstone in the development of constitutional governance. The design presents a stylized medieval procession of barons and knights across a golden band, with a crown centered below in a contrasting violet field. The inscription “MAGNA CARTA 1215” reinforces the historical theme. The bold color blocking and engraved elements combine modern design with historical symbolism. This example is used and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis example of Scott #1265 presents with solid Fine/Very Fine centering and strong structural integrity, supported by clean perforations and only minor reverse issues. The primary detractor is the heavy machine cancel, which crosses key design elements and reduces visual clarity. Despite this, the stamp remains a respectable and collectible example, achieving a Very Good/Fine grade under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.10, 'LA01-0082-03-02-002'),
(474, 'United States', '1266', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '5¢ Blue – International Cooperation Year, 1965\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1266 commemorates International Cooperation Year 1965, highlighting global unity through the United Nations. The design centers on two clasped hands framed by laurel branches, symbolizing peace and collaboration, with “UN” below and the issue title across the top. The engraved blue presentation provides crisp detail and strong symbolic clarity. This example is used and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis example of Scott #1266 offers solid Fine/Very Fine centering and clean structural integrity, supported by intact perforations and only minor reverse issues. The heavy cancel across the central handshake detracts from the design and reduces overall eye appeal. Despite this, the stamp remains a respectable and collectible example, achieving a Very Good/Fine grade under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.10, 'NA01-0225-04-01-003'),
(475, 'United States', '1267', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '5¢ Multicolor – Salvation Army, 1965\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1267 commemorates the 100th anniversary of the Salvation Army, founded in 1865. The design is bold and typographic, featuring “SALVATION ARMY” centrally in black, framed by a red panel above with the anniversary dates and a blue panel below containing the denomination. The phrase “One hundred years of service” reinforces the commemorative purpose. The strong color blocks and clean layout give the stamp a modern, poster-like visual identity. This example is used and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis example of Scott #1267 benefits from Very Fine centering and strong structural integrity, with clean perforations and only minor faults. The heavy machine cancel across the central design reduces eye appeal and limits the final grade. Even so, the stamp remains a solid and collectible example, offering strong centering and recognizable design within the Very Good range under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.08, 'LA01-0084-02-01-002');
INSERT INTO `stamps` (`id`, `country`, `scott`, `count`, `condition`, `hinged`, `gum`, `grade`, `description`, `price`, `location`) VALUES
(476, 'United States', '1267', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '5¢ Multicolor – Salvation Army, 1965\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1267 commemorates the centennial of the Salvation Army, marking one hundred years of humanitarian service from 1865 to 1965. The design is bold and typographic, with “SALVATION ARMY” centered in strong black lettering, framed by a red panel above showing the anniversary dates and a blue panel below containing the denomination. The inscription “One hundred years of service” reinforces the commemorative theme. The clean layout and contrasting colors give the stamp a striking, poster-like visual identity. This example is used and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis example of Scott #1267 shows acceptable Fine/Very Fine centering and retains strong color and design clarity, but the moderate crease significantly impacts structural integrity. The heavy cancel further reduces visual appeal, resulting in a Good grade. Despite these issues, it remains a recognizable and collectible example, particularly as a space-filler within a working collection under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.04, 'NA01-0233-01-01-003'),
(477, 'United States', '1268', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'PG - Partial Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '5¢ Carmine – Dante Alighieri, 1965\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1268 commemorates the 700th anniversary of the birth of Dante Alighieri, the Italian poet best known for The Divine Comedy. The design shows Dante in laurel, seated with manuscript in hand, while a winged figure above displays a “700th Anniversary” banner. Rays, clouds, flame, and an Italian cityscape give the stamp a dramatic, literary atmosphere. The submitted example is used, has been hinged, and shows partial original gum on the reverse.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1268 has solid Fine/Very Fine centering, intact perforations, and an attractive engraved design, but the heavy cancel keeps it from showing at its best. The reverse spotting is minor, and partial gum is noted as condition rather than a grading fault. Overall, it remains a respectable and collectible Very Good/Fine example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.10, 'LA01-0084-02-02-002'),
(478, 'United States', '1268', '003', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fair/Good', '5¢ Carmine – Dante Alighieri, 1965\r\n(Dante 700th Anniversary Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1268 was issued in 1965 to commemorate the 700th anniversary of Dante Alighieri. The engraved design depicts Dante seated with manuscript in hand, accompanied by symbolic imagery including flames and a radiant angel, referencing themes from the Divine Comedy. The inscription “700th Anniversary” appears above, with “United States 5¢” at the base. The carmine color and detailed line engraving create a visually rich and expressive composition.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1268 example shows Fine/Very Fine centering and strong engraved detail, but is significantly impacted by multiple perforation faults. The combination of a pulled perf and additional shortened and nibbed perfs results in a Major Fault classification and a final grade of 29 (Fair/Good). While still collectible, it is best suited as a space-filler example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.02, 'NA01-0233-01-02-003'),
(479, 'United States', '1270', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '5¢ Blue – Robert Fulton, 1965\r\n(Famous Americans Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1270 was issued in 1965 to commemorate Robert Fulton, a pioneer of steam-powered navigation. The engraved design presents Fulton’s portrait at right with a steamboat at left, symbolizing his technological achievement. The typography is bold and balanced, with “ROBERT FULTON 1765–1965” prominently displayed. This example is postally used, has no original gum, and shows a visible machine cancel across the central design.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1270 example presents Fine/Very Fine centering with intact perforations and a clean overall structure aside from a minor reverse ink transfer. The primary detractor is the heavy machine cancel that crosses the portrait, reducing visual appeal. After applying soundness and eye appeal adjustments in accordance with the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the stamp achieves a final grade of 62 (Very Good/Fine), remaining a solid mid-grade collectible example.', 0.10, 'LA01-0082-04-01-002'),
(480, 'United States', '1271', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '5¢ Red – Settlement of Florida, 1965\r\n(Florida Settlement Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1271 commemorates the 400th anniversary of the settlement of Florida, featuring a Spanish explorer raising a banner with heraldic flags and a ship in the background. The bold red field contrasts with the engraved black and gold elements, producing a visually striking mid-1960s commemorative design. This example is postally used, with no original gum, and bears a heavy machine cancel across the design.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1271 shows solid Fine/Very Fine centering and only a minor perf flaw, but the very heavy machine cancel drags it down hard by obscuring much of the design. With the corrected cancel classification, the stamp lands at 57 (Very Good), still respectable—but definitely not winning any beauty contests tonight.', 0.08, 'LA01-0084-02-04-002'),
(481, 'United States', '1272', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '5¢ Green & Black – Traffic Safety, 1965\r\n(Traffic Safety Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1272 was issued in 1965 as a public safety commemorative promoting accident prevention. The design is boldly modern, centered on a stylized traffic signal with a red stop light set against a green field, with the inscription Stop traffic accidents at right and enforcement·education·engineering along the bottom. The strong graphic contrast and simplified forms give the issue a distinctly mid-century look. This example is used, has no original gum, and shows a machine cancel across the face with light ink transfer visible on the reverse.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #1272 is a solid Fine/Very Fine centered example with intact perforations and only a very minor reverse ink transfer fault. Its main drawback is the heavy machine cancel, which crosses the design prominently enough to reduce eye appeal without fully obscuring the subject. After combining centering, soundness, and eye appeal under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the stamp earns a final grade of 64 (Very Good/Fine), remaining a respectable and collectible used example.', 0.10, 'LA01-0082-04-02-002'),
(482, 'United States', '1273', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '5¢ Multicolored – John Copley “Ann Cary Randolph,” 1965\r\n(Famous American Art Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1273 reproduces John Singleton Copley’s portrait of Ann Cary Randolph, rendered in fine engraved detail with soft multicolor tones. The subject is shown in period attire against a dark background, with vertical inscriptions identifying the artist and denomination. The design emphasizes classical portraiture and refined line engraving. This example is unused, has been hinged, and has no original gum, with visible back disturbances and minor faults.\r\n\r\nSummary:\r\nThis example of Scott #1273 shows solid Fine/Very Fine centering and retains a pleasing engraved appearance, but the presence of a stain and additional minor faults results in a Fault classification. With no cancel present and adjustments properly applied, the stamp settles at 45 (Good/Very Good). Not a disaster, but definitely not the one you’d bring to impress anyone at a stamp club meeting either.', 0.05, 'LA01-0084-03-01-002'),
(483, 'United States', '1273', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine', '5¢ Multicolored – John Copley “Ann Cary Randolph,” 1965\r\n(Famous American Art Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1273 reproduces John Singleton Copley’s portrait of Ann Cary Randolph, rendered in fine engraved detail with subtle multicolor shading. The subject is depicted in period dress against a dark background, framed by vertical inscriptions identifying the artist and denomination. The engraving emphasizes texture and tonal depth, typical of mid-century Bureau issues. This example is used, has never been hinged, and has no original gum, with light ink transfer visible on the reverse.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1273 example displays solid Fine/Very Fine centering with intact perforations and a clean, attractive front appearance. The only detracting factor is a very minor ink transfer on the reverse, resulting in a small deduction. With no cancellation to impact eye appeal, the stamp remains a strong collectible example, achieving a final grade of 72 (Fine) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.11, 'NA01-0233-03-02-003'),
(484, 'United States', '1273', '004', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '5¢ Multicolored – John Copley “Ann Cary Randolph,” 1965\r\n(Famous American Art Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1273 reproduces John Singleton Copley’s portrait of Ann Cary Randolph, showing the young subject in period dress against a dark interior background. The design is framed by vertical inscriptions naming Copley as the American artist at left and the denomination at right. The engraved treatment gives the figure soft modeling and fine texture, especially in the dress, hair, and facial features. This example is used, never hinged, and without original gum, with a strong machine cancel and peripheral perforation irregularities.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #1273 has Fine/Very Fine centering with all margins clear of the design, but it is held back by a Minor Fault from several ragged and shortened perforations. The very heavy machine cancel further reduces the stamp’s visual appeal by obscuring much of the portrait. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the combined result is a final grade of 47 (Good/Very Good), making it a collectible but clearly mid-grade example.', 0.05, 'SL01-0001-08-01-004'),
(485, 'United States', '1274', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '11¢ Multicolored – International Telecommunication Union, 1965\r\n(International Telecommunication Union Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1274 commemorates the centennial of the International Telecommunication Union, featuring a stylized world map in gold overlaid with red sinusoidal communication waves and telegraphic symbols. The design emphasizes global connectivity and technological progress, with the dates 1865 and 1965 marking the centennial. The bold 11-cent denomination anchors the lower left. This example is used, has been hinged, and has no original gum, with a visible machine cancel and light ink transfer on the reverse.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1274 example presents Fine/Very Fine centering with intact perforations and only a very minor reverse ink transfer fault. The primary detractor is the heavy machine cancel, which crosses the central design and reduces visual clarity. After applying the appropriate deductions under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the stamp achieves a final grade of 62 (Very Good/Fine), remaining a solid mid-grade collectible example.', 0.10, 'LA01-0082-04-03-002'),
(486, 'United States', '1275', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'PG - Partial Gum', 'Fair/Good', '5¢ Multicolored – Adlai Stevenson, 1965\r\n(Champion of the United Nations Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1275 was issued in 1965 to honor Adlai Stevenson, diplomat, statesman, and United States Ambassador to the United Nations. The design features a finely engraved portrait of Stevenson at right, paired with the United Nations emblem in pale blue at upper left and his surname prominently across the top. The red and blue bands at the bottom add a restrained modern graphic accent. This example is used and shows partial original gum on the reverse, with a strong machine cancel across the face and thinning noted on the back.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1275 example has Fine/Very Fine centering with all margins clear of the design, but its grade is heavily reduced by a Major Fault from thinning on the reverse. The very heavy machine cancel further lowers eye appeal by obscuring much of the portrait. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the combined result is a final grade of 22 (Fair/Good), making this a clearly fault-affected but still identifiable collectible example.', 0.02, 'LA01-0084-03-02-002'),
(487, 'United States', '1276', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine', '5¢ Multicolored – Christmas Angel, 1965\r\n(Christmas Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1276 was issued in 1965 as part of the annual Christmas stamp program. The design features a stylized angel in flight blowing a trumpet, adapted from a traditional Christmas motif. Printed in multiple colors, the composition combines green and gold design elements with red inscriptions, creating a festive and visually distinctive appearance. The simplicity of the artwork emphasizes centering, color, and condition. This example is postally used and remains attractive despite minor condition issues.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #1276 displays Fine/Very Fine centering, with the top margin establishing the centering limit and the right margin providing the greatest spacing. A single perforation-tip crease places the stamp in the Very Minor Fault category and reduces the preliminary grade to Fine 72. The light machine cancellation has minimal impact on presentation and does not warrant an additional adjustment. The final assigned grade is Fine 72, representing an attractive and collectible used example of this 1965 Christmas Angel commemorative issue.', 0.11, 'LA01-0084-04-02-002'),
(488, 'United States', '1276', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine', '5¢ Multicolored – Christmas Angel, 1965\r\n(Christmas Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1276 depicts a stylized angel in flight sounding a trumpet, rendered in green and gold against a white field with bold red inscriptions reading “5¢ U.S. POSTAGE” and “CHRISTMAS.” Issued in 1965 as part of the early U.S. Christmas stamp program, the design emphasizes simplicity and symbolic clarity, representing announcement and celebration. The engraving is crisp, with fine line detail visible in the wings and garment folds. The submitted example is unused, with no original gum, and has never been hinged.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1276 is a solid Fine example with respectable Fine/Very Fine centering and only a minor reverse fault. The clean front, strong engraving, and balanced margins contribute to good overall eye appeal. While the lack of gum and light reverse smudging limit the grade, the stamp remains an attractive and collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.11, 'NA01-0233-04-01-003'),
(489, 'United States', '1276', '004', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine', '5¢ Multicolored – Christmas Angel, 1965\r\n(Christmas Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1276 depicts a stylized angel in flight sounding a trumpet, rendered in green and gold with bold red inscriptions reading “5¢ U.S. POSTAGE” and “CHRISTMAS.” Issued in 1965 as one of the early U.S. Christmas stamps, the design emphasizes clarity and symbolism, representing proclamation and celebration. The engraving is clean, with fine detail visible in the figure and wings. The submitted example is used, with no original gum, and has never been hinged.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1276 example is a solid Fine stamp with Fine/Very Fine centering and only a minor reverse fault. The light machine cancel and strong visual clarity support good eye appeal. While the slight reverse smudging limits the technical grade, the stamp remains an attractive and collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.11, 'SL01-0001-04-01-004'),
(490, 'United States', '1276', '005', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine', '5¢ Multicolored – Christmas Angel, 1965\r\n(Christmas Issue – Engraved and Offset Combination, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1276 features a stylized Christmas angel in flight, blowing a trumpet, rendered in green and gold tones against a white background. The inscription “5¢ U.S. POSTAGE” appears at the top, with “CHRISTMAS” prominently displayed at the bottom in red. The design is simple yet symbolic, evoking traditional holiday imagery with a modern graphic approach. This example is used, never hinged as submitted, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1276 example exhibits Fine/Very Fine centering with all perforations clear of the design, though slightly tighter on the right and top. The light cancellation bleed-through results in only a minor deduction, and the relatively unobtrusive cancel allows the colorful holiday design to remain clear and appealing. Overall, it is a pleasing and collectible example, achieving a final grade of 74 (Fine) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.11, 'SL01-0002-02-04-005'),
(491, 'United States', '1278', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '1¢ Green – Thomas Jefferson, 1968\r\n(Prominent Americans Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1278 features a finely engraved portrait of Thomas Jefferson, presented in a formal bust with strong linear shading and a clean rectangular frame. The inscriptions run vertically along both sides, giving the design a balanced but somewhat constrained visual structure. Issued as part of the Prominent Americans series, it reflects mid-20th century Bureau engraving precision. This example is used, with no original gum, and shows a visible cancel along with minor condition issues affecting overall grade.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used example of Scott #1278 presents with solid Fine/Very Fine centering and a clean, non-obtrusive cancel, but is notably affected by multiple perforation creases that reduce its structural integrity. The resulting Minor Fault classification significantly lowers the overall grade to 58 (Very Good). Despite these faults, the stamp retains acceptable visual appeal and remains a collectible mid-grade example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.08, 'LA01-0085-01-01-002'),
(492, 'United States', '1278', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine', '1¢ Green – Thomas Jefferson, 1968\r\n(Prominent Americans Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1278 presents a finely engraved portrait of Thomas Jefferson, framed by vertical inscriptions and a bold numeral denomination. The design reflects the clean, structured aesthetic of the Prominent Americans series, with strong line engraving and balanced proportions. This example is postally used, with no original gum, and retains a clear overall impression despite moderate cancellation.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1278 example demonstrates respectable Fine/Very Fine centering and strong overall structural integrity, with only a minor perforation irregularity. The primary detractor is the cancel placement across the portrait, which slightly diminishes visual appeal. The resulting grade of 70 (Fine) reflects a solid, collectible example that remains well within acceptable standards under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.11, 'NA01-0227-01-01-003'),
(493, 'United States', '1280', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '2¢ Blue – Frank Lloyd Wright, 1966\r\n(Prominent Americans Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1280 presents a finely engraved portrait of architect Frank Lloyd Wright, set against stylized architectural forms that reflect his design philosophy. The composition is modern and balanced, with strong line engraving and clear inscriptions. This example is postally used, with no original gum, and retains a bold overall appearance despite noticeable cancellation and condition issues.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1280 example shows Fine/Very Fine centering and retains a strong engraved design, but is significantly impacted by a heavy cancel across the portrait and multiple irregular perforations. These factors reduce both technical grade and visual appeal, resulting in a final grade of 49 (Good/Very Good). It remains a lower-grade but still collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.05, 'LA01-0085-01-04-003'),
(494, 'United States', '1280', '004', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '2¢ Blue – Frank Lloyd Wright, 1966\r\n(Prominent Americans Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1280 depicts Frank Lloyd Wright in a finely engraved portrait against a stylized architectural background that evokes his organic modern aesthetic. The name inscription spans the top, while the denomination and postage inscription anchor the lower left. The design is sharply structured and typical of the Prominent Americans series. This example is used, has no original gum, and shows a readable postmark along with a light crease that affects its technical soundness.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1280 is a reasonably centered used example with clear Fine/Very Fine margin presentation and an attractive engraved design. The light crease is the principal grade-limiting factor, and the cancel modestly reduces eye appeal by crossing the portrait. Even so, the stamp remains a collectible example with a final grade of 53 (Very Good) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.08, 'NA01-0227-01-03-004'),
(495, 'United States', '1280', '005', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Good', '2¢ Blue – Frank Lloyd Wright, 1966\r\n(Prominent Americans Series – Engraved, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1280 depicts architect Frank Lloyd Wright, one of America’s most influential designers, shown in a finely engraved portrait with architectural forms in the background suggestive of his organic design philosophy. The layout follows the Prominent Americans series style, with the subject’s name across the top, denomination at lower left, and “U.S. POSTAGE” beneath. The engraving is crisp, with strong line work and a balanced composition typical of mid-20th century Bureau issues.\r\nThis example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum. However, the reverse shows visible discoloration consistent with staining, affecting overall condition.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1280 example benefits from Very Fine centering, with full margins and a well-balanced overall appearance from the front. However, a significant staining issue on the reverse, along with multiple perf creases, results in a Major Fault classification that substantially reduces the final grade to 37 (Good). Despite these condition issues, the stamp retains collectible value as a presentable example of the issue under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.04, 'SL01-0001-03-04-005'),
(496, 'United States', '1281', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '3¢ Violet – Francis Parkman, 1967\r\n(Prominent Americans Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1281 presents a finely engraved portrait of historian Francis Parkman, with bold vertical inscriptions occupying the left side and a right-facing portrait dominating the design. The composition is characteristic of the Prominent Americans series, combining strong typography with detailed engraving. This example is unused, has been hinged, and retains partial original gum. The design remains clear and well defined, though condition issues affect the technical grade.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1281 example presents as a lower-end Fine/Very Fine centered stamp with a clear and attractive engraved design. The multiple perforation creases are the primary grade-limiting factor, resulting in a Minor Fault classification and a reduced overall grade. With a final grade of 53 (Very Good), it remains a collectible example, though better suited to mid-grade collections under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.08, 'LA01-0085-01-03-002'),
(497, 'United States', '1281', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '3¢ Violet – Francis Parkman, 1967\r\n(Prominent Americans Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1281 features a finely engraved portrait of historian Francis Parkman, with bold vertical inscriptions at left and a detailed right-facing portrait. The design reflects the structured, text-and-portrait balance typical of the Prominent Americans series. The engraving is crisp with strong line clarity. This example is used, has no original gum, and shows a light cancel with visible reverse bleed-through.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1281 presents with Fine/Very Fine centering and a clean overall appearance, supported by a light cancel that does not significantly interfere with the design. However, the presence of two shortened perforations and reverse bleed-through places the stamp in the Minor Fault category, limiting the final grade to 58 (Very Good). It remains a solid mid-grade collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.08, 'NA01-0227-02-01-003'),
(498, 'United States', '1281', '005', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Fine/Extra Fine', '3¢ Violet – Francis Parkman, 1965\r\n(Prominent Americans Series – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1281 features a finely engraved portrait of Francis Parkman, noted American historian, facing right. The design is vertically oriented on the left with the inscription “FRANCIS PARKMAN AMERICAN HISTORIAN,” balanced by the portrait occupying the right side. The engraving is crisp, with strong line detail and a clean, formal presentation typical of the Prominent Americans series. This example is used, never hinged as submitted, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1281 example displays strong Very Fine/Extremely Fine centering with well-balanced margins and a clean, attractive presentation. The single shortened perforation is a minimal defect and results in only a small deduction. With no visible cancellation and excellent engraving detail, the stamp benefits from enhanced eye appeal, resulting in a final grade of 87 (Very Fine/Extremely Fine) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.17, 'SL01-0002-02-05-005'),
(499, 'United States', '1282', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair', '4¢ Gray – Abraham Lincoln, 1966\r\n(Prominent Americans Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1282 features a finely engraved right-facing portrait of Abraham Lincoln set against a stylized woodgrain background, evoking a rustic and historical tone. The bold “LINCOLN” inscription at the top and the denomination at lower left frame the composition. The engraving is detailed and characteristic of the Prominent Americans series. This example is used, with no original gum, and shows a visible cancel along with significant structural faults.\r\n\r\nSummary:\r\nThis Scott #1282 presents with solid Fine/Very Fine centering and a recognizable engraved design, but is heavily impacted by a severe crease that dominates its condition. Additional perforation creasing and a moderate cancel further affect its appearance. The resulting grade of 18 (Fair) reflects a significantly compromised example, though it remains identifiable and collectible as a low-grade filler under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.02, 'LA01-0085-01-05-002'),
(500, 'United States', '1283', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '5¢ Blue – George Washington, 1967\r\n(Prominent Americans Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1283 features a finely engraved portrait of George Washington in a soft blue tone, set against a stippled background that gives the design a lighter, almost airy texture. The vertical “UNITED STATES” inscription at left and denomination at upper right frame the portrait in a clean, modern layout typical of the Prominent Americans series. This example is used, with no original gum, and shows a light cancellation with otherwise strong visual presence.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1283 combines strong Fine/Very Fine centering with a light, well-placed cancel that preserves the clarity of the engraved design. Although multiple perforation creases reduce its technical standing to the Minor Fault category, the stamp maintains good visual appeal. The final grade of 62 (Very Good/Fine) reflects a balanced example with both strengths and limitations under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.10, 'LA01-0085-02-01-002'),
(501, 'United States', '1283B', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '5¢ Blue – George Washington, 1967\r\n(Prominent Americans Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked, Booklet Pane Issue)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1283B depicts George Washington in a finely engraved portrait set against a softly stippled blue background, with the vertical “UNITED STATES” inscription at left and the denomination at upper right. The booklet pane version retains the same dignified design as the regular issue, with the crisp Bureau engraving and balanced layout typical of the Prominent Americans series. This example is used, has no original gum, and shows a visible cancel with additional perforation-related faults.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1283B is a Fine/Very Fine centered used booklet issue with a clear engraved portrait of George Washington and decent overall balance. Its grade is limited by multiple perforation creases and one short perf, while the vertical cancel modestly reduces eye appeal. The final grade of 54 (Very Good) reflects a sound mid-grade collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.08, 'LA01-0085-02-02-002'),
(502, 'United States', '1284', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '6¢ Brown – Franklin D. Roosevelt, 1966\r\n(Prominent Americans Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1284 features a finely engraved portrait of Franklin D. Roosevelt facing left, rendered with the detailed crosshatching and tonal depth characteristic of Bureau of Engraving and Printing production. The denomination appears at upper left, while the vertical inscription identifying Roosevelt runs along the right side of the design. The compact layout creates a dignified presentation well suited to the Prominent Americans series. This example is postally used and remains visually attractive despite condition issues affecting the perforations.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #1284 displays Fine/Very Fine centering, with the bottom margin establishing the centering limit and the top margin providing the greatest spacing. Multiple perforation creases and irregular perforation tips place the stamp in the Fault category and reduce the preliminary grade to 47. A light cancellation and strong engraved appearance improve overall presentation and warrant a three-point eye appeal adjustment. The final assigned grade is Very Good 50, representing a presentable used example whose grade is primarily limited by perforation-related faults.', 0.08, 'NA01-0227-02-05-002'),
(503, 'United States', '1284', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine/Very Fine', '6¢ Brown – Franklin D. Roosevelt, 1966\r\n(Prominent Americans Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1284 features a finely engraved left-facing portrait of Franklin D. Roosevelt, rendered with tight crosshatching and subtle tonal gradation typical of Bureau engraving. The denomination appears at upper left, while the vertical inscription “FRANKLIN D. ROOSEVELT U.S. POSTAGE” runs along the right side, balancing the composition. The design is compact and formal, emphasizing Roosevelt’s profile with minimal background detail. This example is used, has no original gum, and shows a light cancel that allows the portrait and inscriptions to remain clearly visible.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1284 presents with Fine/Very Fine centering and a light cancel that preserves the clarity of Roosevelt’s portrait and inscriptions. The single shortened perforation results in only a minor deduction and does not materially impact the overall appearance. With strong engraving detail and balanced margins, the stamp remains an attractive and collectible example, earning a final grade of 75 (Fine/Very Fine) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.13, 'LA01-0085-02-03-003'),
(504, 'United States', '1285', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair', '8¢ Violet – Albert Einstein, 1966\r\n(Prominent Americans Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1285 features a finely engraved portrait of Albert Einstein in a frontal three-quarter view, with his distinctive hair and contemplative expression clearly rendered. “United States” and the denomination appear across the top, while “EINSTEIN” is set vertically at right. The design is compact and modern, with strong line engraving and subtle tonal shading typical of the Prominent Americans series. This example is used, has no original gum, and bears a visible cancel across the face, while also showing notable perforation-related faults.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1285 presents with acceptable Very Good centering and a clearly identifiable portrait despite a moderate cancel. However, the presence of multiple perforation creases and irregular, shortened perfs significantly impacts structural integrity and overall appearance. These combined faults place the stamp firmly in the Fault category, limiting its desirability primarily to a space-filling example, with a final grade of 19 (Fair).', 0.02, 'LA01-0085-02-04-002'),
(505, 'United States', '1286', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '10¢ Bright Lilac – Andrew Jackson, 1967\r\n(Prominent Americans Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1286 presents a finely engraved portrait of Andrew Jackson in a three-quarter view, framed within a clean, modern vertical layout characteristic of the Prominent Americans series. The denomination is positioned at upper left, “UNITED STATES” runs vertically along the left margin, and “ANDREW JACKSON” is prominently displayed across the top. The engraving is detailed and balanced, with strong line clarity and tonal depth. This example is used, hinged, and without original gum, showing a moderate cancel and some minor perforation issues that affect its technical standing.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1286 is a solid mid-grade example with Fine/Very Fine centering and a clear, well-defined engraved portrait. However, multiple perforation creases and a moderately heavy cancel reduce both structural integrity and visual balance. While still collectible and presentable, these combined factors limit the stamp to a final grade of 55 (Very Good), reflecting a technically sound but visibly affected example.', 0.08, 'LA01-0085-02-05-002'),
(506, 'United States', '1286A', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '12¢ Black – Henry Ford, 1968\r\n(Prominent Americans Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1286A features a detailed engraved portrait of Henry Ford in profile, paired with an early automobile in the background, symbolizing his role in industrial innovation. The denomination appears at upper right, “UNITED STATES” spans the top, and “HENRY FORD” anchors the lower portion. The engraving is precise, with strong line work and balanced shading typical of the Prominent Americans series. This example is used, hinged, and without original gum, showing a moderate cancel and clear evidence of structural faults on the reverse.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1286A offers strong Very Fine centering with full margins and a clearly rendered engraved design, but its technical quality is heavily compromised by a large thin on the reverse and multiple perforation creases. These faults place the stamp firmly in the Major Fault category, significantly reducing both structural integrity and desirability. While the portrait remains identifiable and the margins are attractive, the combined defects limit the stamp to a final grade of 32 (Good), reflecting a visually decent but materially flawed example.', 0.04, 'LA01-0085-04-02-002'),
(507, 'United States', '1287', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '13¢ Brown – John F. Kennedy, 1967\r\n(Prominent Americans Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1287, issued in 1967 as part of the Prominent Americans series, features a finely engraved portrait of President John F. Kennedy in three-quarter view. Strong cross-hatched line work creates tonal depth and clarity, while “JOHN F. KENNEDY” appears vertically at right and “UNITED STATES” at the bottom. The 13¢ denomination is placed at lower left. Printed in brown, the stamp presents a dignified and balanced design characteristic of the series. This example is postally used and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1287 combines strong Fine/Very Fine centering with a clear, well-defined engraved portrait of John F. Kennedy. Multiple perforation creases reduce soundness to the Minor Fault category, though the moderate cancel remains unobtrusive and preserves the stamp’s visual appeal. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the final grade is 60 (Very Good/Fine), making it a pleasing and collectible used example.', 0.10, 'LA01-0085-03-01-002'),
(508, 'United States', '1288', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '15¢ Rose Red – Oliver Wendell Holmes, 1968\r\n(Prominent Americans Series – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1288 features a detailed engraved portrait of Oliver Wendell Holmes, one of the most influential American jurists. Issued in 1968 as part of the Prominent Americans Series, the design emphasizes clarity and strong line engraving, with inscriptions “U.S. POSTAGE,” the denomination, and the subject’s name. The portrait dominates the right side, making centering and cancellation placement visually significant. The submitted example is used, with no original gum, and shows a visible machine-style cancellation across the design.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1288 example exhibits Fine/Very Fine centering with clear margins, though slightly unbalanced. The presence of multiple perf creases and shortened perforations results in a Minor Fault classification, significantly reducing the grade. A moderately heavy machine cancel further impacts eye appeal. Despite these issues, the stamp retains strong color and recognizable design detail, making it a solid Very Good example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.08, 'LA01-0085-03-02-002'),
(509, 'United States', '1289', '002', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Very Good', '20¢ Olive Green – George C. Marshall, 1968\r\n(Prominent Americans Series – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1289 depicts George C. Marshall, a key military leader and statesman of the 20th century, in a finely engraved portrait. Issued in 1968 as part of the Prominent Americans Series, the design features strong line engraving, bold lettering, and a balanced layout with “STATESMAN SOLDIER” and “UNITED STATES” framing the portrait. The olive-green ink provides solid contrast and detail. The submitted example is unused, hinged, with partial original gum, and shows visible back condition issues affecting overall preservation.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1289 example displays Very Fine centering with full margins and only slight imbalance, particularly on the right side. However, heavy dirt and spotting on the reverse significantly impact soundness, resulting in a Minor Fault classification. A small corner perf crease adds a secondary issue. While the front retains acceptable color and detail, the overall presentation is reduced by the back condition, yielding a final grade of 57 (Very Good). It remains a serviceable example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, though not without noticeable condition limitations.', 0.16, 'LA01-0085-04-01-002'),
(510, 'United States', '1290', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine', '25¢ Red Brown – Frederick Douglass, 1968\r\n(Prominent Americans Series – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1290 presents a finely engraved portrait of Frederick Douglass, the influential abolitionist, writer, and statesman. Issued in 1968 as part of the Prominent Americans Series, the design features a strong profile portrait with bold inscriptions and detailed line engraving. The red-brown ink highlights the depth of the engraving, especially in the facial features and clothing. The submitted example is used, without original gum, and shows a visible machine-applied cancellation across the central portion of the design.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1290 example displays Very Fine centering with full margins and only slight imbalance, tightest on the left side. A minor perf tip crease results in a small deduction, while a moderately heavy machine cancel across the portrait further impacts eye appeal. Despite these factors, the stamp retains good color and recognizable detail, making it a solid Fine example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.11, 'LA01-0085-04-03-002'),
(511, 'United States', '1291', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '30¢ Violet – John Dewey, 1968\r\n(Prominent Americans Series – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1291 features an engraved portrait of John Dewey, a leading American philosopher and educational reformer. Issued in 1968 as part of the Prominent Americans Series, the design uses a modern layout with bold block lettering and a detailed engraved portrait in violet ink. The clean composition places emphasis on the subject’s face, making centering and cancellation placement highly visible. The submitted example is used, without original gum, and shows a circular machine cancellation across the upper left portion of the design.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1291 example shows Fine/Very Fine centering with clear but slightly uneven margins, tightest at the top and most open at the left. Multiple perf creases result in a Minor Fault classification and a significant deduction, while a moderately heavy cancel further impacts eye appeal. Despite these issues, the stamp maintains recognizable detail and acceptable color, making it a solid Very Good example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.12, 'LA01-0085-05-03-002'),
(512, 'United States', '1291', '003', 'Unused', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '30¢ Violet – John Dewey, 1968\r\n(Prominent Americans Series – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1291 features an engraved portrait of John Dewey, the influential American philosopher and educator. The design is executed in a horizontal format, with bold typography at upper left and the denomination at lower left. Fine engraved lines create depth and texture in the portrait, while the clean layout reflects the restrained style of the Prominent Americans series. This example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1291 example presents acceptable Fine/Very Fine centering with a clear engraved portrait and original never hinged gum. However, the presence of multiple shortened and ragged perforations, along with minor back issues, results in a Fault classification and a significant deduction. The final grade of 46 (Good/Very Good) reflects a balance between decent centering and compromised edge quality, leaving it as a modest but still collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.35, 'NA01-0227-05-03-003'),
(513, 'United States', '1292', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '40¢ Blue – Thomas Paine, 1968\r\n(Prominent Americans Series – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1292 features a finely engraved portrait of Thomas Paine, an influential political writer and revolutionary figure. Issued in 1968 as part of the Prominent Americans Series, the design presents Paine in a formal bust portrait with script lettering and a textured engraved background. The blue ink highlights the detail of the engraving. The submitted example is used, without original gum, and shows a machine-applied cancellation across portions of the design.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1292 example presents Fine/Very Fine centering with slightly uneven margins, tightest on the right side and most open at the top. Multiple perf creases and shortened perforations result in a Minor Fault classification and a significant deduction, further impacted by a moderately heavy cancellation. While the stamp retains recognizable detail and acceptable color, these combined issues reduce overall appeal. It remains a solid Very Good example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.12, 'LA01-0085-05-01-002'),
(514, 'United States', '1292', '003', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '40¢ Blue – Thomas Paine, 1967\r\n(Prominent Americans Series – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1292 features an engraved portrait of Thomas Paine, rendered in a classic bust format with fine line engraving and a textured background. The script “Thomas Paine” at the top contrasts with the bold “U.S. 40¢” denomination at the bottom, creating a balanced and visually appealing composition. The engraving is sharp and consistent, typical of the Prominent Americans series. This example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1292 example exhibits pleasing Fine/Very Fine centering with clean perforations and a well-executed engraved portrait. The only noted issue is an extremely minor gum bend, resulting in a minimal deduction and a final grade of 73 (Fine). With its solid eye appeal and original gum, it remains a desirable and collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.71, 'NA01-0227-05-01-003'),
(515, 'United States', '1293', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '50¢ Carmine – Lucy Stone, 1968\r\n(Prominent Americans Series – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1293 depicts Lucy Stone, a prominent American suffragist and abolitionist, in a finely engraved portrait. The design features a textured background with detailed line engraving and the inscription “LUCY STONE” along the bottom. The denomination is clearly displayed at right. The engraving is sharp, with good clarity in facial detail and consistent ink application. This example is used, without original gum, as expected for a postally handled stamp.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1293 example presents solid Fine/Very Fine centering with a clean, face-free appearance and strong engraved detail. While toning on the reverse and a shortened perforation introduce a Minor Fault classification, the light cancel enhances overall eye appeal. The resulting grade of 62 (Very Good/Fine) reflects a balance between soundness issues and a visually pleasing front, making it a respectable collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.16, 'LA01-0085-05-02-002'),
(516, 'United States', '1294', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '50¢ Violet – Eugene O’Neill, 1968\r\n(Prominent Americans Series – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1294 portrays Eugene O’Neill, the distinguished American playwright and Nobel Prize recipient, in a strong engraved portrait framed by bold lettering including “PLAYWRIGHT” and “UNITED STATES.” Issued in 1968 as part of the Prominent Americans Series, the design uses deep violet ink to emphasize contrast and engraved detail. The submitted example is used, without original gum, and bears a machine-applied cancellation across the portrait and upper design.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1294 example shows Fine/Very Fine centering with slightly uneven margins, tightest at the top and most open at the bottom. Multiple perf creases and shortened perforations result in a Minor Fault classification and a substantial deduction. A moderately heavy machine cancel further impacts the visual presentation. Despite these issues, the stamp retains identifiable detail and acceptable color, making it a solid Very Good example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.12, 'LA01-0085-05-04-002');
INSERT INTO `stamps` (`id`, `country`, `scott`, `count`, `condition`, `hinged`, `gum`, `grade`, `description`, `price`, `location`) VALUES
(517, 'United States', '1294', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Poor', '50¢ Violet – Eugene O’Neill, 1968\r\n(Prominent Americans Series – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1294 depicts Eugene O’Neill, the noted American playwright, in a strong engraved portrait framed by bold lettering including “PLAYWRIGHT” and “UNITED STATES.” Issued in 1968 as part of the Prominent Americans Series, the design uses deep violet ink to emphasize contrast and detail. The submitted example is used, without original gum, and shows a heavy machine-applied cancellation across the central design along with significant structural disturbance visible from both front and reverse.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1294 example shows Fine/Very Fine centering with slightly uneven margins, tightest at the top and most open at the bottom. However, severe creasing, significant perforation damage, and heavy cancellation with bleed-through result in a Severe Fault classification and substantial deductions. The overall presentation is heavily compromised, placing the stamp in the Poor category. Despite identifiable design elements, it serves primarily as a low-grade placeholder example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.01, 'NA01-0227-05-04-003'),
(518, 'United States', '1294', '004', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '$1.00 Violet – Eugene O’Neill, 1967\r\n(Prominent Americans Series – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1294 features an engraved portrait of playwright Eugene O’Neill in a contemplative pose, rendered with strong line work and a textured background. The design incorporates bold inscriptions including “EUGENE O’NEILL,” “UNITED STATES,” and “ONE DOLLAR,” with the denomination expressed textually rather than numerically. The engraving is crisp and detailed, consistent with the Prominent Americans series, and presents a refined, dignified composition. This example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1294 example represents the high-value $1 denomination of the Prominent Americans series and displays pleasing Fine/Very Fine centering with intact perforations and a sharply engraved portrait. The only noted issue is an extremely minor gum bend, resulting in a small deduction and a final grade of 73 (Fine). With its solid eye appeal and original gum, it remains a desirable and collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 2.14, 'SL01-0001-06-04-004'),
(519, 'United States', '1297', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair/Good', '3¢ Violet – Francis Parkman, 1967\r\n(Prominent Americans Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1297 depicts Francis Parkman, an American historian, in a finely engraved violet portrait with vertical inscriptions identifying his name and profession. Issued in 1967 as part of the Prominent Americans series, the stamp reflects the clean engraving and balanced layout typical of the period. This example is used, hinged, and without original gum, with visible condition issues affecting both front and reverse.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1297 example benefits from Fine/Very Fine centering and a readable, unobtrusive cancel, but is significantly impacted by a severe stain and multiple perforation creases. These faults dominate the overall condition, resulting in a final grade of 24 (Fair/Good). Despite its structural issues, it remains a complete and identifiable example within the framework of the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.02, 'LA01-0086-01-03-002'),
(520, 'United States', '1298', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair/Good', '6¢ Black – Franklin D. Roosevelt, 1966\r\n(Prominent Americans Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1298 features a left-facing engraved portrait of Franklin D. Roosevelt, with a simple yet formal layout typical of the Prominent Americans series. The denomination appears at upper left, with vertical inscriptions along the right side. Issued in 1966, the stamp reflects mid-century Bureau engraving clarity and precision. This example is used, hinged, and without original gum, with visible handling and usage effects.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1298 example shows Very Good centering with a tight bottom margin and uneven balance overall. The presence of multiple perforation creases significantly affects soundness, and the horizontal cancel across the portrait reduces eye appeal. The final grade of 27 (Fair/Good) reflects these combined factors under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, resulting in a modest but still collectible example.', 0.02, 'LA01-0086-02-01-002'),
(521, 'United States', '1298', '003', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Good', '6¢ Olive Black – Franklin D. Roosevelt, 1968\r\n(Prominent Americans Series – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1298 depicts Franklin D. Roosevelt in left-facing profile in the clean, restrained style of the Prominent Americans series. The design places the denomination at upper left and the country inscription vertically at right beside Roosevelt’s name, with the portrait occupying most of the stamp’s field. The engraved line work is crisp and direct, giving the issue a dignified and formal appearance appropriate to the subject. This example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1298 example has Very Good/Fine centering, with the bottom margin the limiting side and the left margin the most open. Its overall condition is reduced by several ragged and shortened perforations, which place it in the Minor Fault category, while a minor natural gum bend adds only slight additional concern. With no extra eye appeal adjustment applied, the stamp finishes at 36 (Good). Even with its faults, it remains a collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.05, 'NA01-0229-03-03-003'),
(522, 'United States', '1299', '003', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '1¢ Green – Thomas Jefferson, 1968\r\n(Prominent Americans Series – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1299 presents a sharply engraved portrait of Thomas Jefferson in a formal frontal pose, framed by bold vertical inscriptions and a large numeral 1 at lower right. The design is clean and spare, in keeping with the Prominent Americans series, with strong line work and a balanced rectangular format that emphasizes portrait clarity over ornament. This example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1299 example shows Fine/Very Fine centering with the left margin as the limiting side and the top margin the most generous. Light reverse smudging or spotting, together with a minor natural gum bend, places the stamp in the Very Minor Fault category, yielding a final grade of 72 (Fine). Even with those small back issues, it remains a pleasing and collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.15, 'NA01-0229-01-01-003'),
(523, 'United States', '1304', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '5¢ Blue – George Washington, 1967\r\n(Prominent Americans Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1304 depicts George Washington in a finely engraved blue portrait, with UNITED STATES reading vertically at left, WASHINGTON across the top, and the 5¢ denomination at upper right. Issued in 1967 as part of the Prominent Americans series, it is a cleanly executed mid-century definitive with strong line engraving and soft tonal shading behind the portrait. This example is used, never hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1304 is a well-centered used example with attractive margins for the issue and only a minor perf tip crease from a soundness standpoint. Its main drawback is the heavy horizontal machine cancel crossing Washington’s face, which lowers the overall eye appeal. Even so, the stamp remains a solid collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, with a final grade of 65 (Very Good/Fine).', 0.10, 'LA01-0086-01-04-002'),
(524, 'United States', '1305', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine', '6¢ Black – Franklin D. Roosevelt, 1966\r\n(Prominent Americans Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1305 presents a left-facing engraved portrait of Franklin D. Roosevelt, with U.S. POSTAGE and the 6¢ denomination at upper left and the subject’s name along the bottom. Issued in 1966 as part of the Prominent Americans series, the design features fine line engraving and a balanced, horizontal layout. This example is used, hinged, and without original gum, showing typical postal handling and usage.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1305 stands out for its strong Very Fine/Extremely Fine centering, with full margins and a well-balanced appearance. However, multiple perforation creases introduce a Minor Fault that significantly reduces the overall grade. The final grade of 73 (Fine) reflects a stamp with excellent centering but condition limitations, remaining a solid and collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.11, 'LA01-0086-02-04-002'),
(525, 'United States', '1305E', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '15¢ Rose – Oliver Wendell Holmes, 1968\r\n(Prominent Americans Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1305E features a detailed engraved portrait of Oliver Wendell Holmes, with U.S. POSTAGE and the 15¢ denomination at upper left and the subject’s name at left. Issued in 1968 as part of the Prominent Americans series, the design is bold and portrait-driven, with strong line work and a rich rose coloration. This example is used, never hinged, and without original gum, showing typical postal handling and reverse transfer.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1305E presents with solid Fine/Very Fine centering and a clear, well-defined portrait of Oliver Wendell Holmes. However, multiple minor faults, including reverse smudging, ink transfer, and shortened perforations, combine to reduce the soundness classification. The final grade of 58 (Very Good) reflects a visually decent but condition-limited example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.08, 'LA01-0086-02-02-002'),
(526, 'United States', 'C25', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '6¢ Carmine – Transport Plane in Flight, 1941\r\n(First Airmail Issue of the WWII-Era Transport Series – Perf 11×10½)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #C25 was issued in 1941 as the first stamp in the Transport Plane airmail series. The design depicts a Douglas DC-4 Skymaster in flight, reflecting the growing importance of air transportation for both civilian mail service and national defense. Its bold geometric composition and strong engraved lines create a distinctive appearance among United States airmail issues. Printed in carmine, the stamp remains a popular collectible due to its aviation theme and wartime-era significance. This example is postally used and displays the expected absence of original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #C25 displays Fine/Very Fine centering, with the top margin establishing the centering limit and the side margins remaining well balanced. Ragged and shortened perforations together with reverse discoloration place the stamp in the Fault category and reduce the preliminary grade to 45. A heavy slogan cancellation further reduces eye appeal by five points. The final assigned grade is Good/Very Good 40, representing a collectible but condition-challenged example of this classic 1941 Transport Plane airmail issue.', 0.05, 'LA01-0517-04-01-003'),
(527, 'United States', 'C54', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine', '7¢ Red & Blue – \"Jupiter\" Balloon Airmail, 1959\r\n(Issued for the 100th Anniversary of America\'s First Official Airmail Flight – Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #C54 was issued in 1959 to commemorate the centennial of the historic Jupiter balloon flight from Lafayette, Indiana, recognized as the first official airmail delivery in the United States. The vertical design features a bright red balloon ascending above a crowd of spectators, accompanied by patriotic flags and bold commemorative inscriptions. The contrasting red and blue colors create a visually striking presentation that remains one of the most attractive modern United States airmail commemoratives. This example is postally used and displays the expected absence of original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #C54 displays Fine/Very Fine centering, with the left and bottom margins establishing the centering limit and the top margin providing the greatest spacing. The assigned Very Minor Fault classification reduces the preliminary grade to Fine 72. A moderate machine cancellation lowers presentation by five points, while the stamp\'s vibrant color, clean face, and strong overall appearance restore four points through eye appeal. The final assigned grade is Fine 71, representing an attractive used example of this popular 1959 Jupiter Balloon Airmail commemorative.', 0.11, 'LA01-0519-02-01-003'),
(528, 'United States', 'C54', '004', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '7¢ Red & Blue – Airmail: 100th Anniversary of Balloon Jupiter Flight, 1959\r\n(Airmail Commemorative Issue – Offset Lithography, Perf 11×10½)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #C54 commemorates the centennial of the historic Jupiter balloon flight of 1859, recognized as the first official airmail delivery in the United States. The vertically oriented design features the bright red balloon rising above a crowd, flanked by American flags and framed by patriotic inscriptions. The contrasting red and blue colors create a bold visual presentation that remains one of the most recognizable United States airmail commemoratives of the period. This example is unused, never hinged, and retains full original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #C54 displays Fine/Very Fine centering, with the bottom margin establishing the centering limit and the right margin providing the greatest spacing. A light reverse ink offset places the stamp in the Very Minor Fault category and reduces the preliminary grade to Fine 72. As an unused, never hinged example, cancellation is not applicable, and the strong color, fresh gum, and clean presentation warrant no additional adjustment. The final assigned grade is Fine 72, representing an attractive mint example of this popular 1959 Jupiter Balloon Airmail commemorative.', 0.22, 'NA01-C015-01-01-004'),
(529, 'United States', 'C65', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '8¢ Carmine – Jetliner Over Capitol Dome, 1963\r\n(Regular Airmail Issue – Engraved, Perf 11×10½)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #C65 depicts a modern jet aircraft soaring above the dome of the United States Capitol, symbolizing the speed, efficiency, and technological progress associated with the Jet Age. The bold carmine engraving combines aviation and national imagery in a clean, modern composition characteristic of 1960s United States airmail issues. The design emphasizes rapid communication and transportation while maintaining a strong patriotic theme. This example is postally used and displays the expected absence of original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #C65 displays Fine/Very Fine centering, with the top margin establishing the centering limit and the bottom margin providing the greatest spacing. Multiple perforation-tip creases place the stamp in the Fault category and reduce the preliminary grade to 48. A noticeable machine cancellation and the visible edge defects further reduce eye appeal by four points. The final assigned grade is Good/Very Good 44, representing a collectible used example of this classic Jet Age airmail issue whose grade is primarily limited by perforation-related faults.', 0.05, 'LA01-0519-05-05-003'),
(530, 'United States', 'C90', '004', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '31¢ Red, White & Blue – Jet with Globes, 1976\r\n(U.S. Airmail Issue – Multicolored Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #C90 was issued during the Bicentennial era and features a modern jet aircraft flying above stylized globes representing the Eastern and Western Hemispheres. Bold red and white stripes dominate the design, while a blue field containing the denomination and “USA AIRMAIL” inscription creates a distinctly patriotic appearance. The graphic, contemporary styling reflects the aviation optimism and technological confidence of the mid-1970s. This example is postally used, never hinged, and exhibits the expected absence of original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #C90 displays Fine/Very Fine centering with clear separation between the design and perforations. Reverse dirt spotting, a hinge mark, and a shortened perforation combine to produce a Fault classification, reducing the preliminary grade to 46. A moderate machine cancel and the overall presentation result in a further two-point eye appeal deduction. The final assigned grade is Good/Very Good 44, representing a sound collectible example whose grade is primarily limited by its reverse condition issues rather than its visual appearance.', 0.08, 'NA01-C023-02-02-004'),
(531, 'United States', 'C97', '006', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '31¢ Multicolored – High Jumper, 1980 Summer Olympics\r\n(U.S. Airmail Issue – Olympic Games Commemorative, Photogravure Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #C97 was issued in 1980 to commemorate the Summer Olympic Games and features a high jumper executing the Fosbury Flop technique. The athlete is rendered in bold red, green, and white against a vivid blue background, creating a dynamic sense of motion and athletic achievement. The simple modern composition emphasizes the dramatic silhouette of the jumper while the inscriptions “AIRMAIL,” “USA,” and “31¢” clearly identify the issue. This example is postally used, hinged, and no longer retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #C97 displays Fine/Very Fine centering, intact perforations, and strong multicolored printing. A hinge remnant and light reverse smudging combine to create a Minor Fault classification, reducing the technical grade to 57. The light, well-placed cancel and excellent color contribute a modest positive eye appeal adjustment. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide, the stamp receives a final grade of 59 (Very Good), representing an attractive and collectible used example of the 1980 Summer Olympics airmail issue.', 0.28, 'SL01-0001-03-05-006'),
(532, 'United States', 'C97', '008', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '31¢ Multicolored – High Jumper, 1980 Summer Olympics\r\n(U.S. Airmail Issue – Olympic Games Commemorative, Photogravure Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #C97 was issued in 1980 to commemorate the Summer Olympic Games and depicts a high jumper executing the Fosbury Flop technique. The athlete is rendered in bold red, green, and white against a vivid blue background, creating a dramatic sense of motion and athletic achievement. The inscriptions “AIRMAIL OLYMPICS 1980” and “USA 31¢” identify the issue and reinforce its commemorative purpose. This example is postally used, never hinged, and no longer retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #C97 displays Fine/Very Fine centering, strong color, and fully intact perforations. Light cancellation bleed-through on the reverse results in a Very Minor Fault classification and an adjusted base grade of 72. The dominant factor affecting the final grade is the very heavy machine cancel, which crosses much of the design and reduces eye appeal. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide and supplied fault framework, the stamp receives a final grade of 65 (Very Good/Fine), remaining a collectible example of this popular Olympic airmail commemorative.', 0.36, 'SL01-0001-04-03-008'),
(533, 'United States', 'C115', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Fine', '44¢ Multicolored – Transpacific Airmail 1935 (China Clipper), 1985\r\n(U.S. Airmail Issue – Historic Airmail Flights Series, Photogravure Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #C115 was issued in 1985 to commemorate the fiftieth anniversary of the pioneering Transpacific Airmail service inaugurated by the China Clipper in 1935. The design depicts the aircraft at ground level with crew members visible near the fuselage, highlighting one of the most significant milestones in international aviation and mail transport. The aircraft\'s distinctive propellers, American flag markings, and detailed structural features are presented in a realistic style that reflects the technological achievement of the era. This example is postally used, never hinged, and no longer retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #C115 exhibits Fine/Very Fine centering, strong color, and fully intact perforations. Mild reverse smudging results in a Very Minor Fault classification and an adjusted base grade of 72. The dominant factor affecting the final grade is the very heavy machine cancel, which obscures substantial portions of the aircraft design and significantly reduces eye appeal. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide and supplied fault framework, the stamp receives a final grade of 61 (Very Good/Fine), remaining a collectible example of this important aviation-themed airmail commemorative.', 0.39, 'NA01-C027-02-01-002'),
(534, 'United States', 'C115', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '44¢ Multicolored – Transpacific Airmail 1935 (China Clipper), 1985\r\n(U.S. Airmail Issue – Historic Airmail Flights Series, Photogravure Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #C115 commemorates the fiftieth anniversary of the pioneering China Clipper transpacific airmail flight of 1935. The design depicts the aircraft on the ground with crew members preparing for departure beneath the massive wing and engines of the famous flying boat. The American flag and bright sky backdrop reinforce the theme of technological achievement and international mail service. This example is postally used, hinged, and no longer retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #C115 displays Fine/Very Fine centering, generally sound perforations, and strong original color. A hinge remnant, reverse smudging, and one short perforation combine to create a Minor Fault classification, reducing the adjusted base grade to 58. The very heavy machine cancel is the most significant visual detractor, obscuring important portions of the aircraft design and lowering eye appeal substantially. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide and supplied fault framework, the stamp receives a final grade of 47 (Good/Very Good), making it a representative but condition-limited example of this important airmail commemorative.', 0.21, 'SL01-0001-04-04-003'),
(535, 'United States', 'C115', '004', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '44¢ Multicolored – Transpacific Airmail 1935 (China Clipper), 1985\r\n(U.S. Airmail Issue – Historic Airmail Flights Series, Photogravure Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #C115 commemorates the pioneering China Clipper flight that inaugurated regular transpacific airmail service in 1935. The design depicts the famous aircraft on the ground with crew members visible beneath the wing and engines, preparing for departure. The American flag, bright sky, and bold inscriptions emphasize the significance of this milestone in aviation and postal history. This example is postally used, hinged, and no longer retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #C115 displays Fine/Very Fine centering, intact perforations, and strong color. A hinge mark and light reverse smudging result in a Very Minor Fault classification and an adjusted base grade of 72. The heavy machine cancel reduces eye appeal by obscuring portions of the design and lettering, resulting in a final grade of 65 (Very Good/Fine). Despite the cancellation and minor reverse faults, it remains a respectable and collectible example of this historically significant aviation commemorative.', 0.39, 'SL01-0001-05-01-004'),
(536, 'United States', 'C116', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine', '44¢ Multicolored – Junípero Serra, 1985\r\n(U.S. Airmail Issue – Historic Figures Series, Photogravure Printing, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #C116 commemorates Junípero Serra, the Franciscan missionary associated with the establishment of California’s mission system. The design features a portrait of Serra accompanied by a mission building and a map of Alta and Baja California, creating a visual narrative of his historical influence. Earth-toned colors and fine detail give the stamp a dignified appearance while maintaining strong visual appeal. This example is postally used, never hinged, and no longer retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #C116 exhibits Extremely Fine centering, strong color, and fully intact perforations. Heavy reverse ink transfer results in a Minor Fault classification and reduces the adjusted base grade to 76. A heavy machine cancel crossing the portrait further impacts eye appeal, producing a six-point deduction. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide and supplied fault framework, the stamp receives a final grade of 70 (Fine), remaining an attractive and collectible example distinguished by its superior centering despite the noted reverse and cancellation-related issues.', 0.52, 'NA01-C027-02-02-002'),
(537, 'United States', 'RE191', '003', 'Unused', 'Never Hinged', 'NGAI - No Gum As Issued', 'Good/Very Good', '51¢ Green and Black – Wines Internal Revenue, 1941 Series\r\n(Engraved Printing, Rouletted, No Gum as Issued)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #RE191 is a 51¢ Wines Internal Revenue revenue stamp from the 1941 series, produced for the taxation of wine products in the United States. The engraved design features a large central denomination surrounded by intricate security engraving, ornamental scrollwork, and grapevine motifs reflecting the stamp’s fiscal purpose. The inscriptions “WINES UNITED STATES INTERNAL REVENUE” and “SERIES OF 1941” frame the design, while the black overprint creates strong contrast against the green base engraving. This example is unused, never hinged, and was issued without gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #RE191 displays Fine/Very Fine centering with the design clearly separated from the roulette cuts on all sides. Soundness is limited by several ragged, fuzzy, or shortened roulette cuts, resulting in a Fault classification and a 30-point deduction. The stamp remains unused, never hinged, and free of cancellation, with strong engraving and pleasing color. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide and supplied fault framework, the final grade is 47 (Good/Very Good).', 0.22, 'SL01-0001-10-04-003'),
(538, 'United States', 'RE191', '004', 'Unused', 'Never Hinged', 'NGAI - No Gum As Issued', 'Good/Very Good', '51¢ Green and Black – Wines Internal Revenue, 1941 Series\r\n(Engraved Printing, Rouletted, No Gum as Issued)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #RE191 is a 51¢ Wines Internal Revenue revenue stamp issued as part of the 1941 series used to collect federal taxes on wine products. The design centers on a bold black overprinted denomination, with “51” and “CENTS” prominently displayed against an intricately engraved green background. Ornamental scrollwork, fine security engraving, and grapevine motifs reinforce the stamp’s fiscal purpose, while the inscriptions “WINES UNITED STATES INTERNAL REVENUE” and “SERIES OF 1941” frame the design. This example is unused, never hinged, and was issued without gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #RE191 displays Fine/Very Fine centering with the design clearly separated from the roulette cuts on all sides. Soundness is reduced by multiple ragged, fuzzy, or shortened roulette cuts, resulting in a Fault classification and a 30-point deduction. The stamp remains unused, never hinged, and free of cancellation, with attractive engraving and pleasing color. Under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide and the supplied fault framework, the final grade is 47 (Good/Very Good).', 0.22, 'SL01-0002-01-01-004'),
(539, 'United States', 'E3', '005', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '10¢ Orange – Messenger Running, 1893\r\n(U.S. Special Delivery Issue – Engraved Printing, Perf 12, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #E3 is the 1893 10¢ Special Delivery issue, featuring a uniformed messenger running at left to symbolize speed and immediate service. At right, the tablet inscription states “Secures immediate delivery at any post office,” while the denomination appears in a decorative starburst cartouche at bottom. The orange engraving remains bold and recognizable, though the cancellation affects much of the central design.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nScott #E3 displays Fine/Very Fine centering with the left margin as the limiting side. Minor Fault soundness reduces the base grade to Very Good 55, and the very heavy numeral-in-bars cancel lowers eye appeal by fifteen points. The final assigned grade is Good/Very Good 40.', 13.74, 'NA01-E001-01-03-005'),
(542, 'United States', '300', '007', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '1¢ Green – Benjamin Franklin, 1902\r\n(First Bureau Issue – Engraved, Perf 12, Double-Line Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #300, issued in 1902 as part of the First Bureau Issue, features a finely engraved portrait of Benjamin Franklin within an ornate oval frame. Allegorical figures flank the portrait, with “UNITED STATES OF AMERICA” at the top and “ONE CENT” at the bottom. The engraving reflects the Bureau of Engraving and Printing’s refined production, known for strong detail and consistent impressions.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #300 example achieves a final grade of 66 (Very Good-Fine), combining Fine centering with a single Very Minor Fault. The narrow top and left margins limit the centering, but the stamp remains structurally sound. A light cancel enhances overall appearance, offsetting the minor reverse issue. It remains a solid and collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.10, 'LA01-0014-01-01-007'),
(543, 'United States', '554', '005', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair/Good', '2¢ Carmine – George Washington, 1923\r\n(Fourth Bureau Issue – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #554 features a left-facing engraved portrait of George Washington within an oval frame, surrounded by geometric ornamentation and inscriptions reading “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” and “2 CENTS.” Issued in 1923 as part of the Fourth Bureau Issue, the design reflects a cleaner, more modern engraving style compared to earlier Washington-Franklin issues. The submitted example is used, without original gum, and hinged, showing a bold machine cancel and visible wear along the perforations.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #554 example shows Very Good centering with margins that approach the design and a Minor Fault due to multiple perforation irregularities. The heavy cancel further reduces visual clarity, resulting in a final grade of 24 (Fair/Good). While condition issues limit its desirability, it remains a recognizable and collectible example of this commonly encountered Bureau issue.', 0.02, 'SL01-0002-03-04-005'),
(544, 'United States', '557', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '5¢ Blue – Theodore Roosevelt, 1922\r\n(Fourth Bureau Issue – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #557 features a portrait of Theodore Roosevelt centered within an oval frame, bordered by geometric ornamentation and inscriptions reading “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” and “5 CENTS.” Issued in 1922 as part of the Fourth Bureau Issue, the stamp reflects the strong engraved style of the period, with Roosevelt’s image presented in a formal and dignified manner. The submitted example is used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #557 example shows Fine/Very Fine centering with the design clearly separated from the perforations, though the bottom and right margins are the tightest. Minor soundness concerns, including one short perf and light cancellation bleed-through, reduce the technical grade, while the cancel causes only limited visual distraction. The final grade of 52 (Very Good) reflects a solid collectible example that still presents well within the 1922 issue set.', 0.10, 'LA01-0027-02-02-003'),
(545, 'United States', '721', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '3¢ Violet – George Washington, 1932\r\n(Washington Bicentennial Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #721 is part of the 1932 Washington Bicentennial Issue, featuring a finely engraved portrait of George Washington in a circular vignette. The design is framed by ornamental scrollwork with “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” above and denomination “3 CENTS” below. The violet ink and detailed engraving give the stamp a refined, balanced appearance typical of Bureau production. This example is postally used, hinged, and without original gum, consistent with standard circulation examples.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #721 example presents as a mid-grade collectible with Very Good/Fine centering and only minor technical faults. The tight right margin and heavier cancellation reduce its overall appeal, resulting in a final grade of 41 (Good/Very Good). Despite these limitations, it remains a sound and recognizable example of the Washington Bicentennial issue under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide .', 0.05, 'LA01-0035-02-02-003'),
(546, 'United States', '729', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair', '3¢ Violet – Century of Progress, Chicago, 1933\r\n(Century of Progress Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #729 was issued in 1933 to commemorate the Century of Progress Exposition held in Chicago. The design features the Federal Building rendered in vertical symmetry, flanked by modernist architectural elements symbolizing progress and industry. “U.S. POSTAGE” appears at the top, with “CENTURY OF PROGRESS” and “CHICAGO 1833–1933” framing the central vignette. The engraving is clean and geometric, reflecting the Art Deco influence of the era. This example is used, hinged, and without original gum, typical for postally used commemoratives of the period.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #729 example exhibits Very Good centering but is significantly affected by multiple minor faults in the perforations and a heavy cancellation. These factors combine to produce a final grade of 11 (Fair). Despite its condition issues, it remains a complete and identifi', 0.02, 'LA01-0037-02-01-003'),
(547, 'United States', '737', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '3¢ Purple – Mothers of America, 1934\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Flat Plate Printing, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #737 was issued in 1934 to honor the mothers of America. The design reproduces a portion of James McNeill Whistler’s famous painting Arrangement in Grey and Black No. 1, popularly known as “Whistler’s Mother.” The vignette depicts a seated woman in profile and is accompanied by inscriptions reading “U.S. POSTAGE” and “IN MEMORY AND IN HONOR OF THE MOTHERS OF AMERICA.” Issued in a rich purple color, the stamp commemorated Mother’s Day and remains one of the most recognizable United States commemoratives of the 1930s. This example is postally used and retains good overall detail despite the condition issues noted below.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #737 exhibits Fine centering, with the bottom margin establishing the limiting factor. Several perforation creases place the stamp in the Minor Fault category and reduce the adjusted base grade to Good/Very Good 40. The Century of Progress slogan cancellation adds historical interest while leaving the central design visible. With no further eye appeal adjustment required, the final grade is Good/Very Good 40.', 0.05, 'LA01-0038-01-01-003'),
(548, 'United States', '784', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '3¢ Violet – Susan B. Anthony / Women’s Suffrage Issue, 1936\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11x10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #784 was issued in 1936 to honor Susan B. Anthony and the women’s suffrage movement. The design features a left-facing portrait of Anthony within an oval frame, with “U.S. POSTAGE” above and “SUFFRAGE FOR WOMEN” below. Ornamental circles containing the denomination appear in the lower corners. The violet printing provides strong contrast and highlights the finely engraved portrait. This example is a used single stamp with a clear overall appearance and readily identifiable design details.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #784 displays Very Good centering, with the right margin serving as the primary limiting factor and the bottom margin the next most restrictive. Reverse pencil writing places the stamp in the Extremely Minor Fault category and reduces the grade to Good/Very Good 46. The light wavy-line cancellation preserves the portrait and overall design clarity. With no additional eye appeal adjustment required, the final grade is Good/Very Good 46.', 0.05, 'LA01-0045-03-02-002'),
(549, 'United States', '797', '002', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fair/Good', '10¢ Green – Great Smoky Mountains National Park Souvenir Sheet, 1937\r\n(Souvenir Sheet Issue – Engraved, Imperforate, No Watermark, Plate No. 21696)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #797 is the 1937 Great Smoky Mountains National Park souvenir sheet, issued in conjunction with the 43rd Annual Convention of the American Philatelic Society in Asheville, North Carolina. The central stamp depicts a scenic mountain landscape with a prominent pine tree in the foreground, rendered in green engraving. Surrounding selvage includes inscriptions referencing Postmaster General James A. Farley and the convention details. This example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #797 souvenir sheet displays Fine/Very Fine centering and strong engraved detail, but is significantly impacted by a heavy crease. While the overall design remains clear and the sheet retains original gum, the structural fault reduces the final grade to 29 (Fair/Good). It remains a collectible example, though primarily as a space-filler under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.15, 'LA01-0048-04-01-002'),
(550, 'United States', '803', '007', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '1/2¢ Orange – Benjamin Franklin, 1938\r\n(Presidential Series – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 x 10, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #803 is part of the 1938 Presidential Series, widely known as the “Prexies,” featuring Benjamin Franklin in a left-facing engraved portrait. The design is clean and utilitarian, with a finely lined background and bold inscriptions, including “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” and the denomination at the bottom. Issued during a period of high production efficiency, the stamp reflects the precision of Bureau of Engraving and Printing rotary press methods.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #803 example presents Fine/Very Fine centering with margins clear of the design, though slightly uneven. A light crease and minor perforation irregularities reduce the technical grade, resulting in a Very Good classification. Despite these faults, the stamp maintains strong color, a clear impression, and a clean cancel, making it a respectable and collectible example within its grade range.', 0.08, 'SD22-0004-05-01-007'),
(551, 'United States', '899', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '1¢ Green – Defense, Industry-Agriculture, 1940\r\n(National Defense Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #899 was issued in 1940 as part of the National Defense series, reflecting the United States’ pre-war emphasis on preparedness. The design features the Statue of Liberty superimposed over industrial and agricultural imagery, symbolizing national strength and self-sufficiency. The inscriptions “UNITED STATES OF AMERICA” and “INDUSTRY-AGRICULTURE” reinforce this theme, while the bold green color is typical of the denomination. This example is postally used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #899 presents as a respectable mid-grade example with Fine–Very Fine centering and intact overall structure. The multiple perf creases reduce soundness into the Minor Fault category, and the heavy machine cancel further tempers eye appeal. Despite these deductions, the stamp maintains clear design visibility and balanced proportions, resulting in a final grade of 51 (Very Good) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.08, 'LA01-0054-02-01-003'),
(552, 'United States', '905', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair/Good', '3¢ Violet – Win the War Eagle, 1942\r\n(War Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #905, issued in 1942 during World War II, features a bold eagle with outstretched wings clutching arrows, symbolizing American strength and resolve. The banner “WIN THE WAR” reinforces the wartime message, while stars encircle the eagle, echoing national unity. The violet color and strong graphic design make this one of the more visually striking issues of the period. This example is postally used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #905 example presents Fine–Very Fine centering with a balanced but slightly uneven margin profile. However, a significant staining issue elevates the stamp into the Major Fault category, substantially reducing its technical standing. The heavy machine cancel further diminishes eye appeal. Despite these drawbacks, the stamp retains identifiable design features and remains a collectible, albeit lower-grade, example with a final grade of 24 (Fair–Good) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.02, 'LA01-0054-04-01-003'),
(553, 'United States', '907', '006', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Poor', '2¢ Carmine – Nations United for Victory, 1943\r\n(War Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #907 was issued in 1943 during World War II and reflects Allied unity and cooperation. The design features a stylized bundle of arrows rising upward as a symbol of collective strength, with the inscription “NATIONS UNITED FOR VICTORY” appearing below. The bold carmine color reinforces the patriotic character of the issue and makes the central design readily recognizable. This example is postally used and remains identifiable despite the condition issues noted below.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis used Scott #907 displays Very Good centering, with the left margin serving as the limiting factor and the bottom margin tighter than the top. Significant staining places the stamp in the Major Fault category, while additional perforation defects further impact condition. The heavy machine cancellation substantially obscures portions of the design and warrants a ten-point eye appeal deduction. After all adjustments are applied, the final grade is 8 (Poor – PO).', 0.01, 'SL01-0001-10-01-006'),
(554, 'United States', '908', '005', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '1¢ Green – Four Freedoms: Freedom of Speech and Religion, 1943\r\n(War Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #908, issued in 1943, is part of the Four Freedoms series inspired by President Franklin D. Roosevelt’s vision of fundamental human rights. The design depicts a classical figure holding a torch, symbolizing liberty and enlightenment, with inscriptions referencing “FREEDOM OF SPEECH AND RELIGION, FROM WANT AND FEAR.” The green color distinguishes the 1¢ denomination. This example is postally used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #908 example demonstrates Fine centering with tighter margins on the left and top sides. However, multiple perforation-related issues elevate it to a Fault classification, significantly reducing its technical standing. A heavy cancel further impacts visual appeal. Despite these limitations, the stamp remains a recognizable and collectible example of the Four Freedoms issue, earning a final grade of 31 (Good) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.04, 'SD22-0005-05-06-005'),
(555, 'United States', '1008', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine/Very Fine', '3¢ Violet – NATO, 1952\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1008 was issued in 1952 to commemorate the North Atlantic Treaty Organization (NATO). The design features a torch held jointly by two hands over a globe, symbolizing unity and shared defense, with the inscription “PEACE • STRENGTH • FREEDOM” reinforcing its Cold War-era message. The bold “NATO” lettering and radiating background lines give the stamp a strong, modern visual identity. This example is postally used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1008 example presents Fine–Very Fine centering with slightly tighter margins at the bottom and left. The stamp is largely sound, with only a minor perforation irregularity. A light, unobtrusive cancel enhances overall presentation, resulting in a balanced and visually appealing example. The final grade of 76 (Fine–Very Fine) reflects a solid mid-grade collectible under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.13, 'NA01-0183-02-02-003'),
(556, 'United States', '1030', '009', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fair/Good', '2¢ Carmine – Benjamin Franklin, 1954\r\n(Liberty Series – Rotary Press, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nThis 2¢ carmine issue from the Liberty Series features a finely engraved portrait of Benjamin Franklin, rendered in a classical rectangular frame with “U.S. POSTAGE” at the top and denomination at the lower left. The design reflects mid-20th century Bureau of Engraving and Printing standards, emphasizing clarity, uniform line work, and strong color consistency. The submitted example is used, hinged, and without original gum, with a visible cancel and underlying structural faults affecting overall condition.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1030 example shows solid Fine/Very Fine centering with balanced margins and intact perforations, providing a respectable visual framework. However, a heavy crease introduces a Major Fault that significantly reduces the overall grade, further impacted by a heavy cancel across the design. While condition limits its desirability, the stamp remains a collectible and serviceable example within the Fair/Good range under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.02, 'SL01-0001-10-02-009'),
(557, 'United States', '1031', '005', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '1¢ Green – George Washington, 1954\r\n(Liberty Series – Rotary Press, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1031 features a left-facing engraved portrait of George Washington, framed within a rectangular border typical of the Liberty Series definitives. The design is clean and utilitarian, with “U.S. POSTAGE” along the bottom and the denomination at lower left, reflecting the mid-century emphasis on clarity and mass production efficiency. The submitted example is used, hinged, and without original gum, showing a visible machine cancel and moderate wear consistent with postal handling.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1031 example presents Fine centering with visible but tight margins and intact overall structure. The presence of multiple ragged and shortened perforations introduces a Minor Fault that lowers the grade into the Good/Very Good range before eye appeal adjustments. A heavy machine cancel further reduces visual appeal, resulting in a final grade of 35 (Good). While not a premium example, it remains a solid and collectible representation of this widely used Liberty Series issue.', 0.04, 'SD22-0006-01-02-005'),
(558, 'United States', '1033', '004', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '2¢ Carmine – Thomas Jefferson, 1954\r\n(Liberty Series – Rotary Press, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1033 features a finely engraved portrait of Thomas Jefferson in a rectangular frame, with inscriptions including “JEFFERSON,” “U.S. POSTAGE,” and the 2¢ denomination. The Liberty Series emphasized clarity and uniformity for high-volume postal use, and this issue reflects that intent with strong line work and consistent color. The submitted example is used, hinged, and without original gum, showing a machine cancel and minor reverse condition issues.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1033 example benefits from Fine/Very Fine centering with well-defined margins and solid overall structure. Minor reverse faults and cancellation bleed-through combine to form a Minor Fault classification, lowering the base grade into the Very Good range. A heavy machine cancel further impacts eye appeal, resulting in a final grade of 45 (Good/Very Good). Despite these deductions, the stamp remains a respectable and collectible example of the Liberty Series.', 0.05, 'NA01-0187-01-03-004');
INSERT INTO `stamps` (`id`, `country`, `scott`, `count`, `condition`, `hinged`, `gum`, `grade`, `description`, `price`, `location`) VALUES
(559, 'United States', '1038', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '2¢ Carmine – Thomas Jefferson, 1954\r\n(Liberty Series – Rotary Press, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1033 features a finely engraved portrait of Thomas Jefferson in a rectangular frame, with inscriptions including “JEFFERSON,” “U.S. POSTAGE,” and the 2¢ denomination. The Liberty Series emphasized clarity and uniformity for high-volume postal use, and this issue reflects that intent with strong line work and consistent color. The submitted example is used, hinged, and without original gum, showing a machine cancel and minor reverse condition issues.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1033 example benefits from Fine/Very Fine centering with well-defined margins and solid overall structure. Minor reverse faults and cancellation bleed-through combine to form a Minor Fault classification, lowering the base grade into the Very Good range. A heavy machine cancel further impacts eye appeal, resulting in a final grade of 45 (Good/Very Good). Despite these deductions, the stamp remains a respectable and collectible example of the Liberty Series.', 0.05, 'LA01-0066-04-01-003'),
(560, 'United States', '1039', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '6¢ Red – Theodore Roosevelt, 1954\r\n(Liberty Series – Rotary Press, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1039 features a bold engraved portrait of Theodore Roosevelt, presented in the strong, rectangular format typical of the Liberty Series. The deep red color and fine line engraving emphasize facial detail and contrast, with “THEODORE ROOSEVELT,” “U.S. POSTAGE,” and the 6¢ denomination clearly integrated into the design. Issued during a period of high-volume postal production, the stamp reflects consistent engraving and printing quality. The submitted example is used, hinged, and without original gum, showing a machine cancel and light handling.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1039 example presents solid Fine/Very Fine centering with well-defined margins and only a minimal perforation irregularity. The single shortened perf results in an Extremely Minor Fault, allowing the stamp to retain much of its original grading strength. A moderately heavy cancel slightly reduces eye appeal, bringing the final grade to 68 (Very Good/Fine). Overall, it remains a clean and collectible example within the Liberty Series, with better-than-average presentation for its condition range.', 0.10, 'LA01-0066-04-02-003'),
(561, 'United States', '1043', '004', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '9¢ Purple – The Alamo, 1956\r\n(Liberty Series – Rotary Press, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1043 depicts the historic Alamo mission in San Antonio, rendered in a finely engraved vignette with architectural detail and surrounding foliage. The inscription “THE ALAMO” appears prominently at the top, with “U.S. POSTAGE” and the 9¢ denomination at the bottom. The Liberty Series combined portraits and landmarks to reflect American heritage, and this issue stands out for its scenic composition. The submitted example is used, hinged, and without original gum, with a visible machine cancel and otherwise stable structure.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1043 example shows strong Fine/Very Fine centering with well-defined margins and a visually appealing design. The single shortened perforation introduces only an Extremely Minor Fault, allowing the stamp to retain much of its grading strength. A moderately heavy cancel slightly reduces eye appeal, resulting in a final grade of 68 (Very Good/Fine). Overall, it remains a clean and collectible example of this popular Liberty Series issue.', 0.10, 'NA01-0189-03-01-004'),
(562, 'United States', '1044', '004', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '10¢ Brown – Independence Hall, 1956\r\n(Liberty Series – Rotary Press, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1044 depicts Independence Hall in Philadelphia, an iconic symbol of American independence and the site of both the Declaration of Independence and the Constitution’s signing. The engraving captures the building’s architectural symmetry, central clock tower, and surrounding wings in a balanced rectangular layout. The inscription “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” appears at upper left, with the 10¢ denomination at upper right and “INDEPENDENCE HALL” at the base. The submitted example is used, hinged, and without original gum, with a visible machine cancel and moderate wear.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1044 example displays solid Fine/Very Fine centering with clearly defined margins and a well-preserved design. The presence of several ragged and shortened perforations introduces a Minor Fault classification, lowering the grade into the Very Good range. A heavy machine cancel further affects eye appeal, resulting in a final grade of 45 (Good/Very Good). Despite these factors, the stamp remains a collectible and visually recognizable example of this historically significant Liberty Series issue.', 0.05, 'NA01-0189-03-02-004'),
(563, 'United States', '1047', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '25¢ Blue – Niagara Falls, 1956\r\n(Liberty Series – Rotary Press, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1047 features a finely engraved horizontal view of Niagara Falls, capturing the cascading water and surrounding landscape with strong line detail and tonal contrast. The Liberty Series expanded beyond portraits to include important American landmarks, and this issue highlights natural heritage with a balanced scenic composition. “U.S. POSTAGE” and the 25¢ denomination are clearly positioned within the design. The submitted example is used, hinged, and without original gum, showing a machine cancel and minor perforation irregularities.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1047 example displays Fine/Very Fine centering with well-defined margins and a visually appealing scenic design. Multiple shortened perforations result in a Very Minor Fault classification, causing only a modest deduction from the base grade. A heavy cancel slightly impacts eye appeal, bringing the final grade to 67 (Very Good/Fine). Overall, it remains a clean and collectible example of this popular Liberty Series issue.', 0.10, 'LA01-0189-03-031-003'),
(564, 'United States', '1049', '004', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '30¢ Gray Black – Robert E. Lee, 1956\r\n(Liberty Series – Rotary Press, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1049 features a finely engraved portrait of Robert E. Lee in a formal rectangular frame, with strong line detail and inscriptions including the subject’s name, denomination, and “U.S. POSTAGE.” The gray-black ink provides excellent contrast and clarity typical of the Liberty Series. The submitted example is used, hinged, and without original gum, with a clean front appearance and minor reverse effects.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1049 example shows strong Fine/Very Fine centering with well-defined margins and a clean front presentation. Minor perforation irregularities and light reverse bleed-through result in a Minor Fault classification, reducing the grade to 58 (Very Good). Despite these issues, the stamp retains solid visual appeal and remains a desirable collectible within the Liberty Series.', 0.12, 'SL01-0001-10-03-004'),
(565, 'United States', '1166', '003', 'Mint', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine/Very Fine', '8¢ Multicolor – Gustaf Mannerheim, Champion of Liberty, 1960\r\n(Champion of Liberty Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1166 is the 8¢ multicolor counterpart to the Mannerheim issue, presenting a gold-toned engraved portrait framed within a circular medallion, accented by red and blue symbolic elements and the torch of liberty above. The inscription “Champion of Liberty” reinforces the commemorative theme. The multicolor execution enhances contrast and visual appeal compared to the single-color version. This example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum, with only a minor natural gum irregularity noted on the reverse.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1166 example demonstrates Fine/Very Fine centering with full margins and an attractive multicolor engraved design. The only noted issue is a minor natural gum bend, an extremely minor fault with minimal impact. With strong color and overall visual balance, the stamp achieves a final grade of 75 (Fine/Very Fine), making it a desirable and collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.17, 'LA01-0076-03-04-003'),
(566, 'United States', '1051', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '50¢ Purple – Susan B. Anthony, 1958\r\n(Liberty Series – Rotary Press, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1051 features a finely engraved portrait of Susan B. Anthony, one of the most important leaders of the American women’s suffrage movement. The design presents Anthony in formal dress within a rectangular frame, with her name at left, the 50¢ denomination at lower right, and “U.S. POSTAGE” below. Issued as part of the Liberty Series, the stamp reflects the series’ emphasis on prominent Americans and national heritage subjects. The submitted example is used, hinged, and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1051 example has solid Fine/Very Fine centering with all margins visible and a well-rendered portrait of Susan B. Anthony. Its grade is held back by multiple perforation defects that elevate soundness to the Fault category, and a heavy cancel further reduces eye appeal. Even so, it remains a collectible example of this important Liberty Series issue, with better centering than is often found on stamps in this grade range.', 0.04, 'LA01-0067-03-01-003'),
(567, 'United States', '1054A', '003', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '1¼¢ Blue Green – Palace of the Governors, Santa Fe, New Mexico, 1954 Issue\r\n(Liberty Series – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1054A depicts the Palace of the Governors in Santa Fe, rendered in a detailed engraved style typical of the Liberty Series. The long colonnade and adobe structure are shown in perspective, with inscriptions identifying the location. This example is a vertical pair with intact horizontal centerline perforations. The color is even and consistent across both stamps, with clear, well-defined engraving lines.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1054A vertical pair displays Fine/Very Fine centering with a slightly tight right margin limiting the overall balance. Soundness is strong aside from a minor gum bend, and the perforations remain clean and intact. The pair format enhances presentation, resulting in a final grade of 74 (Fine) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.22, 'NA01-0193-04-01-003'),
(568, 'United States', '1115', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '4¢ Brown – Lincoln-Douglas Debates, 1958\r\n(Lincoln-Douglas Issue – Rotary Press, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1115 commemorates the Lincoln-Douglas debates of 1858, depicting Abraham Lincoln addressing a crowd with Stephen Douglas and others nearby, framed by banners and period detail. The design is rich in narrative, with “LINCOLN-DOUGLAS DEBATES” across the top and “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” below, capturing a pivotal moment in American political history. The submitted example is used, hinged, and without original gum, showing a prominent machine cancel across the central scene.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1115 example offers outstanding Extremely Fine/Superb centering with nearly perfect margin balance, a standout feature for this issue. However, minor perforation faults and a very heavy cancel significantly reduce both technical grade and visual appeal. The final grade of 66 (Very Good/Fine) reflects this contrast between exceptional centering and diminished presentation, though the stamp remains a collectible example of this historically significant commemorative issue.', 0.10, 'LA01-0072-03-02-003'),
(569, 'United States', '1116', '004', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '4¢ Blue – Lincoln Memorial, 1959\r\n(Lincoln Memorial Issue – Rotary Press, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1116 features a stylized engraving of the seated Lincoln statue from the Lincoln Memorial, rendered in a horizontal format with strong tonal contrast. The design includes “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” vertically at left, the 4¢ denomination, and Lincoln’s signature to the right. This issue emphasizes a more modern, simplified engraving style compared to earlier commemoratives. The submitted example is used, hinged, and without original gum, with a visible machine cancel and noticeable reverse staining.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1116 example presents Fine/Very Fine centering with balanced margins and a well-executed engraved design. Heavy reverse staining results in a Minor Fault classification, lowering the technical grade, while a noticeable cancel further impacts eye appeal. The final grade of 47 (Good/Very Good) reflects these combined factors, though the stamp remains a collectible and visually recognizable example of this popular Lincoln issue.', 0.05, 'NA01-0201-04-04-004'),
(570, 'United States', '1120', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '4¢ Carmine – Overland Mail Centennial, 1958\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1120 was issued in 1958 to commemorate the centennial of the Overland Mail service, a key milestone in early American communication and westward expansion. The design depicts a horse-drawn stagecoach traveling across a map of the United States, symbolizing the rapid delivery of mail across vast distances. The inscription “OVERLAND MAIL 1858–1958” arches prominently above the vignette, while “U.S. POSTAGE” and the denomination anchor the lower portion. The engraving is bold and energetic, characteristic of mid-20th century commemoratives. This example is postally used and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1120 example presents with Fine/Very Fine centering and strong visual design, but the combination of minor faults and a heavy cancel lowers the final grade to 49 (Good/Very Good). Despite these limitations, it remains a respectable and collectible example of a popular commemorative issue under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.05, 'LA01-0073-01-04-003'),
(571, 'United States', '1122', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '4¢ Multicolor – Forest Conservation, 1958\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1122 was issued in 1958 to promote forest conservation and environmental awareness. The design presents a stylized woodland scene featuring trees, a deer, and a vertical tree trunk cross-section, symbolizing both natural beauty and responsible resource use. The word “CONSERVATION” appears prominently at the top, reinforcing the theme. The multicolor printing combines green, yellow, and brown tones to create depth and visual contrast, typical of late 1950s commemoratives. This example is postally used and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1122 example shows solid Fine/Very Fine centering and strong color, with only minor perforation faults affecting soundness. The heavy cancel slightly reduces visual appeal, bringing the final grade to 67 (Very Good/Fine). Despite these modest deductions, it remains an attractive and collectible example of a popular conservation-themed issue.', 0.10, 'LA01-0073-01-03-003'),
(572, 'United States', '1125', '004', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '4¢ Blue – José de San Martín, 1959\r\n(Champion of Liberty Issue – Rotary Press, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1125 honors José de San Martín, a प्रमुख leader in South American independence movements and recognized as a “Champion of Liberty.” The design features a formal engraved portrait within a circular medallion, flanked by laurel branches and topped with a torch symbolizing freedom. The inscription “CHAMPION OF LIBERTY” appears above, while “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” and the denomination anchor the lower portion. Issued in 1959, the stamp reflects mid-century commemorative styling with strong engraved detail and a monochromatic blue palette. This example is postally used and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1125 example shows respectable Fine/Very Fine centering but is significantly impacted by perforation creases and multiple shortened perfs, resulting in a Minor Fault classification. The heavy cancel further reduces eye appeal, bringing the final grade to 48 (Good/Very Good). Despite these limitations, it remains a collectible example of a historically meaningful issue within the 2009 PSE grading framework.', 0.05, 'NA01-0205-02-01-004'),
(573, 'United States', '1128', '004', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '4¢ Blue – Arctic Explorations, 1959\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1128 was issued in 1959 to commemorate Arctic exploration, highlighting both early expeditions and modern technological advancement. The design juxtaposes a dog sled team and explorer from 1909 with a contemporary submarine beneath, symbolizing progress in polar exploration. The inscription “ARCTIC EXPLORATIONS 1909” appears at the top, with “1959” marking the centennial. The engraving is detailed and balanced, typical of mid-century commemoratives, with a cool blue palette reinforcing the Arctic theme. This example is postally used and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1128 example benefits from strong Very Fine/Extremely Fine centering and intact perforations, giving it an initially appealing appearance. However, a major staining fault significantly reduces its overall condition, and the heavy cancel further impacts eye appeal. The final grade of 39 (Good) reflects these combined factors, though the stamp remains a collectible example of a visually and historically engaging issue under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.04, 'SL01-0002-04-03-004'),
(574, 'United States', '1130', '002', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '4¢ Black – Silver Centennial, 1959\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press Printing, Perforated 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1130 was issued in 1959 to commemorate the centennial of the discovery of silver in Nevada’s Comstock Lode. The design shows a western mining scene with prospectors, a horse, mining equipment, and mountainous terrain, with “SILVER CENTENNIAL 1859–1959” across the top and “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” below. This example is used, hinged, and without original gum, with visible discoloration on the reverse.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1130 presents well from the front, with Fine/Very Fine centering, a clear mining scene, and a light machine cancel that leaves the design mostly undisturbed. The reverse discoloration and one short perforation reduce the technical grade, but the stamp remains a collectible and attractive used example. Final grade: 60 (Very Good-Fine).', 0.10, 'LA01-0073-04-01-002'),
(575, 'United States', '1132', '003', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Fine', '4¢ Multicolor – 49-Star Flag, 1959\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1132 was issued in 1959 to commemorate the admission of Alaska as the 49th state, reflected in the 49-star American flag. The design features a waving U.S. flag rendered in red, white, and blue, with “JULY 4, 1959” marking the first Independence Day under the new star configuration. The composition emphasizes motion and patriotism, with bold lines and clean engraving typical of late 1950s issues. This example is postally used and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1132 example displays excellent Extremely Fine centering and strong visual appeal, supported by vibrant color and a well-executed design. Minor perforation faults and a noticeable cancel modestly reduce the final grade to 82 (Very Fine), but it remains an attractive and desirable collectible example within the 2009 PSE Grading framework.', 0.15, 'LA01-0073-04-03-003'),
(576, 'United States', '1133', '004', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Fine', '4¢ Multicolor – Soil Conservation, 1959\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Rotary Press, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1133 was issued in 1959 to promote soil conservation and sustainable land management. The design depicts a rural agricultural landscape with rolling fields, a farmhouse, and contour plowing patterns, symbolizing erosion control and responsible farming practices. The inscription “SOIL CONSERVATION” appears prominently across the top, reinforcing the theme. The multicolor printing uses green, brown, and blue tones to create depth and a sense of natural balance, typical of late 1950s commemoratives. This example is postally used and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1133 example displays solid Very Fine centering with full margins and a pleasing overall design. A minor perforation crease and a moderately heavy cancel reduce the final grade to 70 (Fine). Even with these modest deductions, it remains a respectable and collectible example of a classic soil conservation commemorative under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.11, 'SL01-0002-05-01-004'),
(577, 'United States', '1136', '004', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good/Fine', '4¢ Olive Green – Ramon Magsaysay, 1959\r\n(Champion of Liberty Issue – Rotary Press, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1136 was issued in 1959 as part of the Champion of Liberty series, honoring Ramon Magsaysay, former President of the Philippines and a symbol of democratic leadership. The design features a formal engraved portrait within a circular medallion, flanked by laurel branches and a torch above, representing liberty and civic virtue. The inscription “CHAMPION OF LIBERTY” crowns the design, with “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” and the denomination below. The engraving is crisp and dignified, typical of the series. This example is postally used and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1136 example presents Fine/Very Fine centering with a clean and historically meaningful design. Minor perforation faults and a moderately heavy cancel reduce the final grade to 67 (Very Good/Fine). Despite these deductions, it remains a solid and collectible example within the Champion of Liberty series under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.10, 'NA01-0205-02-03-004'),
(578, 'United States', '1138', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '4¢ Carmine Rose – Ephraim McDowell, 1959\r\n(Regular Issue – Rotary Press, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1138 honors Dr. Ephraim McDowell, a pioneering American surgeon known for performing one of the first successful abdominal operations. The design features a finely engraved portrait of McDowell, with his name placed to the right and “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” across the top. The composition is simple and dignified, using a single carmine rose tone that emphasizes the engraved detail and facial expression. Issued in 1959, it reflects the clean, portrait-focused style common to late 1950s regular issues. This example is postally used and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1138 example presents Fine/Very Fine centering and a strong engraved portrait, but multiple perforation creases significantly affect soundness. A moderately heavy cancel further reduces eye appeal, resulting in a final grade of 54 (Very Good). Despite these issues, it remains a solid and collectible example of a classic mid-century U.S. definitive issue.', 0.05, 'NA01-0203-03-03-003'),
(579, 'United States', '1146', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '4¢ Blue – VIII Olympic Winter Games, 1960\r\n(Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1146 commemorates the VIII Olympic Winter Games held at Squaw Valley, California, in 1960. The design is printed in blue and features the Olympic rings above a large snowflake, with the event title prominently displayed at top and the denomination at lower left. The vertically oriented layout is clean and modern, using strong geometric balance to emphasize both the Olympic symbol and the winter theme. This example is used, hinged, and with partial original gum remaining.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1146 is a Fine/Very Fine centered example with all perforations clear of the design and the right side providing the best visual spacing. Its principal technical issue is a light crease, which places it in the Minor Fault category, and the heavy machine cancel further reduces eye appeal. Even so, the stamp retains a recognizable and attractive Olympic design, making it a collectible example at 54 (Very Good) under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.08, 'NA01-0207-03-02-003'),
(580, 'United States', '1200', '002', 'Used', 'Never Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Very Good', '4¢ Violet – Brien McMahon, 1962\r\n(U.S. Commemorative Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1200 commemorates Senator Brien McMahon, associated with the Atomic Energy Act and the promotion of “peaceful uses” of atomic energy. The design features McMahon’s engraved portrait at right, with an atomic symbol at left and the inscriptions “ATOMIC ENERGY ACT” and “PEACEFUL USES,” reflecting the early Cold War-era optimism about nuclear science in civilian life. The submitted example is postally used, never hinged, and has no original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1200 presents Fine–Very Fine centering with the design remaining comfortably clear of the perforations, and it benefits from a clean face with no visible cancel to interfere with the atomic-symbol vignette and portrait. Soundness is the limiting factor due to a nibbed perforation and multiple perf creases, producing a Fault classification under your stated method. Applying your deduction, then adjusting for eye appeal afterward, the stamp concludes at 50 (Very Good) as a sharp-looking but condition-limited example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.08, 'LA01-0079-02-02-002'),
(581, 'United States', '1246', '003', 'Mint', 'Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '5¢ Blue – John F. Kennedy Memorial, 1964\r\n(Kennedy Memorial Issue – Engraved, Perf 11, No Watermark)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1246 was issued in 1964 as a memorial to President John F. Kennedy following his assassination in 1963. The design features a dual vignette: at left, an eternal flame symbolizing remembrance, and at right, a portrait of Kennedy. Surrounding inscriptions include a quote encircling the top and sides and “JOHN FITZGERALD KENNEDY 1917–1963” along the bottom. The engraving is finely executed, with a somber blue tone reinforcing the memorial theme.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1246 example presents as a clean and well-preserved stamp with Fine/Very Fine centering and strong visual appeal. The minor natural gum bend results in an Extremely Minor Fault classification, yielding a final grade of 74 (Fine). Despite slight centering imbalance, the stamp remains a desirable and collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.15, 'NA01-0223-02-01-003'),
(582, 'United States', '1281', '004', 'Mint', 'Never Hinged', 'OG - Original Gum', 'Fine', '3¢ Violet – Francis Parkman, 1967\r\n(Prominent Americans Series – Engraved, Perf 11 x 10½, Unwatermarked)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #1281 depicts Francis Parkman, the noted American historian, in a sharply engraved portrait format typical of the Prominent Americans series. His name appears prominently at left in stacked lettering, with “AMERICAN HISTORIAN” and “U.S. POSTAGE” below, and the 3¢ denomination at lower right. The design is direct and dignified, relying on strong line engraving and a clean portrait to emphasize Parkman’s literary and historical importance.\r\nThis example is unused, never hinged, and retains original gum. The reverse shows a minor natural gum bend, but the stamp otherwise presents as a sound mint example for the issue.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #1281 is a respectable never hinged example with fresh color, sharp engraving, and intact perforations. Its centering fits the Fine/Very Fine range, with the bottom margin the tightest and the right the most open. A minor natural gum bend lowers the soundness slightly, producing a final grade of 74 (Fine). It remains an attractive and collectible example under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.11, 'SL01-0001-06-03-004'),
(583, 'United States', 'C33', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good', '5¢ Red – DC-4 Skymaster Air Mail, 1947\r\n(Air Mail Issue – Engraved, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #C33, issued in 1947, continues the Skymaster theme with a refined depiction of the Douglas DC-4 aircraft in flight. The design presents the plane angled upward across a lined sky, conveying motion and postwar aviation expansion. “AIR MAIL 5¢” appears prominently above, with “U.S. POSTAGE” below. The single-color red engraving emphasizes clean lines and mechanical detail, typical of mid-century air mail issues. This example is postally used and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #C33 example shows Fine/Very Fine centering but is heavily impacted by multiple perforation defects and additional minor issues, resulting in an overall Fault classification. A moderately heavy cancel further reduces eye appeal, bringing the final grade to 36 (Good). Despite its condition, it remains a collectible example of a classic U.S. air mail issue under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.04, 'LA01-0518-02-01-003'),
(584, 'United States', 'C35', '003', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '15¢ Green – Statue of Liberty and New York Skyline, 1947\r\n(Air Mail Issue – Engraved, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #C35, issued in 1947, presents a bold air mail design featuring the Statue of Liberty in the foreground with the New York City skyline rising behind it. An aircraft flies above, symbolizing transatlantic service and the expanding reach of American aviation. The inscription “AIR MAIL” crowns the design, while “UNITED STATES POSTAGE” anchors the base. The green monochrome engraving provides depth and contrast, typical of postwar air mail issues. This example is postally used and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #C35 example displays Fine/Very Fine centering and retains its strong, iconic air mail design. However, multiple perforation irregularities and a moderately heavy cancel significantly affect its presentation, resulting in a final grade of 49 (Good/Very Good). Despite these issues, it remains a collectible example of a classic U.S. air mail issue under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.05, 'LA01-0518-01-03-003'),
(585, 'United States', 'C51', '004', 'Used', 'Hinged', 'NG - No Gum', 'Good/Very Good', '5¢ Blue – Jet Airliner, 1960\r\n(Air Mail Issue – Lithographed, Perf 11)\r\n\r\nDesign Overview: \r\nScott #C51, issued in 1960, reflects the dawn of the jet age with a bold, modernist design. A stylized jet aircraft dominates the center, framed by large “US” lettering and vertical graphic elements suggesting speed and technological progress. The clean, geometric composition and strong blue color embody the transition to contemporary design aesthetics in U.S. postage. This example is postally used and without original gum.\r\n\r\nSummary: \r\nThis Scott #C51 example shows Fine/Very Fine centering and retains its bold, modern jet-age design, but is significantly affected by multiple perforation defects and additional minor faults. A moderately heavy cancel further impacts eye appeal, resulting in a final grade of 43 (Good/Very Good). Despite these issues, it remains a collectible representation of early U.S. jet-era air mail under the 2009 PSE Grading Guide.', 0.05, 'LA01-0519-01-02-004');
